window.LI_DATA = {"captured": "2026-10-27", "captures": ["2026-10-16", "2026-10-17", "2026-10-21", "2026-10-22", "2026-10-23", "2026-10-24", "2026-10-27"], "posts": [{"i": 0, "id": 7520436582504656896, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7520436582504656896/", "ts": "2026-10-26T10:50:04+00:00", "date": "2026-10-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "😪 Yet another job opportunity that I will not be selected for... but that could be great from some in my network, so I decided to share for ", "snippet": "😪 Yet another job opportunity that I will not be selected for... but that could be great from some in my network, so I decided to share for all of you", "chars": 823, "text": "😪 Yet another job opportunity that I will not be selected for... but that could be great from some in my network, so I decided to share for all of you\n\nThe FA is looking for a Chief Technology Officer, reporting to the CEO and sitting on the executive team at Wembley, with a remit that covers technology strategy, data, AI, infrastructure, enterprise systems and cybersecurity across an organisation that runs Wembley Stadium, St. George’s Park, the England teams and the FA Cup.\nThe role works closely with the Chief Digital Officer on the fan-facing product roadmap, and with EURO 2028 coming to the UK and Ireland it’s a big moment to take it on.\nFootball experience isn’t required, and the search is being run by Ivy Street, with applications closing on 13 November.\n\nThe Football Association\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/dtbfXY3v", "qtext": "", "imp": 18365, "rx": 20, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["The Football Association", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-27", 18365, 20, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 1, "id": 7519380362020679682, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7519380362020679682/", "ts": "2026-10-23T12:53:01+00:00", "date": "2026-10-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "After the PHP flaw, who in football is running PHP?", "snippet": "💻 🛑 After the PHP flaw, who in football is running PHP?", "chars": 2489, "text": "💻 🛑 After the PHP flaw, who in football is running PHP?\nI scanned the official websites of all 96 clubs in Europe’s top five leagues, from the front door only. Here is the map.\n\nOn Wednesday a PHP vulnerability was in the news worldwide, two flaws in the language that runs most of the web, found this summer by research teams working with AI models. Everyone covered the flaw. I wanted to know whether it touches our corner of the world, so I pointed my YouTube Football Tracker at the 96 club sites it already stores and read what each home page tells any visitor: headers, cookies, the fingerprints a CMS leaves behind. One passive request per club, no probing, and a lot of reading page source by hand where the script fell short. This tells you the stack, never whether a site is patched. Hold that distinction.\n\nWhat the scan found, 23 October 2026:\n🔴 35 of 96 club sites run PHP, 36%. Three of those are inferred, so the declared figure is 32.\n🟢 61 run a non-PHP stack I could name. No site was left unknown.\n🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿 Premier League: 0 of 20 on PHP, and 12 of 20 sit on two shared platforms.\n🇮🇹 Serie A: 10 of 20.\n🇫🇷 Ligue 1: 13 of 18.\n🇪🇸 La Liga and 🇩🇪 Bundesliga: 6 each.\n🛡️ 63 of 96 sit behind a CDN or WAF.\n🌐 33 expose their origin directly.\n📝 WordPress is the most common CMS, 16 of 96.\n⚙️ Next.js is the most common framework, 28 of 96.\n⚠️ The sharp end: 21 clubs run PHP with nothing in front of the origin. I am not naming them.\n\n🤖 My take: probably no major drama this time.\nThe patch is out, the big hosts and CDNs moved before most of us read the headline, and for a club the job is the boring one, update your versions and keep them updated.\nThe exposure in European club football sits in Italy and France, while the Premier League has a different risk, a dozen clubs on two vendors, so one bad day at a vendor is a bad day for half the league. And a bug that sat in PHP for ten years was pulled out by people pointing AI models at the source, and those tools are now in everyone’s hands, including people who will never file a responsible disclosure.\nIf this is the first of many AI-discovered vulnerabilities, how long can a club site stay one version behind?\n\nFull method, charts and the caveats in the article below.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Mercury Intermedia - Quicksilver Stream, the in-house streaming platform.", "qtext": "", "imp": 766, "rx": 12, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-23", 30, 5, 1, 2], ["2026-10-24", 558, 11, 2, 2], ["2026-10-27", 766, 12, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 2, "id": 7518972280912175104, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518972280912175104/", "ts": "2026-10-22T09:51:27+00:00", "date": "2026-10-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "A guy with a scarf - Ep. 60: Stephen Tallent", "snippet": "A guy with a scarf - Ep. 60: Stephen Tallent", "chars": 1612, "text": "A guy with a scarf - Ep. 60: Stephen Tallent\n“That integration layer is basically everything. That is the duct tape that’s holding up these apps.”\nThis is the second clip from my conversation with Stephen Tallent, CEO of Mercury Intermedia and the architect of Quicksilver Stream, and it’s the bit of the episode I keep coming back to, because it describes the part of a streaming service that eats time and budget long after launch day.\n\nStephen’s team kept building the same thing for customer after customer, the layer that sits between the apps on Roku, Apple TV, connected TV, mobile and web and all the systems running behind them. He doesn’t blame the platforms those customers picked, and Mercury is fully in favour of buying a platform instead of building one from scratch, but every platform hits a wall somewhere and someone has to write the code that gets around it. After enough projects the approach started to look the same every time, one back end that every device talks to, orchestrating a lot of different systems underneath, and Quicksilver Stream came out of those repeated patterns.\n\nI like products that are born this way, from work done over and over on real client projects, because the problem they solve has already been paid for by somebody. I advise Mercury on Quicksilver Stream, so read this as an interestied view.\n\nIf your apps are held together by integrations that have been rewritten more than once, have a look at Quicksilver Stream, or send me a message and I’ll put you in touch with Stephen and the team.\n\nTom Richardson Mike Ligon Rusty Mitchell William J. (Bill) Tallent", "qtext": "", "imp": 10634, "rx": 3, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": true, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Stephen Tallent", "person"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["Tom Richardson", "person"], ["Mike Ligon", "person"], ["Rusty Mitchell", "person"], ["William J. (Bill) Tallent", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 2180, "watch_time_s": 14340, "avg_watch_s": 6, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-22", 1538, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-23", 7439, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-24", 10308, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 10634, 3, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 3, "id": 7518579344240250880, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518579344240250880/", "ts": "2026-10-21T07:50:04+00:00", "date": "2026-10-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 things to consider about the Two Circles Fan Economy 2026 report", "snippet": "🔟 10 Things To Consider about the Two Circles Fan Economy 2026 report", "chars": 2968, "text": "🔟 10 Things To Consider about the Two Circles Fan Economy 2026 report\nWhat the report says, where I agree, and why five ten-year-olds in Turin make me doubt the word \"ownership\"\n\nTwo Circles have put a number on fandom, $1.94tn in 2025. I worked with them on NFL Game Pass International, under the same investor, so I read it with respect and with a fair idea of what it is designed to do.\n\n💰 1. The size. $1.94tn, close to 2% of global output. Sports and entertainment together, no split for sport.\n📈 2. The growth. About 6% a year since 2010 against 3.8% for global GDP, on 5.8bn fans.\n🙋 3. Fandom as the higher choice. Trust in institutions falls since 2005, identity shaped by fandom passes 90%.\n📺 4. Media, advertisers and capital follow. 96 of the 100 most watched US broadcasts in 2025 were live sport, $570bn a year of marketing spend, the $55bn EA take-private.\n🧩 5. Three layers around the fan. Interfaces and infrastructure, attention aggregators, IP owners. Sports IP revenue at a record $174bn in 2025.\n🤝 6. Positive-sum economics. Consuming fandom creates more demand: Apple and Netflix sharing F1, ESPN and FOX One, the BBC and Sidemen watchalong.\n🔁 7. Fan marketing runs on existing fans. 80% of value from existing customers, half of it in fewer than 5% of operating days. Loyalty ahead of penetration.\n🎯 8. Moments and inside-out creative. Plan around fixtures and drops, join conversations fans already have, build from their rituals.\n🏢 9. Four case studies. EA Advertising, Google in product partnerships, PL+ in Singapore, Disney moving to lifetime value per fan.\n📘 10. The playbook. Seven imperatives under \"know fans best\", which is also the Two Circles tagline, mapped onto their own services on the last page.\n\n😎 My take.\nNobody owns a fan.\nThe first half draws a market where value comes from collaboration across layers, and by page fourteen the Premier League is \"taking ownership of the fan value chain\". Companies own rights, data, billing relationships and access points. The attachment belongs to the fan, who can cancel, switch, pirate or simply stop caring on a Tuesday.\nEarlier this year I interviewed five ten-year-old teammates of my son for \"Football Through Their Eyes\". Their football lives on the pitch, on live TV, on the PlayStation, in the Panini album and in arguments with friends, and the memory that mattered most to all five was a match they played themselves.\nA designed journey beats disconnected touchpoints, and I have built both. Fans still pick their own path through whatever you designed, and the companies that grow know which role they play in that ecosystem.\nTwo Circles drew the ecosystem well. I would like the second half of the report to trust the first.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Mercury Intermedia - Quicksilver Stream, the in-house streaming platform.", "qtext": "", "imp": 880, "rx": 10, "cm": 4, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Two Circles", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-21", 114, 3, 1, 3], ["2026-10-22", 734, 9, 4, 3], ["2026-10-23", 789, 9, 4, 3], ["2026-10-24", 820, 10, 4, 3], ["2026-10-27", 880, 10, 4, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 4, "id": 7518244551468314625, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7518244551468314625/", "ts": "2026-10-20T09:39:43+00:00", "date": "2026-10-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SPORTEL Monaco 2026 Day 1: A summary", "snippet": "🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2026, Day 1 One day, eight sessions and a lot of familiar faces", "chars": 1706, "text": "🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2026, Day 1 One day, eight sessions and a lot of familiar faces\n🚗 Out of Torino at 5am, breakfast on the beach with Sarah Butler, and a full day on the Speakers Stage Ravel before driving back home to a rainy Torino. There were fewer booths than in the past and fewer tech vendors, but the stands were really well designed and the floor was crowded all day. The thread running through every session for me: live is still what people pay for, and the money now gets made around it.\n\nHere's what I followed 👇\n🤖 AI strategy and governance: my friend Paola Marinone (BuzzMyVideos) on turning the archive into new sponsorable assets, plus Danny Townsend (SURJ) and Prashant Khanna (JioStar)\n🎯 Personalization in live sport: Kish Patel (Dolby OptiView) and two brothers watching the same race in two different ways\n📺 Owning the conversation when you can't own the rights: Scott Melvin (The Overlap) with YouTube\n🔄 A new chapter for Mediapro\n💰 Where the money is going, and the squeeze in the middle: Hillary Mandel (IMG) and Jeffrey Roth (Bruin Capital)\n⚽ Ligue 1+, one year in, and the 30 minutes before kick-off: Cyprien Castanedo (LFP Media)\n🏅 The road to LA28 and athletes known before day 10: Srinath Sripath (IOC) with YouTube\n🏃 The Ultimate Championship, the clock in the middle of the screen and \"you win or nothing\": Christiano Coraglia (World Athletics)\n\n📝 The full summary with quotes, photos and my recap video with Paola is below 👇\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Mercury Intermedia - Quicksilver Stream, the in-house streaming platform.", "qtext": "", "imp": 4046, "rx": 36, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": true, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["Sarah Butler", "person"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Danny Townsend", "person"], ["Prashant Khanna", "person"], ["Kish Patel", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Scott Melvin", "person"], ["The Overlap", "company"], ["Hillary Mandel", "person"], ["Jeffrey Roth", "person"], ["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Srinath Sripath", "person"], ["Christiano Coraglia", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-21", 3057, 21, 1, 1], ["2026-10-22", 3600, 26, 1, 1], ["2026-10-23", 3868, 31, 2, 1], ["2026-10-24", 3938, 33, 2, 1], ["2026-10-27", 4046, 36, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 5, "id": 7517846358607986689, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7517846358607986689/", "ts": "2026-10-19T07:17:26+00:00", "date": "2026-10-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "🏎️ Left Torino by car at 5am, clouds over the beach, and here I am at SPORTEL Monaco again 🌊", "snippet": "🏎️ Left Torino by car at 5am, clouds over the beach, and here I am at SPORTEL Monaco again 🌊", "chars": 1420, "text": "🏎️ Left Torino by car at 5am, clouds over the beach, and here I am at SPORTEL Monaco again 🌊\n\nEvery year I try to read the mood of an event whose market has changed so much that it now pulls in a very different crowd from the one I first met here, so I follow the conference stage first and then walk the booths and catch up with people.\nToday on the Speakers Stage Ravel I'll be following the AI strategy panel with my friend Paola Marinone of BuzzMyVideos, then Kish Patel of Dolby OptiView on personalization in live sports, and in the afternoon An Dang Duy, CIO of World Athletics, talking broadcast graphics, data and digital for athletics, which is one of my many sport passions and a federation that lives right here in Monaco.\nI'm also here for Mercury Intermedia (Nashville and New York) and their Quicksilver Stream platform, an in-house approach that matches what I keep hearing from big clients who want more control over what they build, aggregating best of breed partners on a foundational architecture they own and can grow with for years. It's one approach and it won't suit everybody, so I'm curious to test it on the floor today.\nIf you're in Monaco, ping me and let's grab a coffee ☕\n\nMore from the stage later.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nRepresenting Mercury Intermedia and Quicksilver Stream", "qtext": "", "imp": 1226, "rx": 29, "cm": 4, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Kish Patel", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["An Dang Duy", "person"], ["World Athletics", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 453, "watch_time_s": 8820, "avg_watch_s": 19, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-21", 1073, 27, 4, 0], ["2026-10-22", 1125, 27, 4, 0], ["2026-10-23", 1169, 27, 4, 0], ["2026-10-24", 1194, 27, 4, 0], ["2026-10-27", 1226, 29, 4, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 6, "id": 7516763986965667840, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7516763986965667840/", "ts": "2026-10-16T07:36:29+00:00", "date": "2026-10-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 61: What six years running sport at AWS taught Julie Souza", "snippet": "🎙️ Ep. 61 of A guy with a scarf is out, with Julie Neenan Souza Souza, six years Global Head of Sports at Amazon Web Services (AWS) and now Executive in Residen", "chars": 2489, "text": "🎙️ Ep. 61 of A guy with a scarf is out, with Julie Neenan Souza Souza, six years Global Head of Sports at Amazon Web Services (AWS) and now Executive in Residence at PwC.\nJulie is someone I wanted on the show for a long time. While she carried the AWS badge it was never the right moment, and it got easier once she stepped away, which turned out to be the better version anyway, because the conversation went where it wanted to go, from a scrappy college sports startup to whether AI is making the next generation duller. We crossed paths at a lot of events over those years and I always admired the energy, and that on stage she brought her whole self, humour and opinions included, which is rare for someone carrying a badge that size.\n\nKey points and quotes:\n🪣 The three-bucket test for any technology project: the business problem, the solution deployed, and the outcome with a number on it. \"If we can't explain it in three big buckets, then there's not a story there.\"\n🕶️ The metaverse locker room a team asked AWS to build, and her first question: \"Why do you want to do that?\" She is seeing \"we want an AI strategy\" land in exactly the same shape now.\n⏳ On measuring too early: \"If you're looking for quick wins, you might have a few of those, but I don't think then you're using it to its full power either.\" The NFL kickoff rule was run through 10,000 simulated seasons before it changed.\n🏈 \"When I was at AWS, I wanted the big thorny problems.\"\n🤝 On who should hold a multi-vendor solution together: \"You want somebody you trust quarterbacking that.\" And on doing it all in-house: \"If anybody's telling you they can do it all, that's probably not true.\"\n🏁 On the argument that regulation means falling behind in the global race: \"What are we racing toward? Like human extinction? Because I'm fine falling behind on that.\"\n🧠 On AI and junior jobs: \"How you know to make high judgment decisions is through experience.\" Stanford's August 2026 data has 22 to 25 year olds in AI-exposed jobs 19% behind their peers, with no gap for experienced workers.\n🥱 On offloading your thinking to an agent: \"I think you're going to be a more boring person.\"\n🏟️ \"I don't think live sports can be disintermediated.\"\nAnd boy do I agree with that!!!\n\nFull conversation in the video, companion article below.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nRepresenting Mercury Intermedia and Quicksilver Stream", "qtext": "", "imp": 817, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Julie Neenan Souza", "person"], ["Amazon Web Services (AWS)", "company"], ["PwC", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 300, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 510, 7, 1, 1], ["2026-10-21", 727, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-22", 751, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-23", 772, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-24", 793, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-27", 817, 11, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 7, "id": 7516404142286893056, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7516404142286893056/", "ts": "2026-10-15T07:46:35+00:00", "date": "2026-10-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "⚽ The season on YouTube so far: 106 days", "snippet": "⚽ The season on YouTube so far: 106 days", "chars": 2798, "text": "⚽ The season on YouTube so far: 106 days\nSince 1 July the 101 club and league channels of Europe’s five big leagues have published 21,001 videos and collected 2.86 billion views. I took the YouTube Football Tracker’s season dashboards apart one chart at a time, for all five leagues together and for each league on its own, and published the six pages on my research hub.\n\n📊 All five leagues\nThe Premier League has 1.1 billion views from 4,287 videos, La Liga 920 million from 6,626, so the league that publishes most earns second most, and an English upload is worth three and a half times an Italian one. Shorts carry every league, from 59% of views in England to 75% in France, and the shortest Shorts and the shortest long-form clips earn the most per video everywhere.\n🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿 The Premier League\nThe league channel sets the pace with Shorts that average close to a million views, Manchester City owns long-form at 745 thousand a video, and the five most consistent channels are the five biggest. A Brighton Short with 6.3 million views and 35 comments asks a question of its own.\n🇪🇸 LALIGA\nBarcelona and Real Madrid take 58% of the views, the league channel owns long-form, and Sevilla gets 96% of its views from Shorts. One Alavés Short is 79% of the club’s season, and Rayo Vallecano has published 22 videos in 106 days.\n🇩🇪 Bundesliga\nBayern takes almost half of all Bundesliga views from fewer videos than the league channel, the clubs stream more than any other league and get the least for it, and the biggest video of the season is a referee’s microphone. The smaller clubs win on engagement, publish on the night and vanish for weeks at a time.\n🇮🇹 Lega Serie A\nJuventus sets the pace on Shorts, the league channel owns long-form, Inter publishes more than anyone and gets less per video, and Napoli goes silent for most of the season. One Como Short is the only one-hit wonder in the whole league.\n🇫🇷 Ligue 1\nPSG takes 70% of the views and the whole of the top eleven, with one eight-second Short at 44 million views, the league channel owns long-form, and nine clubs are silent on more than half the days of the season.\n\n👉 All six pages: https://lnkd.in/dNUg7YHp\n\n📺 The data comes from the YouTube Football Tracker, which follows more than 100 club and league channels every night.\n ⚽ If you run social for a club or a league, it shows you what your rivals publish, when and in which format, and which of their videos actually travel.\n ✒️ If you are a sports journalist, it shows you which stories, clubs and formats are working on YouTube this week, with the numbers to back the piece.\n🔗 https://lnkd.in/dTQqMGYJ\n\nthis is nice but if you are serious about directly growing your YouTube channel look no further then BuzzMyVideos and chat with my friends Paola Marinone and Bengu Atamer", "qtext": "", "imp": 469, "rx": 8, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["The Premier League", "company"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 326, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 355, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-21", 418, 8, 1, 1], ["2026-10-22", 438, 8, 2, 1], ["2026-10-23", 446, 8, 2, 1], ["2026-10-24", 455, 8, 2, 1], ["2026-10-27", 469, 8, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 8, "id": 7516165686491082752, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7516165686491082752/", "ts": "2026-10-14T15:59:03+00:00", "date": "2026-10-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter,", "snippet": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter, and I'm sharing it ", "chars": 1355, "text": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter, and I'm sharing it because a few of the tech and ops people in my network could do this job well.\n\nYou'd own EuroLeague TV from ingest and encoding through CDN, DRM and playback, plus the media asset management, the automated content stack, the archive, content protection, geo-blocking and entitlements, onboarding of third-party content, distribution to partner platforms and the game-night incident process, with nobody reporting to you and the commercial side of partner deals handled by someone else.\nIf one of you gets the job, you owe me a dinner in Barcelona.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dRdV3FCN\n\nP.S.\nA note for whoever wrote the posting, with respect, because the role is described in a lot of detail. One of the requirements is being able to write clear technical requirements and then check what vendors deliver against them, so I read the page that way and couldn't find anything on Euroleague's side: no salary range, no package, no benefits, no word on hybrid, and the page goes from \"Your mission\" and \"Your profile\" straight to \"About us\". That's twenty bullets of responsibilities for a Director role and nothing about what you get back, and the good candidates will ask about that in the first call anyway.", "qtext": "", "imp": 976, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["David Cushnan", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 811, 8, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 846, 8, 0, 1], ["2026-10-21", 941, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-22", 951, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-23", 956, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-24", 965, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 976, 9, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 9, "id": 7516040873026101248, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7516040873026101248/", "ts": "2026-10-14T07:43:05+00:00", "date": "2026-10-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Dolby OptiView IBC 2026, booth tour in three interviews and my take on it", "snippet": "📺📲 Dolby OptiView at IBC 2026, three interviews and one booth tour.", "chars": 1952, "text": "📺📲 Dolby OptiView at IBC 2026, three interviews and one booth tour.\n\nI spent three days on the stand in Hall 5 with a microphone and a camera, and this is what the company brought to Amsterdam, in the order the demos ran:\n🧠 Sports Intelligence, new. Two halves: content intelligence scores every moment of a game for excitement and cuts the clips, viewer intelligence reads the audience and predicts, minute by minute, when a fan is about to leave.\n📺 Dolby OptiView Ads cut loose from the player. It now ships as an SDK that works with any player, so you keep what you run and add the ads piece.\n🤝 Google Ad Manager certification, which Dolby’s release calls the first server-guided ad insertion solution verified within the Google ecosystem. Dolby’s own figure from one deployment: 76% average eCPM lift, 132% average fill-rate lift.\n🎨 Dolby Vision through Dolby OptiView for the first time, with one-button up-conversion for the SDR productions that still make up most of live sport. The Seattle Seahawks are first.\n⏱️ Latency as a setting, with synchronised playback across the audience.\n\nThe three people who built it walked me through it on camera:\n🎙️ Giles Baker, SVP Dolby Cloud Solutions, on the strategy and the three-year view\n 🎙️ Dan Coffey, Director of Product, on the portfolio and the clipping demo, recorded at 8am because the day before the booth was too crowded to film in\n 🎙️ Paul Boustead, VP of Product Strategy, on exit risk and the case for lean-back personalisation\n\nI also wrote down what I would push back on, because a sponsored stand is a captive audience and the questions a buyer should ask are harder than the ones I asked on camera.\n👇 Full write-up, with all three videos embedded below.\nMatthias Ankli Jenny Lou Tom Ramsey Kish Patel Michelle Gong\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 11645, "rx": 30, "cm": 3, "rp": 4, "boosted": true, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Giles Baker", "person"], ["Dan Coffey", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Jenny Lou", "person"], ["Tom Ramsey", "person"], ["Kish Patel", "person"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 7999, 26, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 9502, 29, 3, 4], ["2026-10-21", 11568, 30, 3, 4], ["2026-10-22", 11585, 30, 3, 4], ["2026-10-23", 11616, 30, 3, 4], ["2026-10-24", 11626, 30, 3, 4], ["2026-10-27", 11645, 30, 3, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 10, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-10-14", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter,", "snippet": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter, and I'm sharing it ", "chars": 1355, "text": "Euroleague Basketball is hiring a Streaming & Media Technology Director in Barcelona, which I spotted in David Cushnan's Leaders newsletter, and I'm sharing it because a few of the tech and ops people in my network could do this job well.\n\nYou'd own EuroLeague TV from ingest and encoding through CDN, DRM and playback, plus the media asset management, the automated content stack, the archive, content protection, geo-blocking and entitlements, onboarding of third-party content, distribution to partner platforms and the game-night incident process, with nobody reporting to you and the commercial side of partner deals handled by someone else.\nIf one of you gets the job, you owe me a dinner in Barcelona.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dRdV3FCN\n\nP.S.\nA note for whoever wrote the posting, with respect, because the role is described in a lot of detail. One of the requirements is being able to write clear technical requirements and then check what vendors deliver against them, so I read the page that way and couldn't find anything on Euroleague's side: no salary range, no package, no benefits, no word on hybrid, and the page goes from \"Your mission\" and \"Your profile\" straight to \"About us\". That's twenty bullets of responsibilities for a Director role and nothing about what you get back, and the good candidates will ask about that in the first call anyway.", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["David Cushnan", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 8, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", null, 8, 0, 1], ["2026-10-21", null, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-22", null, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-23", null, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-24", null, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", null, 9, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 11, "id": 7515773250577215488, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7515773250577215488/", "ts": "2026-10-13T13:59:39+00:00", "date": "2026-10-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Netflix has bought the 2030 World Cup for Spain and Andorra, announced today with FIFA, and it will stream all 104 matches live, with one ma", "snippet": "Netflix has bought the 2030 World Cup for Spain and Andorra, announced today with FIFA, and it will stream all 104 matches live, with one match per matchday fre", "chars": 1091, "text": "Netflix has bought the 2030 World Cup for Spain and Andorra, announced today with FIFA, and it will stream all 104 matches live, with one match per matchday free and a package with every Spain game, both semi-finals and the final going to a Spanish free-to-air channel that hasn’t been named yet.\n\nI started in this business in 1987 and I have lost count of the times we said a streamer would never buy the biggest tournament in the world, in a country where football sits next to religion, and we said it with the same confidence we used for “nobody will watch a full match on a phone”. It happened anyway, the way most of those things happened, and it has a name, it’s called transformation.\n\nMaybe Netflix is buying events and leaving league seasons to others, so the Serie A and Ligue 1 tenders he expects in the coming months probably won’t get a Netflix bid.\nThe free-to-air channel that gets the Spain package will be buying the national team from Netflix, and Netflix now decides what Spanish fans see for free during a home World Cup, which is a big shift for the Spanish TV market.", "qtext": "", "imp": 529, "rx": 7, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Netflix", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 480, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 485, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-21", 515, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-22", 521, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-23", 521, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-24", 525, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 529, 7, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 12, "id": 7515758810125283329, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7515758810125283329/", "ts": "2026-10-13T13:02:16+00:00", "date": "2026-10-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "🛑 STOP Making This Mistake with Your API Strategy", "snippet": "🛑 STOP Making This Mistake with Your API Strategy", "chars": 2500, "text": "🛑 STOP Making This Mistake with Your API Strategy\nI am sharing this because it truly resonates with my direct experience in streaming as a B2B vendor.\n\n📺 Any streaming service that reaches some scale ends up living on a long list of devices, web, iOS, Android, Apple TV, Android TV, Fire TV, Roku, Samsung, LG, and the list grows with every distribution deal. Each of those apps is often built by a different team or partner, sometimes in a different language, and each one tends to carry its own copy of the rules the business runs on: who is entitled to what, which offer appears where, what happens when a subscription lapses, how a geo restriction applies to a given match. Those copies drift apart over time, so a fix lands on one platform weeks before it reaches another, and the product team spends a lot of its energy keeping ten apps in agreement with each other.\n\nIn the first clip from my conversation with Stephen Tallent, CEO of Mercury Intermedia, he goes straight at this:\n\n ⚙️ \"So we feel it's pretty important to plan from the beginning to have all your platforms talking to the same APIs and provide as much of the business logic as possible there and really let the clients focus on the pixels. Let them focus on taking their time to make it look the way it needs to look, make it feel the way it needs to feel, making sure that user experience is great, and try to unify as much of the business logic as possible outside of the client, which saves time in some areas, takes a little longer in other areas. Again, if you plan for it, it can be done properly.\"\n\n 📶 \"Let the clients focus on the pixels\" is the line I keep coming back to, because it gives the design and app teams back the work only they can do, how the thing feels on a 65-inch TV and on a phone on the bus, and it puts the rules that make money in one place where they get changed once. I also like that he names the cost, since some work gets slower and the payoff only comes if you decide it on day one, which is the exact decision most services skip when they are racing to get onto the next device.\n\n 😎 Full disclosure, Mercury is a current client of A Guy With A Scarf.\nThe full episode is on the A Guy With A Scarf YouTube channel: https://lnkd.in/dqHguVBw\nIf your service runs on eight or more devices, where does your business logic live today, and how many copies of it are there?\n\n 👉 If you want to see how Quicksilver Stream puts this into practice, ask Mercury for a demo here: https://lnkd.in/dNd9UC8R", "qtext": "", "imp": 211, "rx": 3, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Stephen Tallent", "person"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": {"video_views": 85, "watch_time_s": 1367, "avg_watch_s": 16, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 164, 3, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 166, 3, 1, 2], ["2026-10-21", 190, 3, 1, 3], ["2026-10-22", 193, 3, 1, 3], ["2026-10-23", 194, 3, 1, 3], ["2026-10-24", 200, 3, 1, 3], ["2026-10-27", 211, 3, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 13, "id": 7515286387135459328, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7515286387135459328/", "ts": "2026-10-12T05:45:01+00:00", "date": "2026-10-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "🤖 Looking back to look ahead.", "snippet": "🤖 Looking back to look ahead.", "chars": 1717, "text": "🤖 Looking back to look ahead.\nThese photos are from my SPORTEL Monaco editions since the first one in 2011, including 2014, when I organised the first sports startup competition on that same floor.\n\nMonaco has been a second office for me since the 1990s, partly because the IAAF, now World Athletics, has its home there, so the Grimaldi Forum Monaco in October is familiar ground.\n\nThis year I am there on Monday and intend to spend quite some time at the conference area, which at SPORTEL, where the real business happens in the booths, is the unusual choice.\n 🎙️ Paola Marinone, CEO of BuzzMyVideos, on the corporate AI strategy panel at 10:00\n 🎙️ Kish Patel, Partnerships Lead EMEA at Dolby OptiView, at 10:50 on personalisation and storytelling in live sports.\n 🎙️ An Dang Duy, CIO of World Athletics, at 16:45 on reinventing the athletics experience. Athletics has a sport with lots of events running at once and a fan who sees three of them, and I am curious how far the federation has got in rethinking that from the inside.\n\nThe floor tells its own story too: YouTube has a stand among the leagues and federations, while several of the companies I most want to hear are on stage without a booth, which I will come back to in the recap.\nRecap here after the event, with my own view on what was said and what was avoided.\nIf you are in Monaco on Monday, message me.\n\n 🤣 By the way they still have a startup contest to these days ... I am lobbying to have it renamed to Carlo's Pitch Perfect Innovation Contest, I will let you know how it goes.\n\nIn the pictures: Ciaran Quinn Kevin Roberts 🥲 Andy Wasef Fabio Gallo Jonathan Schecter Katie De Paoli Mark Lichtenhein Julie Neenan Souza\nAlso: thanks to Nick Volante", "qtext": "", "imp": 1120, "rx": 20, "cm": 7, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["World Athletics", "company"], ["Grimaldi Forum Monaco", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Kish Patel", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["An Dang Duy", "person"], ["World Athletics", "company"], ["Ciaran Quinn", "person"], ["Kevin Roberts", "person"], ["Andy Wasef", "person"], ["Fabio Gallo", "person"], ["Jonathan Schecter", "person"], ["Katie De Paoli", "person"], ["Mark Lichtenhein", "person"], ["Julie Neenan Souza", "person"], ["Nick Volante", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1025, 19, 7, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1028, 19, 7, 0], ["2026-10-21", 1064, 19, 7, 0], ["2026-10-22", 1071, 19, 7, 0], ["2026-10-23", 1080, 19, 7, 0], ["2026-10-24", 1100, 20, 7, 0], ["2026-10-27", 1120, 20, 7, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 14, "id": 7514358860472684544, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7514358860472684544/", "ts": "2026-10-09T16:19:22+00:00", "date": "2026-10-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "I was not able to attend Leaders in Sport in London, this week so I wanted to ask any of you who were there if tou could be so kind to tell ", "snippet": "I was not able to attend Leaders in Sport in London, this week so I wanted to ask any of you who were there if tou could be so kind to tell me how it went. Than", "chars": 173, "text": "I was not able to attend Leaders in Sport in London, this week so I wanted to ask any of you who were there if tou could be so kind to tell me how it went. Thanks in advance", "qtext": "", "imp": 1279, "rx": 4, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Leaders in Sport", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1265, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1266, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-21", 1272, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-22", 1275, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-23", 1276, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-24", 1276, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-27", 1279, 4, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 15, "id": 7513707653429104640, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7513707653429104640/", "ts": "2026-10-07T21:11:42+00:00", "date": "2026-10-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Wave 2026 in Turin: Berners-Lee, Ive, Kalshi and DeepMind on Stage", "snippet": "Day one of Wave by Vento at OGR Torino, from the front row of Sala Fucine.", "chars": 1428, "text": "Day one of Wave by Vento at OGR Torino, from the front row of Sala Fucine.\nTim Berners-Lee said out loud that he has no fix yet for what AI answers do to the click, the visit and the economics of publishing.\nJony Ive explained why the man who put multi-touch in every pocket gave the Ferrari Luce real analogue needles and physical controls, and admitted the launch reaction taught him that \"you can make something new and different, that's rubbish, that's very easy\".\nKalshi's Luana Lopes Lara told us CNN, Fox, CNBC and CBS now run her odds instead of polls.\nPetar Veličković of Google DeepMind and Giorgio Chiellini agreed that a model can tell you a goal was likely and still cannot tell you why it happened, which is what coaches are paid for. Beeple brought a robot dog that squats and prints what it sees.\nA strong lineup, and I wrote it all up with quotes and photos. I also wrote what made me uncomfortable as a Torinese: an opening that cast the Agnelli family as disruptors, a requested standing ovation for the host, a moderator from a paper his holding company owns, and reserved front rows while the press stood at the back. I stood up for Berners-Lee. As far as I could see, I was the only one.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nI represent Mercury Intermedia's Quicksilver Stream the in-house streaming platform", "qtext": "", "imp": 581, "rx": 3, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Wave by Vento", "company"], ["OGR Torino", "company"], ["Tim Berners-Lee", "person"], ["Luana Lopes Lara", "person"], ["Petar Veličković", "person"], ["Giorgio Chiellini", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 495, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 499, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-21", 524, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-22", 537, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-23", 540, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-24", 554, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 581, 3, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 16, "id": 7513500228126175233, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7513500228126175233/", "ts": "2026-10-07T07:27:28+00:00", "date": "2026-10-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "At an event in my city Torino. Jony Ive and Tim Berners-Lee will be here later this morning. Will try to live blog that.", "snippet": "At an event in my city Torino. Jony Ive and Tim Berners-Lee will be here later this morning. Will try to live blog that.", "chars": 299, "text": "At an event in my city Torino. Jony Ive and Tim Berners-Lee will be here later this morning. Will try to live blog that.\nToday I have dual hat beyond the scarf. I am here both for my usual A guy with a scarf and also for TORINO e CULTURA per cui scriverò anche in Italiano.\n\nOGR Torino Wave by Vento", "qtext": "", "imp": 196, "rx": 6, "cm": 4, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["TORINO e CULTURA", "showcase"], ["OGR Torino", "company"], ["Wave by Vento", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 175, 5, 4, 0], ["2026-10-17", 180, 6, 4, 0], ["2026-10-21", 192, 6, 4, 0], ["2026-10-22", 194, 6, 4, 0], ["2026-10-23", 195, 6, 4, 0], ["2026-10-24", 195, 6, 4, 0], ["2026-10-27", 196, 6, 4, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 17, "id": 7513164018946326528, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7513164018946326528/", "ts": "2026-10-06T09:11:29+00:00", "date": "2026-10-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Mercury, Quicksilver Stream and a streaming platform you can change without rebuilding it", "snippet": "▶️ A guy with a scarf NEW EPISODE", "chars": 2787, "text": "▶️ A guy with a scarf NEW EPISODE\nCould you run an in-house streaming platform without building every piece of it yourself, and swap an authentication, video or analytics vendor without touching a single client app?\n\n🎙️ That is the question behind my conversation with Stephen Tallent, who founded Mercury Intermedia in 1996 and carries the CEO and CTO roles together. Mercury is a client of mine, so read this with that in mind, and I can vouch for what I saw on screen and what he said on the record.\n\n1️⃣ Control of the endpoints your apps talk to. Stephen prefers the word control to ownership, and Quicksilver Stream keeps the business logic in one set of APIs so the apps can \"focus on the pixels\". His example is an authentication SDK dropped into every client app, which saves time on day one and lands the change in every app at once the day that vendor gets acquired.\n2️⃣ No lock-in, including to Mercury. I challenged a vendor for saying it, and his answer was \"if you provide a good product and you provide good service, they're gonna stay with you whether they have to or not\". Customers keep their data, can swap any piece of the stack and can bring development in-house, which Stephen says a dozen customers have done with Mercury. He also named the tension himself: the customer needs control and the vendor needs to make money.\n3️⃣ A producer builds a page by asking for it, no box-ticking and menu-scrolling. In the demo I described a feature I wanted, a producer testing a new page layout in plain language, and the team did it in front of me on the working platform. I had thought my idea was clever and it already existed, so I felt a mix of embarrassment and surprise.\nOne of the most clever use of AI I have seen in a B2B product so far. Great news for streaming platform producer teams.\n\nWhat intrigued me about Quicksilver Stream is that it is not yet-another-streaming-platform. It sits between the client apps and the vendors a customer chooses, so video platform, or an authentication provider can change underneath it.\nThis conversation exists because the Mercury team read two of my articles, and one sentence in the second, \"the stack is the product\", led to a phone call with Tom Richardson, some strategy work together and now this interview. I write nearly every day and rarely see where a piece ends up, so for anyone asking what writing on LinkedIn returns, this is one of the clearest answers I have.\n\nThe full conversation, with the quotes and three questions to take to your own architecture, is in the article and YouTube video or Spotify podcast.\n\nTo see Quicksilver Stream, visit mercury.io and schedule a demo, and if you would like an introduction, write to me and tell me what you want to see.\nMike Ligon\n\n#streaming #ai #sportstech #b2b #ott", "qtext": "", "imp": 8582, "rx": 29, "cm": 2, "rp": 20, "boosted": true, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Stephen Tallent", "person"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["Tom Richardson", "person"], ["Mike Ligon", "person"]], "tags": ["streaming", "ai", "sportstech", "b2b", "ott"], "series": [], "disc": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 8468, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-17", 8474, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-21", 8544, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-22", 8550, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-23", 8554, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-24", 8568, 29, 2, 20], ["2026-10-27", 8582, 29, 2, 20]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 18, "id": 7512874528348274688, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7512874528348274688/", "ts": "2026-10-05T14:01:10+00:00", "date": "2026-10-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 things to consider about the second Genius Sports CEO letter", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider about the second Genius Sports CEO letter 📝", "chars": 2813, "text": "10 Things To Consider about the second Genius Sports CEO letter 📝\nIn March I wrote about Mark Locke's first letter, the one defending the $1.2 billion Legend deal. Seven months later there is a second one, \"Americans are Going to Keep Wagering\", and this time the subject is prediction markets. Same format, same footnotes, same forward-looking statements notice at the bottom, and the same audience: investors asking what happens to Genius if the courts or Congress take sports contracts away from Kalshi and Polymarket.\n\nHis point in one sentence 🎯 Americans will keep wagering on sport whatever the courts decide, and Genius gets paid however that wagering is routed, because it supplies the exchanges, the market makers behind them and the sportsbooks competing with them for the same customer. The rules decide who pays Genius; the demand, in his telling, guarantees that someone does.\n\nThe 🔟 things to consider:\n1️⃣ It is an investor document that happens to be on LinkedIn, written while the Kalshi cases and the Supreme Court petition are still open.\n2️⃣ The thesis separates the demand for wagering from the structure that carries it, and never describes a scenario in which Genius loses.\n3️⃣ Settlement is where he is strongest: the Western Michigan at Michigan market on 5 September, $18.6 million paid out on a broadcast clock that had one second put back.\n4️⃣ The economics claim, that an exchange dollar pays Genius as well as a sportsbook dollar, \"match or exceed\" in his words, has no public number under it.\n5️⃣ Two Eilers & Krejcik percentages, 69 per cent and 2 per cent, carry the whole \"net benefit\" argument, and DraftKings and FanDuel read the same monitor.\n6️⃣ The politics paragraph names Donald Trump Jr. and Stephanie Cutter in the same breath as a bipartisan bill to ban the contracts, which is more candid than the format usually allows.\n7️⃣ Connecticut's subpoenas have two Genius entities on the list, and the letter gets past that in two sentences.\n8️⃣ The February letter and this one are one argument in two parts, and Prediction.com launched two days later to prove it.\n9️⃣ The timing tells you what the letter is for: after Q2, after the exchange deals, in the first NFL season with real exchange volume, built to be quoted back in a year.\n🔟 What is missing: Sportradar, the leagues, the taxes already in force in Illinois and coming in North Carolina, and anywhere outside the US.\n\nFor paid members there is an appendix 📎 with the company timeline from Betgenius in 2001 to Prediction.com, four years of financials with the three 2026 guidance revisions side by side, the Legend deal terms, and a short Mark Locke bio built only on what I could source.\n\nDo you buy the settlement argument, and would you believe \"match or exceed\" before seeing a number? Share what you think 👇", "qtext": "", "imp": 1072, "rx": 3, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Genius Sports", "company"], ["Mark Locke", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1039, 4, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1039, 4, 2, 2], ["2026-10-21", 1056, 4, 2, 2], ["2026-10-22", 1061, 4, 2, 2], ["2026-10-23", 1063, 4, 2, 2], ["2026-10-24", 1065, 3, 2, 2], ["2026-10-27", 1072, 3, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 19, "id": 7512412948917710849, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7512412948917710849/", "ts": "2026-10-04T07:27:00+00:00", "date": "2026-10-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "FIFA wants fan journey tech for the Women’s World Cup 2027, next-generation wearables and connected substitution boards", "snippet": "⚽ 🤖 FIFA wants fan journey tech for the Women’s World Cup 2027, next-generation wearables and connected substitution boards.", "chars": 2253, "text": "⚽ 🤖 FIFA wants fan journey tech for the Women’s World Cup 2027, next-generation wearables and connected substitution boards.\n\nEarlier this year, at the Bundesliga SportsInnovation event in Düsseldorf, I shared a stage with Nicolas Evans from FIFA, talking 3D data, officiating and the Football Technology Centre, and what stayed with me was how much of FIFA's innovation work now runs through structured channels rather than conversations in a hospitality box.\n\nThis week those channels opened three new doors. The FIFA Innovation Programme has published three challenges:\n⚽ Fan journey technology for the FIFA Women's World Cup 2027 in Brazil, which FIFA says is its first innovation challenge tied to a specific competition. Applications close on 1 November 2026, and the criteria ask for a fully operational solution already delivered across multiple projects, commercial partner integration and measurable ROI. Four weeks is short, but count backwards from kick-off on 24 June 2027 through FIFA's five-step process and there is little other room to deploy anything.\n⚽ Next-generation sports wearables, measuring hydration, load, recovery and stress in real time. Working prototype minimum, no deadline, with validation through FIFA's research partners and academic institutions on offer.\n⚽ Connected substitution boards, hardware that links to the substitution software FIFA already runs and shows player numbers to the stadium. Lightweight, durable, three hours of battery, manual fallback, working prototype minimum, no deadline.\n\nAll three go through the same portal, which runs on a video pitch platform, so expect to pitch on camera.\n\nIf you run fan journey, ticketing, accessibility or in-stadium technology that is already live with reputable clients, the Women's World Cup brief is written for you and the clock is running, so apply. If you build wearables or match operations hardware, the other two are open with no deadline, so get your prototype evidence in order and go. Know a company that fits one of the three? Tag them below.\n\nAnd if you are preparing an application and want a second pair of eyes on the pitch, the positioning or the ROI story, message me.\n\nHat tip to Juan Iraola, whose post is where I first saw the announcement.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2511, "rx": 33, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Bundesliga", "company"], ["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["Juan Iraola", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2486, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2487, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-21", 2501, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-22", 2504, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-23", 2505, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-24", 2506, 33, 3, 1], ["2026-10-27", 2511, 33, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 20, "id": 7512128817939918849, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7512128817939918849/", "ts": "2026-10-03T12:37:58+00:00", "date": "2026-10-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "True story.", "snippet": "True story.", "chars": 428, "text": "True story.\nI was scrolling and my eyes immediately stopped on OTT.\nStreaming. Obviously.\nExcept it wasn’t.\nIt was a monthly planner in Italian.\nIn Italian, the second half of the year goes:\nGIU · LUG · AGO · SET · OTT · NOV · DIC\nAnd OTT is simply ottobre. October.\nIt took my brain a few seconds to accept that nobody was talking about streaming.\nAm I slightly biased and obsessed with streaming?\nPossibly.\nHappy OTT everyone.", "qtext": "", "imp": 230, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 223, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 224, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-21", 226, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-22", 227, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-23", 228, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-24", 229, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 230, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 21, "id": 7511333550453850112, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7511333550453850112/", "ts": "2026-10-01T07:57:52+00:00", "date": "2026-10-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Fanatics Betting & Gaming’s CTO on Building a Sportsbook With AI", "snippet": "Fanatics Betting & Gaming's CTO on Building a Sportsbook With AI", "chars": 2128, "text": "Fanatics Betting & Gaming's CTO on Building a Sportsbook With AI\nIan Botts in conversation with Nick Meacham (SportsPro CEO) at SportsPro AI and Tech 2026\n\nIan Botts runs technology for Fanatics Betting & Gaming, about 920 engineers by his count, gave the funniest, which at an AI conference at 9:30 is a public service.\n\nMy take: he did the rollout in the right order, and almost nobody does. AI went inside the tools people already used, then a platform made the model a swappable part, and only after that did agents go in front of customers. The usual conference version starts with the customer-facing demo and ends with a slide.\n\nA few lines worth keeping:\n\"What problem am I trying to solve?\" His answer when the industry told everyone to adopt AI.\n\"I'm nothing but Claude from here to eternity,\" followed a breath later by \"who knows what's going to happen tomorrow.\" Which is why he built the platform model-agnostic.\n\"Engineers are problem solvers. Writing code is a very small part of building software that is either at scale, works correctly, or God forbid, is actually secure.\"\nAnd on his own coding: he has not shipped production code in a decade, and was asked to stop when it got bad.\n\nThe full piece on A guy with a scarf covers:\n🤖 The sportsbook as a spreadsheet, and why Fanatics bets on FanCash instead of bonuses\n🤖 The adoption numbers (4.7 days a week for engineers) and what they do and do not tell you\n🤖 Meemaw and Daisy, the two agents in production, one beside support staff and one building bet slips in chat\n🤖 Fuel, the reuse library behind a prediction market built in 100 days\n🤖 My reservation about measuring habit instead of outcomes\n\nPaid subscribers also get the appendices: Fanatics' three businesses with 2024 revenue by segment, the financials I could source and the ones I could not, a timeline from GSI Commerce to Ontario, and Ian's bio. Those took longer to check than the article took to write.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nRepresenting Mercury Intermedia and Quicksilver Stream", "qtext": "", "imp": 8929, "rx": 85, "cm": 3, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Fanatics", "company"], ["Ian Botts", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 8883, 85, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 8891, 85, 3, 3], ["2026-10-21", 8910, 85, 3, 3], ["2026-10-22", 8913, 85, 3, 3], ["2026-10-27", 8929, 85, 3, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 22, "id": 7511015251559989248, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7511015251559989248/", "ts": "2026-09-30T10:53:03+00:00", "date": "2026-09-30", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for AI", "snippet": "🤖 What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for A", "chars": 120, "text": "🤖 What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for A", "qtext": "🤖 What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for AI\n\nSean Hunter of Fox, Daniel Kirschner of Greenfly and Chris Wilson of MediaKind on the panel I moderated at SportsPro AI and Tech 2026.\n\nYesterday I noticed a lot of sports people explaining AI and a lot of AI people explaining sports, so I picked a broadcaster, a content operations platform and a video infrastructure vendor and asked each to stay in their own field.\nSean said the data team at Fox now works directly with journalists and commentators, and data scientists \"ex", "imp": 302, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 271, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 271, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-21", 288, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-22", 295, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-27", 302, 4, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 23, "id": 7511015040490029056, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7511015040490029056/", "ts": "2026-09-30T10:52:13+00:00", "date": "2026-09-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for AI", "snippet": "🤖 What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for AI", "chars": 1883, "text": "🤖 What a Broadcaster, a Content Platform and a Video Infrastructure Vendor Say About Building a Sports Media Stack for AI\n\nSean Hunter of Fox, Daniel Kirschner of Greenfly and Chris Wilson of MediaKind on the panel I moderated at SportsPro AI and Tech 2026.\n\nYesterday I noticed a lot of sports people explaining AI and a lot of AI people explaining sports, so I picked a broadcaster, a content operations platform and a video infrastructure vendor and asked each to stay in their own field.\nSean said the data team at Fox now works directly with journalists and commentators, and data scientists \"exist in greater numbers even than before.\" Fox shipped an ElevenLabs feature for the World Cup where fans put questions to Colin Cowherd, a \"catch me up with the highlights\" video pipeline, and announced agentic ad buying at Cannes Lions. His warning on AI features: \"if you just sort of stick a chat box onto an existing product, no one knows what that's for.\"\nDaniel, on the idea that AI makes building easier: \"you can build a lot more and a lot more complex things and a lot more effective things.\" He compared it to spreadsheets, which people expected to end accountants. Greenfly says 25,000 athletes use its app. His view of Europe against the US: Europe asks where to store content, and the US asks how to make money on it.\nChris described how MediaKind, now combined with Harmonic, exposes its platform through MCP so that \"Greenfly can plug in their agent and drive our video pipe,\" and Fox can do the same. On the pace of change: \"Some of those models will not be here in three months,\" so the value is in being able to swap them and fail fast.\nI closed on a sour note, since nobody has cracked fan engagement yet. Daniel's reply: \"I don't think it's a solvable problem.\" Sean agreed. And everyone means something different when they say fan engagement.\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1071, "rx": 19, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sean Hunter", "person"], ["Daniel Kirschner", "person"], ["Chris Wilson", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1052, 19, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1052, 19, 4, 2], ["2026-10-22", 1060, 19, 4, 2], ["2026-10-27", 1071, 19, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 24, "id": 7510944227401330689, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510944227401330689/", "ts": "2026-09-30T06:10:50+00:00", "date": "2026-09-30", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro AI + Tech 2026: what sport is getting back from AI", "snippet": "SportsPro AI + Tech 2026: what sport is getting back from AI", "chars": 60, "text": "SportsPro AI + Tech 2026: what sport is getting back from AI", "qtext": "I spent day one of SportsPro AI + Tech at London Stadium and posted notes from the floor all day.\nA question I asked myself on the way out: have I learned something new?\nMeh, not really. But do I have a better understanding of what our ecosystem is doing in AI and tech, what people are thinking, trying, doing, succeeding at and failing at? Definitely yes.\nSo, a good day for me.\n\nThe sessions worth remembering were the ones where somebody said what didn’t work, why they stopped, or how long it took before anything paid off.\nI appreciate those who put the people involved at the centre of any sol", "imp": 143, "rx": 0, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 94, 0, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 100, 0, 0, 0], ["2026-10-22", 122, 0, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 143, 0, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 25, "id": 7510944005531025408, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510944005531025408/", "ts": "2026-09-30T06:09:57+00:00", "date": "2026-09-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro AI + Tech 2026: what sport is getting back from AI", "snippet": "I spent day one of SportsPro AI + Tech at London Stadium and posted notes from the floor all day.", "chars": 2888, "text": "I spent day one of SportsPro AI + Tech at London Stadium and posted notes from the floor all day.\nA question I asked myself on the way out: have I learned something new?\nMeh, not really. But do I have a better understanding of what our ecosystem is doing in AI and tech, what people are thinking, trying, doing, succeeding at and failing at? Definitely yes.\nSo, a good day for me.\n\nThe sessions worth remembering were the ones where somebody said what didn’t work, why they stopped, or how long it took before anything paid off.\nI appreciate those who put the people involved at the centre of any solution, not in a romantic way, but because, as with every process, if people are not properly on board, the results are rarely positive.\nI say this with humility and respect (as someone who wanders around town with the laptop open in his hands, because there is always a Claude Code session running): sometimes it felt like people from Sports were “lecturing” us about AI, and people from the AI world were explaining sport to us. I guess it’s a very human (not artificial) way of reassuring the audience that they get it, when in reality they are just starting a journey. No need for that. Let’s all do something more useful: let’s double down on what we are experts in (if anything, in my case) and share what we have learned, including what failed, and the half-formed ideas we would bet on, so that somebody in the room holding the other half can pick them up.\n\nOne thing I observed is that we went from AI as magic, “look what I did with it”, to the “AI needs to serve a business objective” mantra, or mood.\nGood. But...\nThe same question kept coming back on stage all day: should you start with the technology or with a business problem? I couldn’t take a position, because I think the question is wrong, or at least neither answer solves it. If I start from a business problem without knowing what the technology can now do differently, I won’t see the problem at all, since my very human brain doesn’t register something as a problem until it has a hint that it can be solved at all, and starting from the technology gives you demos and pilots that die quietly. Tech and business have to be a conversation, and I’ve lived that for most of my career: technical people need to understand the business at least well enough to know which problems are worth their time, business people need to understand the technology at least well enough to imagine what has become possible, and the two sides progress and innovate together.\n😎 That is my take.\n\nMichael Long Chris Stone Steve McCaskill Sepi Motamedi Santiago Manso Chris Golier Carla Palomo Patrick Mostboeck Bastian Zuber Stuart Fenton Bhavesh Vaghela Melanie Moeller Richard Bowman James Ukleja Gabriel Cocca Melissa Lawton Anuroop Talwar Melissa S. Eleni Sharp Hosni Ajala Teodora Ivanova - Limon Alejandro Merino-Madrid Ed Hornby", "qtext": "", "imp": 3365, "rx": 38, "cm": 5, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Michael Long", "person"], ["Chris Stone", "person"], ["Steve McCaskill", "person"], ["Sepi Motamedi", "person"], ["Santiago Manso", "person"], ["Chris Golier", "person"], ["Carla Palomo", "person"], ["Bastian Zuber", "person"], ["Stuart Fenton", "person"], ["Bhavesh Vaghela", "person"], ["Melanie Moeller", "person"], ["Richard Bowman", "person"], ["James Ukleja", "person"], ["Gabriel Cocca", "person"], ["Melissa Lawton", "person"], ["Anuroop Talwar", "person"], ["Melissa S.", "person"], ["Eleni Sharp", "person"], ["Hosni Ajala", "person"], ["Teodora Ivanova - Limon", "person"], ["Alejandro Merino-Madrid", "person"], ["Ed Hornby", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3329, 38, 5, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3337, 38, 5, 1], ["2026-10-22", 3355, 38, 5, 1], ["2026-10-27", 3365, 38, 5, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 26, "id": 7510620663834054656, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510620663834054656/", "ts": "2026-09-29T08:45:06+00:00", "date": "2026-09-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro AI + Tech started just now with Chris Stone.", "snippet": "SportsPro AI + Tech started just now with Chris Stone.", "chars": 492, "text": "SportsPro AI + Tech started just now with Chris Stone.\nBack to the London Olympic Stadium with huge memories of 2012.\nI will try to share interesting stuff today and I will be on stage tomorrow morning.\n\nTo avoid spamming everybody here, follow my summaries on Substack Notes: https://lnkd.in/dYtbZwww\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nRepresenting Mercury Intermedia and Quicksilver Stream", "qtext": "", "imp": 1350, "rx": 34, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Chris Stone", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1310, 34, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1316, 34, 2, 1], ["2026-10-22", 1333, 34, 2, 1], ["2026-10-27", 1350, 34, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 27, "id": 7510562273338257408, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510562273338257408/", "ts": "2026-09-29T04:53:05+00:00", "date": "2026-09-29", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?", "snippet": "🏟️ Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?", "chars": 53, "text": "🏟️ Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?", "qtext": "🏟️ Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?\n 🇩🇪 Germany has picked Munich as its candidate for the 2036, 2040 or 2044 Summer Games, reusing the 1972 Olympic Park.\nI live in an Olympic city, Torino, and I'm writing this from London, where I spent three years on the digital side of the 2012 Games with LOCOG and the BBC. Right after my first Games I wrote a piece called \"There is nothing like the Olympics\", and I still feel the same, so when Munich was picked on Saturday I had to write about it. I also have my own memories of that stadium, including a December night in the 90s under a s", "imp": 296, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 240, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 243, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-22", 269, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 296, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 28, "id": 7510561238649614336, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510561238649614336/", "ts": "2026-09-29T04:48:58+00:00", "date": "2026-09-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?", "snippet": "🏟️ Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?", "chars": 1126, "text": "🏟️ Munich wants the Olympics back. Is it a good idea?\n 🇩🇪 Germany has picked Munich as its candidate for the 2036, 2040 or 2044 Summer Games, reusing the 1972 Olympic Park.\nI live in an Olympic city, Torino, and I'm writing this from London, where I spent three years on the digital side of the 2012 Games with LOCOG and the BBC. Right after my first Games I wrote a piece called \"There is nothing like the Olympics\", and I still feel the same, so when Munich was picked on Saturday I had to write about it. I also have my own memories of that stadium, including a December night in the 90s under a snow-covered roof that creaked a lot more than I liked ❄️\nIn the article:\n 1️⃣ What Munich is proposing, and how close the vote against Cologne-Rhine-Ruhr was\n 2️⃣ The 2025 referendum, and how it compares with Munich's failed 2018 and 2022 bids\n 3️⃣ What reusing a 1972 park costs, starting with a €283m roof\n 4️⃣ Why the broadcast operation has shrunk and is still a big job\n 5️⃣ My problem with 2036, a hundred years after Berlin 1936\n 6️⃣ The IOC timetable, and why Germany has to settle the year before March 2027\nThoughts?", "qtext": "", "imp": 1244, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1232, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1232, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-22", 1240, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-27", 1244, 11, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 29, "id": 7510268530295185409, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510268530295185409/", "ts": "2026-09-28T09:25:51+00:00", "date": "2026-09-28", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 things to consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEOs’ letter", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEO's letter", "chars": 69, "text": "10 Things To Consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEO's letter", "qtext": "10 Things To Consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEO's letter\n\n💌 Cloudflare's 2026 founders' letter, signed by CEO Matthew Prince and co-founder Michelle Zatlyn, says machines now make more requests on the web than people do, and projects 1,000 times more within five years. Here is what that means for sports media, rights holders and the vendors who sell to them.\n\nI saw Dan Rayburn's post about the letter on Saturday night and got hooked. Some of this is technical, and I wrote it so anyone in the industry can follow.\n\n1️⃣ The bot crossover arrived eighteen months early, and the company", "imp": 266, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 241, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 243, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-22", 252, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 266, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 30, "id": 7510268023094747136, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510268023094747136/", "ts": "2026-09-28T09:23:50+00:00", "date": "2026-09-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 things to consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEOs’ letter", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEO's letter", "chars": 2695, "text": "10 Things To Consider after Cloudflare turned 16 and the CEO's letter\n\n💌 Cloudflare's 2026 founders' letter, signed by CEO Matthew Prince and co-founder Michelle Zatlyn, says machines now make more requests on the web than people do, and projects 1,000 times more within five years. Here is what that means for sports media, rights holders and the vendors who sell to them.\n\nI saw Dan Rayburn's post about the letter on Saturday night and got hooked. Some of this is technical, and I wrote it so anyone in the industry can follow.\n\n1️⃣ The bot crossover arrived eighteen months early, and the company with the widest view of web traffic missed its own forecast by more than a year\n2️⃣ Agent traffic shows up on the hosting bill first: a fan asks one question, a dozen sports sites pay to answer it, one gets the click\n3️⃣ Page views were already a weak currency for sponsor reports and agents make them weaker, in both directions\n4️⃣ Since 15 September, new sites with ads block AI agents by default, with separate switches for search, AI training and AI agents\n5️⃣ Sports already has a pricing model for machines, in official data feeds sold to bookmakers, which most publishers are only starting to build\n6️⃣ Italy and Spain made Cloudflare the villain of the piracy fight, with a €14.2 million AGCOM fine, LaLiga blocking 3,000 IPs a weekend and collateral damage on both sides\n7️⃣ Agents tend to recommend whoever is already known, which is bad news for every young sports-tech vendor with a better product and a thinner footprint\n8️⃣ The CEO thinks credibility will beat popularity, and the formats an agent has to cite by name are the ones that keep their value\n9️⃣ A fifth of the web sits behind one company, which is convenient until it writes your crawler policy or goes down on a matchday\n🔟 Cloudflare cut its \"measurers\" and kept building, and sports organisations are heavy on measurers\n\n🤖 My position: treat agent access as a rights product, priced and licensed with the same seriousness as a betting feed, and tell sponsors plainly which part of the audience is people, which part is machines and which part never visited because an agent answered for them. If you are working on either of those problems, get in touch. I want to compare notes.\n\n😁 Paid members on Substack get the companion appendix: what Cloudflare is and how it makes money, a timeline from Harvard business plan to the 2026 letter, a plain-language guide to what it sells today written for people who run sports and media businesses, and bios of Matthew Prince and Michelle Zatlyn.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 6751, "rx": 8, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": true, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Cloudflare", "company"], ["Matthew Prince", "person"], ["Michelle Zatlyn", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 6730, 8, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 6732, 8, 1, 2], ["2026-10-22", 6739, 8, 1, 2], ["2026-10-27", 6751, 8, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 31, "id": 7510231417763762176, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7510231417763762176/", "ts": "2026-09-28T06:58:23+00:00", "date": "2026-09-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Love the \"Booth Tour\" concept, I did not come up  with it, the marketing team at Dolby OptiView did.", "snippet": "Love the \"Booth Tour\" concept, I did not come up  with it, the marketing team at Dolby OptiView did.", "chars": 868, "text": "Love the \"Booth Tour\" concept, I did not come up  with it, the marketing team at Dolby OptiView did.\nIt's a pretty accurate picture of what the weekend in Amsterdam looked like.\nWorking on the my IBC summary that will be posted this Thursday.\nI appreciated the patience of Dan Coffey, Paul Boustead and Giles Baker during the interviews and the support from Matthias Ankli, Jenny Lou and the team throughout the show.\nLeaving for London today - on stage at SportsPro AI+Tech on Wednesday morning.\nI will be back in the stadium where in 2012, after three years of working on the Olympics digital projects, I was lucky to get to watch amazing 100m and 200m finals few steps from the finish line.\nStill grateful to Emanuele Perotti Nigra to this day for that.\n\nThanks Dolby OptiView for including me in your IBC - International Broadcasting Convention newsletter summary.", "qtext": "", "imp": 892, "rx": 22, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Dan Coffey", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Giles Baker", "person"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Jenny Lou", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Emanuele Perotti Nigra", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 875, 21, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 878, 21, 2, 0], ["2026-10-22", 891, 22, 2, 0], ["2026-10-27", 892, 22, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 32, "id": 7509562863842402304, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7509562863842402304/", "ts": "2026-09-26T10:41:47+00:00", "date": "2026-09-26", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ben Swanton wrote this after I asked him to go deeper on a line (6.) in his IBC post, and it's worth reading if you are anywhere near a seni", "snippet": "Ben Swanton wrote this after I asked him to go deeper on a line (6.) in his IBC post, and it's worth reading if you are anywhere near a senior hire in sports an", "chars": 1469, "text": "Ben Swanton wrote this after I asked him to go deeper on a line (6.) in his IBC post, and it's worth reading if you are anywhere near a senior hire in sports and media tech.\n\nHis point is that most hiring briefs describe a job and leave out the ownership clock it is being hired into, so someone brought in during year two of a hold period to build markets and a team can struggle badly in year four, when the same title means producing a defensible growth story on a fixed timeline while half the leadership team watches for the exit.\n\nI'd add the vendor view, because a lot of the companies selling streaming and broadcast tech are PE or VC backed and sitting somewhere on that clock, and their customers end up living with it. A sales leader hired late in a hold period gets pushed toward bookings and long contracts that read well in a data room, and the broadcaster on the other side of the table usually notices at renewal, when the roadmap conversation turns into a pricing conversation.\nBen's two questions, what has to be true in 24 months and who has to be convinced of it, are just as useful on the buying side, and I'd ask them before signing a five-year deal with any supplier.\n\nSo which clock is your main vendor running on?\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media.", "qtext": "A few people picked up on the last point in my IBC post, that almost everyone is hiring to an ownership clock. Carlo De Marchis asked me to go deeper, so here it is.\n\nI wrote recently that candidates should diligence the cap table before they sign. This is the other side of that.\n\nMost hiring briefs describe a job. Very few describe the clock it is being hired into, and the clock usually decides whether the hire works.\n\nYear two of a hold period and year four are different jobs with the same title. In year two you are hiring someone to build: new markets, new segments, a team that will take th", "imp": 459, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Ben Swanton", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "text", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 397, 4, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 404, 4, 1, 1], ["2026-10-27", 459, 4, 1, 1]], "quoted": "Ben Swanton"}, {"i": 33, "id": 7509517024306569216, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7509517024306569216/", "ts": "2026-09-26T07:39:38+00:00", "date": "2026-09-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "title": "My online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media is available to all my Premium annual Substack subscribers included i", "snippet": "My online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media is available to all my Premium annual Substack subscribers included in that package.", "chars": 564, "text": "My online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media is available to all my Premium annual Substack subscribers included in that package.\nif you work in sports and media tech on the vendor side it's a great resource for either people starting in the industry to untangle the hidden complexities of out sector and for people with experience to compare and rethink.\nA digital copy of the book is also included.\nHave a look at the course outline and let me know if you are interested!\nhttps://lnkd.in/eHKzXVrV\n\nA guy with a scarf #sportstech #vendor", "qtext": "", "imp": 5034, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": true, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "vendor"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 5031, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 5031, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 5034, 6, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 34, "id": 7509210111282966529, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7509210111282966529/", "ts": "2026-09-25T11:20:04+00:00", "date": "2026-09-25", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The woman I am married to...", "snippet": "The woman I am married to...", "chars": 28, "text": "The woman I am married to...", "qtext": "On Tuesday 22 September 2026 newcleo started trading on Nasdaq under ticker symbol NWCL.\n \nIn August 2021 I sat down with Stefano Buono and Elisabeth Rizzotti for an interview. At the time, newcleo had not even been officially launched yet.\nI remember leaving that interview thinking: they are going to do something big.\n\nI came from a completely different industry and knew very little about nuclear energy. But I immediately saw the ambition, the vision and the sense of purpose that was driving them.\n \nA very special friend, Christopher Kenna, told me once that what drives me is purpose. Looking", "imp": 727, "rx": 23, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 722, 23, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 722, 23, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 727, 23, 0, 1]], "quoted": "Katie De Paoli"}, {"i": 35, "id": 7509169400957100033, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7509169400957100033/", "ts": "2026-09-25T08:38:18+00:00", "date": "2026-09-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Every time someone writes on LinkedIn a post that contains the words “… this will change XYZ forever,” in Malaysia a beautiful butterfly die", "snippet": "Every time someone writes on LinkedIn a post that contains the words “… this will change XYZ forever,” in Malaysia a beautiful butterfly dies.", "chars": 142, "text": "Every time someone writes on LinkedIn a post that contains the words “… this will change XYZ forever,” in Malaysia a beautiful butterfly dies.", "qtext": "", "imp": 607, "rx": 8, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 601, 8, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 601, 8, 2, 2], ["2026-10-27", 607, 8, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 36, "id": 7508709813267304448, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7508709813267304448/", "ts": "2026-09-24T02:12:04+00:00", "date": "2026-09-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "Make LinkedIn Better for Creators", "title": "Just saw a new cool feature from LinkedIn!", "snippet": "Just saw a new cool feature from LinkedIn!", "chars": 197, "text": "Just saw a new cool feature from LinkedIn!\nFor new posts every handle shows a tooltip for the profile.\nHad to use Dani Markovits and Yessi Bello-Perez for this...\n\nMake LinkedIn Better for Creators", "qtext": "", "imp": 284, "rx": 7, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Dani Markovits", "person"], ["Yessi Bello-Perez", "person"], ["Make LinkedIn Better for Creators", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 278, 7, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 283, 7, 4, 1], ["2026-10-27", 284, 7, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 37, "id": 7508475934778519552, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7508475934778519552/", "ts": "2026-09-23T10:42:43+00:00", "date": "2026-09-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "New logo...", "snippet": "New logo...", "chars": 11, "text": "New logo...", "qtext": "", "imp": 2264, "rx": 24, "cm": 5, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2262, 24, 5, 0], ["2026-10-17", 2262, 24, 5, 0], ["2026-10-27", 2264, 24, 5, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 38, "id": 7508437128540749824, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7508437128540749824/", "ts": "2026-09-23T08:08:31+00:00", "date": "2026-09-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "I am running a research on Automated Video Systems for sports.", "snippet": "I am running a research on Automated Video Systems for sports.", "chars": 935, "text": "I am running a research on Automated Video Systems for sports.\nLet me know if you are interested and have suggestions.\nWhy? My son Miles (11-year-old) has played football for a team in Torino for six years, and on weekends I started filming main matches with an iPhone, then an Osmo Pocket 3, and then I got an XbotGo Falcon on Kickstarter.\nMany parents have questions and are curious about the current tech around this, not only for youth and grassroots, but hey... that's my professional sector, so... I started going deeper.\nSomething I wanted to do for a long time.\nBy the way there is a lot of pre-AI-boom AI into this.\n\nThese are the companies I am considering for now:\nBallerCam, Bepro, FoloCam, Hudl Focus, Joymo, LiveBarn, Min Fotboll, Once Sport Autocam, Pix4Team, Pixellot, Provispo, Reeplayer, SeeUsPlay, Sics, Solidsport, Spiideo, Sportway, Staige, Staylive, Trace, Veo, XbotGo, zone14.\n\nLet me know.\n\n#i-am-no-gartner #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 1451, "rx": 14, "cm": 5, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["XbotGo", "company"], ["Kickstarter", "company"]], "tags": ["i", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1444, 14, 5, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1444, 14, 5, 0], ["2026-10-27", 1451, 14, 5, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 39, "id": 7508257197106270208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7508257197106270208/", "ts": "2026-09-22T20:13:32+00:00", "date": "2026-09-22", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "IBC2026 vendor scan: Streaming, live sport and AI", "snippet": "Question for the group: should this become a stable feature, adding more than 100 companies and extending it beyond the initial effort?", "chars": 149, "text": "Question for the group: should this become a stable feature, adding more than 100 companies and extending it beyond the initial effort?\nAny interest?", "qtext": "🎯 IBC2026 vendor scan: Streaming, live sport and AI\nA hundred companies around and beyond IBC2026, with focus on AI, streaming, sport and live sport. A personal snapshot of the market.\n\nBefore going to Amsterdam I did some research to understand better what I was getting into, and after IBC2026 I went back and finished the job, but also I wanted to understand how AI was pitched, demoed and promoted. I am publishing all of it today.\n\n💯 The full hundred\n24i, 2Coders, Accedo, Actus Digital, Agama and Redge, AI2 Insights, Amagi, Ateme, Avid, Babeleye, Backscreen, Big Blue Marble, Bitmovin, Brightc", "imp": 209, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 199, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 201, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 209, 2, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 40, "id": 7507738528244527104, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7507738528244527104/", "ts": "2026-09-21T09:52:32+00:00", "date": "2026-09-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "IBC2026 vendor scan: Streaming, live sport and AI", "snippet": "🎯 IBC2026 vendor scan: Streaming, live sport and AI", "chars": 2158, "text": "🎯 IBC2026 vendor scan: Streaming, live sport and AI\nA hundred companies around and beyond IBC2026, with focus on AI, streaming, sport and live sport. A personal snapshot of the market.\n\nBefore going to Amsterdam I did some research to understand better what I was getting into, and after IBC2026 I went back and finished the job, but also I wanted to understand how AI was pitched, demoed and promoted. I am publishing all of it today.\n\n💯 The full hundred\n24i, 2Coders, Accedo, Actus Digital, Agama and Redge, AI2 Insights, Amagi, Ateme, Avid, Babeleye, Backscreen, Big Blue Marble, Bitmovin, Brightcove, Broadpeak, Cleeng, Comcast Technology Solutions, Cuez, CuttingRoom, Dailymotion Pro, Deltatre, Dolby OptiView, DTVKit, DVEO, Ease Live, EdgeNext, Eluvio, EMAM PlayBox Neo and SNEWS, Enghouse Networks, EON Media, Evergent, EVS, Gcore, Grabyo, Gracenote, Haivision, Humans Not Robots, Junger Audio, Kaltura, Kiloview, Konstrukt, LiveU, LucidLink, M2A Media, Magnifi, MainStreaming, Media Distillery, Media Links, MediaKind, Mediaproxy, Mercury Intermedia, MNC Software, Monterosa, NETINT, Netskrt, Norsk, NOVELSAT, NPAW, Ocean Blue Software, Planetcast, Profuz Digital, Projective, Quickplay, Quortex, Recombee, Saga, Saranyu, Setplex, Simplestream, Small Pixels, Stats Perform, Stirlitz Media, SubtitleNEXT, Synamedia, SyncWords, Telestream, Telos Alliance, ThinkAnalytics, Thol, Tiledmedia, Touchstream, TSL Products, TVU Networks, TwelveLabs, uniqFEED, VD OTT, Vela, Verimatrix, Veritone, Veset, VIDA, Visionular, Vizrt, White Peaks Solutions, XGen Network, XroadMedia, Yospace, YourStoryz, Zentag AI, Zixi.\nTwo of my current clients are in, with full disclosure.\nI have fact checked stuff as much as I could, but feel free to send me corrections if you think I have misrepresented anything.\n\n📤 Getting it\nThe scan is free to read, the methods and every source are documented, and the full list of a hundred is in it.\nFree subscribers on Substack get the link too, and paying members get the underlying database as a file, so they can sort it, filter it and build on it.\nHere it is: https://lnkd.in/ecijRRaS\n\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "qtext": "", "imp": 10982, "rx": 32, "cm": 22, "rp": 7, "boosted": true, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng", "Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 10953, 32, 22, 7], ["2026-10-17", 10958, 32, 22, 7], ["2026-10-27", 10982, 32, 22, 7]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 41, "id": 7507192021854498816, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7507192021854498816/", "ts": "2026-09-19T21:40:54+00:00", "date": "2026-09-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Some months ago I was designing my new dataviz apps and I really wanted to have them in landscape as it's still the best way to consume a lo", "snippet": "Some months ago I was designing my new dataviz apps and I really wanted to have them in landscape as it's still the best way to consume a lot of data with innov", "chars": 605, "text": "Some months ago I was designing my new dataviz apps and I really wanted to have them in landscape as it's still the best way to consume a lot of data with innovative visual formats. I knew that most users may access them through smartphones or portable devices. So I asked Apple if they could create a smartphone that would work well with my upcoming apps and gave them access in a super confidential preview. They ended up creating this new device, and I hope they will be able to sell it in quantity, even though I suggested they not price it so high; otherwise few people will be able to enjoy my work.", "qtext": "", "imp": 646, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Apple", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 643, 10, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 646, 10, 1, 0], ["2026-10-27", 646, 10, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 42, "id": 7506982134583091200, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7506982134583091200/", "ts": "2026-09-19T07:46:53+00:00", "date": "2026-09-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "⚽ A sports journalist at IBC in Amsterdam told me he is using my YouTube Football Tracker app not to check stats about YouTube channels in t", "snippet": "⚽ A sports journalist at IBC in Amsterdam told me he is using my YouTube Football Tracker app not to check stats about YouTube channels in the top 5 European fo", "chars": 644, "text": "⚽ A sports journalist at IBC in Amsterdam told me he is using my YouTube Football Tracker app not to check stats about YouTube channels in the top 5 European football leagues but to get inspiration for stories from the latest videos published by all channels: 5 league channels and 20+20+20+18+18 club channels.\nThe Latest videos page is the place to go, or check the Viral videos page for more inspiration (if my algo works properly).\n\nI created this app mostly for sports digital strategists and clubs' or leagues' YouTube managers.\nI love this unintended use of things I have done.\nHave a look at https://lnkd.in/dru_mETE\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 899, "rx": 3, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 894, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 898, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 899, 3, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 43, "id": 7506958187539922944, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7506958187539922944/", "ts": "2026-09-19T06:11:44+00:00", "date": "2026-09-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "🤖 LinkedIn proofread!!!", "snippet": "🤖 LinkedIn proofread!!!", "chars": 918, "text": "🤖 LinkedIn proofread!!!\nI was writing a post and just noticed the infamous \"Enhance Post\" button that was removed is now being substituted by a more useful PROOFREAD button - see images below.\nIt has 3 options:\n📕 Review: Fixes spelling, grammar, and punctuation errors.\n📕 Shorten: Makes your post more concise while preserving its meaning.\n📕 Clarity: Makes your post easier to read and understand.\nUse the polish options, powered by AI, to provide a second set of eyes on your post.\n\nOne may ask: Is it an honest attempt to make it really useful or AI-whitewashing?\nFor me personally, the Review button is a lifesaver: I write with so many typos...\nI wrote an article some times ago about the previous contradiction of LinkedIn trying to design the algorithm to prevent AI-slop while having a native \"Enhance with AI\" button in the past. They clearly are trying to address that now.\n\nP.S: this post has been proofread!", "qtext": "", "imp": 284, "rx": 7, "cm": 3, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 282, 7, 3, 0], ["2026-10-17", 283, 7, 3, 0], ["2026-10-27", 284, 7, 3, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 44, "id": 7506702019722772480, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7506702019722772480/", "ts": "2026-09-18T13:13:49+00:00", "date": "2026-09-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Thanks 7Artisans for saving my day in Amsterdam.", "snippet": "Thanks 7Artisans for saving my day in Amsterdam.", "chars": 113, "text": "Thanks 7Artisans for saving my day in Amsterdam.\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention\n\n7Artisans Emily Liu", "qtext": "", "imp": 663, "rx": 11, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["7Artisans", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["7Artisans Emily Liu", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 301, "watch_time_s": 4500, "avg_watch_s": 15, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 663, 11, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 663, 11, 2, 1], ["2026-10-27", 663, 11, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 45, "id": 7506251260820508673, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7506251260820508673/", "ts": "2026-09-17T07:22:39+00:00", "date": "2026-09-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "🤖 Live sport is the ultimate playground for innovation 📺", "snippet": "🤖 Live sport is the ultimate playground for innovation 📺", "chars": 938, "text": "🤖 Live sport is the ultimate playground for innovation 📺\nBut where is AI really making an impact - and how can broadcasters and platforms turn it into measurable value?\nSportsPro Ai + Tech: ⏰ London Day 2 - Sep 30 morning\n\nI am moderating a panel with Sean Hunter, SVP, Core Engineering for Emerging Technology & Data Products (Fox Corporation), Chris Wilson, Head of Product Marketing (MediaKind), Daniel Kirschner, Co-Founder & CEO (Greenfly), at SportsPro AI + Tech to explore:\n✅The shifts every media platform needs to make now to stay competitive in an AI-native future.\n✅The latest breakthroughs in live viewing, from multiview and replays to personalised highlights.\n✅How to deploy AI without weakening control, resilience or service quality\n\nDiscover the full agenda 👉 https://lnkd.in/entzUBuU\n\nP.S. Will be there from the 29th when Blue Note London opens but it is sold out... for the love of music...\n\n#sportstech #ai #streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 2029, "rx": 32, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Sean Hunter", "person"], ["Chris Wilson", "person"], ["Daniel Kirschner", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2016, 32, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2028, 32, 2, 2], ["2026-10-27", 2029, 32, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 46, "id": 7505964663470194689, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7505964663470194689/", "ts": "2026-09-16T12:23:49+00:00", "date": "2026-09-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Questions I Took to IBC 2026. Which Ones Got Answered?", "snippet": "🔟❓Attempts to answer those 10 questions I had for IBC 2026 ⁉️", "chars": 2771, "text": "🔟❓Attempts to answer those 10 questions I had for IBC 2026 ⁉️\nTwo weeks ago I published the ten questions I was taking to IBC 2026.\nI promised myself I would come back and answer every one of them, including the ones where the answer is that I still do not know.\nMy take on the show first: 🟩 quality over quantity, 🟩 less posturing, 🟨 fat portfolio syndrome (everybody now offers everything), and 🟨 a tough market for everyone where sport moved even further centre stage.\n\nMy 10 almost answers:\n1️⃣ Market or calendar? Answered. The exceptional year raised the bar, and sport is now the workload everybody designs against.\n2️⃣ DAZN eating the world? Answered, with a twist. Yes, and the DTC apps left standing are run by people who did the homework. Only the brave are left.\n3️⃣ Fixing what fans complain about? Partly. Ads are moving, discovery is stuck, price is sliding, and buffering is the one I am losing hope on.\n4️⃣ Fan insight reaching production? Partly, and the one I am most encouraged by. The OBS drones at Milano Cortina were built because fans asked to feel closer. That is the second case after vertical video.\n5️⃣ Demo to deployment? Answered, and the answer is a new problem. AI coding shortened both paths and gave us a confused market where nobody knows which of a vendor's twelve modules is the good one.\n6️⃣ AI as cover or as the job? Halfway. AI slop went front page this summer, so posturing gets spotted by everyone now, and vendors adjusted.\n7️⃣ Who runs the tools? Open. Everybody says human in the loop, and the phrase covers two very different states: deliberate control, or a tool that is not ready.\n8️⃣ Nobody publishes the numbers? Open, no progress. The only public metric this industry has is what survives, with a two-year lag.\n9️⃣ Bottom line or top line? 75/25 by my own reading, and the 25 now comes with a serious attempt to show where the revenue comes from. Overdue.\n🔟 What is the buy side buying? Open, and I will not pretend otherwise. One observation from my corner: clients came looking for innovation that makes a measurable difference, and they came prepared to evaluate it.\n\nFour answered, three partly, three open, and the open ones are all about evidence and accountability. The tools got better, the marketing got more truthful, the buyers got more serious, and the industry still cannot show its own numbers.\nIf you read any of these ten differently, tell me. If you are on the buy side and can correct question 10, tell me twice.\nI was there for Dolby OptiView and also represented Mercury Intermedia.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention 10 Things To Consider\n#streaming #ott #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 750, "rx": 11, "cm": 3, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"]], "tags": ["streaming", "ott", "ai"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 737, 11, 3, 4], ["2026-10-17", 748, 11, 3, 4], ["2026-10-27", 750, 11, 3, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 47, "id": 7504570982913695744, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7504570982913695744/", "ts": "2026-09-12T16:05:50+00:00", "date": "2026-09-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "😎 The most original take I grabbed at the IBC 2026 until now: the integrator point of view!", "snippet": "😎 The most original take I grabbed at the IBC 2026 until now: the integrator point of view!", "chars": 1613, "text": "😎 The most original take I grabbed at the IBC 2026 until now: the integrator point of view!\nWe normally focus on the client-side perspective, or on the vendor-side one, but today i met my fellow Italian (even Torinese) friend who is on that side.\n 🔦 He told me that he was looking, as always at IBC, to scout the best vendors to work with and this year he found a very confusing scenario.\n 🤖 If in the past company A was good at MAM software, company B at video platform, C at video players etc... now because of the advent of AI-coding, that makes much easier to build stuff (for those who know how to do it) everybody is now complementing the core offering with a much wider product portfolio - so now company A does also translations, video editing, and anti-piracy.\n 📛 The problem being that who knows which quality these extra elements have!\nAn unexpected impact of AI-coding.\n 🎯 At lunch my friend, who runs a fan engagement company, told me that now even clients are sending him digital games that are not production ready and fail when launched. He is know building a platform to address that properly.\n 😜 A bit deja-vu' for me when in the early 2000s a president of a major sport fed told me: \"Why should I spend millions with you when my son just did his website with Wordpress\".\nTrue story. He was ousted the week after that, but for other reasons. #karma\n\nI am curating a deeper IBC debrief but I wanted to share this in advance, live from the floor (corridor to be precise).\n\nThanks to Enrico and Tom.\nA guy with a scarf\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention\n#streaming #integration #ai #coding", "qtext": "", "imp": 555, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Enrico", "person"], ["Tom", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": ["karma", "streaming", "integration", "ai", "coding"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 193, "watch_time_s": 4740, "avg_watch_s": 24, "saves": 1, "sends": 1, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 555, 10, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 555, 10, 1, 0], ["2026-10-27", 555, 10, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 48, "id": 7504130549792436224, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7504130549792436224/", "ts": "2026-09-11T10:55:43+00:00", "date": "2026-09-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Athletics: Every world record ever set, as a film.", "snippet": "🏃‍♂️ On the day of the World Athletics Ultimate Championships I decided to launch my new athletics interactive experience.", "chars": 2282, "text": "🏃‍♂️ On the day of the World Athletics Ultimate Championships I decided to launch my new athletics interactive experience.\n🏃‍♀️‍➡️ The World Record Film turns the whole history of athletics records into cards you flip through — one film for the men, one for the women, 33 cards each.\n\n🏃‍♂️‍➡️ My love for athletics started when i was a kid, Pietro Mennea, Dwight Stones, etc… then professionally in 1991 when I worked more than one year for the Tokyo World Championships and saw the magic of Carl Lewis and Mike Powell world record.\n\n🏃 Two films — men and women, 33 cards each, in World Athletics' own event order.\n📈 The staircase — every mark that ever held the record; the width of each step is how long it survived.\n👻 The ghost race — press play and every era runs at once. Bolt crosses the line 9.6 m ahead of 1912.\n🕳️ The pit — jumps and throws laid out at the distance they actually went, oldest at the back.\n❓ Cards that explain themselves — why a ratified record sometimes goes backwards: hand timing, rule changes, a redesigned javelin.\n📊 Stats for one event — nations, athletes, age and reign length, narrowed to the card you're on.\n🌍 Nations — who owned each record, year by year. The USA held the 100 m for 77 of its 114 years.\n🏅 Where records fall — every record as a dot, gold for the Olympics, cyan for the Worlds, grey for everywhere else. Grey wins.\n📅 Records per day — all 1,382 marks on a 366-day calendar. 84.5% fall between May and September; 106 days have never produced one.\n🎂 Age — how old they were, with a decade slider to watch it move.\n⏳ How long records last — the longest reigns ever, each drawn from the day it was set to the day it fell.\n⚖️ One ranking, men and women — every standing record in a single list. The oldest is 43 years old.\n🔗 Every card has its own link — and it reloads exactly where you left off.\n\nApologies to those who feel spammed by my new interactive sport venture... but I am having so much fun designing and building what I had in mind for years and launching them to get feedback. My inner kid is happy. My real kids are addicted users. Hope more join the obsession. Let me know if you are interested in building stuff like this on your platform.\nA guy with a scarf Realtime Fan Engagement\n\n#athletics #trackandfield #fans #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 896, "rx": 9, "cm": 4, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["World Athletics", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Realtime Fan Engagement", "showcase"]], "tags": ["athletics", "trackandfield", "fans", "ai"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 892, 9, 4, 3], ["2026-10-17", 892, 9, 4, 3], ["2026-10-27", 896, 9, 4, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 49, "id": 7503761915937140736, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7503761915937140736/", "ts": "2026-09-10T10:30:53+00:00", "date": "2026-09-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What if there is another way to buy a streaming tech stack?", "snippet": "❓ What if there is another way to buy a streaming tech stack?", "chars": 2120, "text": "❓ What if there is another way to buy a streaming tech stack?\nOwn the platform, rent the expertise, keep the right to change your mind on every supplier.\n\n🤖 I spent most of my career on the vendor side of streaming. At every renewal the same clients asked the same two questions. Can we own more of this? Can we depend on you less? I answered with contract clauses. Exit terms, data export, an escrow nobody opened. They rented, we held the keys.\n\n📰 A few months ago I wrote about consolidation narrowing choice and ownership coming back as a buying criterion. A team in Nashville/NYC called me, read a few of my sentences back to me and said this is what we are building. That team is Mercury Intermedia. They announced Quicksilver Stream this week. I advise them, paid, so read me as interested.\nWhat those clients were asking for, and what an in-house streaming platform gives them:\n\n🎛️ Control. The decisions about the platform sit on the client's side of the table.💰 Investment. Fees accumulate into an asset instead of expiring with each contract.\n🔌 Pluggable vendors. DRM, payments, data, ads swapped at the server side, apps untouched.\n🛡️ Security. A platform that survives whatever happens to the company that built it.\n📈 A long horizon. A stack that grows with the business instead of forcing a rebuild every four or five years.\n\nThe counter-arguments are real and they are in the article. Ownership without operational excellence is a marketing word. A license plus support looks like SaaS, until the vendor gets bought and only one of you keeps a platform. Control has a running cost. The price is capital, and the same buyers sign three-year SaaS deals worth more.\n\nI understand this model may not be for everybody. It makes a lot of sense for rights holders and media brands that expect to be running their own audience relationship in ten years and want agency over the thing that runs it.\n\nFull article below.\nI am at IBC in Amsterdam from Friday to Sunday. If you want to argue any of this, or hear more, come and find me.\n\n#streaming #license #b2b #livesport IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "qtext": "", "imp": 872, "rx": 13, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Mercury Intermedia", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": ["streaming", "license", "b2b", "livesport"], "series": [], "disc": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 867, 13, 1, 3], ["2026-10-17", 867, 13, 1, 3], ["2026-10-27", 872, 13, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 50, "id": 7503149183856685056, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7503149183856685056/", "ts": "2026-09-08T17:56:07+00:00", "date": "2026-09-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I finally built the NFL standings I designed years ago. Love it? Hate it?", "snippet": "🏈 I finally built the NFL standings I designed years ago. Love it? Hate it?", "chars": 1639, "text": "🏈 I finally built the NFL standings I designed years ago. Love it? Hate it?\nIt's yet another A guy with a scarf Sport Interactive Experience!\nI sketched this when I was following NFL Game Pass International week by week. I pitched it more than once, but it never got budget or consensus, so it went in a drawer. This week the 2026 season kicks off, and the drawer is empty. The NFL Season Tower is live and free at nfl.aguywithascarf.com.\nAll 272 regular-season games on one screen and it shows the standings as a shape: wins stack up, losses hang down, and you can replay the season week by week.\n\nHow it works:\n1️⃣ A season as a shape. All 32 teams on one baseline, wins stack up, losses hang down, games still to play hang from the ceiling. A good season is tall and a bad one is deep.\n2️⃣ Every box is the opponent's colour, so each tower is a schedule as well as a scoreboard, and strength of schedule is visible at a glance.\n3️⃣ Replay any season. Press play and the standings rebuild week by week, and teams visibly overtake each other as they go.\n4️⃣ Next games always in sight, hanging above your team's tower in order.\n5️⃣ Click any box for the box score, any team for the roster and schedule.\n6️⃣ Nothing is simulated. 2026 starts tomorrow with every box on the ceiling. Go and look while it is still empty.\n\nIf you want the Season Tower on your platform, in your brand, with your sponsor on it, write to me. And if you want the Lego wall version in your lobby, write to me about that too.\n\nYou can find it all at: https://lnkd.in/dzPQ4Juf\n\nNational Football League (NFL) the LEGO Group\n#sportstech #ux #data #ai #fanexperiences", "qtext": "", "imp": 1123, "rx": 10, "cm": 5, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["National Football League (NFL)", "company"], ["the LEGO Group", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "ux", "data", "ai", "fanexperiences"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1122, 10, 5, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1122, 10, 5, 2], ["2026-10-27", 1123, 10, 5, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 51, "id": 7502985862939365376, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7502985862939365376/", "ts": "2026-09-08T07:07:08+00:00", "date": "2026-09-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "⏰ REMINDER: I am hosting a LIVE WEBINAR at 3pm CET today", "snippet": "⏰ REMINDER: I am hosting a LIVE WEBINAR at 3pm CET today", "chars": 942, "text": "⏰ REMINDER: I am hosting a LIVE WEBINAR at 3pm CET today\nImmerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment.\nWith:\n🤖 Dan Coffey (Director of Product, Dolby OptiView)\n🤖 Sander de Vries (Head of Media Technology & Change, Southfields NL),\n\nDolby OptiView will showcase its new AI-powered Sports Intelligence Platform at IBC2026 next week, promising more personalised fan engagement, automated highlights and smarter sports streaming. \nWill cover:\n- Understand how viewers engage with content and what keeps them watching\n- Act on those insights to drive higher ad revenue and deliver relevant experiences at the right moment\n- Deliver synchronized experiences that keep every fan in the same moment\n- Experience consistent, premium playback in Dolby Vision and Dolby Atmos across every device\n\n🗓️ Today, September 8th\n⏰ 9am ET | 3pm CET\n🔗 RSVP now: https://lnkd.in/gcjn4g-c\n\n#livesports #webinar #monetization #personalization #IBC2026", "qtext": "", "imp": 477, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dan Coffey", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Sander de Vries", "person"], ["Southfields NL", "company"]], "tags": ["livesports", "webinar", "monetization", "personalization", "ibc2026"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 477, 11, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 477, 11, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 477, 11, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 52, "id": 7502708793567313920, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7502708793567313920/", "ts": "2026-09-07T12:46:09+00:00", "date": "2026-09-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "asks a question to Jan Ozer: Untangling MoQ", "snippet": "A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jan Ozer: Untangling MoQ", "chars": 271, "text": "A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jan Ozer: Untangling MoQ\nI had a curiosity before the IBC - International Broadcasting Convention and turned to an expert to get a short answer.\nCheck the full article and wtach the video here below or on substack.\n\n#streaming #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 963, "rx": 8, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Jan Ozer", "person"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": ["streaming", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 962, 8, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 962, 8, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 963, 8, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 53, "id": 7502632229454540800, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7502632229454540800/", "ts": "2026-09-07T07:41:55+00:00", "date": "2026-09-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "snippet": "A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "chars": 2618, "text": "A guy with a scarf asks a question to...\nUntangling MoQ with Jan Ozer\n\nLatency is the debate that comes back every trade show and rarely gets interesting. While putting together my 10 questions for IBC 2026 I kept running into three letters, MoQ, so I asked the person I trust most on codecs and delivery, Jan Ozer, to explain what it is and why anyone in sports should care.\n\nWhat Jan told me, in his words:\n1️⃣ \"MoQ stands for Media over QUIC. It's a new way to deliver live video over the internet with less than a second of delay, even when the audience is very large.\"\n2️⃣ \"Today, to achieve low latency in a broadcast setting, streaming platforms generally choose between two imperfect options.\"\n3️⃣ \"WebRTC provides extremely low latency and works well for interactive applications like video calls, telehealth and remote control. But WebRTC is designed primarily for two-way communication. Distributing a WebRTC stream to millions of viewers requires specialized servers and substantial infrastructure. It's too expensive for general broadcasts like the Super Bowl or the World Cup.\"\n4️⃣ \"HLS and DASH handle one-to-many broadcasting very efficiently and work well with existing content delivery networks. But even their low latency versions typically remain several seconds behind the action.\"\n5️⃣ \"MoQ is designed to fill the gap between them. It's primarily for one-to-many broadcasting, not two-way interaction.\"\n6️⃣ \"A broadcaster publishes a stream. Viewers subscribe to the stream. And relay servers distribute copies from locations close to the viewers. A relay can receive one copy of the stream and pass it to many viewers, much like a conventional content delivery network.\"\n7️⃣ \"That gives MoQ the real-time performance associated with WebRTC and the massive scale associated with HLS and DASH.\"\n8️⃣ \"The most obvious markets are live sports betting, auctions, gaming and other large events where even a few seconds of delay can spoil the experience. If your neighbor cheers before you see the goal, or the betting market changes before your video catches up, latency matters.\"\n9️⃣ \"MoQ is still an emerging standard. Products and early deployments exist, but the specification and supporting ecosystem are not yet finished.\"\n🔟 \"MoQ is not replacing WebRTC, HLS or DASH tomorrow. But it may eventually provide something the industry has always wanted: real-time one-to-many broadcasting at internet scale and affordable costs.\"\n\n#IBC2026 #MoQ #streaming #sportstech #latency\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 433, "rx": 6, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Jan Ozer", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ibc2026", "moq", "streaming", "sportstech", "latency"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 169, "watch_time_s": 3119, "avg_watch_s": 18, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 433, 6, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 433, 6, 1, 1], ["2026-10-27", 433, 6, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 54, "id": 7502453456759275520, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7502453456759275520/", "ts": "2026-09-06T19:51:32+00:00", "date": "2026-09-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Kimi Antonelli progression at Monza F1 GP - from 19 to winner as seen on https://lnkd.in/eH_aAWXn", "snippet": "Kimi Antonelli progression at Monza F1 GP - from 19 to winner as seen on https://lnkd.in/eH_aAWXn", "chars": 196, "text": "Kimi Antonelli progression at Monza F1 GP - from 19 to winner as seen on https://lnkd.in/eH_aAWXn\n\nA guy with a scarf Formula 1 Mercedes-AMG PETRONAS Formula One Team Andrea Kimi Antonelli\n#f1 #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 316, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Formula 1", "company"], ["Mercedes-AMG PETRONAS Formula One Team", "company"], ["Andrea Kimi Antonelli", "person"]], "tags": ["f1", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 315, 6, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 315, 6, 0, 2], ["2026-10-27", 316, 6, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 55, "id": 7501929747254497281, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7501929747254497281/", "ts": "2026-09-05T09:10:30+00:00", "date": "2026-09-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "As seen on the IBC - International Broadcasting Convention official website: Dolby OptiView will showcase its new AI-powered Sports Intellig", "snippet": "As seen on the IBC - International Broadcasting Convention official website: Dolby OptiView will showcase its new AI-powered Sports Intelligence Platform at IBC", "chars": 713, "text": "As seen on the IBC - International Broadcasting Convention official website: Dolby OptiView will showcase its new AI-powered Sports Intelligence Platform at IBC2026 next week, promising more personalised fan engagement, automated highlights and smarter sports streaming. \nArticle here: https://lnkd.in/e7ekSt4W\n\nI am hosting a LIVE WEBINAR on Tuesday 8 Sep at 3:30pm CET exactly on that with Dan Coffey and Sander de Vries, two key people behind the Sports Intelligence Platform.\nregister here: https://optiview.dolby.com/streamon-immerse-monetize-personalize-live-sports/\n\nMeet the Dolby team (and A guy with a scarf) in Amsterdam - Stand Number: 5.F86\nMatthias Ankli Michelle Gong Jenny Lou\n#streaming #live #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 876, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Dolby", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"], ["Jenny Lou", "person"]], "tags": ["streaming", "live", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 871, 12, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 875, 12, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 876, 12, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 56, "id": 7501897611679883264, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7501897611679883264/", "ts": "2026-09-05T07:02:49+00:00", "date": "2026-09-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "🚗 ⚽  🎾 Another day that ends in Y, and another morning I check all my new Interactive Sport Experiences.", "snippet": "🚗 ⚽  🎾 Another day that ends in Y, and another morning I check all my new Interactive Sport Experiences.", "chars": 945, "text": "🚗 ⚽  🎾 Another day that ends in Y, and another morning I check all my new Interactive Sport Experiences.\nNew features added almost daily.\nNice visuals they told me but it's the clicking and moving things around that make themn fun to use.\nWith full keyboard functionality you can go from one card to another and compare in-flight players, drivers, races, stats etc... all in one screen.\nSome people told me at some point this becomes a bit addictive, like when you hop from song to song on Spotify.\nThis weekend is all about Monza and Us Open... and the top 5 leagues - while we wait for the UEFA club comps and the NFL!\nA new entry, still not public, can be seen in these screenshote, awaiting for validation from sport experts before launching.  ⛳\nAnybody interested to build it for their website or digital touchpoints?\n\nFormula 1 ATP Tour WTA Lega Serie A The Premier League Bundesliga Ligue 1 LALIGA UEFA PGA TOUR\n#sport #stats #ai #dataviz", "qtext": "", "imp": 813, "rx": 10, "cm": 9, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Formula 1", "company"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["WTA", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["The Premier League", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "company"], ["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["UEFA", "company"], ["PGA TOUR", "company"]], "tags": ["sport", "stats", "ai", "dataviz"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 810, 10, 9, 2], ["2026-10-17", 811, 10, 9, 2], ["2026-10-27", 813, 10, 9, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 57, "id": 7501218579888091136, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7501218579888091136/", "ts": "2026-09-03T10:04:35+00:00", "date": "2026-09-03", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "If you are curious who si doing what with AI in the football clubs community have a look at the great work my friend Lamberto Siegahas done ", "snippet": "If you are curious who si doing what with AI in the football clubs community have a look at the great work my friend Lamberto Siegahas done here!", "chars": 145, "text": "If you are curious who si doing what with AI in the football clubs community have a look at the great work my friend Lamberto Siegahas done here!", "qtext": "Thank you to everyone who commented, wrote in privately, and suggested corrections on the AI Football Observatory over the past few weeks — a special thanks to Fabio Lalli for his constant support and advice on this initiative.\n\nThe methodology changed. It's no longer a qualitative editorial judgment: every AI initiative now gets a score based on operational status (from pilot to structural deployment) and public evidence (from a generic claim to a measured result with real numbers). Industry sources count for 60% of an official source. Each value-chain area covered adds points.\n\nSeptember's r", "imp": 119, "rx": 3, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Lamberto Siega", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 119, 3, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 119, 3, 1, 0], ["2026-10-27", 119, 3, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Lamberto Siega"}, {"i": 58, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-09-03", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Dolby OptiView", "with": null, "title": "🔔 Next week, tune into our live webinar on how to immerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment.", "snippet": "🔔 Next week, tune into our live webinar on how to immerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment.", "chars": 908, "text": "🔔 Next week, tune into our live webinar on how to immerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment.\n\nIn this session, Dan Coffey (Director of Product, Dolby OptiView), Sander de Vries (Head of Media Technology & Change, Southfields NL), and Carlo De Marchis (Founder, A guy with a scarf) will share an exclusive first look at what's next from Dolby at IBC.\n\nWe’ll be previewing how we’re helping customers:\n- Understand how viewers engage with content and what keeps them watching\n- Act on those insights to drive higher ad revenue and deliver relevant experiences at the right moment\n- Deliver synchronized experiences that keep every fan in the same moment\n- Experience consistent, premium playback in Dolby Vision and Dolby Atmos across every device\n\n🗓️ Tuesday, September 8th\n⏰ 9am ET | 3pm CET\n🔗 RSVP now: https://lnkd.in/gcjn4g-c\n\n#livesports #webinar #monetization #personalization #IBC2026", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 30, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dan Coffey", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Sander de Vries", "person"], ["Southfields NL", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["livesports", "webinar", "monetization", "personalization", "ibc2026"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 30, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", null, 30, 0, 3], ["2026-10-27", null, 30, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 59, "id": 7500821272503750656, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7500821272503750656/", "ts": "2026-09-02T07:45:49+00:00", "date": "2026-09-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Deep Dive on LIV Golf", "snippet": "I spent the last week researching LIV Golf and ended up with a quite complete deep dive which I posted in full on my Substack for the paid membership level.", "chars": 2432, "text": "I spent the last week researching LIV Golf and ended up with a quite complete deep dive which I posted in full on my Substack for the paid membership level.\nI started from the filed accounts at Companies House, PIF’s own annual report, the OWGR board’s decision, the PGA Tour’s published terms, and the Financial Times reporting that broke the bankruptcy preparation. Where the published numbers disagree with each other, I have said so and shown both rather than picking the one that suits the argument. Around 10,000 words, eight appendices, every figure dated and sourced.\n\nWhat's inside:\n⛳ 2021-2022: buy the supply. Origins, Centurion, and the signing money, name by name.\n⛳ The legal war, and the press release that ended it. The antitrust suit, the framework agreement negotiated in secret, and the three years of nothing that followed.\n⛳ The audience that never showed up. The CW and Fox deals, the ratings, and the world ranking fight LIV changed its own format to win.\n⛳ What worked. The majors, the Ryder Cup, and the one part of the business that did what LIV promised, the public attendance.\n⛳ What the accounts say, and what LIV says. The filings against the briefings.\n⛳ The fund. PIF at $913bn, its sports portfolio, and the squeeze that ended this.\n⛳ 29 April 2026. The pullout, and the season coming apart in public.\n⛳ 2026: the players move. The Returning Member Program, who took it, who did not.\n⛳ LIV 2.0, and who pays for it. BC Partners, the prepackaged bankruptcy, and the players being asked to fund their own league.\n⛳ The merger. Why it is dead, on the record.\n⛳ What it bought. The three things LIV moved, including the ones the PGA Tour kept.\n⛳ Appendix A: the cast. Twenty-two people, what they did, where they are.\n⛳ Appendix B: who joined. Thirty-three signings with dates and reported fees.\n⛳ Appendix C: who left, and where they went.\n⛳ Appendix D: the teams. All thirteen, captains and rosters.\n⛳ Appendix E: the tournaments. Every season, and every 2026 event with its winner.\n⛳ Appendix F: the broadcast deals. From YouTube to the CW to Fox, and what each was worth.\n⛳ Appendix G: the timeline. October 2021 to the bankruptcy preparation, in order.\n⛳ Appendix H: attendance. What LIV published, what it did not, and the one comparison that explains the whole thing.\n⛳ Sources. Fifty-one, with full links.\n\n#golf #sportbiz A guy with a scarf\n\nFind the full article here:\nhttps://lnkd.in/eNhur5xm", "qtext": "", "imp": 6845, "rx": 28, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LIV Golf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["golf", "sportbiz"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 6827, 28, 3, 2], ["2026-10-17", 6828, 28, 3, 2], ["2026-10-27", 6845, 28, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 60, "id": 7500471053421453312, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7500471053421453312/", "ts": "2026-09-01T08:34:11+00:00", "date": "2026-09-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "🎾 A tennis tournament in one screen, interactive.", "snippet": "🎾 A tennis tournament in one screen, interactive.", "chars": 1748, "text": "🎾 A tennis tournament in one screen, interactive.\n\nWith my team we invented, designed, built, launch, and operated sport website for a living for many years. Then the relevance and budgets for those digital touchpoint I guess evaporated with the insurgence of social media and other channels. I may talk about that and the current status of sport websites in another post.\n\n 🎾 I love UX challenges, so this time after the World Cup project, I wanted to test my skills on making a full tennis tournament bracket into one full landscape web page.\n\nSo here it is the new feature of my tennis ranking app: Draws.\nhttps://lnkd.in/edrQFPRj\n🎾 Clicking on each player you highlight the path and get the full data on the side panels\n 🎾 Clicking on a match you get the basic scores (for now)\n 🎾 Try it full screen - looks great (there's a button for that)\n 🎾 You can use arrows on a keyboard to move from one tournament to the next\n 🎾 I just included Grand Slams and Masters 1000 for now - easy to add 500 and 250\n\n ❌ I know it's not optimal on mobile vertical, I should instead do something sharable on social etc... but still I had fun with this and I love the result.\nFeel free to send any comments, bugs, feedbacks, and new ideas, please.\n\nWho knows if someone on the tennis side falls in love with this dataviz and it can make it to some official sport or media outlet... ( Andrea Gaudenzi 😎 )\n\nHire me if you are interested for this or more dataviz.\nP.S. I could not resist but to select an example with a random Italian player in focus...   🇮🇹\n\nBuilt with passion by Carlo De Marchis (A guy with a scarf).\n\n#UX #fans #tennis #ai\nATP Tour ATP Media WTA (USTA) United States Tennis Association Sky Italia Wimbledon Infosys IBM Dave Gabel ESPN Rich Mills", "qtext": "", "imp": 441, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Andrea Gaudenzi", "person"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["ATP Media", "company"], ["WTA", "company"], ["(USTA) United States Tennis Association", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["Wimbledon", "company"], ["Infosys", "company"], ["IBM", "company"], ["Dave Gabel", "person"], ["ESPN", "company"], ["Rich Mills", "person"]], "tags": ["ux", "fans", "tennis", "ai"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 440, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 440, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 441, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 61, "id": 7500163051917234176, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7500163051917234176/", "ts": "2026-08-31T12:10:17+00:00", "date": "2026-08-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🚗 How tall are F1 drivers?", "snippet": "🚗 How tall are F1 drivers?", "chars": 166, "text": "🚗 How tall are F1 drivers?\n\nFormula One Standing after Race 12.\nPart of my interactive dataviz project F1viz.\nThe film.\n\nFormula 1 Realtime Fan Engagement #ai #coding", "qtext": "", "imp": 313, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [], "tags": ["ai", "coding"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 110, "watch_time_s": 1805, "avg_watch_s": 16, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 313, 2, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 313, 2, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 313, 2, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 62, "id": 7500134873576177665, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7500134873576177665/", "ts": "2026-08-31T10:18:19+00:00", "date": "2026-08-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Questions I Am Taking to IBC 2026", "snippet": "🔢 10 Questions I Am Taking to IBC 2026", "chars": 1179, "text": "🔢 10 Questions I Am Taking to IBC 2026\n😎 My personal curiosity vibe-checking to-do list\n\nI forced myself to put down ten questions I will be looking to make sense of when in Amsterdam, as an exercise that helped me research a lot.\n\nSo preemptive apologies: with all this content coming from different sources and my memory, I have for sure made mistakes or omissions. Let me know.\n\n1️⃣ Is the market in a good state, or is 2026 just a good calendar?\n2️⃣ Is DAZN eating the sports streaming world or do independent DTC apps still have a place?\n3️⃣ Will any of the new features fix the thing fans complain about?\n4️⃣ We know more about fans than we ever have. Is any of it changing how the thing gets made?\n5️⃣ What is the honest distance between the booth demo and the deployment?\n6️⃣ Is AI a marketing cover, or is it doing the job now?\n7️⃣ If the tools work, who is left to run them?\n8️⃣ Nobody publishes the numbers, so what are we all trading on?\n9️⃣ Are vendors selling the bottom line or the top line?\n🔟 What is the buy side actually trying to buy this year?\n\nA guy with a scarf at IBC - International Broadcasting Convention #ai #streaming Dolby OptiView Mercury Intermedia", "qtext": "", "imp": 3908, "rx": 31, "cm": 9, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Mercury Intermedia", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "streaming"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Mercury Intermedia", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Mercury Intermedia"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3888, 31, 9, 3], ["2026-10-17", 3889, 31, 9, 3], ["2026-10-27", 3908, 31, 9, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 63, "id": 7499371469223772160, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7499371469223772160/", "ts": "2026-08-29T07:44:49+00:00", "date": "2026-08-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "I am reflecting on the latest news about innovative sport properties being in trouble lately, from staff cuts up to 50% to vendors suing cli", "snippet": "I am reflecting on the latest news about innovative sport properties being in trouble lately, from staff cuts up to 50% to vendors suing clients (which is a ver", "chars": 1588, "text": "I am reflecting on the latest news about innovative sport properties being in trouble lately, from staff cuts up to 50% to vendors suing clients (which is a very rare thing to happen in our industry). LIV Golf Kings League Baller League etc...\nI see a group of people super happy about these failures, as they predicted them in advance.\nI have a lot of empathy for anybody (I mean real people) impacted negatively by these situations, unpaid wages, unpaid invoices, being laid off after having worked with passion and intent for years.\nBut I am still a big fan of new stuff entering our industry because they force the traditional sport giants to rethink, which very often they would not do by themselves.\nExternal forces have brought a lot of needed innovation into what we do, even if in some cases they do not work as expected.\nSo for the industry as a whole it is a net positive.\nBasically the big guys have an innovation department paid out by others - why do they complain about it?\n\nExample: take football, do you think the gods of football would have implemneted any rule change if kings League did not happen? I moderated a panel pre-COVID in Germany where an ex-NFL exec was lightly joking with an ex-UEFA Gen Sec, that their football had the courage to change the rules of the game every year if they found something was off or to be improved and our football did not change anything ever.\nThis season, positive feedback from coaches and commentators after the first Lega Serie A matchday for effective time improvement.\nI am sure many of you will disagree.\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 679, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["LIV Golf", "company"], ["Kings League", "company"], ["Baller League", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 257, "watch_time_s": 7080, "avg_watch_s": 27, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 679, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 679, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 679, 9, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 64, "id": 7498999466985762816, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7498999466985762816/", "ts": "2026-08-28T07:06:37+00:00", "date": "2026-08-28", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "When I saw this post yesterday and especially the photo, I was really surprised.", "snippet": "When I saw this post yesterday and especially the photo, I was really surprised.", "chars": 1077, "text": "When I saw this post yesterday and especially the photo, I was really surprised.\nWhat Xavier Leclercq says below is exactly why I created the book\n\"Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media: 10 Things To Consider\".\nI create a lot of content based on my experiences and explorations, in various formats, but in this book I really tried to distill what I saw and went through in 35 years on the vendor side at Deltatre working with the biggest global clients in sports and media.\nAs much as I put my effort in research, create, design, build, and launch, I am always putting too little in promoting what I do properly, I am the famous anti-growth hacker!\nSo seeing someone using what I created is always a super-treat.\n\nSo thanks Xavier, you made my day. Looking forward to meet at the IBC - International Broadcasting Convention\n\nI will bring some copies to the IBC in Amsterdam.\nWhere to find the book:\n 📕 Paperback on Amazon\n 💻 Kindle on Amazon\n 📰 PDF for all paid subscriber on Substack\nAll links below in the comments.\n\nA guy with a scarf - 10 Things To Consider", "qtext": "In July 1935 the US Army flew its brand new bomber, the Boeing Model 299. It crashed on take-off and killed the test pilot, one of the most experienced in the country. He had forgotten to release the lock that holds the controls still on the ground.\n\nThe verdict at the time: \"too much aeroplane for one man to fly.\"\n\nThe fix was a card. Pilots wrote down what to check before take-off, and the aircraft went on to fly 1.8 million miles without a serious accident. We know it today as the B-17.\n\nChecklists are made for experts. Load makes all of us forget things we know perfectly well.\n\nWhich bring", "imp": 250, "rx": 7, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Xavier Leclercq", "person"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Deltatre", "company"], ["Xavier", "person"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 250, 7, 3, 2], ["2026-10-17", 250, 7, 3, 2], ["2026-10-27", 250, 7, 3, 2]], "quoted": "Xavier Leclercq"}, {"i": 65, "id": 7498644302512689152, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7498644302512689152/", "ts": "2026-08-27T07:35:19+00:00", "date": "2026-08-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Dolby OptiView Newsletter", "snippet": "Dolby OptiView", "chars": 14, "text": "Dolby OptiView", "qtext": "", "imp": 205, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 200, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 204, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 205, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 66, "id": 7498643759845437442, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7498643759845437442/", "ts": "2026-08-27T07:33:10+00:00", "date": "2026-08-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "LIVE WEBINAR, Sep 8: Immerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment", "snippet": "I am getting ready to host an intriguing live webinar with Dolby OptiView focusing on what we may call Sports Intelligence and how partners can build something ", "chars": 1312, "text": "I am getting ready to host an intriguing live webinar with Dolby OptiView focusing on what we may call Sports Intelligence and how partners can build something new if they complement and overlap in a smart way and with an open mind.\n\nLIVE WEBINAR: Immerse, monetize, and personalize every live sports moment\n\n🗓️ Tuesday, September 8th - ⏰ 9am ET | 3pm CET\n🔗 RSVP now: https://lnkd.in/d-3VN2qW\n\nIn this webinar, join\n🤖 Dan Coffey (Director of Product, Dolby OptiView ),\n🤖 Sander de Vries (Head of Media Technology & Change, Southfields NL )\n🤖 Hosted by: Carlo De Marchis (Founder, A guy with a scarf )\nfor an exclusive first look at what’s next from Dolby at IBC - International Broadcasting Convention.\n\nThey’ll be previewing how they’re helping customers:\n📺📲 Understand how viewers engage with content and what keeps them watching\n📺📲 Act on those insights to drive higher ad revenue and deliver relevant experiences at the right moment\n📺📲 Deliver synchronized experiences that keep every fan in the same moment\n📺📲 Experience consistent, premium playback in Dolby Vision and Dolby Atmos across every device\n❓ But I will also ask an original question: can innovation travel upstream?\n\n-----\nA Guy with a Scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 510, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Dan Coffey", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Sander de Vries", "person"], ["Southfields NL", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 508, 12, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 510, 12, 0, 2], ["2026-10-27", 510, 12, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 67, "id": 7496803524266225664, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7496803524266225664/", "ts": "2026-08-22T05:40:44+00:00", "date": "2026-08-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Top 5 Football Leagues: An Alternative Interactive Experience", "snippet": "⚽ Top 5 leagues are kicking off — football is back.", "chars": 2311, "text": "⚽ Top 5 leagues are kicking off — football is back.\nAn interactive way to watch a whole season at once — and to see not just the points teams win, but the ones they let slip.\n\n📊 So I built a little something — yet another of my sport interactive experiences.\n👇 https://lnkd.in/dnsqCT9x\n\n⚽🏈 And TBFH, to be fully honest: this is a rework of an NFL visualization I’ve been chipping away at for years — which I’ll finally launch at the start of September. This football version just… jumped the queue.\n\n💻 I wanted one big landscape screen where I could keep all the matches of a season in view at once — every result, every club, the whole shape of a league in a single glance.\n🤖 But there’s a second idea in here, and it’s not mine — it’s my son Miles’ (11), who plays for a Juventus elite academy team. He looks at the table differently - he is also a big consumer of all my interactive experiences from his iPad, with the unwanted side effect of the number of sports data and stats question from him increase dramatically, which I totally am not able to reply to.\n\nHis thinking: at the start of a Serie A season, every team already\nowns all its points. 38 games × 3 = 114 points, sitting in the bank. From there you don’t earn points — you try not to lose them. Lose a match, 3 gone. Draw, you still drop 2. A tie isn’t a small win; it’s mostly points let slip.\n\nHe imagined having a physical “thing” in the locker room where the team manually moves blocks from the upcoming games to either the positive (win) or negative (lost) or both sides (tie) of the tower, maybe made with Lego?\n\nSo instead of a normal table, each team is a tower: what it kept stacks up, what it dropped pulls down — and a draw shows on both sides, because it’s +1 earned and 2 lost at the same time. Two teams on equal points can look completely different once you see the points they gave away. I call them towers as they started vertical then I also did a horiziontal version which works best on landscape, both models are available.\n\nIt’s a very simple visualization — honestly not the most visually satisfying thing I’ve made 🙂 — but it’s the best way I’ve found so far to see a season the way Miles sees it. Data updates once a day as matches are played.\n\nLega Serie A The Premier League LALIGA Ligue1 Bundesliga A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 656, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["The Premier League", "company"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Ligue1", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 655, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 655, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-27", 656, 4, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 68, "id": 7496478345241710592, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7496478345241710592/", "ts": "2026-08-21T08:08:35+00:00", "date": "2026-08-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺📲 IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "snippet": "📺📲 IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "chars": 567, "text": "📺📲 IBC - International Broadcasting Convention\nAs I am preparing for Amsterdam I did a quick scan of all emails in my inbox from IBC Vendors and extrapolated all  🤖 AI-focused marketing messages.\nHere in the image is a literal sequence of sentences from those email, in random order...\nJust to arrive well prepared for the vibe and be educated to distinguish signal from noise.\n 😜 I already wrote about my hope for  🤖 AI becoming the HOW not WHY anymore.\n\nA guy with a scarf Untangling AI Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n#ai #events #conferences", "qtext": "", "imp": 271, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai", "events", "conferences"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 271, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 271, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 271, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 69, "id": 7496237241271287809, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7496237241271287809/", "ts": "2026-08-20T16:10:31+00:00", "date": "2026-08-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "Thanks David Cushnan and James Emmett for mentioning me again in their must-read newsletter Worth Knowing for my article on the Ultimate Cha", "snippet": "Thanks David Cushnan and James Emmett for mentioning me again in their must-read newsletter Worth Knowing for my article on the Ultimate Championships. Leaders ", "chars": 168, "text": "Thanks David Cushnan and James Emmett for mentioning me again in their must-read newsletter Worth Knowing for my article on the Ultimate Championships. Leaders in Sport", "qtext": "", "imp": 218, "rx": 3, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["David Cushnan", "person"], ["James Emmett", "person"], ["Leaders in Sport", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 218, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 218, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-27", 218, 3, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 70, "id": 7496087399341563905, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7496087399341563905/", "ts": "2026-08-20T06:15:06+00:00", "date": "2026-08-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ The top 5 football leagues all start this week", "snippet": "⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ The top 5 football leagues all start this week", "chars": 840, "text": "⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ ⚽ The top 5 football leagues all start this week\nWe will see who starts winning on the pitch, but if you are curious about what happens on YouTube look no further that my YouTube Football Tracker:\n 📊 YTFT - link in the comments\n ⚽ All top 5 leagues channels\n ⚽ All clubs channels\n ⚽ A solid array of stats and visualizations\n\nA club social media manager told me: \"The best place to understand what others are doing on YouTube, we used it to define our new season strategy. Loved it.\"\n\nThere are so many platforms clubs are active on, but I decided to focus on YouTube's public available data, and built a system that saves every day values so I can compare and trend.\nIt's all available or free, I am just asking to login with google so I can control the tech stack costs to serve those who are really interested.\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 360, "rx": 4, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 360, 4, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 360, 4, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 71, "id": 7495724508780265472, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7495724508780265472/", "ts": "2026-08-19T06:13:06+00:00", "date": "2026-08-19", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I will be at IBC with team Dolby OptiView meet me there and check out all the cool stuff that will be happening in Amsterdam (and online).", "snippet": "I will be at IBC with team Dolby OptiView meet me there and check out all the cool stuff that will be happening in Amsterdam (and online).", "chars": 158, "text": "I will be at IBC with team Dolby OptiView meet me there and check out all the cool stuff that will be happening in Amsterdam (and online).\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "At #IBC2026, Dolby OptiView is bringing together everything rights holders, service providers, and streamers need to engage fans and turn that engagement into revenue. Join us at 5.F86 and we’d love to show you in person.\n \nBook a meeting & learn more: https://lnkd.in/eZB4rkQ7\n\n𝗢𝘂𝗿 𝗜𝗕𝗖 𝟮𝟬𝟮𝟲 𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘂𝗽 👇\n🍻 𝗛𝗮𝗽𝗽𝘆 𝗵𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗗𝗼𝗹𝗯𝘆\nWrap up day one with professionals across the streaming and media technology industries. And yes, we’re bringing back our classic tradition of serving your favorite Belgian beers! Space is limited - RSVP with password: dolbyibc26 ﻿\n \n🎙️ 𝗣𝗮𝗻𝗲𝗹 | 𝗕𝗲𝘆𝗼𝗻𝗱 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗹𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁: 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝘀𝗽𝗼", "imp": 176, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 176, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 176, 4, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Dolby OptiView"}, {"i": 72, "id": 7495719686085730304, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7495719686085730304/", "ts": "2026-08-19T05:53:56+00:00", "date": "2026-08-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I created a home for my latest Interactive Sport Experiences", "snippet": "I created a home for my latest Interactive Sport Experiences", "chars": 248, "text": "I created a home for my latest Interactive Sport Experiences\n ⚽ World Cup\n 🎾 Tennis\n 🚗 F1\nand many more in the works...\nHaving so much fun doing this.\nHopefully someone will notice.\nLink in the comments.\n\nProduced with passion by A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1010, "rx": 19, "cm": 8, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1010, 19, 8, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1010, 19, 8, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 73, "id": 7494381700500692993, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7494381700500692993/", "ts": "2026-08-15T13:17:16+00:00", "date": "2026-08-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "At IBC Amsterdam, if you want to be the GOAT, please do tell me about new features you are launching and what value they provide to clients ", "snippet": "At IBC Amsterdam, if you want to be the GOAT, please do tell me about new features you are launching and what value they provide to clients and the industry at ", "chars": 392, "text": "At IBC Amsterdam, if you want to be the GOAT, please do tell me about new features you are launching and what value they provide to clients and the industry at large, don’t tell me it’s done with AI.\nI don’t care and neither do your clients.\nI hope we really move with AI from it being the WHY at being the HOW.\nIt’s Ferragosto. So that’s the vibe.\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "qtext": "", "imp": 450, "rx": 8, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 152, "watch_time_s": 1987, "avg_watch_s": 13, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 450, 8, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 450, 8, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 74, "id": 7494264980939309056, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7494264980939309056/", "ts": "2026-08-15T05:33:28+00:00", "date": "2026-08-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "📺 📲  Smart article by Jacob Feldman on Sportico about advancements in live streaming experiences (one of many core passions of mine).", "snippet": "📺 📲  Smart article by Jacob Feldman on Sportico about advancements in live streaming experiences (one of many core passions of mine).", "chars": 1019, "text": "📺 📲  Smart article by Jacob Feldman on Sportico about advancements in live streaming experiences (one of many core passions of mine).\n\n\"The Seattle Seahawks will become the first team to leverage Dolby OptiView this Saturday during their opening preseason game, though the San Francisco-based tech company has also worked with NASCAR and the NFL on streaming projects.\nDolby’s streaming offering prioritizes low latency for viewers and ways for content owners to adjust the feed on a per-user level, including showing different ads to different people at different times, based on their preferences or how they’re watching.\" \n“A stats-focused person may be very open to watching an ad at specifically the time when the storytelling-minded person is really engaged in what’s happening,” Dolby Cloud Solutions SVP Giles Baker said, citing one hypothetical example.\"\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/dHPHHqG2\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 444, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Jacob Feldman", "person"], ["Sportico", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Giles Baker", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 444, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 444, 2, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 75, "id": 7493914255617044480, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7493914255617044480/", "ts": "2026-08-14T06:19:48+00:00", "date": "2026-08-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "My friend Gabriella Vacca knows a thing or two about managing thousand of people in a media and tech organization, and what an effective lea", "snippet": "My friend Gabriella Vacca knows a thing or two about managing thousand of people in a media and tech organization, and what an effective leader can do to be eff", "chars": 951, "text": "My friend Gabriella Vacca knows a thing or two about managing thousand of people in a media and tech organization, and what an effective leader can do to be effective in those scenarios. Once you are running that scale of people across several countries, the \"why\" stops travelling through you in person and has to be built into the structure.\nValues (yeah, I know,  boring...) and how you transmit them, become the vehicle for leadership.\nTransparency being a key one for tech functions:\n\"Transparency is not, well, I'll give you access to all the reports that I have. And so, no, that's too specific for the business to really understand what it means. So you need to find the right language that can resonate with your customers.\"\n\nI guess you have been in some sports and media meeting rooms - in either side - and have faced a similar situation. Have you?\n\nFull A guy with a scarf's episode in my LinkedIn newsletter and on Substack.\n\n#leadership", "qtext": "", "imp": 176, "rx": 2, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Gabriella Vacca", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["leadership"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 61, "watch_time_s": 661, "avg_watch_s": 10, "saves": 0, "sends": 1, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 176, 2, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 176, 2, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 76, "id": 7493718831807496192, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7493718831807496192/", "ts": "2026-08-13T17:23:16+00:00", "date": "2026-08-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "😎 Playback Pioneers 2026 goes live today and I'm one of the people featured in it.", "snippet": "😎 Playback Pioneers 2026 goes live today and I'm one of the people featured in it.", "chars": 521, "text": "😎 Playback Pioneers 2026 goes live today and I'm one of the people featured in it.\n\nI've spent most of my career on the vendor and rights holder side of sports media, in rooms where technology decisions get made quickly and explained badly. Reports like this one help, because they put names and reasoning next to the products.\n\nThanks to 5V Media and 5V Video on shipping it. Worth reading if you work anywhere near streaming, rights, or fan experience.\n\nFull report link: https://lnkd.in/dgPJw4mA\n\n#PlaybackPioneers2026", "qtext": "", "imp": 289, "rx": 7, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["5V Media", "company"], ["5V Video", "company"]], "tags": ["playbackpioneers2026"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 286, 7, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 289, 7, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 77, "id": 7493553560421539840, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7493553560421539840/", "ts": "2026-08-13T06:26:32+00:00", "date": "2026-08-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "❌ What clients are telling me about LinkedIn right now", "snippet": "❌ What clients are telling me about LinkedIn right now", "chars": 1373, "text": "❌ What clients are telling me about LinkedIn right now\nThree complaints I have heard in two weeks, and why I am listening before I argue back.\n\nI have heard a few clients commenting in a similar fashion while I have been discussing supporting and augmenting their marketing strategy, specifically for LinkedIn. So even if this is purely anecdotal and empirical, I will try to consolidate what I have heard recently.\nIt's their voice, not mine.\n\n 1️⃣ \"LinkedIn as a channel is becoming problematic for us. With AI, everybody is an expert on anything, posting images, visuals, PDFs and articles that are flooding the space, even when what they publish is not strictly AI slop.\"\n 2️⃣ \"So even if LinkedIn is trying to address the problem with a new algorithm and new tools, we still feel this has made it harder for us to invest effort into that platform.\"\n 3️⃣ \"The new algorithm, which may need some time to become accurate at a significant level, seems to be punishing the people and brands it was built to help.\"\n\nAs LinkedIn is my main channel for that line of business, this is not great to hear.\nI know I should have all the right arguments to counter this, and it is also good to listen to the client side even when you may not agree 100%, and then try to restart from there.\nSo I do not have answers for now.\nI am sure Dani Markovits may have something to say here...", "qtext": "", "imp": 4194, "rx": 11, "cm": 9, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Dani Markovits", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 4194, 11, 9, 0], ["2026-10-17", 4194, 11, 9, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 78, "id": 7493213402790699008, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7493213402790699008/", "ts": "2026-08-12T07:54:52+00:00", "date": "2026-08-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "F1 Season Interactive Experience", "snippet": "🚗 F1 Interactive Experience", "chars": 2900, "text": "🚗 F1 Interactive Experience\nAfter World Cup and Tennis, my third attempt at creating an alternative experience for interactive sport. A showcase.\nLive at https://lnkd.in/dksqnmgk\n\n❓❓❓ Why\nWhy I am doing this?\nBottom line: I want to be hired as a dataviz creative director of a sport/media entity.\nThe real story: These are the kind of things I used to deisgn, build, launch, operate with the at Deltatre for many years since I started working on “the web” since 1994!!! I always loved interactive experiences for sport and I was always looking for the dataviz holy grail, which in our industry could be defined as that idea, experience, visual, or stats that you launch and is then adopted clients in sport and media and eventually by fans worldwide. So now that I can do it all by myself and pivot quickly, I decided to spend these summer months dedicating a portion of my professional time to explore.\nI want to show there are alternatives to what is a standard web experience for spot websites, and regain a bit of passion in how these are done today.\n❌ A web experience, when everything is social media?\nI don’t have the ambition to replace official websites, some of which are good, some of which are average, some of which are style-1999 even these days, with major issues we would not be allowed to have in the 2000s and our clients would have kicked are digital-asses for.\n❌ A landscape-first, when everything is vertical-mobile?\nTrue that. Dumbest approach these days, when even athletics is moving the clock and time in the middle optimized for vertical videos. But to deep dive properly I needed a landscape model. In the end that could work well also unattended on a big screen... I even created a 1248 cards film for all World Cup players… why? I have no idea, I like wierd stuff. And did a YouTube video of that too.\n\n🤖 How\nI started playing around with the World Cup Bracket from a original design I saw online and then started making it interactive, … and then I lost control and started adding all types of visualization and interactivity. From there I did not stop.\nThe basic idea see if we can do two things:\nThe master view: Put everything on one page and make interactivity serve\nThe film: Create a deep dive film-style set of cards to highlight in a pre-establish order (ranking, …)\nEverything is built with a simple tech stack in this case (I am doing more complex B2B stuff too) using a mix of front-end models as needed.\nThe programming is all done through Claude Code with extended hours of conversation and feature definitions, visual design and UX refinement.\n\n🎛️ What\nF1viz turns the 2026 season into a set of cards you flip through — one film for the Drivers, one for the Constructors, one for the Races. Every card is a small data-story you can read at a glance or dig into. This guide walks through every screen and every marker.\n\n#f1 #sportstech #coding #ai Formula 1", "qtext": "", "imp": 1149, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Formula 1", "company"]], "tags": ["f1", "sportstech", "coding", "ai"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1149, 16, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1149, 16, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 79, "id": 7492326947872636928, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7492326947872636928/", "ts": "2026-08-09T21:12:25+00:00", "date": "2026-08-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "If you are a product person or a curious person Lenny’s podcast is a must watch. This episode with Netflix’s CTPO Elizabeth Stone is a great", "snippet": "If you are a product person or a curious person Lenny’s podcast is a must watch. This episode with Netflix’s CTPO Elizabeth Stone is a great opportunity to disc", "chars": 209, "text": "If you are a product person or a curious person Lenny’s podcast is a must watch. This episode with Netflix’s CTPO Elizabeth Stone is a great opportunity to discover it.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dTkCFj-J\nLenny Rachitsky", "qtext": "", "imp": 310, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 310, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 310, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 80, "id": 7492097750717612032, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7492097750717612032/", "ts": "2026-08-09T06:01:40+00:00", "date": "2026-08-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "World Athletics Ultimate Championship: An Innovation Attempt - I will Be Watching", "snippet": "🏟️ World Athletics Ultimate Championship: An Innovation Attempt - I will Be Watching on September 11-13", "chars": 2984, "text": "🏟️ World Athletics Ultimate Championship: An Innovation Attempt - I will Be Watching on September 11-13\n\n💾 My love for athletics. I started in this industry in 1987 and spent nearly two years living inside athletics, preparing for the 1991 World Championships in Tokyo. I worked with the federation for years after that and helped launch the first iaaf.org website.\n\n⏱️ What you learn on that kind of build is that athletics is a multi-sport event happening in one location under one production. Multiple broadcast feeds converging on a master production that decides, second by second, which discipline goes live. Replays owed to concurrent events, moments missed because the schedule shifted, and those surreal silences...\n\nInnovations:\n🎬 Broadcast\n 1️⃣ Rebuilt graphics with AR, athlete headshots and new data layers\n 2️⃣ Live win probabilities and projected finishes, weighted to recent form, updated in real time\n 3️⃣ Race clock moved to the bottom of the screen so the feed works vertically\n 4️⃣ Fan input into the graphics design before launch\n 5️⃣ A second-by-second show script, shared between broadcast and event presentation\n 6️⃣ An unnamed new viewing experience, announced and not yet detailed\n🏟️ Competition and presentation\n 1️⃣ A black infield, with only the areas in use lit\n 2️⃣ One field event at a time wherever the schedule allows\n 3️⃣ Centre-court-style interviews shown in-stadium and live on the world feed\n 4️⃣ A bespoke sound identity, with Mondo Duplantis delivering the Ultimate Anthem\n 5️⃣ Live acts opening each warm-up, plus a full lighting rig\n 6️⃣ Live lane draws for the 200m, staged the day before\n🏃 Athlete experience\n 1️⃣ The Day Before on 10 Sept: red carpet, media day, kids' athletics challenge led by Jefferson-Wooden and Tebogo, press conference with Coe and Bolt\n 2️⃣ Footage rights reserved so athletes get video and photography of their own discipline for their own channels\n 3️⃣ Tara Davis-Woodhall competing and serving as lead content creator\n 4️⃣ Club Ultimate by Noah Lyles, an athlete-only after-party he helped shape\n 5️⃣ No medals. A participation token for all, and a champion's trophy that connects to it\n 6️⃣ Dedicated integration for athletes' families\n📱 Digital\n 1️⃣ A new World Athletics app: live results, stories, video, predictions, games, notifications, built to run year-round\n 2️⃣ A season-long Road to the Ultimate tracker, live since 2025\n\n📌 My take: I will be watching.\nThe compact format means fewer athletes and fewer events, but I focus on commercial and experiential aspects. I am curious about whether athlete footage rights become a standard term, the new broadcats format works well, the app survives to the next edition, guaranteed payment holds talent against a league offering equity, athletes will see this as a pinnacle event in their career.\n\n📖 Full piece: sourced facts, the full innovations list, and athlete, media and fan reactions kept separate from my own reading. Link below. 👇\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1282, "rx": 11, "cm": 5, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["World Athletics", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1281, 11, 5, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1282, 11, 5, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 81, "id": 7491068557586251776, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7491068557586251776/", "ts": "2026-08-06T09:52:01+00:00", "date": "2026-08-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About Streaming the World Cup", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider About Streaming the World Cup", "chars": 1485, "text": "10 Things To Consider About Streaming the World Cup\nIt took me a few weeks to work out what actually happened in streaming across those six weeks. I collected a lot of sources, starting from the great work Dan Rayburn does on these occasions, and tried to distil some findings.\n\n10 things I took from it:\n1️⃣ The night streaming beat linear, once\n2️⃣ Chaotic streaming audience number\n3️⃣ Focus point: Brazil, YouTube and beyond\n4️⃣ The delivery record from the infrastructure side\n5️⃣ UHD was available at source and most rights holders declined it\n6️⃣ Latency? Synchronization\n7️⃣ The money came from a stoppage in play\n8️⃣ Acquisition was great, what about retention?\n9️⃣ Record piracy takedowns\n🔟 Capacity forecast is a science, in football… a bit less\n\nMy take: we have solved the engineering, or most of it. Live sport streams at global scale now and it holds. What we do not have is any industry system to tell us how it went exactly.\nIs there anybody who could enforce transparency based on a global standard for streaming stats, and do we even need one? From my partial observation point I would say yes.\n\n👇 Full article with every number, the caveats and a long source list is here below.\n🔢 A lot of figures in there, so please tell me if I messed up anything major.\n\nA guy with a scarf FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1033, "rx": 13, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1033, 13, 3, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1033, 13, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 82, "id": 7490794467009155072, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7490794467009155072/", "ts": "2026-08-05T15:42:53+00:00", "date": "2026-08-05", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Great to see Jeff Geels in this list!", "snippet": "Great to see Jeff Geels in this list!", "chars": 104, "text": "Great to see Jeff Geels in this list!\nStill remember smart chat & drinks in some place in midtown NYC...", "qtext": "𝐀𝐧𝐧𝐨𝐮𝐧𝐜𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐋𝐞𝐚𝐝𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐔𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐫 𝟒𝟎 𝐂𝐥𝐚𝐬𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝟐𝟎𝟐𝟔.\n\nFor the twelfth consecutive year, we've been on a global search to identify and recognise the leaders aged under 40 who are continually pushing boundaries in sport, rewriting the rules of the game and making their mark on the industry.\n\nAnd we're delighted to announce the individuals that have been inducted into the Class of 2026.\n\n👏 Congratulations to Aahna Mehrotra, Claire Bloomfield, Arthur Guisasola, Darren Henry, Michael Sansoni, Sophie Painter, Elizabeth Cohen and many many more.\n\nFind out more about the Class and for info on the big celebr", "imp": 106, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Jeff Geels", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 106, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 106, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Leaders in Sport"}, {"i": 83, "id": 7490675341850705920, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7490675341850705920/", "ts": "2026-08-05T07:49:31+00:00", "date": "2026-08-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 59: Gabriella Vacca - International Executive in Telco and Streaming - Movement Is Life", "snippet": "Ep. 59: Gabriella Vacca - International Executive in Telco and Streaming - Movement Is Life", "chars": 2806, "text": "Ep. 59: Gabriella Vacca - International Executive in Telco and Streaming - Movement Is Life\nWhat changes when your team stops being people you know by name\n\nWe studied computer engineering together in Turin, she built an international career: Olivetti, Omnitel, Bouygues Telecom, AT&T, ESPN Mobile, Gogo, ten years at Comcast, five years as CTO of Sky Italia.\n\nHighlights from the conversation 👇\n🏗️ Under 300 people the why travels through you personally. Above that you build it into the organization, using the Gartner pyramid with a values layer underneath and KPIs that serve  both the tech org and the business.\n🩺 Transparency, properly defined. You get your blood test back but you don’t need a doctor simply to read your scores. You need interpretation, empathy and plain language: Am I fine? Can I get better? Should I be seriously worried? That is what the business needs from technology.\n⛔ She once stopped a meeting with lawyers in the room because her own team had withheld a data point. \"Please forgive us for failing to uphold our commitment to transparency”. \n🌲 Intended change is like crossing a forest that you have never crossed before. You keep the far side in view, bring the right equipment, hire the skills you do not have, and say out loud what you know and what you do not.\n😴 She needs seven hours of sleep, and during COVID at Comcast that was operational data, which is why the plan started with shifts for everybody and a designated backup for every team member, including herself, so operations could continue 24/7 if anyone became unavailable.\n📺 Live media changed the way her team measured performance. Once the damage is on screen, it cannot be undone, so the team tracked near misses and how often they switched to contingency.\n📈 Turin taught her that risk is personal, so failure feels personal. Moving to the States rewrote that mindset into four words: “Movement is life, Movement is learning”. Be satisfied with 92%, because investing 100% of your energy in a single destination leaves every other opportunity unexplored.\n🏊 \"I define myself as a swimmer and not a diver.\" The senior leader’s job is to make sense of complexity so that others can make sound decisions, including when the right technology decision is the wrong decision for the company.\n🎙️ She now hosts The Reality of Women at Work Podcast on the EveCareers Network. Her definition of luck is readiness plus opportunity, combined with the ability to actually say yes.\n🧠 Three good things for every bad one, every day, because the brain latches onto the negative and must be trained to do otherwise.\nOf everything we discussed, the line that stayed with me most was: \"I want to be a chip in your brain. I don't want to be a line in your to-do list.\"\n\n#leadership #streaming #womenatwork A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 528, "rx": 14, "cm": 0, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Gabriella Vacca", "person"], ["Bouygues Telecom", "company"], ["AT&T", "company"], ["ESPN", "company"], ["Gogo", "company"], ["Comcast", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["leadership", "streaming", "womenatwork"], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 528, 14, 0, 4], ["2026-10-17", 528, 14, 0, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 84, "id": 7490347184639451137, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7490347184639451137/", "ts": "2026-08-04T10:05:32+00:00", "date": "2026-08-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "The One Man Brand", "title": "This is my current working setup, in my country home's wine cellar - as it has the more pleasant temperature in the house.", "snippet": "This is my current working setup, in my country home's wine cellar - as it has the more pleasant temperature in the house.", "chars": 1062, "text": "This is my current working setup, in my country home's wine cellar - as it has the more pleasant temperature in the house.\nI am researching, designing, building, launching, and trying to get some sense of what's going on in the industry.\nGood to be on/off the grid somehow but meeting people is still a crucial experience to understand the market.\nI will be at IBC in September with my friends at Dolby OptiView checking their new product features, and looking forward together.\nLet me know if you have time for a quick sync, it's always good to exchange notes and feel the vibes in Amsterdam.\nI am super curious to see what people will have as their main focus, now that AI is still there, center stage, but definitively not a great differentiation lever anymore.\nWe all know it's not an easy market so I will prefer and listen more actively to realistic point of views and stories. Not sure we still need the usual IBC-overhyped-all-excited vibe.  😎\n\nIBC - International Broadcasting Convention\nMatthias Ankli Jenny Lou Michelle Gong\n#streaming #conference #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 612, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["IBC - International Broadcasting Convention", "company"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Jenny Lou", "person"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"]], "tags": ["streaming", "conference", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 612, 9, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 612, 9, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 85, "id": 7489964926325792768, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7489964926325792768/", "ts": "2026-08-03T08:46:35+00:00", "date": "2026-08-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The 3M Tournament", "snippet": "💰 💵 💶 Now that the FIFA kerfuffle is over, the good news is we know there is a lot of capital ready to be invested in great football.", "chars": 391, "text": "💰 💵 💶 Now that the FIFA kerfuffle is over, the good news is we know there is a lot of capital ready to be invested in great football.\n\nWhat about a 3M Tournament?\nAbout cities more then clubs.\nWhat if a player played in all those cities and more with an M.\nDrafted this idea in 2023 but with no success :)\nLet me retry now.\nAny feedback welcome\nCheck the LinkedIn newsletter article below...", "qtext": "", "imp": 1966, "rx": 8, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1962, 8, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1966, 8, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 86, "id": 7489595553203294208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7489595553203294208/", "ts": "2026-08-02T08:18:49+00:00", "date": "2026-08-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "💰 💵 💶 Now that the FIFA kerfuffle is over, the good news is we know there is a lot of capital ready to be invested in great football.", "snippet": "💰 💵 💶 Now that the FIFA kerfuffle is over, the good news is we know there is a lot of capital ready to be invested in great football.", "chars": 1680, "text": "💰 💵 💶 Now that the FIFA kerfuffle is over, the good news is we know there is a lot of capital ready to be invested in great football.\n\nSo this morning I thought, let's extract from my idea archives, or should I say \"Stupid Idea Lab\", what I tried hard to have someone listen to, with zero success, at the start of 2023, a month after my solo career began.\nMaybe this time I will be luckier...\n\nIt's called\n ⚽ THE 3M TOURNAMENT 🇮🇹 🇪🇸 🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿\n\nSo the stupid idea is that I noticed that there are 3 big football cities starting with M, all with 2 great clubs, Madrid, Manchester, Milano, where the winner is the city, not the club.\n\n1️⃣ 3-day tournament in summer\n2️⃣ All clubs play each other, then 2 cities qualify for the final\n3️⃣ In the final, each city fields one club for the first half and its rival for the second.\n4️⃣ Involve the city as a whole, social and political value\n5️⃣  Live streaming can be syndicated to players' social channels\n 👇 See the few slides below for more details\n\n ⚽ Apart from the M thing, I love the idea of a city where the two clubs share one goal for once.\nThat is so revolutionary in today's fandom. A break from the daily confrontation for once, for the love of football.\n\n 💡 This is the original 2023 presentation, horrible slides I have to admit... but I left it untouched, pre-AI era.\n Most people told me why it cannot work, but 2026 is not 2023 anymore... we have seen stuff. So, hope never dies.\n\nReady to invest? DM me.\n\nYours truly, Carlo De Marchis, sport utopian and A guy with a scarf\n\nManchester United Manchester City Football Club Real Madrid C.F. Club Atlético de Madrid AC Milan FC Internazionale Milano\n#football #investing #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 682, "rx": 7, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [], "tags": ["investing", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 677, 7, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 682, 7, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 87, "id": 7488848977820336128, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7488848977820336128/", "ts": "2026-07-31T06:52:12+00:00", "date": "2026-07-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "🎾🎾🎾🎾🎾", "snippet": "🎾🎾🎾🎾🎾", "chars": 1501, "text": "🎾🎾🎾🎾🎾\nFor the love of Tennis and building interactive experiences for fans.\nI am introducing a new Tennis experience as a way to showcase what we can build out of the same (or even more limited data sets) if we explore outside of the rigid limits of 2000s style website for sport. A bit anachronistic, I know, to focus on sport websites in 2026, but I can finally build what I imagine.\n\nAfter my FIFA World Cup multi-dimensional and quite comprehensive interactive experience that I have designed and built while watching more games than I expect, being Italy out, it's now time for Tennis.\n\nThis is more limited, just a start, focusing on the ATP ranking (updated weekly) and showing all tournament and matches for each player\n🎾 100 Players Cards with career and 2026 summary info\n🎾 All tournaments played so far and summary info (Grad Slam, Master 1000, 500, 250...)\n- 🎾 All matches summary info\n🎾 All finals - Won or lost\n🎾 Opponents (played more than once or just once)\n🎾 Rivals - for the top 10 how they played against the other 9\nWorks great on laptops in landscape and well on mobile in vertical.\n\nWould love to see some sports or media property working with me on this and launch for real  😍.\nMore sports coming. Getting ready for a great NFL season and may look at F1 (my son asking for it).\nTry using it and let me know.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dcSeK2e6\n\nA guy with a scarfA guy with a scarf Realtime Fan EngagementRealtime Fan Engagement\n#tennis #dataviz #fanengagement #ux #interactive #sportstech", "qtext": "", "imp": 814, "rx": 13, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Realtime Fan Engagement", "showcase"]], "tags": ["tennis", "dataviz", "fanengagement", "ux", "interactive", "sportstech"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 814, 13, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 814, 13, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 88, "id": 7488472582929743873, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7488472582929743873/", "ts": "2026-07-30T05:56:32+00:00", "date": "2026-07-30", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽  📺  📲 Keep fans in sync: How to ensure viewer synchronization for live sports.", "snippet": "⚽  📺  📲 Keep fans in sync: How to ensure viewer synchronization for live sports.", "chars": 2152, "text": "⚽  📺  📲 Keep fans in sync: How to ensure viewer synchronization for live sports.\nI recently wrote about it and here comes a very clear explanation byt someone who is a bit more techie than me on streaming tech  😎: Jan Ozer\nWhat he is basically saying is:\n\n1️⃣ Measure both numbers. Track glass-to-glass latency from the pitch to the screen, and track whether every viewer sees the same play at the same moment. Report them separately.\n\n2️⃣ Instrument drift and viewer spread. Drift is how far one viewer has slipped from the target. Viewer spread is the gap between your earliest and your latest viewer. Spread is the number to put on the dashboard.\n\n3️⃣ Test the spoiler path. Compare your stream against a live social feed on a second device. If the feed announces the goal first, you have a synchronization defect.\n\n4️⃣ Match the protocol to the use case. WebRTC at 0.2 to 1 second for betting and co-watch. HESP at 1 to 9 seconds for interactive sports. HLS at 9 seconds and above for broadcast-style OTT with maximum device reach. Run more than one where the audience needs it.\n\n5️⃣ Set targetOffset deliberately. Configure the default in the dashboard or API, then override per source or per viewer in the player where you need finer control.\n\n6️⃣ Define the drift window. Set minimumOffset and maximumOffset around the target. Inside the window the player runs at normal speed. Outside it the player adjusts playback speed by around 8% to bring the viewer back.\n\n7️⃣ Set forceSeekOffset as the hard limit. Past that line the player performs a hard seek to the target. This is also the mechanism that holds sync on older devices with no support for fine speed adjustment.\n\n8️⃣ Keep the window asymmetric. Defaults sit at 0.66x targetOffset behind and 1.5x ahead. Allow more room behind the target than in front of it.\n\n9️⃣ Verify on real devices. Check the same match on phone, tablet, smart TV, and legacy player, on different networks, and record the spread across all of them.\n\n#streaming #sportstech\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiViewDolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "Have you ever experienced the spoiler effect, hearing the roar of the crowd celebrating a goal before you actually see it happen on screen? 🥅\n\nThat's a synchronization issue. We often talk about latency in live sports streaming, but ensuring a synchronized experience for viewers across devices and locations is even more critical.\n \nIn this piece, Jan Ozer an expert in the streaming industry, breaks down how streaming platforms can deliver a synchronized viewing experience with Dolby OptiView If you're building or scaling a live sports streaming service, this one is for you.\n\nRead now: https://", "imp": 93, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Jan Ozer", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["streaming", "sportstech"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 93, 4, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 93, 4, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Dolby OptiView"}, {"i": 89, "id": 7488254416060321793, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7488254416060321793/", "ts": "2026-07-29T15:29:37+00:00", "date": "2026-07-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "1248 Players and stats - The FIFA World Cup 2026", "snippet": "1248 Players and stats - The FIFA World Cup 2026", "chars": 95, "text": "1248 Players and stats - The FIFA World Cup 2026\n12 minutes - The film\nhttps://lnkd.in/dFGw7duZ", "qtext": "", "imp": 264, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 264, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 264, 2, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 90, "id": 7488162417118658561, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7488162417118658561/", "ts": "2026-07-29T09:24:03+00:00", "date": "2026-07-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "⚽ In a day when FIFA may be in the news for something bigger than this, I have decided to launch some new features for my interactive World ", "snippet": "⚽ In a day when FIFA may be in the news for something bigger than this, I have decided to launch some new features for my interactive World Cup experience.", "chars": 912, "text": "⚽ In a day when FIFA may be in the news for something bigger than this, I have decided to launch some new features for my interactive World Cup experience.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dhDkVUPw\n\n🔢 The FIFA World Cup by the numbers - fully sourced by FIFA's series of articles posted on fifa dot com few days ago.\n91 visual cards on all facts and figures as published officially and a series of what is still missing and I would like to know.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dVbidFbx\n\n🏃‍♂️‍➡️ 1248 players card. Beautiful visuals and summary stats to scroll through and discover new players and also those who did not even played a minute, beyond Kante...\nhttps://lnkd.in/dmapXRpx\n\n❓ One question for FIFAFIFA. Why not take this and integrate it into fifa dot come after a review? I guess fan would love ti. And yes I know a thing or two about building websites like that... so it's not a naive request.  ⁉️\n\n#football #dataviz #ai #analytics", "qtext": "", "imp": 503, "rx": 7, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA", "company"], ["FIFA", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "dataviz", "ai", "analytics"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 503, 7, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 503, 7, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 91, "id": 7487848146635575296, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7487848146635575296/", "ts": "2026-07-28T12:35:15+00:00", "date": "2026-07-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "🚫 I started analyzing the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States by the numbers article on FIFA dot com and I found som", "snippet": "🚫 I started analyzing the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States by the numbers article on FIFA dot com and I found some discrepancies on t", "chars": 929, "text": "🚫 I started analyzing the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States by the numbers article on FIFA dot com and I found some discrepancies on the host city page.\n\nThe three discrepancies in FIFA's host city goal table\nFIFA's host city page lists matches and goals for each of the 16 venues. The match counts are correct and sum to 104. The goal counts sum to 310 against a published tournament total of 308. Thirteen cities are accurate. Three are wrong. Equally distributed among host countries.\n\n 🇲🇽 Mexico City: published 24, actual 16.\n 🇺🇸 Los Angeles: published 24, actual 26.\n 🇨🇦 Vancouver: published 19, actual 23.\n\nSorry FIFA (thanks for all those numbers) but I am building a fact & figures extension for my interactive World Cup experience and need accurate data.\n\nThis is the page in question: https://lnkd.in/dMHwuaEQ\nUPDATE: the page has been corrected. Thank you, Carlo. 😎 😏\n#dataviz #bug #football", "qtext": "", "imp": 1234, "rx": 7, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["FIFA", "company"]], "tags": ["dataviz", "bug", "football"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1233, 7, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1234, 7, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 92, "id": 7487786943498326017, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7487786943498326017/", "ts": "2026-07-28T08:32:03+00:00", "date": "2026-07-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "✍ Substack and Pangram Labs - Simple Guide for Readers and Writers", "snippet": "✍ Substack and Pangram Labs - Simple Guide for Readers and Writers", "chars": 2078, "text": "✍ Substack and Pangram Labs - Simple Guide for Readers and Writers\nWhat I have learned in the last week on the Substack platform and the Chrome extension I have developed to make it easier to read\n\n 🤖 Since July 21st 2026, all Substack posts and notes (100 chars min and no videos as of this writing) are now scanned through the Pangram engine to score how much of it was likely written by hand or with AI assistance.\n\n ⚙️ A Chrome Extension\nAs clicking every time on the 3 dots to check each post and note annoyed me quite a bit I decided to develop a super simple chrome extension (you can install it easily on your browser) that would show for each post and note a small popup with all Pangram score info and details.\n 🌐 How to get my Chrome Extension and how to install it\nIf you are a nerd: install manually from this compressed folder.\nExpand it and then go to the manage extension on Chrome, enable developer mode, then use Load unpacked.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dpHtegix\nIf you are a not nerd: I have published on the Chrome Extension store as un listed but I am still waiting for approval from google. Will share the link ASAP…\n\n ✏️ For Writers/Creators on Substack\nThe good thing that I did discover a bit later (my bad) is that you can test your article before publishing to see if what you have done scores in a way that you believe is accurate.\nHow to access this feature: click continue top right from the edit page, you will see something like this at the bottom of the page.\nIf you click Scan for AI text you will get the score for what you have written until now, like this below.\nYou can then go back and edit the text to finish your work.\nYou can also disable (and re-enable in case) AI detection for your post.\n\n 😎 How I make this\nAs you may have noticed there is a text that is personal to my account A guy with a scarfA guy with a scarf that explains my approach to using AI for this type of content.\nHow do you create / edit that? t’s in teh same window if you have not created it yet or you can then subsequently edit with the 3 dots menu.\n\n#ai #creator #writing", "qtext": "", "imp": 212, "rx": 3, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Substack", "company"], ["Pangram Labs", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "creator", "writing"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 212, 3, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 212, 3, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 93, "id": 7487422450595651584, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7487422450595651584/", "ts": "2026-07-27T08:23:41+00:00", "date": "2026-07-27", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Everyone Is Removing AI While Shipping AI, and What “Made with AI” Really Means", "snippet": "Debate of the week!", "chars": 19, "text": "Debate of the week!", "qtext": "🤖 Everyone Is Removing AI While Shipping AI, and What “Made with AI” Really Means\n\n👀 Contradictions? Debate of the week or month?\n\n🎛️ Spotify and LinkedIn are cleaning up AI content while launching new AI-based tools. Substack is betting the other way. All three are chasing a signal none of them seems to be able to really measure.\n\nBut the fundamental question is:\n❓ WHAT DOES \"MADE WITH AI\" REALLY MEAN?\n\n🤖 Have you heard of Pangram Labs before?\n\n🧠 I have been ruminating on this all weekend and have tried to put things in perspective in the article below isolating Facts, Opinions and my take...", "imp": 387, "rx": 2, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 387, 2, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 387, 2, 2, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 94, "id": 7487422235637510144, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7487422235637510144/", "ts": "2026-07-27T08:22:50+00:00", "date": "2026-07-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Everyone Is Removing AI While Shipping AI, and What “Made with AI” Really Means", "snippet": "🤖 Everyone Is Removing AI While Shipping AI, and What “Made with AI” Really Means", "chars": 795, "text": "🤖 Everyone Is Removing AI While Shipping AI, and What “Made with AI” Really Means\n\n👀 Contradictions? Debate of the week or month?\n\n🎛️ Spotify and LinkedIn are cleaning up AI content while launching new AI-based tools. Substack is betting the other way. All three are chasing a signal none of them seems to be able to really measure.\n\nBut the fundamental question is:\n❓ WHAT DOES \"MADE WITH AI\" REALLY MEAN?\n\n🤖 Have you heard of Pangram Labs before?\n\n🧠 I have been ruminating on this all weekend and have tried to put things in perspective in the article below isolating Facts, Opinions and my take... plus a lot of original source links.\n\n😎 Spoiler alert: I do not have a final answer, do you?\n\nFinal curiosity: how will that article be rated by Pangram? 😏\n\n#ai #madewithAI #creative #innovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 849, "rx": 8, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Spotify", "company"], ["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Substack", "company"], ["Pangram Labs", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "madewithai", "creative", "innovation"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 849, 8, 3, 2], ["2026-10-17", 849, 8, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 95, "id": 7487038938059100162, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7487038938059100162/", "ts": "2026-07-26T06:59:45+00:00", "date": "2026-07-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "2 others", "title": "Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media: 10 Things To Consider", "snippet": "Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media: 10 Things To Consider", "chars": 1231, "text": "Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media: 10 Things To Consider\nThis is your field manual for operating on the vendor side of sports and media technology.\n\n\"10 Things to Consider: Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\" is a structured, practical guide built from more than three decades of experience across product, delivery, partnerships, commercial strategy, and client engagement.\nThe book 200 pages contains 51 working lists organised across 10 categories, each designed to be revisited at specific moments: before engaging a client, before committing to a deal, when a project changes direction, or when pressure increases during delivery. These are not theoretical frameworks. They are checkpoint tools for experienced practitioners navigating complex, high-stakes environments where structure and behaviour determine outcomes more than technology alone.\nWhether you are a founder, a sales leader, a delivery manager, or an advisor working with sports and media organisations, this book gives you a shared vocabulary and a decision-support structure that sharpens judgment over time.\nPaperback or Kindle.\nBUY it on Amazon: https://amzn.eu/d/03pDBBGl\n\n#sportstech #b2b #streaming #sales #delivery", "qtext": "", "imp": 165, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "b2b", "streaming", "sales", "delivery"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 165, 9, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 165, 9, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 96, "id": 7486676659702722561, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7486676659702722561/", "ts": "2026-07-25T07:00:11+00:00", "date": "2026-07-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "Make LinkedIn Better for Creators", "title": "As I was editing my latest post I noticed changes on the LinkedIn editing window!", "snippet": "As I was editing my latest post I noticed changes on the LinkedIn editing window!", "chars": 497, "text": "As I was editing my latest post I noticed changes on the LinkedIn editing window!\nI am always curios and engaged whenever this platform innovates, as you may have noticed, I have a lot of things I would like them to change and introduce.\nQuite notable is the emphasis on the collab new feature.\n1. Who you post to\n2. Who can comment\n3. Collabs\nNice new UI more prominent.\nWill use with all my relevant pages from now on.\n\nI am here waiting for more\nYessi Bello-Perez 😊\n#linkedin #productmanagement", "qtext": "", "imp": 299, "rx": 1, "cm": 3, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Yessi Bello-Perez", "person"]], "tags": ["linkedin", "productmanagement"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 297, 1, 3, 0], ["2026-10-17", 299, 1, 3, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 97, "id": 7486674792247468032, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7486674792247468032/", "ts": "2026-07-25T06:52:46+00:00", "date": "2026-07-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": "A guy with a scarf", "title": "My son Miles (11yrs old) asked me to create this graphic for him and print it to post it in his room...", "snippet": "My son Miles (11yrs old) asked me to create this graphic for him and print it to post it in his room...", "chars": 186, "text": "My son Miles (11yrs old) asked me to create this graphic for him and print it to post it in his room...\nSo I though I could shared it here too on Saturday morning.\nWhat's the story here?", "qtext": "", "imp": 1134, "rx": 2, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1134, 2, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1134, 2, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 98, "id": 7486345717301833728, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7486345717301833728/", "ts": "2026-07-24T09:05:08+00:00", "date": "2026-07-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Hear me out.", "snippet": "Hear me out.", "chars": 1344, "text": "Hear me out.\n⚽ 📲 📺 This is about ads during live games.\nWatching the World Cup on DAZN in Italy, as a paying subscriber.\n1️⃣ Main point: I am not a fan of 30 secs when I am joining a live game that is ongoing, I want to watch as soon as the streaming wheels can bring me live video on the device I am on (small or big screen) and tend to despise the brand, I am exposed to, badly.\nI am sure those are the most expensive slots.\nI am sure brands are eager to delay my watching... in the most dramatic moment.\nI am sure that huge studies and focus groups have been run, so that will win the argument for sure, against my anecdote-based reasoning.\n2️⃣ Secondary point: I am ok with non-full takeover ads, L-shaped, double screen, whatever is a creative attempt to innovated ads on streaming.\n\n✅ Solution (maybe): a more sophisticated ad-serving mechanism that lets me join live ASAP and assigns a debt to me as a user joining live when the game started, and uses SSAI/SGAI or similar tech to serve those ads (or more personalized ones) the first time there is a pause in the game.\nHave a better idea, or comment, please share.\n\nIf you think this is naive because I do not understand the commercial underpinning logic, take a hike :) or join me for a chat and a coffe in a traditional \"bar\" in Torino, Italy.\n\n#advertising #streaming #sportstech #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 7936, "rx": 65, "cm": 19, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["DAZN", "company"]], "tags": ["advertising", "streaming", "sportstech", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 7936, 65, 19, 2], ["2026-10-17", 7936, 65, 19, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 99, "id": 7485603185961664513, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7485603185961664513/", "ts": "2026-07-22T07:54:35+00:00", "date": "2026-07-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "My friends at LinkedIn News Europe asked what advice I'd give someone who wants to work across cultures. Benjamin Braun's answer in the late", "snippet": "My friends at LinkedIn News Europe asked what advice I'd give someone who wants to work across cultures. Benjamin Braun's answer in the latest Leadership Lesson", "chars": 2976, "text": "My friends at LinkedIn News Europe asked what advice I'd give someone who wants to work across cultures. Benjamin Braun's answer in the latest Leadership Lessons is immersion. Watch the films, read the local press before you land.\n😎 My story runs a different way, and so does my approach.\nI never chose to work internationally. From my first day in sports production in the late 80s, it was the only environment I knew. Different countries, different languages, different ways of getting things done, every week, every month, for years on end.\nBack then being multicultural started with something dull. Knowing which electrical plugs a country ran on. The logistics of producing anything abroad before digital existed. You earned respect for a place by getting the small things wrong first.\n📲 Digital changed the mechanics of all of it. You read a country's press over coffee, sit in a call with a team in Seoul before lunch, share a working file across four time zones by the evening. Translation is instant. Local context is one search away. The preparation Benjamin Braun describes takes an afternoon now, where it used to take months and a shelf of paper. What digital left untouched is the time it takes for people to trust you. Tools got faster than that. Over the years I have come to know people properly through a screen. Working relationships that ran for a decade on calls, messages and shared files, with real depth in them. Then you finally meet one of those people in a hotel lobby in Doha or Munich and something else arrives, an emotion no call has ever given me.\n💡 True story. Some of my biggest contracts started with a LinkedIn message from people I had stopped working with five or seven years earlier. One line, out of nowhere, from a room I had left long ago. I still have those messages saved.\nWe were never tourists. On a big event you might work with local partners for two, three, four years before anything happened on the field. That kind of time with people builds a connection a short visit never gives you.\n🏃‍♀️ Tokyo, 1991. World Championships in athletics. We spent months there the year before, producing everything in two writing systems, Latin and Japanese, across printouts and live broadcast, all of it synchronised by hand. Pre-internet. It was the hardest and most fascinating start I could have asked for. It showed me how far a culture can sit from your own assumptions, and how much you gain by paying attention.\n✅ Two words carry most of it for me. Curiosity and respect. Every so often, in a handful of rooms, I still catch a trace of old imperial habit in the way someone speaks to a local partner. Usually from people who would be mortified to hear it put that way. It quietly closes doors they never see close.\n\nThere are still things about the way I work that I cannot explain to a colleague from somewhere else, so my own work here is far from finished.\n\n#LeadershipLessons Solange Uwimana Yessi Bello-Perez A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 345, "rx": 10, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn News Europe", "showcase"], ["Benjamin Braun", "person"], ["Solange Uwimana", "person"], ["Yessi Bello-Perez", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["leadershiplessons"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 345, 10, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 345, 10, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 100, "id": 7484871573435342849, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7484871573435342849/", "ts": "2026-07-20T07:27:25+00:00", "date": "2026-07-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ 🇪🇸 Spain are champions.", "snippet": "⚽ 🇪🇸 Spain are champions.", "chars": 856, "text": "⚽ 🇪🇸 Spain are champions.\nWon 7 matches, 1 draw vs Cape Verde.\n14 goals scored but only 1 conceded.\nSo obviously Unai Simon is a hero but... looking at my unsophisticated interactive dataviz I also noticed something.\nI put all lineups and subs in one screen. The whole defense dept for Spain is very stable, they just subbed 3 times and very vare late in the game.\nUnai Simon, Cubarsi' and Cucurella always on the pitch, every match, every second, more players played in all matches.\nArgentina screen looks different, and so do many other teams - have a look.\n\n💡 🤖 Side note: I think more and more that FIFA should create a progranm for Dataviz creators which maybe a thing from now on... I am trying to collect names and craeet a small community maybe?\n\nFIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\nA guy with a scarf\n#football #ai #dataviz", "qtext": "", "imp": 1342, "rx": 14, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "ai", "dataviz"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1342, 14, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1342, 14, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 101, "id": 7484500037335609344, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7484500037335609344/", "ts": "2026-07-19T06:51:04+00:00", "date": "2026-07-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Road to the Final: A field guide to the World Cup 2026 bracket app", "snippet": "I produced a FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States movie \"Road to the final - The Film\", but it's not what you expect.", "chars": 1210, "text": "I produced a FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States movie \"Road to the final - The Film\", but it's not what you expect.\nAs a data storyteller, dataviz passionate and interactive fan digital experiences expert (?!) I created a monstrous 800+ cards sequence with all 104 matches, every goal, save, stats, fantasy, power ranking, controversy (yep, there is a controversy tracker), tactical evolution, links to YouTube official highlights , Alexandre Bogachev dataviz, official deep FIFA reports PDF.\nWhat started as an interactive bracket became more and more by the day.\nI am not sure someone will really stay or click through the whole thing but you can also go to a stage or select a single team.\nChek the film, tthe tactical view which compares, for each team ,all matches lineups and subs in one screen, and have fun with the controversy list.\nAll in one page.\n\nThis is the full story.\nTry it! Click and explore.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dhDkVUPw\n\nThe final question is not who will win tonight but is: all this blossoming of similar dataviz and interactive experiences around the world cup enabled by the latest AI-coding and design tools a good or a bad thing?\n\n#football #ai A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1108, "rx": 17, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["Alexandre Bogachev", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "ai"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1108, 17, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1108, 17, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 102, "id": 7483808337114017793, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7483808337114017793/", "ts": "2026-07-17T09:02:30+00:00", "date": "2026-07-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I am having fun in som many ways during this FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States but most of them are non-fan cases,", "snippet": "I am having fun in som many ways during this FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States but most of them are non-fan cases, quite aligned with ", "chars": 1089, "text": "I am having fun in som many ways during this FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States but most of them are non-fan cases, quite aligned with my sport/media tech background.\nMostly it is about building a ever-growing set of experiences for my Road to the Final website.\n\nI am researching and collecting data, info, and media coverage throughout the ecosystem from a variety of sources.\nI recently started collecting a list of controversies during the tournamentwhich is clearly a non-100%-factual kinf of info.\nSources are a mix of official data and mostly media and socisal reactions, so highly debatable per-se.\nVAR is in many of them. I do have all VAR official events.\n\nThe ONE THING I thought I would be able to find, as I already mentioned in a comment to a previous post, is the video, audio, and transcript of each referee's VAR announcement.\n\nI do not expect FIFA to make it available as part of the official fifa-com match web page (why not?) but I imagine a broadcaster would do it...\nNONE FOUND.\nAnyone?\nGood idea, bad idea?\nLet me know...\n\n#football #VAR #AI", "qtext": "", "imp": 729, "rx": 8, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "var", "ai"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 729, 8, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 729, 8, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 103, "id": 7483060206428495872, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7483060206428495872/", "ts": "2026-07-15T07:29:41+00:00", "date": "2026-07-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 58: Dani Markovits - Must Watch LinkedIn Expert on the Platform's Credibility Shift & Sport", "snippet": "Ep. 58: Dani Markovits - Why LinkedIn Now Measures the Person Behind the Post", "chars": 2082, "text": "Ep. 58: Dani Markovits - Why LinkedIn Now Measures the Person Behind the Post\nThe Shake Content CCO on the credibility shift, athletes moving in, and why the person now carries the brand.\n\nI sat down with Dani Markovits for episode 58 of A guy with a scarf, summer edition. Dani was one of LinkedIn's first creator managers and spent four years there. He now runs client work at Shake Content.\n\n🎯 People show up with intent. People open LinkedIn ready to connect and talk business. That is why the DMs work and the trust holds.\n🎙️ Earn the right to go off-topic. Lead with your expertise, then add the human side. That mix is what makes a profile worth following.\n🏟️ Sport is arriving late. Athletes finally see LinkedIn as the one place the audience wants to hear about the life beyond the pitch. As Dani put it, they don't want to be known only as the tennis guy or the football lady.\n📊 Small audiences can win. \"You don't need a huge audience.\" Who you get in front of is what counts.\n🤖 The algorithm now checks who is posting. LinkedIn weighs whether you have the credibility to speak on a topic. The same football post travels further from me than from Dani, and that is the right call. Reach can dip. Engagement holds or climbs.\n💰 LinkedIn is finally serving creators. A native creator marketplace, video monetization, a call-out feature. Brands understand that experts drive the decisions, and in B2B they all live here.\n🙂 Build around the person. Only low single digits of people actually post on LinkedIn. The room to stand out is wide open.\n🏢 Companies changed their minds. Four years ago firms blocked employees from posting. Now they push them and even hire on the strength of their LinkedIn activity. Brand equity flows through your own people.\n📬 And after telling everyone else to do it for years, Dani launched his own newsletter, \"LinkedIn/actually.\"\n\nMy position: LinkedIn rewarding credibility over raw reach is the healthiest move the platform has made in years. It favors people who know their subject and show up as themselves.\n\n#linkedin #creators #content #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 999, "rx": 20, "cm": 4, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dani Markovits", "person"], ["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Shake Content", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["linkedin", "creators", "content", "ai"], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 999, 20, 4, 5], ["2026-10-17", 999, 20, 4, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 104, "id": 7482670495608258560, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7482670495608258560/", "ts": "2026-07-14T05:41:07+00:00", "date": "2026-07-14", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 83: Philipp Männer - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business", "snippet": "🏁 Ep. 83: Philipp Männer, WRC Promoter - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business", "chars": 161, "text": "🏁 Ep. 83: Philipp Männer, WRC Promoter - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business\nWhy the US is WRC's number one digital market, with no event and no local hero.", "qtext": "🏁 Ep. 83: Philipp Männer, WRC Promoter - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business\nWhy the US is WRC's number one digital market, with no event and no local hero.\n\nFor episode 83 of Retention Zone I sat down with Philipp Männer, Senior Director Media Rights and OTT at WRC Promoter. Rally has one of the toughest live products in motorsport, and it has become a real global streaming business. Here is what stuck with me:\n🎯 One role owns all of distribution. Broadcast licensing and owned-and-operated platforms under one person. Still rare, and it matches how fragmented distribution actually is now.", "imp": 556, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 555, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 556, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 105, "id": 7482670266590941184, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7482670266590941184/", "ts": "2026-07-14T05:40:12+00:00", "date": "2026-07-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 83: Philipp Männer - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business", "snippet": "🏁 Ep. 83: Philipp Männer, WRC Promoter - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business", "chars": 1804, "text": "🏁 Ep. 83: Philipp Männer, WRC Promoter - How Rally Built a Direct-to-Fan Business\nWhy the US is WRC's number one digital market, with no event and no local hero.\n\nFor episode 83 of Retention Zone I sat down with Philipp Männer, Senior Director Media Rights and OTT at WRC Promoter. Rally has one of the toughest live products in motorsport, and it has become a real global streaming business. Here is what stuck with me:\n🎯 One role owns all of distribution. Broadcast licensing and owned-and-operated platforms under one person. Still rare, and it matches how fragmented distribution actually is now.\n🏎️ The product is unique. Rally is a time trial across ~20 stages, Thursday to Sunday. WRC All Live carries every stage live, 20 to 25 hours per event. Fans behave like golf fans. Once they are in, they watch everything.\n📺 Rally.TV is a service and a brand. One home for WRC and ERC, plus a FAST channel across Europe, a pay channel, and a DAZN deal.\n🇺🇸 The US is WRC's number one digital market. Grown organically through digital, led by the More Than Machine doc on YouTube. An event is planned in the US in 2027.\n🔁 Retention first. A fan-requested interactive data overlay just shipped, with more data and AI storytelling coming. Growth moves never come at retention's expense.\n💶 Pricing is the next frontier. 13 euros a month, 120 a year today. Installments launched last August and were a big success. Localization, freemium, and flexible payments are next, market by market.\n📅 Events and drivers beat seasonality. Local heroes pull their home audiences, and that matters more than the calendar for a sport this spread out.\nFIA World Rally Championship (WRC)\n\n🎧 Full article and episode below.\n\n-----\n🎧 Retention Zone – Episode 83\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1355, "rx": 19, "cm": 2, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Philipp Männer", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Philipp Männer", "person"], ["FIA World Rally Championship (WRC)", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1351, 19, 2, 5], ["2026-10-17", 1355, 19, 2, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 106, "id": 7482116562825625600, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7482116562825625600/", "ts": "2026-07-12T16:59:59+00:00", "date": "2026-07-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Accenture Italia — AI Thought Leadership Lead role", "snippet": "Accenture Italia — AI Thought Leadership Lead role", "chars": 1667, "text": "Accenture Italia — AI Thought Leadership Lead role\nPerché credo non farò Apply.\n\nEppure penso che, per:\n🤖 la mia esperienza all'Università di Torino negli anni '80 in Expert Systems con Martelli\n🤖 la mia esperienza come thought leader, public speaker e Chief Evangelist dagli anni 2010\n🤖 la mia guida di un AI Innovation Lab pre-ChatGPT nei primi anni 2020\n🤖 le mie ultime venture, A guy with a scarf e LinkedIn Top Voice dal 2023\n🤖 i molti e svariati progetti AI lanciati dal 2023 a oggi\nsarei una scelta che, per quanto inusuale, porterebbe valore, curiosità ed energia a questo ruolo.\nE invece, PARADOSSALMENTE, appena ho iniziato la procedura di apply, molto AI-based, il mio entusiasmo si è spento di colpo.\nHo avuto paura?\nSono un outsider. Non credo che verrò considerato da questo sistema.\nVoi che dite? Sbaglio?\n#AI #recruiting\n-----\nAccenture Italia — AI Thought Leadership Lead role\nWhy I think I won't Apply.\n\nAnd yet I believe that, thanks to:\n🤖 my experience at the University of Turin in the '80s in Expert Systems with Martelli\n🤖 my experience as a thought leader, public speaker and Chief Evangelist since the 2010s\n🤖 my leadership of an AI Innovation Lab pre-ChatGPT in the early 2020s\n🤖 my latest ventures, A guy with a scarf and LinkedIn Top Voice since 2023\n🤖 the many and varied AI projects launched from 2023 to today\nI would be a choice that, however unusual, would bring value, curiosity and energy to this role.\nAnd yet, PARADOXICALLY, the moment I started the apply process, very AI-based, my enthusiasm went out all at once.\nWas I afraid?\nI'm a  clear outsider. I don't think I'll be considered by this system.\nWhat do you say? Am I wrong?", "qtext": "", "imp": 3599, "rx": 9, "cm": 3, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Accenture Italia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Accenture Italia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "recruiting"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3599, 9, 3, 0], ["2026-10-17", 3599, 9, 3, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 107, "id": 7482059482944946176, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7482059482944946176/", "ts": "2026-07-12T13:13:10+00:00", "date": "2026-07-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "And here we are - 4 matches to go...", "snippet": "And here we are - 4 matches to go...", "chars": 454, "text": "And here we are - 4 matches to go...\nLots of new features in my interactive World Cup Bracket.\nClick anywhere to discover.\nIt will stay after the final as a way to find everything (almost) from one page.\nStrong emotions, lots of drama.\nMaybe I should find a way to track all controversies per match, per team, per player.\nNot sure someone is keeping track of those in a accurate way...\nWill reserach.\nHave fun.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dhDkVUPw\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 249, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 249, 4, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 249, 4, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 108, "id": 7481995267240529920, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7481995267240529920/", "ts": "2026-07-12T08:58:00+00:00", "date": "2026-07-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I’m not a big fan of this type of controversy, but one other source of truth could be, and I specifically said could, the footage from the s", "snippet": "I’m not a big fan of this type of controversy, but one other source of truth could be, and I specifically said could, the footage from the spider cam.  It may o", "chars": 383, "text": "I’m not a big fan of this type of controversy, but one other source of truth could be, and I specifically said could, the footage from the spider cam.  It may or may not show some instability if the ball touched the wire, depending on how sensitive the camera is to that.\nAny broadcast production people around to comment?\n\nFIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "qtext": "", "imp": 13549, "rx": 18, "cm": 14, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 13549, 18, 14, 0], ["2026-10-17", 13549, 18, 14, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 109, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-07-12", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "TORINO e CULTURA", "with": null, "title": "Dentro Area 73: i Laboratori di Reply House", "snippet": "🤖 Dentro Area 73: i laboratori di Reply House Dai digital human al cinema generativo, fino ai marmi che non esistono più in natura e sono stati riportati in vit", "chars": 1709, "text": "🤖 Dentro Area 73: i laboratori di Reply House Dai digital human al cinema generativo, fino ai marmi che non esistono più in natura e sono stati riportati in vita da un algoritmo.\n\nSeconda puntata del mio racconto sull'apertura della De Sonnaz per Torino e Cultura.\nIl percorso entra nei laboratori, guidato da Filippo Rizzante, CTO di Reply: Area 73, dedicata a multisensorialità e immersività, i digital human che riconoscono le persone e rispondono in tempo reale, gli AI Studios e la produzione video, fino all'AI Film Festival di Venezia, dove quest'anno sono arrivati cinquanta lungometraggi da tutto il mondo. 🎬\n\n🎨 La storia che mi ha colpito di più me l'ha raccontata Giorgia Fortuna, Partner di Machine Learning Reply, autrice del lavoro sui marmi: le superfici dell'edificio sono lastre stampate, generate a partire dalla libreria storica di Marazzi. Dentro quell'archivio ci sono marmi che in natura non esistono più, cave esaurite. L'algoritmo li ha riportati in vita, e poi è iniziata la parte difficile: il forno, i colori che si bruciano, le lastre da stampare una dopo l'altra.\n\n✨ La terza e ultima puntata sarà dedicata al mio progetto: \"Torino Moves. The building in the city, the city in the building\". Circa trentacinque immagini di Torino in bianco e nero, generate con l'intelligenza artificiale e installate sulle vetrate delle sale riunioni del primo e del secondo piano. Un lavoro site specific pensato sala per sala, dove sul vetro, al posto dei classici puntini di schermatura, corrono i prompt che hanno generato ogni immagine, scritti in cinque lingue: sono le parole a creare la trasparenza.\ncarlodemarchis\n\n#ReplyHouse #Area73 #Torino #TorinoMoves #IntelligenzaArtificiale #AIart", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 5, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Reply", "company"], ["Filippo Rizzante", "person"], ["Giorgia Fortuna", "person"], ["carlodemarchis", "showcase"]], "tags": ["replyhouse", "area73", "torino", "torinomoves", "intelligenzaartificiale", "aiart"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 5, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", null, 5, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 110, "id": 7481242043474894848, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7481242043474894848/", "ts": "2026-07-10T07:04:57+00:00", "date": "2026-07-10", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Reply House De Sonnaz: da caserma a casa dell’innovazione", "snippet": "One of the many other things I spend my time on...", "chars": 174, "text": "One of the many other things I spend my time on...\nA long project finally open.\nPhotography beyond photography as part of a huge architectural project in Torino.\n[In italian]", "qtext": "Dopo anni di intenso lavoro, ieri ho vissuto l'apertura di Reply House nell'ex caserma De Sonnaz a Torino.\n\nPer me è stato un momento speciale, perché una parte del mio lavoro vive dentro questo edificio. Le 35 immagini in bianco e nero della città, la serie Torino Moves, sono stampate sui vetri delle sale riunioni: i prompt che le hanno generate, scritti in cinque lingue, creano la trasparenza sul vetro al posto dei soliti puntini. \"The building in the city, the city in the building\".\nCarlo De Marchis carlodemarchis\n\nHo scritto un articolo sull'apertura e la conferenza stampa moderata da Tati", "imp": 223, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 223, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 223, 2, 0, 0]], "quoted": "TORINO e CULTURA"}, {"i": 111, "id": 7480903591961116672, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7480903591961116672/", "ts": "2026-07-09T08:40:04+00:00", "date": "2026-07-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Story About Latency and Synchronization at the FIFA World Cup", "snippet": "⚽ 📺 📲 A Story About Latency and Synchronization at the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "chars": 2251, "text": "⚽ 📺 📲 A Story About Latency and Synchronization at the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\nWhat multi-device viewing taught me about synchronization, latency drift, and the notification that beats the picture.\nFor years we have argued about latency. This World Cup handed me a different problem. Synchronization.\nI watched in Italy on DAZN, across a living room, a studio and a country house. Same match, several screens, every screen sitting in its own moment. The kids' tablet ran a few seconds off the big screen. My phone, notifications on, buzzed seconds before anything happened on the television. Most of the time it was right. Someone was about to score.\nHere is why. The alert fires off the live data feed the moment the goal goes in, and reaches your phone in a second or two. The picture is still crawling through encode, packaging, the CDN and the buffer on your screen, so the notification lands first. The phone already knows the score the television has not shown.\nI have worked behind the scenes in this industry long enough to feel a small, stupid smugness when it happens. Not a good look for a grown man. I own it. The real cost lands on everyone who does not know why, feels the spoiler, and blames the stream.\nThe sharp edge of sync is that notification, and it is the cheapest thing to fix, because it is addressed to a single device.\nThe encouraging part is that some of the biggest names are already moving. The BBC is closing the iPlayer gap. Sky built Real Time. Amazon says its delivery stays synchronized across your devices. MLB holds its alerts back by thirty seconds. Tennis TV, YouTube TV and ESPN ship real spoiler controls.\nDolby OptiView is focus heavily on stream syncronization.\nI pulled the full rundown together for paying members. Who has shipped what, how far along they are, and which moves you can borrow if you run a platform. Link in the comments.\nIf you run a sports platform, give every fan a delayed-alert option today, then go and sync the stream. Whoever lands the notification on the goal instead of ahead of it wins a second every fan will feel.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2214, "rx": 24, "cm": 7, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2214, 24, 7, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2214, 24, 7, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 112, "id": 7479849309748981760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7479849309748981760/", "ts": "2026-07-06T10:50:44+00:00", "date": "2026-07-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ ⏱️ 📺 I watched England at 3am last night from Turin, half asleep, fully in. Moments like that are why I do this work, and they are why I l", "snippet": "⚽ ⏱️ 📺 I watched England at 3am last night from Turin, half asleep, fully in. Moments like that are why I do this work, and they are why I love collaborating wi", "chars": 1336, "text": "⚽ ⏱️ 📺 I watched England at 3am last night from Turin, half asleep, fully in. Moments like that are why I do this work, and they are why I love collaborating with Dolby OptiView.\n\nWhat I value most about this collaboration is the access to go under the hood at product level. I get to sit with the engineering, understand how the video is built, how latency gets managed, how the experience holds together live at the scale of a World Cup. That is the part of the job I have always chased. When I collaborate with a company, I want to be inside the product, close to the people making the decisions, learning how it actually works.\n\nThe bigger pull is feeling part of advancing sport experiences for fans, and that has never felt more alive than right now.\nThis tournament is where the future of watching is being written in real time.\nI put together 5 trends I see emerging from the World Cup, from parallel feeds and interactivity, latency as a feature, smarter ways to show ads, and the battle to own the fan's attention. Every one of them rests on the foundation that makes the magic possible, or breaks it.\n\nRead the piece below, then go watch a match at an unreasonable hour.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/dnTAyeJ3\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 13472, "rx": 12, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 13472, 12, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 13472, 12, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 113, "id": 7479824079542226945, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7479824079542226945/", "ts": "2026-07-06T09:10:28+00:00", "date": "2026-07-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: Sky Buys ITV. The UK Broadcast Map Just Got Redrawn.", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider: Sky Buys ITV. The UK Broadcast Map Just Got Redrawn.", "chars": 2780, "text": "10 Things To Consider: Sky Buys ITV. The UK Broadcast Map Just Got Redrawn.\nHow I read it? There is such a level of agitation in the global media industry that it's either a signal of high energy and opportunity (I doubt it) or a delayed check with reality coming home.\n\n📺 The biggest shake-up in British commercial television in a generation.\nI read it on LinkedIn before it hit the wires, straight from Sky CEO Dana Strong's post. Fitting, given where we all live now.\nHere is the shape of it. Comcast bought a streaming platform, an advertising business and the reach to run both. ITV Studios walks away as a standalone company, which sets up the next deal before this one closes. Regulators hold the timeline, and news plurality is the pressure point.\n\n10 Things To Consider 👇\n1️⃣ 💷 The money. £1.2bn cash at completion, up to £200m more in 2028 if ITV advertising tops £1.7bn in FY2027. The top line rides on next year's ad market.\n2️⃣ 🎬 ITV Studios walks free. Standalone, cash-generative, and a takeover target the day this closes. Watch this one.\n3️⃣ 📡 Comcast bought the platform. ITVX, the ad inventory and the data, wired into the Sky and Peacock stack.\n4️⃣ 📊 Advertising is the whole game. Analysts put a combined Sky and ITV above 70% of UK TV advertising. The companies point to 10% of digital ad spend. The regulator has to decide which market it is measuring.\n5️⃣ 📰 ITN is the hardest knot. Sky ends up an indirect 20% shareholder in the newsroom that feeds ITV, Channel 4 and Channel 5.\n6️⃣ ⚖️ The regulators have real work to do. CMA, Ofcom, and a possible public interest review on plurality. Longstop date 6 July 2028.\n7️⃣ 🔒 The content lock-in. A £2.1bn supply deal to 2032 keeps Coronation Street, Emmerdale, Love Island and I'm a Celebrity free-to-air.\n8️⃣ 🌍 One move in a bigger wave. Paramount and Warner Bros. Discovery, Banijay and All3Media. Legacy media scaling up against Netflix and YouTube.\n9️⃣ 🏢 The NBCUniversal endgame. Sky and ITV M&E fold into NBCUniversal once Comcast completes its planned separation.\n🔟 👥 The people questions land first. Who runs content, whether two newsrooms survive, who moves to Osterley. The share price barely moved, up about 1% to 83p, because the market priced this in months ago.\n\n📩 There is a lot more under the hood. Paid members on my Substack get the full breakdown:\n📄 The annotated deal, every number from the official filing in plain English\n🗓️ A regulatory tracker from the signed agreement to the 2028 longstop\n🧩 A vendor briefing on where the openings sit for the next 18 months\n🗺️ A map of the whole consolidation wave\n\n#SportsMedia #Streaming #MediaAndEntertainment #ITV #Sky\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView, enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2174, "rx": 13, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Sky", "company"], ["ITV", "company"], ["Comcast", "company"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "streaming", "mediaandentertainment", "itv", "sky"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2174, 13, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2174, 13, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 114, "id": 7479437873893572608, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7479437873893572608/", "ts": "2026-07-05T07:35:50+00:00", "date": "2026-07-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "snippet": "FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "chars": 905, "text": "FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\nWhat about confederations, how are they doing?\nWe  have now just 2 R16 played - 4 confeds are still in, 2 have gone home.\n⚽ UEFA — much the strongest survivor: 7 of 16 still alive; its exits are mostly at R32 (6) plus 3 from the group stage. UEFA teams may play in all 4 QFs.\n⚽ CONMEBOL — evenly spread: 3 still in, and one each out at R16, R32 and group. May play in 2 QF.\n⚽ CONCACAF — 2 still in, 1 out at R16, and half the confederation (3) gone in the groups. May play in 2 QF.\n⚽ CAF — 2 still in, but a brutal R32 (7 knocked out there). May play in 2 QF.\n⚽ AFC — none left; 7 fell in the groups, 2 in R32.\n⚽ OFC — its single entrant (New Zealand) went out in the group stage.\n\nAdded a new feature to the interactive Road to the Final - select a confederations (top right)\nhttps://lnkd.in/dufp4u9R\n\nRealtime Fan Engagement A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 402, "rx": 5, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["Realtime Fan Engagement", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 402, 5, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 402, 5, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 115, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-07-05", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Cleeng", "with": null, "title": "🧠 Cleeng Pro began as a simple idea, but our team has transformed it into a powerful reality for subscription brands and content creators lo", "snippet": "🧠 Cleeng Pro began as a simple idea, but our team has transformed it into a powerful reality for subscription brands and content creators looking to launch and ", "chars": 437, "text": "🧠 Cleeng Pro began as a simple idea, but our team has transformed it into a powerful reality for subscription brands and content creators looking to launch and scale their vision without roadblocks.\n\nWatch Anna Sobiak, our Product Designer, explain how her team created an intuitive 6-step checklist to help you set up Cleeng Pro in less than 60 minutes!\n\nExperience it firsthand! Create your free account today: https://lnkd.in/e_KT6zc6", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 67, "cm": 1, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Anna Sobiak", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 67, 1, 5], ["2026-10-17", null, 67, 1, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 116, "id": 7479066238170718208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7479066238170718208/", "ts": "2026-07-04T06:59:05+00:00", "date": "2026-07-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "16 Teams Left and What a Night: I Built an Interactive Experience", "snippet": "⚽ 16 Teams Left and What a Night: I Built An Interactive Experience", "chars": 1913, "text": "⚽ 16 Teams Left and What a Night: I Built An Interactive Experience\n The last 16 are set, and the next round starts deciding who reaches the quarter finals.\n\n⚙️ I built a World Cup 2026 bracket that will make me no money and disappear in fourteen days. I would do it again tomorrow.\n📱 Last night I watched Argentina beat Cape Verde 3-2 on three devices, while working on the same interactive Road to the Final I was watching the game through. Argentina scored, a story card drifted in with the goal and the minute, Messi climbed to seven on the top-scorer card. I sat there grinning like I had just shipped something for millions of people.\n\nA few things building it reminded me:\n🌊 The flow is real. Add a feature, try an idea, refine the UX, check the data, add another. Hours vanish. Anyone who has shipped a live product for a big event knows the feeling.\n👥 Fewer people see what I make now. Years ago a full product team and I built these for millions of fans. This one I made alone, and a few hundred might tap around it. I still love making things for sports fans.\n⚡ Speed is a trap. The new tools let me build almost any idea I have, fast. Being able to make something is a weak reason to make it. The real question is why, and whether it generates value for anyone.\n🎯 I am still bad at that. I have built a few things lately and pulled little real value out of them. This bracket sits on that list. I keep building because to understand a shift in technology you have to get inside it and stay a while. The weekly parade of shiny releases, tried once and forgotten, teaches me nothing.\n🤨 Final provocation: why aren't official tournament websites this cool?\n\n🔗 Live while the football lasts: https://lnkd.in/dufp4u9R\n🎨 Built on the original bracket design by Emilio Sansolini.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1221, "rx": 19, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1221, 19, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1221, 19, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 117, "id": 7478747827368632321, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7478747827368632321/", "ts": "2026-07-03T09:53:50+00:00", "date": "2026-07-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "\"Probably because Ghana never colonised Europe\": a history lesson", "snippet": "\"Probably because Ghana never colonised Europe\"", "chars": 1206, "text": "\"Probably because Ghana never colonised Europe\"\nA history lesson hiding inside the World Cup team sheets.\n\nI keep hearing the same thing at this World Cup, in comment threads and on live broadcasts. A squad comes on screen and someone starts counting faces, deciding out loud who looks like they belong in the shirt. The tone runs from lazy to openly racist.\n\nThe sharpest reply I have seen to all of it came from one exchange online.\n\n❌ Tweet: Not a single white player in the Ghana team.\n✅ Reply: Probably because Ghana never colonised Europe.\n\nI work with data, so I wanted to check it properly. I took all 48 nations at the FIFA World Cup 2026 and marked which had been a colony or a protectorate, who ran them, and the year they reached independence or sovereignty.\n\nThe full table, all 48 nations with their rulers and independence dates, plus a note on why turning this much history into a grid felt uncomfortable, is in the piece.\n\nTo be clear, I have no opinion on where players should choose to play, which passport they carry, or how a nation grows its football (asking for a friend). I did not check anyone's origin. This maps countries and their history, and individual players stay out of it.", "qtext": "", "imp": 3734, "rx": 17, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3734, 17, 3, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3734, 17, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 118, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-07-03", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Dolby OptiView", "with": null, "title": "⚽️🏟️ The World Cup isn't just the biggest football tournament ever - it's a glimpse into the future.", "snippet": "⚽️🏟️ The World Cup isn't just the biggest football tournament ever - it's a glimpse into the future.", "chars": 591, "text": "⚽️🏟️ The World Cup isn't just the biggest football tournament ever - it's a glimpse into the future.\n\nLive sports streaming is shifting from a single broadcast to interactive, multi-feed, low-latency experiences. The difference is the streaming foundation behind it, built to deliver video, interactivity, and monetization together at global scale.\n\nFrom interactive viewing experiences to the infrastructure powering billions of fans simultaneously, here are the top 5️⃣ trends that show where sports streaming is headed.\n\nDive in: https://lnkd.in/gCHNPC85\n\n#livesports #streaming #worldcup", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 28, "cm": 0, "rp": 6, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": ["livesports", "streaming", "worldcup"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 28, 0, 6], ["2026-10-16", null, 28, 0, 6], ["2026-10-17", null, 28, 0, 6], ["2026-10-17", null, 28, 0, 6]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 119, "id": 7478449607887351808, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7478449607887351808/", "ts": "2026-07-02T14:08:49+00:00", "date": "2026-07-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Another simple consideration about VAR at FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States - and no, I am not obsessed about VAR ", "snippet": "Another simple consideration about VAR at FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States - and no, I am not obsessed about VAR if you ask.", "chars": 935, "text": "Another simple consideration about VAR at FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States - and no, I am not obsessed about VAR if you ask.\nAs I launched my Road to the Final visualization tool recently, yesterday I added a lot more features and also integrated all VAR incidents, but the data I can get are minimal.\nI can extract the minute at which it happened and the type of VAR decision, then I can in some cases extrapolate the player involved.\n\nSo I thought, and that is the core of my post today:\n\"Is someone, somewhere, somehow recording and transcribing all referees verbal statements at the stadium and compiling a list?\"\n\nI do not have the time or tools to do that for all matches, but i thought that a broadcaster or streamer or media org of some form could and should do it.\nLet me know, there are still 17 days of games.\n\nRoad to the final: https://lnkd.in/dufp4u9R\n\nThanks,\nCarlo\n\n#football #var #sportstech", "qtext": "", "imp": 594, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "var", "sportstech"], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 594, 6, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 594, 6, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 120, "id": 7478131340627021824, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7478131340627021824/", "ts": "2026-07-01T17:04:08+00:00", "date": "2026-07-01", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Bending Spoons’ Luca Ferrari, in his own words: the AOL bet, the playbook, and the line between PE and product", "snippet": "I posted about Bending Spoons in November of 2025, i think t's ok to repost today.", "chars": 101, "text": "I posted about Bending Spoons in November of 2025, i think t's ok to repost today.\n#investing #nasdaq", "qtext": "🤖 I get asked about Bending Spoons a lot—maybe because I’m Italian, maybe because their deals are loud. I don’t have inside info; just a long view of this industry and what’s public.\nThis time, though, the CEO Luca Ferrari finally spelled out a strategy that feels coherent and different: operator-first, product-heavy, buy-to-hold. That fits their roots as top-tier app builders. If they really put hands on product, modernize distribution, and squeeze more life (and cash flow) out of assets that were under-loved, the thesis makes sense.\nThe question is durability: can they keep doing this at sca", "imp": 743, "rx": 0, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Bending Spoons", "company"]], "tags": ["investing", "nasdaq"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 743, 0, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 743, 0, 2, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 121, "id": 7478069103438532609, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7478069103438532609/", "ts": "2026-07-01T12:56:50+00:00", "date": "2026-07-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Road to the Final — or how a bracket refused to leave me alone", "snippet": "🏆 Road to the Final: I turned a circular World Cup bracket into something you can click.", "chars": 1701, "text": "🏆 Road to the Final: I turned a circular World Cup bracket into something you can click.\nA living web page, built from a design that stopped my scroll.\nIt started with Emilio Sansolini. He posted a World Cup knockout bracket shaped as a circle: crests and flags on the outside, the trophy dead centre, coloured threads pulling each nation inward. Poster-grade work. Go follow him. 👏\n\nThen came the itch. I've spent an embarrassing share of my life at the crossroads of sport, media and technology, and to me a beautiful static image is a dare. So a bit of Claude Coding and some UX discipline, and I built a version you can play with.\n\nWhat it does:\n🔵 The full circle, same soul as Emilio's, flags first and lighter\n👆 Tap any flag for the group table, every match, and highlight links where FIFA has posted them\n⚽ Tap any match for scorers, minutes, venue, and how it was settled (right down to penalties)\n🟢🔴⚪ Every crest wears its status in its border: still in, out, or yet to play\n🔮 Upcoming rounds show the possible teams (\"one of BRA / NOR\") narrowing as results land\n📱 Dark mode, style options, and it scales down to a phone\nIt's data-driven and hosted free on GitHub Pages, so I can nurse it through the whole tournament in five-minute bursts.\n👉 https://lnkd.in/dSxJ5awn\n\nNow the ask.\n🗣️ What reads instantly, and what makes you squint? What would you click? What did I overthink, and what did I miss entirely? Your eyes are the best fuel this kind of project ever gets.\nGrazie, and enjoy the football. ⚽\nFIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2222, "rx": 16, "cm": 13, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Emilio Sansolini", "person"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Dataviz"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2222, 16, 13, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2222, 16, 13, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 122, "id": 7477699103200559104, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7477699103200559104/", "ts": "2026-06-30T12:26:35+00:00", "date": "2026-06-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "That Ghost Goal Took Me to the BBC Radio", "snippet": "Last week I was on the BBC Radio for the first time.", "chars": 1800, "text": "Last week I was on the BBC Radio for the first time.\nThe programme was The Inquiry, presented by Tanya Beckett, and the question was a big one. Is technology ruining sport?\nMy answer is no. Technology is rewriting the contract. It makes the calls more accurate and it takes some of the argument out of the game, and both of those are true at the same time.\nThey opened the episode with me on the Lampard ghost goal. Bloemfontein, 2010, England against Germany, a ball a clear foot over the line that nobody gave. That miss is the reason goal line technology and VAR exist today. I walked through how VAR actually works, because plenty of fans still think the machine overrules the referee. It does not. The room advises. The referee decides.\nThen three voices I have been thinking about since. Otto Koblinger on why Hawk-Eye is never perfect. Odilon Roble on sport as one of the last safe places we are allowed to disagree. Matt Moore on the pressure of measuring a player on every single serve.\nMy father shot football for La Stampa for thirty years, back when one photograph was sometimes the only proof a ball had crossed the line. I grew up around those negatives. So I do not treat this technology as something that fell from the sky. The game ran on the eye and the argument for a century before any of it arrived.\nHere is the position I left them with. Get the call right and keep the game worth watching. That is the whole brief. The sports that hold both halves will be fine. The ones that win every review and lose the room will not.\nEvie Yabsley Amelia Cox\n\nFull episode here: https://lnkd.in/dyhPq97c\n\n#SportsTech #VAR #SportsMedia #Broadcasting #FIFAWorldCup\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2406, "rx": 15, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["BBC", "company"], ["Tanya Beckett", "person"], ["Evie Yabsley", "person"], ["Amelia Cox", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "var", "sportsmedia", "broadcasting", "fifaworldcup"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2406, 15, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2406, 15, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 123, "id": 7477402148566269952, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7477402148566269952/", "ts": "2026-06-29T16:46:35+00:00", "date": "2026-06-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I'm a judge for the SportsPro Media Awards 2026!", "snippet": "I'm a judge for the SportsPro Media Awards 2026!", "chars": 296, "text": "I'm a judge for the SportsPro Media Awards 2026!\nAn interesting view inside innovation into out industry with some entries from past years really being great learnings.\nAn intense but cool process. See you in Madrid then!\n\nEntries close Tuesday, 30th June.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dN74QY-W\n#SPMEDIAAWARDS", "qtext": "", "imp": 679, "rx": 24, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"]], "tags": ["spmediaawards"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 679, 24, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 679, 24, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 124, "id": 7475856585106296832, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7475856585106296832/", "ts": "2026-06-25T10:25:04+00:00", "date": "2026-06-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Proposal: A VAR decisions section on every fifa.com match page", "snippet": "⚽ ❌ ✅ 👀 Proposal: A VAR decisions section on every fifa.com match page", "chars": 2225, "text": "⚽ ❌ ✅ 👀 Proposal: A VAR decisions section on every fifa.com match page\nMy proposal is simple.\nFIFA should add a dedicated VAR decisions section to every match page on fifa.com. One section per fixture, published after the final whistle, every critical call explained in plain language, including the incidents the video team checked and left alone. 📄\nEngland 0-0 Ghana gave us the case study. Ezri Konsa went through Prince Kwabena Adu in the box. No penalty, no on-field review, no explanation. Carlos Queiroz said VAR had gone for a coffee. ☕\nHere is the catch. FIFA's visible transparency mostly speaks when the process turns formal. The most argued-about moment of the match was the one it skips: the silent check. 🔇\n\n😎 Full disclosure. I am not a referee, and I have no decisive opinion on the Konsa call, because I do not know every rule in play. That is why I want this. I want to see the reasoning. I have spent close to forty years in broadcast, data and streaming, so it comes from somewhere even if I turn out to be wrong.\n\nHere is the case:\n1️⃣ The non-call is the gap. The silent check produces no announcement and no explanation. A match page closes exactly that hole.\n2️⃣ One official home. Under the fixture on fifa.com, signed by the refereeing department. No leaks, no selective briefings, no guesswork.\n3️⃣ Plain language. Incident, minute, law, the on-field call, whether VAR checked it and why. Thirty seconds to read.\n4️⃣ It protects officials. A reasoned public record shows the work. The vacuum is what feeds the pile-on.\n5️⃣ The pieces already exist. The Premier League runs Match Officials: Mic'd Up and clarified more than 325 incidents through its Match Centre account. IFAB already lets competitions have the referee explain decisions publicly.\n6️⃣ Boring, fast, mobile, multilingual. Posted the morning after, the same template every match.\nThe argument about referees is permanent. Feed it accurate material and it gets better, and shorter. 🔁\n\nFIFA has opened the process up this World Cup, with mics on referees and decisions on the big screens. The last step is one page.\nBuild it. 🏗️\n\nWould you read it after every match? 👇\n\nFIFA - FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "qtext": "", "imp": 7533, "rx": 37, "cm": 9, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["FIFA", "company"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 7529, 37, 9, 5], ["2026-10-17", 7533, 37, 9, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 125, "id": 7475493764275277824, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7475493764275277824/", "ts": "2026-06-24T10:23:21+00:00", "date": "2026-06-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "When nobody invites you to Cannes Lions and you spend the day staring into the abyss.", "snippet": "When nobody invites you to Cannes Lions and you spend the day staring into the abyss.", "chars": 135, "text": "When nobody invites you to Cannes Lions and you spend the day staring into the abyss.\nCannes Lions International Festival of Creativity", "qtext": "", "imp": 735, "rx": 9, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Cannes Lions International Festival of Creativity", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 735, 9, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 735, 9, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 126, "id": 7475063473157128192, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7475063473157128192/", "ts": "2026-06-23T05:53:31+00:00", "date": "2026-06-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 82: Yannick Ramcke - OneFootball, Churn and the Holy Grail", "snippet": "Retention Zone Ep. 82 — Yannick Ramcke (GM OTT, OneFootball)", "chars": 1745, "text": "Retention Zone Ep. 82 — Yannick Ramcke (GM OTT, OneFootball)\n\n⚽ OneFootball and the Closing Monetisation Gap\nTwo years after his first Retention Zone visit, Yannick Manuel Ramcke is back. We checked what held up and what shifted in the subscription business, and the answers got sharp.\n\nHere is what stuck with me 👇\n🎯 Churn is still the holy grail. The mood moved from acquisition at all costs to retention and bottom line, because OTT adoption is saturated and growth has plateaued.\n💸 Two levers, two logics. Acquisition is a matter of spending money. Retention is a matter of pricing, because a subscription is a value exchange the fan reassesses every month. \"You need to prove yourself every single month.\"\n🤖 AI sits across the whole stack. Data was never the bottleneck. What changed is everything downstream: shorter feedback loops, faster iteration, personalisation that scales down to cohorts and individuals.\n🏗️ One platform, three models. The white label build lets clubs and rights owners run D2C on OneFootball, pulling the platform across AVOD, TVOD and SVOD at once.\n📺 The freshest insight: club TV channels at €1.99 to €2.99 show surprising retention. Price it low enough and cancelling costs more effort than staying. \"The shown consideration is not even a consideration anymore.\"\n💡 His bet for the industry: \"Where there is engagement, there's willingness to pay.\" For some segments, short form itself could become a paid product, as long as it stays live.\n\n🎧 Full article and episode below.\n\nGilles Domartini Alexis Gaï Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Adrianna Panek\n\n-----\n🎧 Retention Zone – Episode 82\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1172, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["OneFootball", "company"], ["Yannick Manuel Ramcke", "person"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Adrianna Panek", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1172, 16, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1172, 16, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 127, "id": 7472924321427009537, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7472924321427009537/", "ts": "2026-06-17T08:13:18+00:00", "date": "2026-06-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Can You Imagine New York Being Cheerful? A personal take and some memories", "snippet": "🗽🏀 ⚽ Can you imagine New York being cheerful?", "chars": 2118, "text": "🗽🏀 ⚽ Can you imagine New York being cheerful?\n\nALERT: Personal and a bit humourous, emotional, piece.\n\nI was half-listening to Seth Meyers the other night when he said New York is in a party mood right now.\nMy first thought: wait. New York City?\nYou can say many true things about that city. Cheerful has never been one of them. It is spiky. It is fast. It is a little annoyed with you for walking too slow.\nThis week it went and got happy anyway.\nThe Knicks won the title in five, their first since 1973. Fifty-three years of waiting, paid back in a single night. The World Cup is in town. ⚽\n\nNobody waited longer than Spike Lee, courtside through every miserable spring.\nYears ago, before you could order anything online, I used to buy his screenplays at a Barnes & Noble in Manhattan. Last November he came to Turin, my city, to collect a lifetime award at the Torino Film Festival, and I got to put a question to him at the press conference. The New York man in my town, after all the years I spent buying his New York stories.\nI support Torino. Granata. Our last Scudetto was 1976. I know what a long wait feels like, so I am the last person who will tell a happy crowd to calm down.\nHere is the thought that snuck up on me.\n\nI work in sports media and tech. On a normal Tuesday it can feel like a silly way to spend a life. Doctors keep people alive. Scientists move us forward. I help keep a stream steady when a shot ends a 53-year drought.\nThen a night like this lands and the whole equation flips. What we build lets millions of people sit inside the same feeling at the same second.\n\nIn Atlanta, a 40-year-old goalkeeper from Cape Verde named Vozinha held Spain to 0-0 in his country's first ever World Cup. Seven saves. He wept at the final whistle. His mother could not get a US visa in time to be there. Joy and a closed door, in the same shot.\nBack in 2008, working the Olympics, the people at NBC told me a thing I never managed to put down.\n\nSport is all about a flag, a hero, and a story.\n\nStill true. This week had all three.\nI did not have any of it on my list for 2026. I am very glad to be wrong.", "qtext": "", "imp": 729, "rx": 8, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 729, 8, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 729, 8, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 128, "id": 7472578116645781504, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7472578116645781504/", "ts": "2026-06-16T09:17:36+00:00", "date": "2026-06-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A live interview with Paola Marinoneand Philipp Männer FIA World Rally Championship (WRC) today at 4pm.", "snippet": "A live interview with Paola Marinoneand Philipp Männer FIA World Rally Championship (WRC) today at 4pm.", "chars": 103, "text": "A live interview with Paola Marinoneand Philipp Männer FIA World Rally Championship (WRC) today at 4pm.", "qtext": "", "imp": 715, "rx": 5, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["Philipp Männer", "person"], ["FIA World Rally Championship (WRC)", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 715, 5, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 715, 5, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 129, "id": 7472552810295984128, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7472552810295984128/", "ts": "2026-06-16T07:37:03+00:00", "date": "2026-06-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About Fox and Roku", "snippet": "🏈 📺 10 Things To Consider About Fox and Roku", "chars": 1856, "text": "🏈 📺 10 Things To Consider About Fox and Roku\nFox just paid $22 billion for the home screen.\n\nThe Fox Corporation and Roku deal is a move to own the front door to 100 million living rooms. Roku in 2026 is a connected-TV advertising business sitting on an operating system. The hardware is the smallest part of the story. Fox bought the layer that decides what a household sees first when the TV wakes up.\n\nHere are 10 Things To Consider 👇\n\n1️⃣ The home screen is the real asset. Device sales are under 10% of Roku revenue.\n2️⃣ The engine is CTV advertising. Roku ad revenue hit $613M last quarter, up 27%.\n3️⃣ Free ad-supported TV is now Fox's main bet. Tubi and The Roku Channel under one roof.\n4️⃣ Subscription stays in play. Fox now owns the in-app purchase rails on Roku devices.\n5️⃣ Live sport is the anchor. NFL, MLB, the World Cup and Fox News pull the whole household in.\n6️⃣ Neutrality is the big question. A content owner now runs an open platform.\n7️⃣ The hardware is a drag. Low-margin manufacturing inside a content and advertising business.\n8️⃣ Aggregation is the thesis. Whoever owns the front door owns discovery.\n9️⃣ The market is unconvinced. Fox stock fell as much as 18% on the news.\n🔟 The signal travels. Every broadcaster now has to ask who owns their front door.\n\n📌 The full piece goes deeper: the deal in detail, why the market flinched, and the European read, where Roku is far smaller and the home screen belongs to Samsung, Amazon and LG.\n\n🔒 For paid subscribers: five questions worth asking, and what the deal means for every vendor in our industry, from CTV ad-tech and measurement to FAST infrastructure, billing and sports-rights holders.\n\n#SportsMedia #Streaming #CTV #Roku #FoxRoku #Advertising\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1438, "rx": 14, "cm": 4, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Roku", "company"], ["Fox Corporation", "company"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "streaming", "ctv", "roku", "foxroku", "advertising"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1438, 14, 4, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1438, 14, 4, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 130, "id": 7472185128702824448, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7472185128702824448/", "ts": "2026-06-15T07:16:01+00:00", "date": "2026-06-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I am monitoring YouTube Channels for all 48 FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States teams + 6 confederations + 1 (FIFA).", "snippet": "I am monitoring YouTube Channels for all 48 FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States teams + 6 confederations + 1 (FIFA).", "chars": 251, "text": "I am monitoring YouTube Channels for all 48 FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States teams + 6 confederations + 1 (FIFA).\nYouTube Football Tracker\nytft.aguywithascarf.com\n\nBuilt with passion improved constantly by A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 290, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 290, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 290, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 131, "id": 7471838548858392576, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471838548858392576/", "ts": "2026-06-14T08:18:50+00:00", "date": "2026-06-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Last night I wanted to research the DAZN interactive element while watching FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States matc", "snippet": "Last night I wanted to research the DAZN interactive element while watching FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States match Brasil - Morocco, ", "chars": 2319, "text": "Last night I wanted to research the DAZN interactive element while watching FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States match Brasil - Morocco, on my laptop, but had to stop after few minutes for the incredibly disturbing racist and fascist messages that the chat contained - especially against Morocco.\nAlso, I was watching with my son Miles 11yrs old and others.\n\nThis is not just yet another case of \"we should not have fans chat\", I have been involved for too long to simplify my solution to that.\n Two aspects.\n1. The sadly obvious acknowledgement of how these occasion highlights the worst parts of the worst people (especially if the can hide their identity, but not exclusively). Also considering there maybe legal consequences.\n2. The fact that even in the age of AI these things are still happening, makes me doubt we really need fans chat - even if for years I hoped they could add value. We are back in 1999…\n\nI am curious to see if DAZN makes some changes going forward.\nMy suggestion apart from removing the chat all together.\n1. Are we sure DAZN is using the most advanced tool to clean the chat in real time? (no idea)\n2. Can DAZN add a selection UI to just get interactive elements and other controlled experiences (maybe it was there, did not see it, I will check again tonight). We would have loved to engage with those the whole game...\n3. I don't want to only talk about the platform here... What is with these people! Personal responsibility should not be diluted by troubled digital interface\n4. I wanted to see if I could report users but the speed of the flow was so high that it was impossible to do that\n5. DAZN has a page dedicated to this - link in the comments - with an explanation of their methodology this statement: \"Our commitment\nAt DAZN, we are dedicated to maintaining a vibrant, safe, and inclusive environment for all sports fans. Your support and collaboration are key to making it happen.\"\n\nSo no analysis from me today on the FIFA World Cup fan digital engagement.\n\nImages below:\n1. How we watched on the kitchen table of my country home\n2. Composition (no AI my manual terrible image stitching) of what we did not want to watch\nI have some images with the hateful comments which I am not sharing to avoid promoting their intent.\n\nand by the way - great match!", "qtext": "", "imp": 3501, "rx": 22, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["DAZN", "company"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3501, 22, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3501, 22, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 132, "id": 7471499432723660800, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471499432723660800/", "ts": "2026-06-13T09:51:18+00:00", "date": "2026-06-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "😎 Let me try again!", "snippet": "😎 Let me try again!", "chars": 753, "text": "😎 Let me try again!\n😎 I have asked for this the LinkedIn product team already in the past.\n\n🎙️ Hear me out!\n\n👩‍🎨 Evolving algorithm, new UI elements (subtle but present), new mobile app behaviours.\nOut of the many things it is often said that one of the KPI for content to be surfaced is how many time people are saving it.\nI understand space on screens is limited but with the new layout on web I would really love to have a save button alongside like, comment, repost, send.\nMaybe it goes before send as it can have a number attached.\nI appreciate there maybe good reasons not to implement that.\n\n💁‍♂️ That's it. I predict it will increase the habit of saving for later consumption. I use it a lot myself. LinkedIn is my no.1 source for industry news.", "qtext": "", "imp": 261, "rx": 2, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 261, 2, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 261, 2, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 133, "id": 7471468271066771457, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471468271066771457/", "ts": "2026-06-13T07:47:28+00:00", "date": "2026-06-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ SPOBIS asked me to write for their FIFA World Cup Special newsletter.", "snippet": "⚽ SPOBIS asked me to write for their FIFA World Cup Special newsletter.", "chars": 2022, "text": "⚽ SPOBIS asked me to write for their FIFA World Cup Special newsletter.\nhttps://spobis.com/\n\nThe piece looks at the sports tech and media stories that will shape the FIFA World Cup 2026. Three host countries. 48 teams. 104 matches. That scale rewrites how the tournament gets officiated, produced, and pushed out to fans around the world.\n\nI went into the areas I track every week. Officiating technology and the trust questions that come with it. The broadcast rights map across the US and the rest of the world. FIFA+ and DAZN building direct lines to the audience. The Preferred Platform deals putting official World Cup content on YouTube and TikTok.\n\nItaly will watch this one from the sofa. Third tournament in a row. So I cover the 2026 World Cup the way I follow most football now, reading where the money, the screens and the attention move next.\n\nThank you to the SPOBIS team for the invitation and the trust.\n\n🇩🇪 Auf Deutsch\nSPOBIS hat mich gebeten, für die World-Cup-Special-Ausgabe des Newsletters zu schreiben.\n\nDer Beitrag behandelt die Sporttech- und Medienthemen, die die FIFA Weltmeisterschaft 2026 prägen werden. Drei Gastgeberländer. 48 Mannschaften. 104 Spiele. Diese Dimension verändert, wie das Turnier geleitet, produziert und an Fans auf der ganzen Welt ausgespielt wird.\nIch gehe auf die Bereiche ein, die ich jede Woche verfolge. Schiedsrichtertechnologie und die Vertrauensfrage, die damit einhergeht. Die Landkarte der Übertragungsrechte in den USA und im Rest der Welt. FIFA+ und DAZN, die direkte Verbindungen zum Publikum aufbauen. Die Preferred-Platform-Deals, die offizielle WM-Inhalte auf YouTube und TikTok bringen.\nItalien schaut diesmal vom Sofa zu. Das dritte Turnier in Folge. Also begleite ich die WM 2026 so, wie ich heute den meisten Fußball verfolge, mit Blick darauf, wohin sich Geld, Bildschirme und Aufmerksamkeit als Nächstes bewegen.\nDanke an das SPOBIS-Team für die Einladung und das Vertrauen. Link zum Newsletter in den Kommentaren.\n\nA guy with a scarf with Henri Müller", "qtext": "", "imp": 430, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SPOBIS", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Henri Müller", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 430, 9, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 430, 9, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 134, "id": 7471101102718078977, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471101102718078977/", "ts": "2026-06-12T07:28:29+00:00", "date": "2026-06-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the Drake Star Sports Tech Q1 2026 Report", "snippet": "📊 10 Things To Consider About the Drake Star Sports Tech Q1 2026 Report", "chars": 1707, "text": "📊 10 Things To Consider About the Drake Star Sports Tech Q1 2026 Report\n\nDeep diving the Drake Star quarterly report has become quite a habit for me. This quarter the headline says $130.6 billion across 239 deals. One transaction, Paramount Skydance buying Warner Bros. Discovery, accounts for $110 billion of it. The real story starts after you set it aside, and the report itself half admits it, footnoting WBD out of its own charts.\n\nThe 10 things:\n1️⃣ Read the headline number twice: 107 deals describe the market, the $130B does the marketing\n2️⃣ Sports data companies want to be media companies (Genius Sports + Legend, $1.2B)\n3️⃣ Youth sports is now a private equity platform thesis (LiveBarn, $400M)\n4️⃣ The B2B team operations layer is consolidating fast (Teamworks + PFF + Sportlogiq)\n5️⃣ Prediction markets crossed the institutional line (Kalshi at $22B, ICE into Polymarket, and days ago Sportradar + Kalshi)\n6️⃣ Wearables outgrew the locker room, and the cycle is back in its up phase (WHOOP at $10.1B)\n7️⃣ Creator-led formats are capitalized competitors (Kings League, $63M)\n8️⃣ 80% of financings were early-stage: the 2028-2030 M&A inventory is being seeded right now\n9️⃣ Over $4B in new funds is aimed at the vendor layer (Otro, Bruin, Athvance)\n🔟 Public markets reward scale: decide if you are the platform or the add-on\n\n💎 Paid subscribers get an extra this week: the Vendor Implications Memo, every item translated into one decision for B2B vendors, in the statement / In practice / When it works format from my book.\n\nMohit Pareek, CFA #sportstech #investing\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 638, "rx": 10, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Drake Star", "company"], ["Drake Star", "company"], ["Mohit Pareek, CFA", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "investing"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 633, 10, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 638, 10, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 135, "id": 7470836270399680512, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470836270399680512/", "ts": "2026-06-11T13:56:08+00:00", "date": "2026-06-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ Where To Watch the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States World Cup - In All Countries. All Broadcasters", "snippet": "⚽ Where To Watch the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States World Cup - In All Countries. All Broadcasters", "chars": 1644, "text": "⚽ Where To Watch the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States World Cup - In All Countries. All Broadcasters\nA simple tool to explore who has which match where.\n\n📺 Ever wondered where a World Cup match is being shown outside your own country? I built a tool for exactly that kind of curiosity.\n\n🤖 It is online and live now: every 2026 World Cup match, every broadcaster, every link, in one page you can explore.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dnmuFpa3\n\n👀 It is made for poking around. Pick any country and see where each match airs there. Pick a broadcaster and see every game it carries and how many countries it reaches. Filter by team to follow one side across the tournament. Set your own country and the whole thing narrows to your local options.\n\nℹ️ A few things worth knowing:\n→ Not every broadcaster holds rights to every match. Coverage is sold game by game, so the list of broadcasters changes from one match to the next. FOX shows 70 of the US games, FS1 the other 34. BBC and ITV split the UK between them.\n→ Some broadcasters link you straight to the exact match page. FOX One in the US has a direct link for all 104 games. Others only send you to a generic homepage, so both kinds are marked clearly.\n→ Reach varies a lot. The widest single broadcaster covers 22 countries. Plenty of others are one country only.\n\n✅ The data comes straight from FIFA.com. It runs as one static page that costs\nnothing to host.\nTake a look and explore. Happy to share how it came together.\nSomething my friends Ciaran Quinn and Christiano Coraglia have always be passionated about.\n\n❌ Let me know if you see any major issue.\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 593, "rx": 9, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["Ciaran Quinn", "person"], ["Christiano Coraglia", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 591, 9, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 593, 9, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 136, "id": 7470357287929348097, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470357287929348097/", "ts": "2026-06-10T06:12:49+00:00", "date": "2026-06-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "YTFT Now Tracks All 48 World Cup Teams On YouTube, Who Will Win The Content Game?", "snippet": "🚀 New on the YouTube Football Tracker: FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "chars": 1229, "text": "🚀 New on the YouTube Football Tracker: FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\n\nThe World Cup is already being played on YouTube.\n\nFrom today YTFT tracks the official channels of all 48 qualified teams, FIFA and the six confederations. 62 channels. 22.3 billion lifetime views. Snapshotted daily through the tournament.\n\nThree weeks of pre-tournament data, and a few things stand out:\n\n📈 811.9M views gained in 27 days, before a single competitive ball has been kicked\n🏆 FIFA alone added 429.8M, built largely on archive Shorts: Buffon's save on Zidane, Beckenbauer lifting the trophy at home, a Coutinho curler\n🎵 The all-time number one is still Jung Kook's Dreamers from Qatar 2022, at 452.5M views\n🇯🇵 Japan runs the most efficient channel of all 48 teams: 75.5K views per video\n🌍 CAF went heavy on Shorts: 207 of its 278 new videos\n🇺🇾 Uruguay out-published everyone with 415 videos in three weeks\n🇺🇸 The United States is touring its host cities in long-form, in English and Spanish\n\nFive pages to explore: Latest, Trends, Viral Videos, All-time and All-Time Top.\n\nFree sign-in here 👇\nhttps://lnkd.in/dru_mETE\n\nThe tournament starts tomorrow. The tracker counts every day of it.\n\nBuilt with Claude Code. #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 717, "rx": 12, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 717, 12, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 717, 12, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 137, "id": 7470207092847529984, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470207092847529984/", "ts": "2026-06-09T20:16:00+00:00", "date": "2026-06-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Late night AI post", "snippet": "Late night AI post", "chars": 831, "text": "Late night AI post\nAs I am deep into Claude Code for a much needed refactor for YTFT, as I am expanding the scope and channels, I just saw this...\n\nAnthropic\nBuilt for your most ambitious work\nClaude Fable 5, our next generation of intelligence, is available in Claude Code.\nIt works more like a seasoned engineer: designed to investigate your codebase before it acts, use relevant context as it works, and check its work before reporting it’s done. It also excels at long-running, asynchronous work, so you can hand it bigger tasks and let it run.\nThrough June 22, Claude Fable 5 is included in your subscription and draws from your plan’s usage, with no additional costs. After that, you can keep using it with usage credits.\nTry it: Select Claude Fable 5 with /model. Available at $10 input / $50 output per million tokens.\n\n#ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 386, "rx": 0, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Anthropic", "company"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 386, 0, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 386, 0, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 138, "id": 7470032625169125377, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470032625169125377/", "ts": "2026-06-09T08:42:44+00:00", "date": "2026-06-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: Sports Tech and Media at the FIFA World Cup 2026", "snippet": "⚽ 10 Things To Consider: Sports Tech and Media at the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "chars": 1996, "text": "⚽ 10 Things To Consider: Sports Tech and Media at the FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\n🎙️ I recorded an interview with BBC World Service on officiating technology, still to air. Prepping for it pulled me deep into the VAR and offside machinery, so I widened the lens.\nThe 2026 World Cup puts the whole sports media stack on one table. Officiating, rights, streaming, platforms, AI. Most of it has appeared before. This time every piece has been enhanced, and several are new.\n\nHere are the 10 things I am watching as the football starts. 👇\n1️⃣ ⚽ The offside system now alerts the assistant referee directly. Faster calls, fewer long stoppages.\n2️⃣ 🧍 Every one of the 1,248 players is 3D scanned into a digital twin, used for offside decisions and broadcast replays.\n3️⃣ 🔧 Officiating is really four suppliers. Hawk-Eye, Lenovo, Adidas with KINEXON, and Hisense.\n4️⃣ 🎥 Referees wear body cameras, and some markets get audio of their decisions. The biggest trust upgrade in the package.\n5️⃣ 💰 Fox is paying under $500m for US rights valued at $1bn to $1.5bn, on a deal locked in back in 2014.\n6️⃣ 📺 US streaming is fragmented by design. Fox, FS1, Fox One, Tubi, Telemundo, Peacock.\n7️⃣ 📱 YouTube and TikTok are a promotional layer. The first ten minutes and highlights flow through the broadcasters' own channels.\n8️⃣ 🎬 FIFA+ launched exclusively on DAZN on 4 June, five days before kickoff.\n9️⃣ 🤖 Football AI Pro gives all 48 teams the same analytics baseline.\n🔟 🌍 None of this exists below the elite. The gap to grassroots football widens every cycle.\n\n👉 For my Substack Subscribers (paid) I went deeper on the officiating tech on its own. The full breakdown of VAR, semi-automated offside, the connected ball, referee comms and what is new for 2026 is in a separate deep dive.\n\n#FIFAWorldCup2026 #SportsTech #SportsMedia #Broadcasting #VAR\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1473, "rx": 14, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["fifaworldcup2026", "sportstech", "sportsmedia", "broadcasting", "var"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1473, 14, 1, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1473, 14, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 139, "id": 7469654978102456320, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7469654978102456320/", "ts": "2026-06-08T07:42:06+00:00", "date": "2026-06-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🇮🇹 ⚽ An intense afternoon in Parma for Festival della Serie A. 🟦", "snippet": "🇮🇹 ⚽ An intense afternoon in Parma for Festival della Serie A. 🟦", "chars": 2003, "text": "🇮🇹 ⚽ An intense afternoon in Parma for Festival della Serie A. 🟦\nI was invited by Luisella Fusco and Lega Serie A to take part in the Editorial & Social, Media Operations & Broadcast, Digital commission, four and a half hours inside the Benedictine library of San Giovanni Evangelista.\n\nThe full run of sessions:\n📱 Social Media & Content\nSeason recap and AI in content creation with Antonio Caianiello, then the Creator Economy meets Football round table with Mattia Fontana (Juventus), Stefano Perazzo (Inter), Mariano Angelico (Udinese) and creator Agnese Nespoli.\n🎙️ My bit: I asked the clubs where YouTube sits in the platform mix, drawing on the tracking platform I built for the top clubs across Europe's big five leagues. Short version, Inter posts video every single day, Roma runs a different model, and YouTube is now the highest direct-monetisation channel for several of them. YouTube Football Tracker\n\n🛰️ Media Operations & Broadcast\nIntro by Luisella Fusco, production innovation with Andrea Mazzola, the private 5G test at San Siro with Carlo Di Tullio (Milan), and RefCam 2.0 with Carsten Voßkühler (Riedel).\n\n🎙️ My sessions: I moderated the Ecosistema Dati round table. With Edoardo Degli Innocenti (Kama.Sport) and Alice Cirimelli  on owning and certifying Serie A's data, and with Leigh Maxwell (Hawk-Eye Innovations) on advanced semi-automated offside, 3D officiating and the AR feed built for a Gen Z audience.\n\n🖥️ Digital\nSerie A Digital Platform and the Club Programme with Simone Cevasco (Lega Serie A) and Andrea Marini (Deltatre).\n\nOne line to take away: in five years Serie A has built a stack it now owns end to end, from the tagging room at Lissone to a certificate on a single event.\nFull write-up coming on A guy with a scarf.\n\nGrazie Luisella Fusco and the Lega Serie A team for the invitation, and the lovely Cena di Gala.\nMirko Mastropietro\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2097, "rx": 40, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Luisella Fusco", "person"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Antonio Caianiello", "person"], ["Mattia Fontana", "person"], ["Stefano Perazzo", "person"], ["Mariano Angelico", "person"], ["Agnese Nespoli", "person"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["Andrea Mazzola", "person"], ["Carlo Di Tullio", "person"], ["Carsten Voßkühler", "person"], ["Edoardo Degli Innocenti", "person"], ["Kama.Sport", "company"], ["Leigh Maxwell", "person"], ["Hawk-Eye Innovations", "company"], ["Simone Cevasco", "person"], ["Andrea Marini", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Mirko Mastropietro", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2097, 40, 2, 3], ["2026-10-17", 2097, 40, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 140, "id": 7468894368758718464, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7468894368758718464/", "ts": "2026-06-06T05:19:42+00:00", "date": "2026-06-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📍 I'm in Parma today as a speaker at the ⚽ Festival della Serie A.", "snippet": "📍 I'm in Parma today as a speaker at the ⚽ Festival della Serie A.", "chars": 1567, "text": "📍 I'm in Parma today as a speaker at the ⚽ Festival della Serie A.\nLega Serie A\n\n🎙️ This afternoon I'm on the Media Operations & Broadcast, Digital commission, the \"Ecosistema Dati\" round table. I'll be joined by Kama.Sport on blockchain and generative AI, and Hawk-Eye Innovations on AR and Gen Z feeds, all of us digging into the data ecosystem that sits behind the modern broadcast and digital experience.\n\nℹ️ A quick word on what the Festival is, for those outside Italy. It's a three-day open event run by Lega Serie A together with local authorities and Banca Ifis. June 5 to 7, free entry for everyone, the whole city centre turned into a meeting point for Italian football. Third edition, and it keeps getting bigger, with 24 panels across two rooms, a Fan Zone, a Digital Creative Hub, a Kings League event, and the Legendary awards, including Alessandro Del Piero as Legendary Player. 🏆\n\n📅 Yesterday at the Teatro Regio they unveiled the calendar for the 2026/27 season. 380 matches, kick-off the weekend of 22-23 August, last whistle on 29-30 May 2027. ⚽ The opening fixtures are already out, including Torino-Milan on matchday one, which tells you where my attention will be in August.\n\n🤝 If you're in Parma today, let's meet. You'll find me around the Biblioteca Monastica dei Padri Benedettini di San Giovanni Evangelista during the day, and at the Cena di Gala this evening. Send me a message and we'll carve out a moment.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 273, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Kama.Sport", "company"], ["Hawk-Eye Innovations", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 273, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 273, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 141, "id": 7468557827482017793, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7468557827482017793/", "ts": "2026-06-05T07:02:25+00:00", "date": "2026-06-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 10 shifts redefining the live sports video experience", "snippet": "📺 10 shifts redefining the live sports video experience", "chars": 1458, "text": "📺 10 shifts redefining the live sports video experience\n\nI wrote a long-form piece for the Dolby OptiView blog.\n\nI started designing, building, and launching digital video players and platforms  around 2008. The video experience has always been at the core of my interests. Working alongside the Dolby OptiView team with Paul Boustead has reinvigorated this theme for me. This piece is everything I've been thinking about since then, applied to where live sports streaming is heading right now.\n\nHere are the 10 shifts I have observed:\n\n1️⃣ The player has become the product\n2️⃣ The right latency is a retention tool\n3️⃣ HDR and spatial audio for live are becoming table stakes\n4️⃣ Personalization needs to mean something\n5️⃣ Ad insertion is becoming an experience design problem\n6️⃣ Each platform needs its own experience design\n7️⃣ Quality of Experience monitoring has become essential\n8️⃣ Interactive features need real-time infrastructure from the ground up\n9️⃣ The video stack is becoming a revenue engine\n🔟 Streaming is reaching broadcast-grade quality for live\n\nI'm honoured to be a contributor to the Dolby OptiView blog. Big thanks to Matthias Ankli, Jenny Lou, and the team for making this happen and for being great collaborators throughout the process.\n10 Things To Consider\n\n🔗 Full article: https://lnkd.in/dk9ygDxv\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 587, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Jenny Lou", "person"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 587, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 587, 4, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 142, "id": 7468318657098543104, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7468318657098543104/", "ts": "2026-06-04T15:12:02+00:00", "date": "2026-06-04", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "FIFA+ -> DAZN today", "snippet": "FIFA+ -> DAZN today", "chars": 38, "text": "FIFA+ -> DAZN today\nURL is redrirected", "qtext": "Today marks an exciting new chapter for FIFA’s streaming platform FIFA+. After successfully building and scaling the platform into a global destination for football content, FIFA is now transitioning FIFA+ to DAZN to leverage its world-class streaming capabilities and accelerate the shared ambition of creating the global home of football for fans.\n\nA huge thank you to everyone at DAZN and FIFA whose dedication, expertise, and collaboration made this milestone possible.\n\nLooking forward to seeing what this next phase of our partnership will bring for fans around the world.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/dp8S", "imp": 1316, "rx": 7, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1315, 7, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1316, 7, 2, 1]], "quoted": "Johannes Holzmüller"}, {"i": 143, "id": 7468300839024033792, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7468300839024033792/", "ts": "2026-06-04T14:01:14+00:00", "date": "2026-06-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Some stats from the official FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States squad list as published by FIFA.", "snippet": "Some stats from the official FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States squad list as published by FIFA.", "chars": 244, "text": "Some stats from the official FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States squad list as published by FIFA.\n⚽ 1248 Players\n⚽ Avg Age 27.4\n⚽ 73% play abroad\n⚽ Oldest is not Ronaldo!\ncheck below for more...\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 412, "rx": 3, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 407, 3, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 412, 3, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 144, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-06-04", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "GamingonPhone", "with": null, "title": "FIFA has officially announced FIFA World Cup: Launch Edition, a new football simulation game arriving exclusively on Netflix Games alongside", "snippet": "FIFA has officially announced FIFA World Cup: Launch Edition, a new football simulation game arriving exclusively on Netflix Games alongside the 2026 tournament", "chars": 789, "text": "FIFA has officially announced FIFA World Cup: Launch Edition, a new football simulation game arriving exclusively on Netflix Games alongside the 2026 tournament kickoff.\n\nDeveloped with Delphi Interactive, the title marks a major shift in FIFA’s gaming strategy following its split from Electronic Arts (EA), signaling a move toward a broader, multi-partner ecosystem.\n\n💡 Key highlights\n\n▪️ Launching June 11, 2026, in sync with the World Cup\n▪️ Features all 48 teams, 1,200+ players, and 16 stadiums\n▪️ Smartphone-as-controller gameplay via QR connection\n▪️ Free for Netflix subscribers\n\nThis collaboration reflects FIFA’s ambition to make football gaming more accessible and reach billions of fans globally, while also strengthening Netflix’s growing push into interactive entertainment.", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 69, "cm": 1, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FIFA", "company"], ["Netflix", "company"], ["Delphi Interactive", "company"], ["Electronic Arts (EA)", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 69, 1, 5], ["2026-10-17", null, 69, 1, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 145, "id": 7467813853024948225, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7467813853024948225/", "ts": "2026-06-03T05:46:07+00:00", "date": "2026-06-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "One-Hit Wonders: what happens when a single club video swallows a whole season", "snippet": "🎯 One-Hit Wonders: what happens when a single club video swallows a whole season.", "chars": 2390, "text": "🎯 One-Hit Wonders: what happens when a single club video swallows a whole season.\nOne Osasuna short pulled 38.2 million views.\nThat clip alone is 48% of everything Osasuna has ever uploaded to YouTube. In their entire YouTube lifetime.\n\nI built a feature inside YouTube Football Tracker (YTFT) that ranks club videos by how far they sit above their channel's own season average.\n\n📐 The math:\nLift = video views ÷ channel's average views per video this season\n\nI ran it across the top five European leagues. The top ten this season:\n\n⚽ 1. C.A. Osasuna (LaLiga) · Víctor Muñoz golazo · Lift 8,980\n⚽ 2. Real Sociedad (LaLiga) · the Saturday derby win · Lift 7,233\n⚽ 3. Real Sociedad (LaLiga) · Take Kubo's first goal · Lift 7,182\n⚽ 4. RCD Espanyol de Barcelona (LaLiga) · \"We love our academy\" · Lift 7,068\n⚽ 5. RCD Espanyol de Barcelona (LaLiga) · \"The Minion's journey\" · Lift 5,264\n⚽ 6. Real Sociedad (LaLiga) · \"bocata de otra galaxia\" · Lift 4,741\n⚽ 7. RCD Espanyol de Barcelona (LaLiga) · dogs adoption walk-out · Lift 3,642\n⚽ 8. Deportivo Alavés (LaLiga) · \"Work, work, work\" · Lift 2,471\n⚽ 9. RCD Mallorca (LaLiga) · \"MATEEEOOOO\" · Lift 2,212\n⚽ 10. Sunderland AFC (Premier League) · \"DAN BALLARD\" · Lift 2,125\n\nNine of the top ten belong to LALIGA. The tenth is Sunderland.\n\nSome of the surprises:\n🐶 An Espanyol walk-out with eleven rescue dogs (8.6M views)\n🥪 A Real Sociedad kit-man sandwich short before the Real Madrid game (13.7M views)\n👶 Two Espanyol academy reels with over 12 million views each\n\nThe big clubs are absent from this list and the math is the reason. Real Madrid, Barcelona, Bayern, PSG produce viral content as a baseline. Their season average is already pulled high. When everything is big, nothing stands far above the line.\n\nFull article with the story behind each clip, and what they tell us about how YouTube's algorithm is reshaping football social content: [link]\n\n🌐 YTFT (ytft.aguywithascarf.com) is a web app I've built that tracks publishing behaviour and performance across YouTube channels of football clubs and league bodies. I've started adding national federation channels in preparation for the 2026 FIFA World Cup. The site is open with free sign-in. Get in touch if you want to talk through what the data is showing.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1135, "rx": 12, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["C.A. Osasuna", "company"], ["Real Sociedad", "company"], ["Real Sociedad", "company"], ["RCD Espanyol de Barcelona", "company"], ["RCD Espanyol de Barcelona", "company"], ["Real Sociedad", "company"], ["RCD Espanyol de Barcelona", "company"], ["Deportivo Alavés", "company"], ["RCD Mallorca", "company"], ["Sunderland AFC", "company"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1135, 12, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1135, 12, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 146, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-06-03", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Sportradar", "with": null, "title": "The future of sports media is data, innovation, and deeper fan engagement. 💡", "snippet": "The future of sports media is data, innovation, and deeper fan engagement. 💡", "chars": 677, "text": "The future of sports media is data, innovation, and deeper fan engagement. 💡\n\nMark Holland, SVP, Media Products at Sportradar, joined the SportsPro Insider Series for their session titled: Engage. Grow. Monetise: The data-driven approach to sports media in 2026.\n\nAlongside Dennis Galejski (DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga), Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez (LALIGA) and moderator Carlo De Marchis, Mark explored where sports media is headed and how Sportradar is deploying the products and solutions that help leagues and rightsholders engage audiences, unlock growth, and drive monetization.\n\nLearn how Sportradar is combining data with next-gen technology solutions: https://ow.ly/mwLl50Z7gwC", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 38, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Mark Holland", "person"], ["Sportradar", "company"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Dennis Galejski", "person"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez", "person"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["Sportradar", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "text", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 38, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", null, 38, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 147, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-06-03", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Dolby OptiView", "with": null, "title": "💭 From our latest blog by Carlo De Marchis, founder of A guy with a scarf:", "snippet": "💭 From our latest blog by Carlo De Marchis, founder of A guy with a scarf:", "chars": 778, "text": "💭 From our latest blog by Carlo De Marchis, founder of A guy with a scarf:\n\nWhat happens between the feed and the fan is where the real value lives.\n\nWe talk a lot about content. Rights deals, league strategies, platform wars. All valid, all important. But there is a quieter revolution happening underneath all of that noise, and it has to do with what the viewer actually sees and hears when they press play.\n\nThe video experience layer has become the new battleground. The technology that sits between the feed and the fan is where the action is. For live, this matters even more.\n\nGet the full guide on the top 10 trends redefining what fans see, hear, and feel when they press play.\n\nRead now: https://lnkd.in/es4AdhX6\n\n#livesports #video #streaming #livestreaming #content", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 14, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["livesports", "video", "streaming", "livestreaming", "content"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 14, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", null, 14, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 148, "id": 7467206198417743872, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7467206198417743872/", "ts": "2026-06-01T13:31:31+00:00", "date": "2026-06-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 days to the start of the World Cup and as we won’t be able to listen to the Italian anthem there here it is 3000+ kids 250 teams in centr", "snippet": "10 days to the start of the World Cup and as we won’t be able to listen to the Italian anthem there here it is 3000+ kids 250 teams in central Italy for a big t", "chars": 198, "text": "10 days to the start of the World Cup and as we won’t be able to listen to the Italian anthem there here it is 3000+ kids 250 teams in central Italy for a big tournament enjoying our national anthem", "qtext": "", "imp": 520, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 225, "watch_time_s": 2222, "avg_watch_s": 9, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 520, 11, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 520, 11, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 149, "id": 7466830698654601216, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7466830698654601216/", "ts": "2026-05-31T12:39:25+00:00", "date": "2026-05-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ 🇫🇷 Pretty dense 24h for Paris Saint-Germain on the pitch and on YouTube", "snippet": "⚽ 🇫🇷 Pretty dense 24h for Paris Saint-Germain on the pitch and on YouTube", "chars": 384, "text": "⚽ 🇫🇷 Pretty dense 24h for Paris Saint-Germain on the pitch and on YouTube\nAlso top performer in many dimensions in Ligue 1 on YouTube.\nCheck the stats...\n\n🎬 I just launched YouTube Football Tracker — every official YouTube channel across Europe's top 5 leagues, plus the road to World Cup 2026. Free, pre-beta, built solo. Would love to know if it's useful.\n\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com", "qtext": "", "imp": 230, "rx": 1, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Paris Saint-Germain", "company"], ["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 3, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 230, 1, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 230, 1, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 150, "id": 7466100573893914625, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7466100573893914625/", "ts": "2026-05-29T12:18:10+00:00", "date": "2026-05-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: FIFA Packaged a Gaming Ecosystem Two Weeks Before Kickoff", "snippet": "10 Things To Consider: ⚽ FIFA Packaged a 🎮 Gaming Ecosystem Two Weeks Before Kickoff", "chars": 1452, "text": "10 Things To Consider: ⚽ FIFA Packaged a 🎮 Gaming Ecosystem Two Weeks Before Kickoff\n\n⚽ My son Miles is 11, plays at a football academy here in Torino, and lit right up when FIFA's new gaming news broke. He is exactly the fan FIFA wants to reach.\n🎮 He also plays EA SPORTS FC 26 on his PlayStation, and he has no phone of his own. FIFA's new flagship is mobile-first and built around Netflix. The console in his room already belongs to EA.\n📅 That is the whole story in one room. On 28 May FIFA announced an updated Digital Football Strategy with Roblox, Konami, Netflix, SEGA, Gamefam, Mythical, Epic and Solace on board. The World Cup opens on 11 June. Fourteen days.\n🧠 The strategy is sound. A multi-partner ecosystem is the right answer to losing EA, and the FIFAe esports layer already works at scale. The timing and the distribution are where it slips.\n\n📝 I wrote it up as 10 Things To Consider, leading on the go-to-market and what the move says about the FIFA brand without the game that made it famous. Full read 👉 below\n\n🔒 For paid Substack subscribers I added the practitioner's cut: an 8-point launch checklist any rights holder or vendor can run before a multi-partner play, the 6 signals I am tracking through the World Cup window, and a follow-up where I grade FIFA against the checklist once the game lands.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1706, "rx": 12, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["EA SPORTS", "company"], ["Netflix", "company"], ["Roblox", "company"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1706, 12, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1706, 12, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 151, "id": 7465679203418652672, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7465679203418652672/", "ts": "2026-05-28T08:23:47+00:00", "date": "2026-05-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What 45 minutes with LALIGA, Bundesliga and Sportradar told me about data and live sport in 2026", "snippet": "⚽ What 45 minutes with LALIGA, Bundesliga and Sportradar told me about data and live sport in 2026", "chars": 1861, "text": "⚽ What 45 minutes with LALIGA, Bundesliga and Sportradar told me about data and live sport in 2026\n\n🎙️ Yesterday I moderated the SportsPro Insider Series webinar with three people who sit at the centre of this question. Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez (LALIGA), Dennis Galejski (Bundesliga Media), and Mark Holland (Sportradar). 45 minutes, three rounds, one clear signal.\n\n📊 Ideas the industry has talked about for fifteen years are now live in the feed, working at scale. The leagues that own their data infrastructure are pulling ahead. Dennis walked us through the Bundesliga's bet on Sportec Solutions and a number that changed the room. Four million data points per match used to be the baseline. With 3D skeletal tracking, it is roughly 200 million.\n🏟️ Rebeca showed how a campaign that never started from a data brief became one. The Jornada Retro vintage matchday sold around 40,000 jerseys and drove roughly 3 million in turnover, then generated the data now feeding next season. Heritage reaches one fan, fantasy and live voting reach another, and LALIGA is building for both.\n🛰️ Mark gave the partner view from ten years inside Sportradar. Broadcasters want the how and the why now, not the raw stat. AI Researcher, 4sight, GameFrame. The tools that turn live data into storytelling are moving from possibility into adoption.\n\n🔁 My single biggest takeaway. The fan engagement loop now closes inside the live broadcast itself. Data starts the flow, fans drive the choice, broadcast closes it, clubs deliver the player. That is product design.\n▶️ The full 45 minutes is available as a replay on the SportsPro site. If you work in sports media, streaming, or on the vendor side, it is worth your time.\nWatch: https://lnkd.in/da6K74ji\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2472, "rx": 23, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LALIGA", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["Sportradar", "company"], ["Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez", "person"], ["Dennis Galejski", "person"], ["Mark Holland", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2472, 23, 1, 3], ["2026-10-17", 2472, 23, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 152, "id": 7465330041632210946, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7465330041632210946/", "ts": "2026-05-27T09:16:21+00:00", "date": "2026-05-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "LIVE with SportsPro (La Liga, Bundesliga, Sportradar) at 4pm CET: Data, fans, and what live sport looks like in 2026", "snippet": "🎙️ Today I’ll be moderating a SportsPro webinar", "chars": 651, "text": "🎙️ Today I’ll be moderating a SportsPro webinar\nLIVE AT 4pm CET\nEngage, grow, monetise: The data-driven approach to sports media in 2026.\n\nThree guests, 45 minutes, online. The brief is wide and that’s the point. I want a conversation that moves between what’s happening today and what comes next, with enough room for the speakers to disagree if they need to.\n\n📅 Date: Wednesday 27 May, 3:00 pm BST / 4:00 pm CET / 10:00 am EDT\n⏱️ Duration: 45 minutes\n🎙️ Moderator: Carlo De Marchis\n👥 Speakers: Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez (LALIGA), Dennis Galejski (Bundesliga Media), Mark Holland (Sportradar)\n\nSportsPro webinar registration page: https://lnkd.in/da6K74ji", "qtext": "", "imp": 779, "rx": 14, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez", "person"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Dennis Galejski", "person"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["Mark Holland", "person"], ["Sportradar", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 779, 14, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 779, 14, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 153, "id": 7465010109870526465, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7465010109870526465/", "ts": "2026-05-26T12:05:03+00:00", "date": "2026-05-26", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "LIVE tomorrow At 4pm CET!", "snippet": "LIVE tomorrow At 4pm CET!", "chars": 25, "text": "LIVE tomorrow At 4pm CET!", "qtext": "Sports data is rapidly becoming central to how audiences are grown, engaged and monetised across sports media.\n\nWhile some organisations are successfully turning data into a competitive advantage, many are still facing challenges and lagging behind.\n\nLive tomorrow, join leaders from Bundesliga International GmbH, Sportradar, LALIGA and more as we explore how sports media strategies are evolving across broadcast, streaming and digital in our exclusive webinar, 'The data-driven approach to sports media in 2026'.\n\nWednesday 27th May | 3-3:45PM BST\n\n💡 Find out more: https://lnkd.in/ed93bxcM\n\nRebec", "imp": 356, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 356, 4, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 356, 4, 1, 1]], "quoted": "SportsPro"}, {"i": 154, "id": 7465007517836902400, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7465007517836902400/", "ts": "2026-05-26T11:54:45+00:00", "date": "2026-05-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "💬 A few people asked me if the new platform tracks when football clubs publish on YouTube, not just what they publish.", "snippet": "💬 A few people asked me if the new platform tracks when football clubs publish on YouTube, not just what they publish.", "chars": 1592, "text": "💬 A few people asked me if the new platform tracks when football clubs publish on YouTube, not just what they publish.\n📄 It does. And I put together a 7-page PDF using the The Premier League League as an example. All 20 clubs plus the league HQ channel. 14,539 videos. 298 days since the season started on 1 August 2025.\n\nWhat's inside:\n📊 The aggregate view. Videos published per day for the whole league. Where the volume spikes, where the dips sit, what the weekend rhythm looks like.\n📈 Every channel, individually. 21 day-by-day charts on the same scale, ordered by total season videos. Manchester United at the top, Wolves at the bottom. The publishing operation behind each club becomes obvious.\n🔥 The publishing heatmap. Day-of-week by hour-of-day for the whole league. One slot lights up brighter than anything else: Saturday 23:00 CET, 529 posts. Sunday 23:00 right behind. The match-highlight reflex is league-wide and you can set your watch by it.\n🗓️ Every channel's heatmap. 21 individual heatmaps. The Premier League channel doesn't play the same game as the clubs. Its peak is Thursday 12:00 CET, not Saturday night. Build-up week, aggregator role, completely different rhythm.\n\n⚽ The PDF is attached. If you want to dig into your own league, club, or channel, the platform is free to sign in:\nytft.aguywithascarf.com\n🌍 It covers the top 5 European leagues, the FIFA Club World Cup 2026, and a viral-videos cross-league view. Day-of-week and hour-of-day heatmaps, per-channel publishing calendars, zero-day counts, top videos.\n\n👇 Curious to hear which patterns surprise you most.", "qtext": "", "imp": 596, "rx": 9, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["The Premier League", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 593, 9, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 596, 9, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 155, "id": 7464597965999910915, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7464597965999910915/", "ts": "2026-05-25T08:47:20+00:00", "date": "2026-05-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The $500 Million Bargain: What the Fox-FIFA Deal Says About How Sports Rights Get Priced", "snippet": "💰 The $500 Million Bargain", "chars": 2035, "text": "💰 The $500 Million Bargain\n\n📊 Fox is paying less than $500 million to broadcast the 2026 World Cup. Industry analysts put the real market value at between $1 billion and $1.5 billion.\n\n🏟️ Tariq Panja in the The New York Times this weekend filled in how that happened. A soundproof room below FIFA's Zurich headquarters in March 2014. A decision made under pressure to avoid a Fox lawsuit over the Qatar date change. An extension granted eleven years before the tournament, at a price set against a 2014 view of the world.\n\n🎞️ I have been around long enough to remember when media rights cycles ran three and four editions deep as standard. Most of my working life has been shaped by those cycles, by what they paid for, by what they assumed about the next decade. The Fox-FIFA case is the most visible example of a pattern that runs through sports rights every time the calendar gets tight. Federations trade competition for certainty under pressure, and discover years later what certainty actually cost.\n\n🎯 The wider point sits above the news. Federations now know long deals carry real downside. The question is whether their growth models are good enough to price what they are signing. Fox-FIFA was a failure of forecasting under pressure. The federations signing the next round of long deals are betting their forecasting has improved. Some of those bets will be right. The ones that are wrong will look like Fox-FIFA in 2036.\n\n📬 I wrote it up on A Guy With A Scarf. Free article on the deal and the lesson, with a paid appendix for subscribers that goes deeper. The appendix covers the full arc of FIFA's US English-language rights from 1994 to 2030, five long-dated rights bets that aged worst and five that aged best across thirty years of sports media, the Telemundo and Canadian extensions no one is talking about, the Burzaco testimony layer with the legal context, a working framework of seven questions to ask before signing past one cycle, and what all of this means for the 2030 World Cup auction.\n\n👇 Full Article below", "qtext": "", "imp": 3254, "rx": 21, "cm": 6, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["The New York Times", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3252, 21, 6, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3254, 21, 6, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 156, "id": 7464252167441342464, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7464252167441342464/", "ts": "2026-05-24T09:53:15+00:00", "date": "2026-05-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽📊 Arsenal racked up 929 million YouTube views this season. The smallest Premier League club still cleared 15 million.", "snippet": "⚽📊 Arsenal racked up 929 million YouTube views this season. The smallest Premier League club still cleared 15 million.", "chars": 1459, "text": "⚽📊 Arsenal racked up 929 million YouTube views this season. The smallest Premier League club still cleared 15 million.\n\nI added up every video the Premier League's clubs published this season. No league in Europe spreads attention like this 👇\n🔴 Arsenal Football Club — 929M views, 84% from Shorts. Nearly a billion.\n🟣 The Premier League League (league channel) — 778M\n🔴 Manchester United — 611M (more long-form than its rivals)\n🔴 Liverpool Football Club — 552M\n\nWhile you normally focus on the top, here it's also important to focus on the floor. Every single PL club passed 15M season views. In Spain, Italy, Germany and France, viewership collapsed after the top few. In England, even the bottom of the table is a serious media operation.\n\nAnd watch the strategy split:\n📱 The giants — Arsenal, Liverpool — ride Shorts for reach.\n🎬 The smaller clubs — Bournemouth, Wolves, Crystal Palace — go almost entirely long-form.\nDifferent sizes, different bets — but in the PL, everyone is playing.\n\n📌 One caveat that matters: these are views for videos published this season only — not lifetime channel totals. New output, head-to-head.\nI track this daily across the top-5 European leagues\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\n\nWhich league's YouTube strategy surprises you most? 👇\n\n#Football #PremierLeague #SocialMedia #YouTube #SportsBusiness #ContentStrategy\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf\nFor great YouTube growth tools talk to my friends at BuzzMyVideos", "qtext": "", "imp": 2990, "rx": 34, "cm": 14, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["Arsenal Football Club", "company"], ["The Premier League", "company"], ["Manchester United", "company"], ["Liverpool Football Club", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "premierleague", "socialmedia", "youtube", "sportsbusiness", "contentstrategy"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2990, 34, 14, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2990, 34, 14, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 157, "id": 7464251090365022208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7464251090365022208/", "ts": "2026-05-24T09:48:59+00:00", "date": "2026-05-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽📊 FC Barcelona just crossed 1 BILLION YouTube views for videos published in a single season.", "snippet": "⚽📊 FC Barcelona just crossed 1 BILLION YouTube views for videos published in a single season.", "chars": 1384, "text": "⚽📊 FC Barcelona just crossed 1 BILLION YouTube views for videos published in a single season.\n\nI added up every video La Liga's clubs published this season. The numbers here are on a different scale entirely 👇\n🔵🔴 FC Barcelona — 1.08 BILLION views, nearly double Real Madrid, and uniquely strong on Live (101M) alongside its Shorts.\n⚪ Real Madrid C.F. — 565M, 79% from Shorts.\n🟢⚪ LALIGA (league channel) — 676M, the most balanced of the giants.\n\nBut here's what really sets La Liga apart from Italy, Germany or France: depth.\nA dozen clubs cleared 15M season views — Betis (162M, 96% Shorts!), Atlético, Real Sociedad, Osasuna, Espanyol, Sevilla… In the other big leagues, viewership collapsed after the top 4-5. In Spain, the mid-table is genuinely competitive on YouTube.\nThe lesson: La Liga clubs — not just the giants — have figured out Shorts. The distribution of attention is far flatter than anywhere else in Europe.\n\n📌 One caveat that matters: these are views for videos published this season only — not lifetime channel totals. New output, head-to-head.\nI track this daily across the top-5 European leagues\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\n\nWhich league's content strategy surprises you most? 👇\n\n#Football #LaLiga #SocialMedia #YouTube #SportsBusiness #ContentStrategy\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf\nFor great YouTube growth tools talk to my friends at BuzzMyVideos", "qtext": "", "imp": 306, "rx": 7, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["FC Barcelona", "company"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["FC Barcelona", "company"], ["Real Madrid C.F.", "company"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "laliga", "socialmedia", "youtube", "sportsbusiness", "contentstrategy"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 306, 7, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 306, 7, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 158, "id": 7463960270462197760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463960270462197760/", "ts": "2026-05-23T14:33:22+00:00", "date": "2026-05-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽📊 PSG got 459 million YouTube views this season. 91% came from Shorts.", "snippet": "⚽📊 PSG got 459 million YouTube views this season. 91% came from Shorts.", "chars": 1339, "text": "⚽📊 PSG got 459 million YouTube views this season. 91% came from Shorts.\n\nI added up every video Ligue 1's clubs published this season. One club isn't just leading — it's playing a different game entirely 👇\n\n🔵🔴 Paris Saint-Germain — 459M views, 91% from Shorts. The most Shorts-skewed giant in all of Europe's big five. PSG has essentially turned its channel into a Shorts engine.Then the field:\n🔵⚪🔴 Ligue 1 (league channel) — 158M, nicely balanced\n⚪🔵 Olympique Lyonnais Groupe — 47M, and the contrarian: long-form-led\n⚪🔵 Olympique de Marseille — 43M, Shorts-driven\n…and that's the top four. Below it, the league shares scraps — most clubs under 8M for the whole season.\n⚽ The interesting tension: PSG maximizes reach through Shorts volume, while Lyon bets on long-form depth in the same league, same week. Two completely different theories of what a club channel is for.\n\n📌 One caveat that matters: these are views for videos published this season only — not lifetime channel totals. New output, head-to-head.\nI track this daily across the top-5 European leagues\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\nShorts reach or long-form depth — which would you build on? 👇\n\n#Football #Ligue1 #SocialMedia #YouTube #SportsBusiness #ContentStrategy\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf\nFor great YouTube growth tools talk to my friends at BuzzMyVideos", "qtext": "", "imp": 276, "rx": 2, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["Paris Saint-Germain", "company"], ["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["Olympique Lyonnais Groupe", "company"], ["Olympique de Marseille", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "ligue1", "socialmedia", "youtube", "sportsbusiness", "contentstrategy"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 276, 2, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 276, 2, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 159, "id": 7463957794950606848, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463957794950606848/", "ts": "2026-05-23T14:23:31+00:00", "date": "2026-05-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽📊 One club. 512 million views. 86% of them under 60 seconds.", "snippet": "⚽📊 One club. 512 million views. 86% of them under 60 seconds.", "chars": 1426, "text": "⚽📊 One club. 512 million views. 86% of them under 60 seconds.\n\nI added up every video the Bundesliga's clubs published this season on YouTube. The gap at the top is something else 👇\n\n🔴 FC Bayern München — 512M views, and a staggering 86% from Shorts. That's not a channel, that's a Shorts machine.\nThen everyone else:\n⚽ Bundesliga (league channel) — 209M, far more balanced, long-form-led\n🟡⚫ Borussia Dortmund — 61M, Shorts-driven\n🔴 Bayer 04 Leverkusen — 31M\n…and that's the entire group above 30M. Below the top four, the whole league shares scraps — most clubs under 10M for the season.\n\nTwo patterns jump out:\n1️⃣ Germany is Shorts country — nearly every club leans Shorts-first.\n2️⃣ SC Freiburg is the contrarian — long-form only, no Shorts at all. A deliberate bet against the grain.\nThere's no single right answer. Bayern's reach is built on volume and Shorts; the league channel wins on depth. Different goals, different playbooks.\n\n📌 One caveat that matters: these are views for videos published this season only — not lifetime channel totals. New output, head-to-head.\nI track this daily across the top-5 European leagues\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\nShorts reach or long-form depth — which would you build a club channel on? 👇\n\n#Football #Bundesliga #SocialMedia #YouTube #SportsBusiness #ContentStrategy\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf\nFor great YouTube growth tools talk to my friends at BuzzMyVideos", "qtext": "", "imp": 153, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["FC Bayern München", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["Borussia Dortmund", "company"], ["Bayer 04 Leverkusen", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "bundesliga", "socialmedia", "youtube", "sportsbusiness", "contentstrategy"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 153, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 153, 4, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 160, "id": 7463932163097296896, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463932163097296896/", "ts": "2026-05-23T12:41:40+00:00", "date": "2026-05-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽📊 Same league. Wildly different YouTube playbooks.", "snippet": "⚽📊 Same league. Wildly different YouTube playbooks.", "chars": 1344, "text": "⚽📊 Same league. Wildly different YouTube playbooks.\n\nI pulled every video Lega Serie A's clubs published this season and added up the views. The result says a lot about how football clubs actually think about content 👇\n\n🏆 The Serie A league channel leads with 307M views — and ~80% of it is long-form.\nBut the big clubs flip the script:\n🔵⚫ FC Internazionale Milano — 207M views, 87% from Shorts\n⚪⚫ Juventus Football Club — 171M, 74% Shorts\n🔴⚫ AC Milan — 102M, Shorts-led\n🟡🔴 AS Roma — 78M, but 89% long-form\n\nTwo clubs in the same league, same week — one betting everything on Shorts, the other on long-form storytelling. There's no single \"right\" strategy. There's only what fits your audience.\n\nAnd after the top 5? Viewership falls off a cliff — Atalanta at 7.5M, everyone else under 6M. The attention gap inside one league is staggering.\n\n📌 One caveat that matters: these are views for videos published this season only — not lifetime channel totals. New output, head-to-head.\n\nI track this daily across the top-5 European leagues\n👉 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\n\nWhat would you do if you ran a club's channel — chase Shorts reach or build long-form depth? 👇\n\n#Football #SocialMedia #YouTube #SportsBusiness #ContentStrategy #SerieA\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf\nFor great YouTube growth tools talk to my friends at BuzzMyVideos", "qtext": "", "imp": 906, "rx": 15, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["FC Internazionale Milano", "company"], ["Juventus Football Club", "company"], ["AC Milan", "company"], ["AS Roma", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "socialmedia", "youtube", "sportsbusiness", "contentstrategy", "seriea"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 906, 15, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 906, 15, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 161, "id": 7463504279186845696, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463504279186845696/", "ts": "2026-05-22T08:21:25+00:00", "date": "2026-05-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Two YouTube approaches in the UK: TNT Sports vs. BBC", "snippet": "🎬 Two YouTube approaches in the UK. TNT Sports vs. BBC", "chars": 1666, "text": "🎬 Two YouTube approaches in the UK. TNT Sports vs. BBC\n\nSame platform. Same week. Opposite plays.\n\n📺 TNT Sports just paywalled the Champions League final for the first time since the competition was rebranded in 1992. Arsenal v PSG behind a subscription. Europa League final and Conference League final too, both with English clubs in them.\n\n🎯 The BBC went the other way. Live build-up and the first 10 minutes of every home nations World Cup fixture streamed free on BBC Football's new YouTube and TikTok channels.\n\n🤔 How can both broadcasters be right at the same time?\n\nIn the new piece I work through:\n\n⚽ The 33-year history of how the UK final stayed free across four different mechanisms before this season's paywall\n\n📊 Why TNT's commercial logic is clean but the brand cost may outlast the spreadsheet\n\n🚀 Why the BBC is on YouTube and TikTok but skipping Instagram, and why that is Meta's product gap to close\n\n⏱️ Why 10 minutes is the negotiated number and the three ways the model could fail\n\n📅 The July check-in I will run on actual numbers after the finals and the World Cup group stage\n\nPlus four appendices on the live stream mechanics, the full UK World Cup rights deal, and TikTok GamePlan.\n\nThe main article is here below.\n🔒 The full piece including all four appendices is available for paid subscribers on Substack.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\n🎬 Just launched a new project: YouTube Football Tracker.\nTracks every club and league channel across Europe's top 5, plus the road to World Cup 2026. Built solo, free, sign-in to unlock everything.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1445, "rx": 7, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1445, 7, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1445, 7, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 162, "id": 7463133950744473600, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463133950744473600/", "ts": "2026-05-21T07:49:52+00:00", "date": "2026-05-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "YouTube Football Tracker: Tracking the Top 5 Leagues and Clubs use YouTube (and more)", "snippet": "A guy with a scarf - LAUNCH", "chars": 2336, "text": "A guy with a scarf - LAUNCH\n🎬 YouTube Football Tracker: Tracking how the Top 5 Leagues and Clubs use YouTube (and more)\n\nA small side project goes public today. Built solo, free to use, free sign-in to unlock the full set of pages.\n\n⚽ It tracks the official YouTube channels of every club and league across Europe's top 5 (Serie A, La Liga, Premier League, Bundesliga, Ligue 1), plus the road to FIFA World Cup 2026 (48 qualified nations, FIFA, the 6 confederations), plus side lenses for top players, clubs outside the big-5, and women's football.\n🧩 Why I built it: club YouTube data sits in 100 separate browser tabs. Nobody outside the clubs themselves gets the whole picture. I wanted one.\n🤔 Why a dashboard in the age of AI? Fair question. My answer is that I still get more ideas from looking at the data first and strategising later. The dashboard is the prompt generator. AI sits on top, not under. Short AI notes already sit alongside the charts on the site today, reading the data next to the chart you're looking at. A Compare view, a Claude-powered AI Analysis, and an Ask Data layer are invite-only for now. More advanced AI features will follow for A Guy With A Scarf premium subscribers on Substack.\n🎛️ Every Top-5 page is effectively three pages in one. A cascading filter lets you zoom from all leagues, to one league, to a single club. Same charts, three lenses.\n🙏 The idea came from working with Paola Marinone and Bengu Atamer at BuzzMyVideos, who pulled me deep into how YouTube actually works. This tracker applies that lens to football.\n🚧 Pre-beta. Built solo. Public YouTube data only. Not affiliated with any club, league, FIFA, or YouTube.\n⏱️ One caveat worth flagging: YouTube's public view counts update on Google's schedule, sometimes a few hours late, occasionally a day or two. Lifetime totals are always right. Daily attribution can shift. The site retrofits as soon as the API catches up.\n\n💬 If you try it, tell me what's broken, what's missing, what you'd want to see next. And the bigger question I keep asking myself: does it make sense? Is it useful? That's the feedback I really need.\n\n🔗 ytft.aguywithascarf.com\n\n#ai #FootballBusiness #SportsMedia #YouTube #SportsTech\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2151, "rx": 31, "cm": 10, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["YouTube Football Tracker", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "footballbusiness", "sportsmedia", "youtube", "sportstech"], "series": ["YouTube Football Tracker"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2151, 31, 10, 0], ["2026-10-17", 2151, 31, 10, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 163, "id": 7462880769967812608, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462880769967812608/", "ts": "2026-05-20T15:03:49+00:00", "date": "2026-05-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📢 1 week to go. LIVE!", "snippet": "📢 1 week to go. LIVE!", "chars": 608, "text": "📢 1 week to go. LIVE!\n \nI will be moderating the SportsPro panel for our upcoming Insider Series webinar:\n\n📑 ⚽ The data-driven approach to sports media in 2026.\n \n• Dennis Galejski - Head of Product Management | Bundesliga International GmbH\n• Mark Holland - SVP, Media Products | Sportradar\n• Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez - Director, Media | LALIGA\n\nTogether, we explore how sports organisations are using data to drive audience growth, fan engagement, monetisation and more personalised digital experiences.\n \nMay 27th | 3-3:45PM BST\nRegister now: https://lnkd.in/ed93bxcM\n\n#Webinar #Laliga #Bundesliga #sportsdata", "qtext": "", "imp": 1070, "rx": 7, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Dennis Galejski", "person"], ["Mark Holland", "person"], ["Rebeca Díaz Gonzalez", "person"]], "tags": ["webinar", "laliga", "bundesliga", "sportsdata"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1070, 7, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1070, 7, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 164, "id": 7462370226144366592, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462370226144366592/", "ts": "2026-05-19T05:15:06+00:00", "date": "2026-05-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 81: Gilles Domartini on Agents in Production and the Death of the Dashboard (Part 2 of 2)", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone Ep. 81 Part 2 is out.", "chars": 2948, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone Ep. 81 Part 2 is out.\n\nGilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, on Agents in Production and the Death of the Dashboard (Part 2 of 2)\n\nFour agents in production, the guardrails behind them, and a revised forecast on when the dashboard finally dies.\n\nPart one covered what Gilles saw across his US tour: a frozen enterprise, a booming creator economy, a transfer of value out of legacy SaaS into AI infrastructure. The product answer in one line: ship working systems inside the workflows clients already use, and build the guardrails before the agents.\n\n🤖 At NAB Cleeng announced agents in production, framed against AI slop and slideware. Partners and clients want proof at scale.\n\n🎯 The onboarding agent acts as a coach across every step on the platform, paired with the freemium tier that lets new customers start Cleeng for free up to 10,000 subscribers.\n\n📊 The analyst agent is the one most used by existing clients. A typical deployment carries around 60 dashboards covering MRR, ARPU, LTV, upgrades, downgrades, campaign management across web, Roku, app stores. Two hours of Monday morning digging becomes a single prompt: \"Roku had an issue. Your debit cards are converting at a lower rate. Win-back campaign twenty-two performed well, this one didn't, we recommend you do this.\"\n\n⚙️ The offer setup agent takes the recommendation and creates the campaign inside the platform. The analyst spots the opportunity. The offer agent ships it.\n\n🔒 The hard part isn't the AI. The guardrails consumed the time. Client data segregation, PII removal, role-based access at the prompt level. Finance sees financial data. Customer care sees user interactions and churn risk. Selling an agent into a media business means selling a security model, a permissions architecture and an audit trail.\n\n💬 UX as quality of interaction. Claude, Gemini and ChatGPT moved the bar. Cleeng is working toward an experience that feels like a subscriber retention coach sitting next to you.\n\n📅 The September 27 prediction. At IBC 2024 Gilles predicted dashboards would be gone within two years. He revised the call: \"We will still have a dashboard in September 2026. But in September 2027, I'm definitely not sure.\" Cleeng is moving fast on headless. Partners create an offer or pull analytics from inside Slack, copilot, MediaKind, Accedo, Univision or ATP.\n\n🎯 The bet. The winners over the next 24 months are the vendors who ship working systems inside their clients' existing workflows, with the permissions architecture to make those systems trustworthy, and who move faster than the consolidation around them. The dashboard era ends on a date Gilles will not yet name. The work to be useful after that date starts now.\n\nGilles Domartini Alexis Gaï Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Adrianna Panek\n#ai\n-----\n🎧Retention Zone – Episode 81\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 475, "rx": 11, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Adrianna Panek", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 472, 11, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 475, 11, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 165, "id": 7461648484857937920, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7461648484857937920/", "ts": "2026-05-17T05:27:09+00:00", "date": "2026-05-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.", "snippet": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.", "chars": 1205, "text": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.\n\n📙 200 pages. 51 working lists across 10 categories. A field manual for anyone operating on the vendor side of sports and media technology.\n\n📙 I wrote it because most of the conversations I have with founders, sales leaders and product people circle the same recurring moments. The pitch that needs rebuilding. The renewal that feels shaky. The PoC that drags. The org chart that no longer fits the strategy. I put the lists I actually use into one book so you can pull the right one out when you need it.\n\n📙 It is built from decades of work across product, delivery, partnerships, strategy and client engagement. No theory. Just the questions worth asking before the meeting, the deal, the hire, the pivot.\n\n📙 Read it cover to cover if you want. Or keep it on your phone and pull out the right list when you need it. Pre-pitch. Pre-renewal. Pre-board. Pre-PoC. The lists are built to work as a tool, used in the moment.\n\n57,83 € on Kindle, or free with Kindle Unlimited.\n\n📙 The book is here: https://lnkd.in/daY9dPmm\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 936, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 933, 9, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 936, 9, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 166, "id": 7461338974675066881, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7461338974675066881/", "ts": "2026-05-16T08:57:16+00:00", "date": "2026-05-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "16 May 1976, and the neighbourhood heard the Toro anthem", "snippet": "⚽ 🟥 50 years ago today. The day I put the Toro anthem on the balcony for the whole neighbourhood to hear", "chars": 2451, "text": "⚽ 🟥 50 years ago today. The day I put the Toro anthem on the balcony for the whole neighbourhood to hear\n\n16 May 1976. I was eleven. When the news came from Perugia that Juventus had lost and Torino were champions of Italy, I did the most sacred thing I could think of: I put the stereo on the balcony of our flat in Turin, turned the volume to the maximum, and played the Toro anthem.\nI still have the granata flag from that day. No shield sewn on. There had not been time yet.\nMy father Piero De Marchis was at the Stadio Comunale that Sunday as a staff photographer for La Stampa. There is a photograph that catches him at pitch-side just behind Graziani while the celebration grows around them. Fifty years on, I have written a long piece about that afternoon. Here are the layers:\n\n⚽ The team. A working-class side built at the Filadelfia, modernised by Gigi Radice's full-pitch pressing, captained by Claudio Sala. Pulici top scorer with 21 goals, Graziani 15. Final table Torino 45, Juventus 43.\n\n🏘️ The city. From my balcony I could see la casa del Toro thirty metres away: a ten-storey block in Vanchiglietta where Claudio Sala and other players lived. In 1973–74 I used to go roller-skating with the daughter of then-coach Gustavo Giagnoni. I watched Aldo Agroppi play football with his son in the garden in front of the building. They were idols already, and the distance was smaller.\n\n⛰️ The hill. Twenty-seven years and twelve days after Superga, the title came back. Gian Paolo Ormezzano, then editor of Tuttosport, wrote the headline that still defines the club: \"Lassù qualcuno ti ama\". Up there, someone loves you.\n\n📺 The docuseries. From Friday on Sky Sport, a three-part series with the very same title. Sky Creative Agency used generative AI to bring historical photographs to life from the protagonists' accounts. A cultivated, legitimate use of the technology, where AI fills the holes in visual memory. The memory of the Toro has always been made of holes, of lost home movies, of grainy photographs. Those machines now give back what time had taken.\nBeing Toro is a grammar. A syntax of melancholy, of waiting, of loyalty to an idea of football that is also an idea of the world. You support Toro for the pleasure of never choosing the easy road.\n\nI wrote this one for me, for my father, for the boys still up there at Superga, and for anyone who has ever put a stereo on a balcony for a reason that only a city can understand.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2973, "rx": 23, "cm": 5, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2969, 23, 5, 0], ["2026-10-17", 2973, 23, 5, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 167, "id": 7460965949668732928, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7460965949668732928/", "ts": "2026-05-15T08:15:00+00:00", "date": "2026-05-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.", "snippet": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.", "chars": 1205, "text": "📙 BOOK: 10 Things To Consider is now available on Kindle.\n\n📙 200 pages. 51 working lists across 10 categories. A field manual for anyone operating on the vendor side of sports and media technology.\n\n📙 I wrote it because most of the conversations I have with founders, sales leaders and product people circle the same recurring moments. The pitch that needs rebuilding. The renewal that feels shaky. The PoC that drags. The org chart that no longer fits the strategy. I put the lists I actually use into one book so you can pull the right one out when you need it.\n\n📙 It is built from decades of work across product, delivery, partnerships, strategy and client engagement. No theory. Just the questions worth asking before the meeting, the deal, the hire, the pivot.\n\n📙 Read it cover to cover if you want. Or keep it on your phone and pull out the right list when you need it. Pre-pitch. Pre-renewal. Pre-board. Pre-PoC. The lists are built to work as a tool, used in the moment.\n\n57,83 € on Kindle, or free with Kindle Unlimited.\n\n📙 The book is here: https://lnkd.in/daY9dPmm\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 472, "rx": 5, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 472, 5, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 472, 5, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 168, "id": 7460626137900142592, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7460626137900142592/", "ts": "2026-05-14T09:44:43+00:00", "date": "2026-05-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 81: Gilles Domartini on the Frozen Enterprise and the Transfer of Value (Part 1 of 2)", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone Ep. 81 is out in two parts", "chars": 2187, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone Ep. 81 is out in two parts\nGilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, on the Frozen Enterprise and the Transfer of Value (Part 1 of 2)\n\nA frozen enterprise, a booming creator economy, and the rotation of capital out of legacy SaaS.\n\nThe most useful number from my conversation with Gilles is 24. That is the horizon beyond which large investors and enterprise buyers in media tech currently cannot see. For the first time in 20 years of tech, the next two years are harder to predict than the past 20.\n\n📍 Two months in the US, both coasts, NAB Las Vegas and the Devoncroft executive summit. Gilles came back with a clear read.\n\n📉 Investment in media and entertainment tech declining 2 to 4% on average every year. The slowdown is constant.\n\n🚀 One category is booming. The creator economy keeps growing on YouTube, Instagram and TikTok against Warner Bros. and Paramount. SMB creators are the fast-growing middle where margins remain less compressed.\n\n🥶 Why enterprises are frozen. Large investors deploy on four to six-year horizons. They cannot model that today. Paramount-Skydance, Netflix and Warner Bros., Harmonic-MediaKind. Deltatre going through organisational changes too. CFOs demanding savings. AI sitting on top as a productivity question with change management attached. The result is a frozen state in parts of the enterprise category.\n\n💸 Where the money went. Atlassian, Salesforce, Snowflake, GitLab dropped fifty percent in three months. That money was reallocated to Anthropic, Google, NVIDIA. Twelve Labs and Eleven Labs landing big funding.\n\n⏱️ The 24-month window is short. The required moves are not subtle. Waiting carries a specific price: missing the window and reading about the consolidation that followed in someone else's deck.\n\n🔜 In part two: the four agents Cleeng put into production at NAB, the guardrails behind them, and Gilles's revised prediction on when the dashboard finally dies.\n\nFull article below 👇\n\nGilles Domartini Alexis Gaï Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Adrianna Panek\n#ai\n-----\n🎧Retention Zone – Episode 81\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 879, "rx": 22, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Adrianna Panek", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 879, 22, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 879, 22, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 169, "id": 7459568108291244032, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7459568108291244032/", "ts": "2026-05-11T11:40:29+00:00", "date": "2026-05-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Do You Have Agency?", "snippet": "🎯 Do You Have Agency?", "chars": 2124, "text": "🎯 Do You Have Agency?\n\nIt is the buzzword of the moment. Every CEO talking about hiring, every VC explaining who made it, every leadership consultant who used to say \"ownership.\" I heard it enough times that I went to document myself, because the surface meaning was hiding something more specific.\n\nHere is what I found, and why it matters now.\n\n🤖 AI is what makes agency valuable\nWhen execution gets cheap, deciding what to execute is the only premium left. Two teams with the same AI stack will diverge based on who points the tools at the right problems. AI is excellent at doing. It is still terrible at choosing.\n\n⚡ Agency is acting when acting is the right move\nPeople with it see a gap and close it. People without it see a gap and write a memo. Bravery for its own sake is a different thing.\n\n🏟️ Our industry punishes it\nSports and media tech runs on long sales cycles, federation politics, broadcaster relationships, procurement reviews. The structure rewards waiting. In decades inside this industry, the pattern is consistent: the good outcomes come from someone deciding to do something that was not in the brief. The vendors that grow figured out how to move at the speed of the audience while the rest of the industry moves at the speed of the procurement department.\n\n💼 The PE signal\nPrivate equity buyers do not wait. The playbook is mixed in outcomes, sometimes real value, sometimes hollowed-out EBITDA gains. What is consistent is the speed differential, and that tells you how much agency the previous ownership was leaving on the table.\n\n🔑 The takeaway\nThe opposite of agency is permission. Stop asking for it.\n\n📋 For paid subscribers\nThe Do You Have Agency? Checklist. Thirty questions across three roles in sports and media tech: leadership, vendor, and client. Designed to make you flinch. If none of them do, the test is broken. Or you are.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media now also in Kindle version.", "qtext": "", "imp": 967, "rx": 11, "cm": 3, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 967, 11, 3, 0], ["2026-10-17", 967, 11, 3, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 170, "id": 7458420646918635520, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7458420646918635520/", "ts": "2026-05-08T07:40:52+00:00", "date": "2026-05-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "It's a good day when the Leaders in Sport newsletter Worth Knowing by James Emmett & David Cushnan mentions you.", "snippet": "It's a good day when the Leaders in Sport newsletter Worth Knowing by James Emmett & David Cushnan mentions you.", "chars": 1023, "text": "It's a good day when the Leaders in Sport newsletter Worth Knowing by James Emmett & David Cushnan mentions you.\n\nNot sure about the \"aplomb\": 😎\nAplomb refers to total composure, self-assurance, or poise, especially under pressure. Derived from the French for \"perpendicularity\" (literally \"according to the plummet\"), it implies a balanced, upright, and unflappable demeanor. It is also used to describe the perfect vertical alignment of an object or garment.\n\nFrom the newsletter: \"4) What’s DAZN up to now? \nTen years into the global sports streaming project (who remembers the pronunciation palaver that followed the launch in 2016?) and DAZN continues to make well-financed moves. Last week it announced a $100m deal to acquire Rick Allen’s ViewLift streaming tech company. The rationale for that deal is mapped out with typical aplomb here by Carlo de Marchis.\"\n\nTrue that the article got positive reactions also from the one involved. But I have seen much deeper analysis so if you are interested get out and search.", "qtext": "", "imp": 801, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Leaders in Sport", "company"], ["James Emmett", "person"], ["David Cushnan", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 801, 9, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 801, 9, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 171, "id": 7458061352146763776, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7458061352146763776/", "ts": "2026-05-07T07:53:10+00:00", "date": "2026-05-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📚 April recap from A Guy with a Scarf:", "snippet": "📚 April recap from A Guy with a Scarf:", "chars": 1375, "text": "📚 April recap from A Guy with a Scarf:\n\n10 Things To Consider\n🔟 PGA TOUR's Automated Content System\nhttps://lnkd.in/dSPu_P9K\n🏎️ Apple TV's F1 Streaming in the US\nhttps://lnkd.in/dxN4HvRD\n🏈 NFL Media Rights Renegotiation\nhttps://lnkd.in/dCZhycwK\n🏏 $3.4B in IPL Franchise Sales\nhttps://lnkd.in/dnP7pi8E\n📲 DAZN Creators & Playmakers Programme\nhttps://lnkd.in/d6ugv552\n💶 CVC Capital Partners FY25 Results\nhttps://lnkd.in/d3gMF5Pw\n📺 FIFA × YouTube Preferred Platform Deal\nhttps://lnkd.in/dzdSHN36\n🌐 Global SportsTech 2026\nhttps://lnkd.in/dh-ygUQQ\n🤖 Stanford AI Index Report 2026\nhttps://lnkd.in/d5D_XkBb\n🎾 PwC Emerging Sports Report — March 2026\nhttps://lnkd.in/dRGDgu_q\n\nMore posts:\n🇩🇪 SportsInnovation 2026 — Dolby OptiView × Sportec\nhttps://lnkd.in/dKueamYi\n🏃 SportsPro London 2026 — Five Sessions\nhttps://lnkd.in/dfiCHYRX\n📺 YouTube TV's Custom Multiview Pro\nhttps://lnkd.in/dGJtGGnP\n⚽ StreamOn Debrief — the Ligue 1 story\nhttps://lnkd.in/ds_rASt9\n🤖 AI, Content & the Future of Work\nhttps://lnkd.in/dk_xqGc5\n⚙️ A love letter to heads of IT\nhttps://lnkd.in/dHZaV7qA\n👩‍🎨 A love letter to corporate design leads\nhttps://lnkd.in/d-47_Tcf\n🎙️ Live Sports Conversation Stream — Dolby OptiView webinar\nhttps://lnkd.in/d9KfNzt9\n\nSubscribe to A Guy with a Scarf 👇\nhttps://lnkd.in/ddmvMF-Q\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView — Enabling live sports experiences.", "qtext": "", "imp": 879, "rx": 12, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 19, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 879, 12, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 879, 12, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 172, "id": 7457705397194428416, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7457705397194428416/", "ts": "2026-05-06T08:18:44+00:00", "date": "2026-05-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider After the 2026 NAB Streaming Summit", "snippet": "📺 📲 10 Things To Consider After the 2026 NAB Streaming Summit", "chars": 2733, "text": "📺 📲 10 Things To Consider After the 2026 NAB Streaming Summit\nTwo years ago I was on stage at the Streaming Summit in Vegas. This year I was not. So I did what we do when we miss the room. I read everything.\n\nVery thankful that Dan Rayburn publishes every session in full, with video. I pulled the transcripts and reverse engineered the conference over a weekend.\n\n🔟 10 Things To Consider\n🤖 1. AI moved from the roadmap to the rundown. The PGA TOUR session with Phil Green and Eric Hanson put the number on it: 7,000 AI highlight clips a week. That is a working production line.\n🏟️ 2. Live sports is still the hardest problem in streaming. The Engineering the Live Sports Pipeline panel said it plainly. Live is the R&D lab. Everyone else is the customer. Yannick Manuel Ramcke from OneFootball reminded the room that even with every telemetry pipeline lit, eyes on the glass still matters.\n📺 3. Pay TV still owns 85% of US live sports viewing. Dan Rayburn opened day two with that humility check. Build sports streaming products with that number in mind.\n🧩 4. The distribution math is in flux. FAST, AVOD, SVOD, carrier-OTT all coexist. The Bundling and Distribution panel with Mike Callahan, Brian Dunlap, Chris Buckley and Steven \"Claw\" Jalicy made it clear: there is no template.\n🎯 5. Personalization is moving from a row on the home screen to the live moment. Patrick Carroll at NASCAR and Paul Boustead at Dolby OptiView are building personalization that lives inside the experience itself.\n🔍 6. Discovery is still broken, and chatbots are not the rescue. Bernd Riefler's study across ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini made that clear. The metadata work matters more than the chatbot.\n⚡ 7. Ultra-low latency at premium bitrate is now table stakes. Derik Yarnell at Comcast walked through the 30 Mbps RealTime4K stream of Super Bowl LX with Dolby Vision and Atmos.\n🧠 8. The frame-based streaming model may be on its way out. Sandeep Tiku at DAZN argued for transmitting change and reconstructing the experience on the device. Take it seriously.\n🎬 9. Content libraries are becoming searchable scene by scene. John Sculley at VideoMagic and Keir Shepherd at Encompass made the case. Library is now a verb. You query it.\n🔗 10. The stack is the product. The PGA TOUR runs a 165,000 sq ft facility weaving multiple vendors. Whoever owns the integration owns the experience.\n\nRead the article. Then book the flight for 2027.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad\n-----\nI am available to be hired for Strategy, Product, and Marketing augmentation beyond the A guy with a scarf activities. If that fits something you are working on, get in touch. 😎", "qtext": "", "imp": 1826, "rx": 30, "cm": 6, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Dan Rayburn", "person"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["Phil Green", "person"], ["Eric Hanson", "person"], ["Yannick Manuel Ramcke", "person"], ["Mike Callahan", "person"], ["Brian Dunlap", "person"], ["Steven \"Claw\" Jalicy", "person"], ["Patrick Carroll", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Bernd Riefler", "person"], ["Derik Yarnell", "person"], ["Sandeep Tiku", "person"], ["Keir Shepherd", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ad"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1826, 30, 6, 5], ["2026-10-17", 1826, 30, 6, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 173, "id": 7457357533738037248, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7457357533738037248/", "ts": "2026-05-05T09:16:27+00:00", "date": "2026-05-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro London 2026, Day Two: The Reality Check Sharpened", "snippet": "🏃‍➡️ SportsPro London 2026, Day Two: The Reality Check Sharpened", "chars": 2605, "text": "🏃‍➡️ SportsPro London 2026, Day Two: The Reality Check Sharpened\nDay two went deeper into the media track. Same signal as day one, different instruments.\n\n🤖 Paola Marinone (BuzzMyVideos) joined me on camera at the Oval. Her opening line set the tone. Asking yourself the right questions is what produces the right answers. Intellectual honesty is the prerequisite for new business models, new ideas, new value. The silver bullet is the part that does not exist. Her bridge to Bengu Atamer's YouTube-as-TV thesis was clean. New assets, new sponsorship packages, new revenue lines on the P&L. This is business.\n🤖 Chris Stone (SportsPro) opened the media track with a twelve-month scan. Big tech is in sports rights for real. YouTube streamed an NFL game. @Apple holds F1 in the US. Netflix has NFL Christmas Day. ESPN launched D2C. In Europe, the BBC and YouTube partnership with fifty bespoke channels is the watershed. The Bundesliga UK approach across Sky Sports, Amazon, BBC iPlayer, YouTube and Mark Goldbridge's channel is the case study to watch. Casimiro will broadcast all 104 games of the 2026 World Cup. Chris's painful number: ninety-six of the top one hundred US TV shows in 2025 were sports. The UK top ten is Traitors and I'm a Celebrity. Top five sports take eighty percent of all media rights revenue.\n🤖 David Gibbs (WBD Sports Europe) with Greg Burns (Reuters Imagen) walked through the @HBO Max consolidation. 130M subscribers, 35,000 live events this year. Milano Cortina pulled 3X the viewing subscribers of Beijing 2022. His answer to Nick Meacham's live versus non-live question: none of the non-live stuff exists if the live product is not as good as it can be. His closer: you come for Liverpool and you stay for Dragons.\n🤖 Patrick Mostboeck (Sportradar) took the AI session into product. Generative AI gives new tools, it does not collapse the work. Headline deployment: NBA Peacock Performance View on Peacock. One in four NBA users opted into the augmented stream. Less is more. Next layer is prediction.\nBoth David Gibbs and Patrick Mostboeck acknowledged me from the stage. After thirty-five years building and launching streaming services for sport, that recognition lands. Streaming did not become what it is today by accident. Thank you both.\nProve the ROI one use case at a time. Pick the lowest hanging fruit. Let the next use case earn the right to exist. That is the only signal worth carrying out of the Oval.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView.\n-----\nCheck out my book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1190, "rx": 21, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"], ["Chris Stone", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Netflix", "company"], ["ESPN", "company"], ["BBC", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["Amazon", "company"], ["Mark Goldbridge", "person"], ["David Gibbs", "person"], ["WBD Sports Europe", "company"], ["Greg Burns", "person"], ["Reuters Imagen", "company"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["Sportradar", "company"], ["David Gibbs", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1190, 21, 1, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1190, 21, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 174, "id": 7455890697682915328, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7455890697682915328/", "ts": "2026-05-01T08:07:46+00:00", "date": "2026-05-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About DAZN Buying ViewLift", "snippet": "🎯 10 Things To Consider About DAZN Buying ViewLift", "chars": 2923, "text": "🎯 10 Things To Consider About DAZN Buying ViewLift\n\nA $100M deal that says more about where the sports OTT vendor market is going than about ViewLift itself.\n\nI sat across from Manik Bambha and the ViewLift team a few years back in a similar process. Prepared in a way rarer than it should be in this industry.\n\n1️⃣ Price tells a story. $100M is small. Bain Capital and Nextalia bought Deltatre in 2022 for around $800M. DAZN is paying for entry, relationships, and speed. Disclosure. I worked at Deltatre for many years before leaving. Everything I write about it comes from public sources only.\n2️⃣ Timing is the calendar. Main Street Sports Group is winding down. 20 NBA and NHL teams in the open market before 2026-27.\n3️⃣ The product is optionality. Per Sports Business Journal, DAZN is pitching three models. ViewLift Classic, full rights at $8-15M annually, and a streaming hub.\n4️⃣ B2B2C and SaaS is the actual strategy. DAZN CEO Shay Segev wants to be infrastructure as well as a destination.\n5️⃣ The end-to-end sports OTT vendor model is shifting. NBA's investment arm took an equity stake in MediaKind in September 2025. Three months later MediaKind acquired Harmonic's video business for $145M. The vendor-client boundary is no longer where it used to be.\n6️⃣ The market is consolidating. Deltatre acquired Endeavor Streaming in July 2025. Bending Spoons bought Brightcove for $233M and Vimeo for $1.38B. Now DAZN buys ViewLift.\n7️⃣ The deal structure tells a story. ViewLift shareholders are rolling under the DAZN umbrella. Equity in a buyer is only attractive if you believe the buyer is going somewhere worth being. DAZN has indicated it will be profitable this year with accelerating growth. (UPDATED)\n8️⃣ The competitive set is crowded. Amazon, ESPN, Fubo, Victory+, YouTube TV, Gray Media, Scripps, Nexstar, and Kiswe are all in the same conversations.\n9️⃣ The Ted Leonsis fact. ViewLift was founded by Leonsis. The product encodes a worldview that comes from the team side of the business.\n🔟 The B2B vendor lesson. Technology expertise alone does not earn a seat at the rights conversation. The pure technology supplier role is being squeezed from above by hyperscalers and from below by content operators.\n\n❓ What I am still unsure about\nWhether DAZN can run three models at once. Whether the bet on local-rights fragmentation is correct.\nDAZN moved from distant national rights bidder to operational US partner in a single $100M transaction. The people inside ViewLift, including Manik Bambha and the leadership Rick Allen anchors, are the reason this deal has a real chance of delivering in practice as much as on paper.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n\n#SportsStreaming #SportsBusiness #OTT #MediaTech", "qtext": "", "imp": 5480, "rx": 72, "cm": 15, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["ViewLift", "company"], ["Manik Bambha", "person"], ["ViewLift", "company"], ["Bain Capital", "company"], ["Nextalia", "company"], ["Deltatre", "company"], ["Main Street Sports", "company"], ["Sports Business Journal", "company"], ["Shay Segev", "person"], ["MediaKind", "company"], ["Harmonic", "company"], ["Endeavor Streaming", "company"], ["Bending Spoons", "company"], ["Brightcove", "company"], ["Vimeo", "company"], ["Ted Leonsis", "person"], ["Rick Allen", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportsstreaming", "sportsbusiness", "ott", "mediatech"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 5477, 72, 15, 2], ["2026-10-17", 5480, 72, 15, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 175, "id": 7455658801921318913, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7455658801921318913/", "ts": "2026-04-30T16:46:17+00:00", "date": "2026-04-30", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "DAZN buys Viewlift", "snippet": "DAZN buys Viewlift", "chars": 18, "text": "DAZN buys Viewlift", "qtext": "Today we accelerated our US growth plans with the announcement of our agreement to acquire ViewLift.\n\nI’m delighted to welcome Rick Allen, Manik Bambha and the entire ViewLift team to the DAZN family as we take this next step in our ambitious journey.\n\nAt DAZN, we are focused on building the largest sports platform destination for every fan, club and sport in the world. We are already the largest global sports platform, and this move strengthens that position.\n\nThe timing is important. The US sports ecosystem is changing fast, and teams and leagues need flexible, future-proof solutions as trad", "imp": 383, "rx": 5, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 380, 5, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 383, 5, 1, 1]], "quoted": "Shay Segev"}, {"i": 176, "id": 7455499264111116288, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7455499264111116288/", "ts": "2026-04-30T06:12:21+00:00", "date": "2026-04-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro London 2026: Five Sessions, One Honest Conversation", "snippet": "🤖 SportsPro London 2026: five sessions that made the room sound honest", "chars": 2982, "text": "🤖 SportsPro London 2026: five sessions that made the room sound honest\n\nI moderated a panel on monetising fragmented audiences with Bobby Nightingale (Bath Rugby) and Stefan Lavén (Data Talks).\nBobby opened with the most honest line of the event. Bath runs ticketing, CRM, CDP, email, e-wallets, push, most not talking to each other. Bath has a data identification problem, not a growth one. Stefan argued for the plumbing. Bobby pushed back: plumbing matters once you have a clear value exchange.\nStefan's stat: first ROI comes from reactivating idle fans in the database. Bobby retargeted opted-outs. One signed shirt, one email, 42% back. On AI, Bobby says the value is in workflows.\nA plug for Retention Zone. Reactivation is underrated.\n\nNick Meacham (CEO, SportsPro) opened with eight industry dreams against eight realities. Media rights revenues are hyper-concentrated at the top. Investors are a release valve, not a saviour. Sport is not built for venture capital. Engagement is massive, the bucket is leaky. Fans do not like paying. AI delivers efficiency, not yet top-line growth. He warned us this would be a reality check. He did not hold any punch.\n\nMichael Payne with Samuel Agini (Financial Times) was fifty years of stories nobody had written down. Cortina was far more successful than anyone dared imagine. The TV product is in good shape. The organisation running the show is the issue. On Russia, he expects them back for LA28. Two predictions that stayed with me: within a decade the NFL makes a hostile takeover of World Rugby, and Volleyball World (where Payne now chairs after CVC's investment) has overtaken football as Japan's number two sport.\nA call for Olympic athletes' media rights.\nRead the book. He is the Michael Wolff of sport business.\n\nBengu Atamer (BuzzMyVideos) made the case that YouTube has become TV. 1.1 billion watch hours. Fastest-growing segment is over 65. Four-asset framework: always-on, searchable, archive, exclusivity. Each one is a sponsorship product. Her FIFA-YouTube World Cup breakdown was the clearest of the day. Three layers: ten minutes of live rights to creators, archive vault opened up, co-livestreams. F1 is sitting on a double-digit million sponsorship package on YouTube and giving it away free. Her question: how much money are you leaving on the table.\n\nClaudio Demmer (VfL Wolfsburg) presented the most operationally honest AI case study of the day. 91 percent weekly active users. 100 active GPTs. Five service companies replaced. Six-figure productivity gain.\nThe framing made me uncomfortable. Blind excitement and low empathy. A recipe for disaster.\n\n🤖 The takeaway. The hype cycle is exhausting itself. What is left is harder, slower, more rewarding. Stop pitching the Northern Star. Pick the lowest hanging fruit, prove the ROI, let the next use case earn the right to exist.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2450, "rx": 45, "cm": 6, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Bobby Nightingale", "person"], ["Bath Rugby", "company"], ["Stefan Lavén", "person"], ["Data Talks", "company"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Michael Payne", "person"], ["Samuel Agini", "person"], ["Volleyball World", "company"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Claudio Demmer", "person"], ["VfL Wolfsburg", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2449, 45, 6, 4], ["2026-10-17", 2450, 45, 6, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 177, "id": 7455146751704924160, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7455146751704924160/", "ts": "2026-04-29T06:51:35+00:00", "date": "2026-04-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "YouTube TV’s Custom Multiview, and the Pro-YouTube Echo Chamber Around It", "snippet": "📺 📲 YouTube TV's custom multiview, the architecture question, and the pro-YouTube echo chamber around it (an all-things YouTube post)", "chars": 2970, "text": "📺 📲 YouTube TV's custom multiview, the architecture question, and the pro-YouTube echo chamber around it (an all-things YouTube post)\n\nYouTube TV switched on fully customisable multiview this week. Four live streams, mix and match across packages including NFL Sunday Ticket. The user value is real. Multiple games on Sunday afternoon, RSN paired with a national broadcast, the sports bar pattern reproduced in a single subscription. That is the win.\n\nDan Rayburn flagged the limitations cleanly within an hour of the announcement: live only, family-friendly exclusions, less than 5% of older devices on a reduced UX, no computer support. The computer exclusion is the most telling. This is a living-room and mobile product.\n\nThe architecture question is where this gets interesting. My assumption is server-side compositing, with YouTube doing the work on its own infrastructure and delivering a single unified feed. I may be wrong. YouTube's 2023 launch post stated they \"moved the processing requirements to happen on YouTube's servers.\" Jan Ozer's December 2025 analysis at Streaming Learning Center confirms the architecture and gives the most rigorous breakdown of why it matters. He contrasts YouTube TV's server-side approach with FuboTV's client-side Apple TV implementation that reached only around 13% of their base, and Formula 1 TV's client-side build-your-own with similar availability gaps. Server-side is the only way this scales across older Chromecasts, smart TVs, mobile, and streaming sticks. Client-side multiview is a graveyard.\n\nI have spent years building advanced video experiences and adoption is always the blocker. The capability has usually been there. Working with Dolby OptiView on advanced live experiences, this launch reads as a useful data point on where the compute should live in your stack. YouTube made a deliberate architectural choice. Other operators thinking about advanced video features should ask the same question of their own systems.\n\nRichard Gillis raised a fair question this week about the volume of pro-YouTube content in B2B sports media right now. The launch is real. The architecture is right. The discourse around YouTube is also currently louder than the actual rights distribution map suggests it should be. Holding both at once is the job.\nToday at SportsPro London I am meeting BuzzMyVideos. Bengu Atamer's work on the \"Event Trap\" and the comparison of Roblox and YouTube data on creator-led versus broadcast event approaches is the kind of analysis the industry needs more of. Their YouTube stats work has actually inspired a project I am building in the YouTube analytics space, still in stealth. The questions are better because of this week's launch.\n\nFull piece here below 👇\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n----- Check out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 2055, "rx": 25, "cm": 12, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dan Rayburn", "person"], ["Jan Ozer", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Richard Gillis", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2055, 25, 12, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2055, 25, 12, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 178, "id": 7454851447743221760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7454851447743221760/", "ts": "2026-04-28T11:18:09+00:00", "date": "2026-04-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About SportsPro London 2026. I Will Be There!", "snippet": "Flying to London for SportsPro - see you there!", "chars": 47, "text": "Flying to London for SportsPro - see you there!", "qtext": "", "imp": 218, "rx": 2, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 218, 2, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 218, 2, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 179, "id": 7452991759153844224, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7452991759153844224/", "ts": "2026-04-23T08:08:25+00:00", "date": "2026-04-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Love Letter to Heads of IT", "snippet": "⚙️ 🩷 A love letter to heads of IT", "chars": 2212, "text": "⚙️ 🩷 A love letter to heads of IT\n\nDear Head of IT,\n\nYou were already the person who says no to everything for good reasons. Security, compliance, data protection, vendor sprawl, that one time somebody wanted Dropbox on the corporate network. You usually get blamed for slowing things down and rarely thanked when nothing catches fire.\n\nThis month your job changed again.\n\nBlocking does not work anymore. Approval queues do not work anymore. Lovable, Replit Agent, v0, Bolt, Claude Code, Cursor. Every smart non-developer with a weekend and a credit card can now ship a working full-stack app. Internal dashboards, microsites, scrapers, small CRM replacements. And your CEO just sent the 6am email asking why the company is \"not using AI enough.\"\n\nA true story from the early 2000s that explains exactly where we are. I was in a meeting with the president of a large European organisation. He turned to the room and asked why the company should spend several million on a new website when his son had built one in a weekend on WordPress for a thousand euros. The guy was fired a week later for unrelated reasons. The question never was. Twenty years on, swap WordPress for Lovable, swap the son for a product marketer with Claude Code, swap the weekend for an afternoon. That question is now in every executive's head.\n\nMy take: stop being the last line of defence. Start being the first line of enablement. Sanction two or three build-your-own-app tools officially with SSO and audit logs. Build a fifteen-minute review conversation, not a six-week approval form. Own the AI and infrastructure budget before every department starts paying for it on personal cards.\n\nThe smart people in the company are building whether you bless it or not. Your job is to make sure they build well.\n\nFull article with an appendix on Lovable, Replit, v0, Bolt, Claude Code, Cursor, and what each one lets people actually create, below 👇\n\nUntangling AI Vincenzo Lagattolla Donald Res Paul Boustead #ai\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 767, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["Vincenzo Lagattolla", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 767, 9, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 767, 9, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 180, "id": 7452633497669705728, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7452633497669705728/", "ts": "2026-04-22T08:24:49+00:00", "date": "2026-04-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Love Letter to Corporate Design Leads", "snippet": "👩‍🎨 A love letter to corporate design leads", "chars": 2069, "text": "👩‍🎨 A love letter to corporate design leads\n\nDear Head of Design,\nYou were already at the mercy of everybody's taste. The CEO has opinions. The CMO has references. The founder's partner once worked in fashion. You manage to ship a coherent system anyway. You deserve a medal. You usually get \"can we try it in blue.\"\nThis month your job changed (again).\n\nClaude Design went live on April 17. ChatGPT Images 2.0 shipped on April 21. Four days apart. Both sit inside tools your colleagues already pay for. Every single person in your company can now generate a pitch deck, a landing page prototype, a one pager, an infographic, a UI mockup. No Figma license. No design review. A prompt box and some confidence.\nThe good part is real. Your team can stop spending afternoons on quick internal banners and start spending them on the three decisions per quarter that actually shape the product.\nThe bad part is the physics problem. When everyone can produce, everyone produces. Monday morning the CEO forwards you a deck generated overnight with a version of your logo nobody has ever seen before, and asks if you can \"polish it a bit\" before the board meeting at noon.\n\nMy take: the design leads who win the next eighteen months are the ones who stop fighting the proliferation and start shaping it. Claim the design system conversation loudly. Define what the tools should not be used for. Move from gatekeeper to editor in chief.\nThe tools are here. The colleagues are excited. The output is coming whether you bless it or not. Pick up the pen.\n\nFull article with an appendix on what Claude Design and ChatGPT Images 2.0 actually let people create, plus a specific section on what this means for sports and media organisations, below 👇\n\nRich Mills Charlie DeMarco Dani Markovits Matteo Barbero Helen Arvanitopoulos Jennifer Crakow Edo van Dijk\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1390, "rx": 19, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Rich Mills", "person"], ["Charlie DeMarco", "person"], ["Dani Markovits", "person"], ["Matteo Barbero", "person"], ["Helen Arvanitopoulos", "person"], ["Jennifer Crakow", "person"], ["Edo van Dijk", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1390, 19, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1390, 19, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 181, "id": 7452253717933961216, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7452253717933961216/", "ts": "2026-04-21T07:15:42+00:00", "date": "2026-04-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About SportsPro London 2026. I Will Be There!", "snippet": "I will be at SportsPro London April 29 and 30, on stage on Day 1", "chars": 496, "text": "I will be at SportsPro London April 29 and 30, on stage on Day 1\nPANEL ALERT: 🎙️ From unknown to unified: How sports organisations turn fragmented audiences into revenue\n\nBobby Nightingale - Head of Fan Growth & Content – Bath Rugby\nStefan Lavén - CEO & Founder - Data Talks\nAadil Mukhtar - Head of Marketing - Growth & Technology - United Rugby Championship\n\nSee you there!\nPaola Marinone Bengu Atamer Nick Meacham William Jones Chris Stone Michael Payne A guy with a scarf 10 Things To Consider", "qtext": "", "imp": 3296, "rx": 46, "cm": 9, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Bobby Nightingale", "person"], ["Bath Rugby", "company"], ["Stefan Lavén", "person"], ["Data Talks", "company"], ["Aadil Mukhtar", "person"], ["United Rugby Championship", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["William Jones", "person"], ["Chris Stone", "person"], ["Michael Payne", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["10 Things To Consider", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3296, 46, 9, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3296, 46, 9, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 182, "id": 7451876355828498432, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7451876355828498432/", "ts": "2026-04-20T06:16:12+00:00", "date": "2026-04-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the Stanford AI Index Report 2026", "snippet": "🤖 10 things to consider About the Stanford AI Index Report 2026", "chars": 2126, "text": "🤖 10 things to consider About the Stanford AI Index Report 2026\n\nPart of my Untangling AI series.\nStanford University HAI released the ninth edition of the AI Index. 423 pages. The most comprehensive piece of independent measurement we have on the field.\n\n🚀 1. AI capability is accelerating. The plateau argument is finished. SWE-bench Verified went from 60% to nearly 100% in a single year.\n🌏 2. The U.S. and China are running neck and neck. The best U.S. model leads by 2.7% as of March 2026.\n✈️ 3. The US talent pipeline is collapsing. Researchers moving to the US dropped 89% since 2017, 80% in the last year alone. AI skills are growing fastest in the UAE, Chile, and South Africa.\n🧩 4. The jagged frontier is the most useful concept in the report. Maths olympiad gold, yet 50.1% on reading analog clocks. Robots succeed at 12% of household tasks.\n⚡ 5. Adoption hit 53% population-level in three years. Faster than the PC or the internet. Organisational adoption at 88%.\n🤖 6. Agent deployment is still in the single digits. The noise is enormous. The installed base is tiny.\n📉 7. Productivity gains are real and already moving the labour market for the young. US developers aged 22 to 25 dropped nearly 20% from 2024.\n🛡️ 8. Safety measurement is visibly failing. AI incidents rose from 233 to 362. Independent evaluation belongs in the contract.\n🏛️ 9. AI sovereignty is the new organising principle of national policy. You are building for a policy map that fragments every quarter.\n🤝 10. Experts and the public are 50 points apart on AI's impact on jobs. A technology in motion.\n\nMy position after reading all 423 pages: stop waiting for the dust to settle. It will not. The companies that win the next five years are the ones making bets now, with imperfect information, while keeping a short diary of what they got wrong so they can correct quickly.\n\nFull article and source links below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 597, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["Stanford University", "school"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider", "Untangling AI"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 597, 11, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 597, 11, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 183, "id": 7450843865818742784, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7450843865818742784/", "ts": "2026-04-17T09:53:27+00:00", "date": "2026-04-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider about the PwC Emerging Sports Report March 2026", "snippet": "🎾📊 10 things to consider about the PwC Emerging Sports Report March 2026", "chars": 2177, "text": "🎾📊 10 things to consider about the PwC Emerging Sports Report March 2026\n\nWhen fans, federations and capital finally agree on the direction\n\nPwC's Project Bounce report (March 2026), commissioned by Iris Advisors, uses Tie Break Tens as its case study. The broader read matters more. Short-form sports formats have moved from side project to investment thesis. Fans, rightsholders, and investors are pulling in the same direction for the first time in a decade.\n\nThe numbers I would screenshot before a board meeting 👇\n\n1️⃣ The average age of major sport fans in the US sits at 47. Golf 51, MLB 48, tennis 47, NHL and NFL 46, NBA 43.\n2️⃣ Current under-45s will be 85% of the global population by 2045. Today they are 68%.\n3️⃣ Media supply has decoupled from time available to watch it. The competitive set is no longer other sports.\n4️⃣ Younger fans are not disengaging from sport. They are curating harder. 18-34s over-index +36 points on social follow.\n5️⃣ Short-form and community-driven content is where younger fans live. 53% of 18-34s consume sports on TikTok and Instagram.\n6️⃣ Emerging markets are pulling the innovation curve. Saudi 54%, Qatar 50%, UAE 47% openness to new formats. Global average sits at 24%.\n7️⃣ Legacy properties are moving. MLB pitch clock delivered +9% attendance, the biggest one-year gain in 30 years.\n8️⃣ The bigger bet is new formats endorsed by governing bodies. FIBA 3x3, The Hundred, Unrivaled, Tie Break Tens.\n9️⃣ Investors are voting with their allocations. 55% expect priority for emerging assets, 38% for established.\n🔟 Racquet sports are the clearest live case study. +55% participation growth globally 2020-2025, tennis media rights at a 6% CAGR.\n\nThe short-form window in sports is open right now. The people who moved first on streaming in 2008, social in 2014, and OTT rights in 2019 set the rules for everyone else. Same pattern. Same stakes.\n\nFull article with sources and glossary in the comments 🔗\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1566, "rx": 19, "cm": 3, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1566, 19, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1566, 19, 3, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 184, "id": 7450453361541193728, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7450453361541193728/", "ts": "2026-04-16T08:01:44+00:00", "date": "2026-04-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "StreamOn Debrief: Beyond the Broadcast — What the Ligue 1+ Story Actually Tells Us", "snippet": "Last Tuesday I moderated the StreamOn live webinar, presented by Dolby OptiView, with Cyprien Castanedo from LFP Media and Paul Boustead from Dolby OptiView.", "chars": 1184, "text": "Last Tuesday I moderated the StreamOn live webinar, presented by Dolby OptiView, with Cyprien Castanedo from LFP Media and Paul Boustead from Dolby OptiView.\n38 minutes. No slides. No prepared answers.\n\nWe covered:\n\n📡 What the Ligue 1+ hybrid model actually looks like and why the OTT is the innovation lab\n🎬 Whether the video experience is a commodity (it is not, and Paul explains exactly why)\n📺 The 4K paradox: 95% of subscribers could not receive it, and they still demanded it at launch\n🕹️ Why interactivity adoption is an organisational problem, not a product problem\n📊 What happens when a rights holder owns fan data for the first time in 20 years\n💰 How personalised ad delivery drove 60%+ revenue increases for Dolby OptiView clients\n\nThe full debrief is on the newsletter below 👇\nThe VOD is here if you want to watch the full session.\nhttps://lnkd.in/d6AyD7Dp\n\nLFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel Matthias Ankli Michelle Gong Tamara Orjonikidze\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1390, "rx": 26, "cm": 2, "rp": 6, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel", "company"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1387, 26, 2, 6], ["2026-10-17", 1390, 26, 2, 6]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 185, "id": 7449358695018340352, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7449358695018340352/", "ts": "2026-04-13T07:31:55+00:00", "date": "2026-04-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 📲 🇮🇹 POV: You are in Italy preparing a live webinar from a bakery in Torino (Vanchiglietta neighbourhood - shaped as little Manhattan on t", "snippet": "📺 📲 🇮🇹 POV: You are in Italy preparing a live webinar from a bakery in Torino (Vanchiglietta neighbourhood - shaped as little Manhattan on the map) and you cann", "chars": 695, "text": "📺 📲 🇮🇹 POV: You are in Italy preparing a live webinar from a bakery in Torino (Vanchiglietta neighbourhood - shaped as little Manhattan on the map) and you cannot resist to shoot a promo photo for the webinar with Ligue1 and Dolby OptiView on top of a festival of focacce and pizze, sparing the other side with croissant and pastry...\nI know I am making someone jealous on a Monday morning (rainy here after a week of unreal 27 degrees weather). That was intentional.\nJoin me tomorrow at 9am CET - (that early yes, Paul is down under), recording from home. I could not clear the right with the bakery to do it from here. That could another idea for a new format.\n\nA guy with a scarf goes #foodie", "qtext": "", "imp": 681, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["foodie"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 678, 12, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 681, 12, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 186, "id": 7448295681771085824, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7448295681771085824/", "ts": "2026-04-10T09:07:53+00:00", "date": "2026-04-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About NAB 2026", "snippet": "🎯 10 things to consider About NAB Show 2026", "chars": 2973, "text": "🎯 10 things to consider About NAB Show 2026\n\nI am not going to be at NAB this year. But I always pay attention to what happens in Las Vegas in April. NAB is a checkpoint for our industry. This year it lands at a complicated geopolitical moment, and it is also a checkpoint for AI deployment. If you are going to Las Vegas with a plan for a plan, stay home. It is time to call BS on anything that is not a serious, honest attempt to embed AI into how this industry operates.\n\n10 things to consider:\n\n1️⃣ The Sports Summit expanded to four days and opened to everyone. Jon Miller from NBC Sports headlines after NBC's February run of the Milan Cortina Olympics, Super Bowl LX, and the NBA All-Star Game.\n\n2️⃣ Dan Rayburn'S Streaming Summit put JioHotStar's 72 million concurrent viewers on the main stage. That number reframes what streaming at scale means for live sports.\n\n3️⃣ NAB added a second AI Pavilion to the show floor. The gap between companies doing the work and companies performing the work is about to become visible.\n\n4️⃣ AWS built a basketball court in the West Hall Lobby with Maple Leaf Sports & Entertainment. Computer vision, real-time data, live performance insights.\n\n5️⃣ Creator economy registration numbers jumped 200% over 2025. Social media content producers up 150%. The Creator Lab expanded in the newly completed Central Hall.\n\n6️⃣ The sports business side of NAB is becoming a destination. Leagues, franchise operators, athlete-led media companies, sports investment firms. The four-day Sports Summit open to all attendees is the structural move that makes this real.\n\n7️⃣ Sports betting got its own session. \"Sports Betting and the New Fan Economy\" examines wagering, data rights, and regulation.\n\n8️⃣ The globalization conversation is on the agenda, and the timing could not be more loaded. How do you build fans beyond borders when borders are getting harder?\n\n9️⃣ Women's sports got a dedicated session track. \"Women's Sports at an Inflection Point\" moved the conversation from a panel mention to a strategic discussion about rights economics and distribution.\n\n🔟 The show itself got a physical upgrade. Las Vegas Convention Center renovations complete.\n\n👀 My clients at NAB Show 2026:\n\n🔵 Dolby OptiView (booth W2223) is showcasing ad engine improvements, hybrid dynamic ad insertion (SSAI and SGAI), enhanced live-to-VOD workflows, and Sports Intelligence for personalized fan experiences. Paul Boustead speaks Monday at the Streaming Summit on personalized fan experiences. Giles Baker joins the Sports Theater on Tuesday for \"The Fan Experience Reimagined.\"\n\n🟢 Cleeng (booth W2918) is bringing agentic AI capabilities to subscriber management, moving from insights to action on churn prevention and offer configuration. Their Merchant of Record solution ties payment infrastructure directly into retention workflows. Platforms like ATP Media, TOD (beIN), and Sinclair's Tennis Channel are already on the platform.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1697, "rx": 14, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["NAB Show", "company"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Dan Rayburn", "person"], ["NAB Show", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Giles Baker", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1697, 14, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1697, 14, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 187, "id": 7447950737185161217, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7447950737185161217/", "ts": "2026-04-09T10:17:11+00:00", "date": "2026-04-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Live Sports, Live Conversation. Stream On Webinar April 14 at 9am CET", "snippet": "⚽ 📺 📲 Live webinar for live sport!", "chars": 967, "text": "⚽ 📺 📲 Live webinar for live sport!\n📣 Don’t miss the upcoming StreamOn live sports session!\n\nJoin me with industry leaders Cyprien Castanedo (Ligue1) and Paul Boustead (Dolby OptiView), as they explore the game-changing innovations shaping the future of ⚽ live sports.\n\n🧠 Cyprien is the tech brain behind the recent league DTC streaming.\n🤖 Paul is the product mind of the Dolby video efforts.\n\nWe will go beyond our own experiences and current operating models and discuss our ambitions in the video experience space.\n\nDiscover how technology, fan engagement, and new business strategies are revolutionizing the delivery and monetization of live sports.\n\nKey topics include overcoming legacy barriers, meeting the demands of today’s audiences, and creating sports experiences that drive deeper engagement and unlock new growth opportunities.\n\nSave the date: \n🗓️ Tuesday, April 14th\n⏰ 9 AM CET\n🔗 https://lnkd.in/ds6vqdac\n\nMichelle Gong Matthias Ankli A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1247, "rx": 24, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1247, 24, 1, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1247, 24, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 188, "id": 7446076818681446400, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7446076818681446400/", "ts": "2026-04-04T06:10:54+00:00", "date": "2026-04-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider on the DAZN Creators Playmakers Programme", "snippet": "🔟 📲 10 things to consider on the DAZN Playmakers Programme", "chars": 2764, "text": "🔟 📲 10 things to consider on the DAZN Playmakers Programme\n\nWhen this landed on April 1st, 😎 I checked the date twice. A global creator programme from DAZN? Sounded like a prank. But the more I read, the more it connected to everything DAZN has been building over the past year. So I dug in.\n\nHere are my 😎 10 things to consider:\n\n1️⃣ This already happened once, and it worked. The Club World Cup creator programme last summer reached 2.7 billion viewers. 80% engaged with non-live content. Playmakers makes it permanent.\n\n2️⃣ The CMO is an e-commerce guy. Adam Knappy spent 14 years at THG. His career is conversion funnels and customer acquisition. That tells you how this programme will be measured.\n\n3️⃣ DAZN already owns a creator company. Team Whistle, acquired in 2023, gives Playmakers an in-house production and distribution engine. Real advantage.\n\n4️⃣ The timing is deliberate. FIFA World Cup this summer. FIFA+ relaunching on DAZN. Bundesliga, Serie A, Champions League rights across markets. The content canvas is massive.\n\n5️⃣ The creator value proposition is vague. Platform access and affiliate links are table stakes. Merch and event access are nice. They don't pay rent. The \"and more\" is doing a lot of work in that sentence.\n\n6️⃣ The real test is editorial freedom. 83% trust stat is industry boilerplate. Sports fans smell managed content immediately. If creators feel corporate, they lose credibility fast.\n\n7️⃣ The Bundesliga model is the real benchmark. Live Friday-night match rights to YouTube creators. CazéTV holding FIFA World Cup rights in Brazil. Those are rights deals. Playmakers is social content and event access. Different category.\n\n8️⃣ \"Every sport\" is probably too ambitious at launch. Football creators are abundant. Other sports have thin pools. The Club World Cup programme worked because it was focused. One sport. One event. One moment.\n\n9️⃣ The playbook looks familiar. If you watch camera or music gear content on YouTube, you've seen this model. Sony drops a new body, every channel has a video up within days. AKAI launches the MPC Sample, same wave. Coordinated seeding that looks organic. DAZN may be building the sports version of this.\n\n🔟 Count the layers DAZN is stacking. Live rights. Whistle. DAZN+. FIFA+. FanZone. Matchroom through 2031. And now Playmakers. That is a lot of moving parts. Creators go where the brand channel cannot. The question is whether the org chart can deliver all of this at the same time.\n\nFull article and appendix with 14 comparable programmes below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1808, "rx": 14, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Adam Knappy", "person"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["CazéTV", "company"], ["Sony", "company"], ["AKAI", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1807, 14, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1808, 14, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 189, "id": 7445782824256827392, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7445782824256827392/", "ts": "2026-04-03T10:42:41+00:00", "date": "2026-04-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🚫 Want a bad idea for this weekend?", "snippet": "🚫 Want a bad idea for this weekend?", "chars": 454, "text": "🚫 Want a bad idea for this weekend?\n🤖 If you want to be excessively bombarded with content about Sports, Media, Tech, Fans, AI, and pseudo-leadership thoughts you should definitively subscribe to A guy with a scarf newsletter on LinkedIn!\n👉 https://lnkd.in/ddmvMF-Q\n\nWeird funny photo without a scarf... for once, with 3 french icons.\nCompletely out-of-brand.\nNeed some weirderies (neologism?!) to intellectually survive this period...\n\nThe One Man Brand", "qtext": "", "imp": 1079, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["LinkedIn", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1079, 12, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1079, 12, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 190, "id": 7444658833907539969, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7444658833907539969/", "ts": "2026-03-31T08:16:21+00:00", "date": "2026-03-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 80: Pier Luigi Spagna (WBD) – Retention as an Engine for Profitable Growth", "snippet": "Retention Zone Ep. 80: Pier Luigi Spagna (WBD) – Retention as an Engine for Profitable Growth", "chars": 2440, "text": "Retention Zone Ep. 80: Pier Luigi Spagna (WBD) – Retention as an Engine for Profitable Growth\nHow Warner Bros. Discovery Thinks About Churn, Sport, and Scale\n\nMy guest is Pier Luigi Spagna, Vice President of EMEA Retention & Engagement at Warner Bros. Discovery. A fellow Italian with nearly 20 years in DTC marketing across betting, food delivery, startups, and streaming. I found him deeply invested in the craft of retention. The kind of person who has seen enough industries to know what actually works and what is just noise.\n\nAnd yes we do gesticulate a bit and have quite some energy. That's why you want Italians in your key positions 😎 🇮🇹\n\nA few things that stayed with me from this conversation:\n⚙️ Retention is an engine for profitable growth. Pull the acquisition blanket too far on one side and retention suffers. Pull it the other way and you are not growing fast enough. The balance is the job.\n📊 At WBD, churn is a company-wide KPI. When the CEO calls it out, everyone aligns. Pier Luigi's team owns that number. They recently moved from marketing into the product growth organization, which gives them direct influence on what subscribers see inside the platform.\n⚽ Sport is a fascinating retention challenge. Expensive to acquire, hard to make profitable, easy to churn from. The trick is dual viewership: getting sport subscribers hooked on entertainment content while they are watching their event. The retention rate after a major sport event on HBO Max is significantly higher than it was on Discovery Plus, which was already higher than Eurosport. Content scheduling is a retention lever.\n🤖 AI helps with scale and optimization. The strategy still comes from humans. \"I don't think it's gonna entirely replace us. I think it's gonna allow us to do what we do in a better way that we couldn't possibly get otherwise.\"\n🌍 Running 40+ European markets plus APAC means global strategy and local nuances. Machine learning provides the backbone. The local adaptation is still manual. Nobody has cracked that at scale yet.\n\nThis is a must watch episode for anyone working in subscription, streaming, or fan engagement.\n\nBy the way well deserved mention for our common friend Andrea Fanfani\n\nGilles Domartini Alexis Gaï Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Adrianna Panek\n\n-----\n🎧Retention Zone – Episode 80\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1970, "rx": 40, "cm": 7, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Pier Luigi Spagna", "person"], ["Warner Bros. Discovery", "company"], ["Pier Luigi Spagna", "person"], ["Warner Bros. Discovery", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Adrianna Panek", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1970, 40, 7, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1970, 40, 7, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 191, "id": 7444288519197315072, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7444288519197315072/", "ts": "2026-03-30T07:44:51+00:00", "date": "2026-03-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider When Reading the Global SportsTech Ecosystem Report 2026", "snippet": "⚽ 🏈 🏌‍♂️ 📰 10 things to consider", "chars": 1416, "text": "⚽ 🏈 🏌‍♂️ 📰 10 things to consider\nThe Global SportsTech Ecosystem Report 2026 from SportsTechX and PEAK | SportsTech Conference is out. Investment data, M&A tables, ecosystem mapping, startup tracking. Heavy on numbers. I read it and turned it into one of my 10 things to consider lists.\n\n1️⃣ $8.8B in 2025 is a recovery number, and it changes the conversation\n2️⃣ Europe overtaking North America in 2025 funding is the most structurally interesting data point in the report\n3️⃣ The average deal value jump from $28.1M to $43.6M tells you the market is maturing\n4️⃣ 87% of acquisitions made by SportsTech companies is the most underappreciated number in the report\n5️⃣ The M&A data tells a cyclical story that matters for anyone timing their strategy\n6️⃣ The \"For Fans\" sector dominates investment, and the gap is widening\n7️⃣ The DTC trend section confirms what several recent deals have already shown\n8️⃣ The NBA Europe section is the most forward-looking content in the report\n9️⃣ The ecosystem map reveals how unevenly the industry's support structure is distributed\n🔟 The PE deep-dive is where the report touches the question it cannot fully answer\n\nFull piece below 👇\n\nRohn Malhotra\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1926, "rx": 23, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["SportsTechX", "company"], ["PEAK | SportsTech Conference", "company"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Rohn Malhotra", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1926, 23, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1926, 23, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 192, "id": 7443983161740599297, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7443983161740599297/", "ts": "2026-03-29T11:31:28+00:00", "date": "2026-03-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A guy with a scarf is now also on Instagram.", "snippet": "A guy with a scarf is now also on Instagram.", "chars": 445, "text": "A guy with a scarf is now also on Instagram.\nWhy? I wanted to have a channel where I could also share some more informal stuff at times which do not fit with Linkedin or Substack.\nExpect the following:\n- a insta-version of articles and posts\n- a daily \"thing\" extracted from my book \"10 things to consider\" (that's a cool one)\n- quick stories on what's happening in our industry that gets my quick attention\n\nFollow: @aguywithascarf on instagram", "qtext": "", "imp": 292, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Instagram", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 292, 6, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 292, 6, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 193, "id": 7443194997681676288, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7443194997681676288/", "ts": "2026-03-27T07:19:35+00:00", "date": "2026-03-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📣 Don’t miss the upcoming #StreamOn live sports session!", "snippet": "📣 Don’t miss the upcoming #StreamOn live sports session!", "chars": 1074, "text": "📣 Don’t miss the upcoming #StreamOn live sports session!\n\nJoin me with industry leaders Cyprien Castanedo (Ligue1) and Paul Boustead (Dolby OptiView), as they explore the game-changing innovations shaping the future of ⚽ live sports.\n\n🧠  Cyprien is the tech brain behind the recent league DTC streaming.\n🤖 Paul is the product mind of the Dolby video efforts.\n\nWe will go beyond our own experiences and current operating models and discuss our ambitions in the video experience space.\n\nDiscover how technology, fan engagement, and new business strategies are revolutionizing the delivery and monetization of live sports.\n\nKey topics include overcoming legacy barriers, meeting the demands of today’s audiences, and creating sports experiences that drive deeper engagement and unlock new growth opportunities.\n\n⚽ 📺 📲 Live webinar for live sport!\n\nSession details:\n🗓️ Thursday, April 9th\n⏰ 9 AM CET (a great breakfast companion)\n🔗 RSVP: https://lnkd.in/ds6vqdac\n\nMichelle Gong Matthias Ankli A guy with a scarf\n#streaming #monetization #livesports #fanengagement #futureofsport", "qtext": "", "imp": 686, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Ligue1", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Michelle Gong", "person"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["streamon", "streaming", "monetization", "livesports", "fanengagement", "futureofsport"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 686, 12, 0, 4], ["2026-10-17", 686, 12, 0, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 194, "id": 7442870573761785856, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7442870573761785856/", "ts": "2026-03-26T09:50:26+00:00", "date": "2026-03-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Just discovered: Non-League Day Turns 15", "snippet": "⚽ 🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿 Just discovered: Non-League Day Turns 15", "chars": 2862, "text": "⚽ 🏴󠁧󠁢󠁥󠁮󠁧󠁿 Just discovered: Non-League Day Turns 15\nHow a social media experiment became English football's most effective grassroots initiative\n\n⚫⚪ To be honest, until a few days ago, I had never heard of Non-League Day. I came across it through a Juventus Football Club post. The club announced a collaboration with three English non-league sides that play in black and white: Maidenhead United Football Club , Chorley FC, and Barnton FC. Signed shirts sent from Turin. Barnton, nicknamed \"Cheshire Juve,\" has fans travelling between Cheshire and the Allianz Stadium. I'm based in Turin. I'm a Torino FC fan, so Juventus news lands on my radar whether I want it to or not. This one caught my eye.\n\n🏟️ Non-League Day: every year during an international break, fans across England are encouraged to visit their local non-league club. Pay a few quid. Have a pint. Stand close enough to hear the centre-back organising the line. Started in 2010 by QPR fan James Doe. This Saturday is the 15th edition. Nearly 1,000 clubs taking part.\n\n📊 Last year: 149,060 spectators across Steps 1-4. 12% attendance increase. A LiveScore survey this week found 55% of professional club fans are open to attending a non-league match. 4 in 10 cannot name their local non-league club. Discovery problem.\n\n🌍 Juventus is the second major European club to engage. Athletic Bilbao launched Euskal Non-League Day in 2023 through its Foundation. Now in its fourth edition. Close to 170 affiliated Basque clubs. The only initiative of its kind in LaLiga.\n\n💡 Here's what surprises me. The English football pyramid is one of the most extraordinary structures in global sport. Thousands of clubs connected through promotion and relegation, from the Premier League to village football. And the top brands barely use it. The pyramid is a readymade activation platform: local reach, authentic community engagement, massive geographic spread, year-round fixtures. The kind of thing a brand strategist would design from scratch if it didn't already exist. Yet most sponsors concentrate at the top two or three tiers and ignore everything below. The pyramid is sitting there. Open for business. The industry keeps walking past it.\n\n⚽ My son plays youth football in Turin. I wrote a children's book about this, Calcio Ribelle, where a group of kids invent their own version of football built on fair play, creativity, and inclusion. Watching what Non-League Day represents, the spirit is the same. Football as a community activity, run by people who care about the game for reasons that have nothing to do with broadcast rights or sponsorship decks.\n\nFull article below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1641, "rx": 19, "cm": 15, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Juventus Football Club", "company"], ["Maidenhead United Football Club", "company"], ["Barnton FC", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1638, 19, 15, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1641, 19, 15, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 195, "id": 7442708025636798464, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7442708025636798464/", "ts": "2026-03-25T23:04:32+00:00", "date": "2026-03-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤔 Is perfectionism a strength?", "snippet": "🤔 Is perfectionism a strength?", "chars": 2899, "text": "🤔 Is perfectionism a strength?\nPerfectionism can be a strength but it can also slow momentum, drain teams, and delay impact.\n❓ How do you decide when \"good enough\" is better than \"perfect\", for yourself or your team?\nMy friends at the #LinkedInNewsUK team asked me to elaborate on this.\n\n😎 My honest take: perfectionism is almost never a strength. What people call \"perfectionism as a strength\" is usually just high standards combined with good taste. Actual perfectionism, in the product space, is a misallocation of resources toward an abstraction. Perfection doesn't exist as a deliverable. You can't ship it, you can't measure it, and you can't iterate on it.\n\nWhat you can do is right-size your effort to the moment, the user, and the stage of the product.\n\nRight-sizing means asking: what does this feature, this release, this sprint actually need to deliver value right now? An MVP that reaches users and generates real feedback is worth more than a polished product that stays in staging for three extra months. The learning you get from contact with the market is irreplaceable. No amount of internal refinement substitutes for it.\n\n🤔 And here's the question I keep coming back to, personally: am I improving the work or just managing my anxiety? A lot of late-stage perfectionism is about fear of judgment, not about making something better. Once I started recognizing that pattern in myself, it became easier to let things go. In product terms, that translates into teams protecting their work from scrutiny, resisting feedback, delaying launches. The anxiety is the same, just distributed across a group.\n\nThis connects to a punk ethic for professional life, something I wrote about recently on A guy with a scarf. Punk (in music) was never about waiting until you had perfect technique or the right studio or the approval of gatekeepers. It was about having something to say and putting it out there. The energy and the intent mattered more than the polish. That attitude translates directly into product work: ship it, learn from it, make the next version better. The iteration is the craft.\n\nNone of this means lowering standards.\n\nRight-sizing is a discipline. It requires judgment about where to invest deeply (core architecture, data integrity, user trust) and where to stay lean (UI details in early stages, edge cases that affect 2% of users, internal tooling). That judgment is what separates experienced product teams from junior ones.\n\nPerfection is comfortable to chase because it postpones the vulnerable moment of putting something into the world. Right-sizing forces you to be honest about what matters now and what can wait. That takes more courage than endless polishing.\n\nI guess at my best I am a punk-attitude engine that aims to deliver the highest quality needed and at times surprise the client, the user, the audience with some unexpected gems.\n\nYessi Bello-Perez", "qtext": "", "imp": 1069, "rx": 14, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Yessi Bello-Perez", "person"]], "tags": ["linkedinnewsuk"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1069, 14, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1069, 14, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 196, "id": 7442495812003315712, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7442495812003315712/", "ts": "2026-03-25T09:01:16+00:00", "date": "2026-03-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the IPL Franchise Sales and the Impact on Sports Tech", "snippet": "🏏 10 things to consider about $3.4 Billion in IPL Franchise Sales", "chars": 2424, "text": "🏏 10 things to consider about $3.4 Billion in IPL Franchise Sales\n\n🏏 Two IPL franchises changed hands this week for a combined $3.4 billion. Royal Challengers Bangalore went to a consortium including Blackstone, David Blitzer, Aditya Birla Group, and the Times of India Group for $1.78 billion. Rajasthan Royals went to a US-led group featuring the Walmart and Ford families for $1.63 billion. My friend Vinit Mehta published a strong analysis on the sports-tech implications in his Mehtta Ventures Dubai newsletter, and I wanted to amplify it for this audience because these deals deserve attention beyond the cricket press.\n\nHere are the 10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ 🏏 The valuation multiples (16x and 25x on 2008 bids) reflect NFL-level scarcity with only 10 IPL teams\n2️⃣ 💰 Blackstone's first-ever sports investment landed in cricket, and David Blitzer added IPL to a portfolio spanning the NBA, NFL, NHL, MLB, EPL, and MLS\n3️⃣ 🌍 Cricket has entered the global sports asset class conversation for real. Western investors can no longer treat it as a regional story\n4️⃣ 📊 BCCI posted a ₹3,358 crore surplus in FY25 and is projecting ₹6,728 crore for FY26. The IPL media rights deal alone was worth $6.2 billion\n5️⃣ 🚀 Indian sports-tech startups like Stupa Sports Analytics and Str8bat are positioned to benefit directly as franchise owners invest in analytics, fan engagement, and performance tech\n6️⃣ 📺 The 2025 IPL final drew 169 million TV viewers. Any fan engagement technology proving itself in cricket has a market size argument that dwarfs most European football deployments\n7️⃣ 🔄 The 2027+ media rights cycle becomes the real accelerator, and these franchise sales give BCCI serious ammunition\n8️⃣ 🇺🇸 Cross-pollination between US sports ownership and cricket is accelerating through shared ownership networks. Vendor introductions follow ownership connections\n9️⃣ ⚠️ Risks are real: high multiples, regulatory shifts (the 2025 Online Gaming Act already reshaped the sponsorship landscape), and the usual hype cycle dangers\n🔟 🧭 If you work in sports media and technology, you need an India thesis. You also need to deeply understand the market before making any moves\n\nFull piece below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 777, "rx": 13, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Vinit Mehta", "person"], ["Mehtta Ventures Dubai", "company"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 777, 13, 2, 3], ["2026-10-17", 777, 13, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 197, "id": 7442128400183623680, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7442128400183623680/", "ts": "2026-03-24T08:41:18+00:00", "date": "2026-03-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "AI, Content, and the Future of Work: Three Pieces of Thinking That Won’t Leave My Head", "snippet": "🤖 AI, Content, and the Future of Work: Three Pieces of Thinking That Won't Leave My Head", "chars": 2848, "text": "🤖 AI, Content, and the Future of Work: Three Pieces of Thinking That Won't Leave My Head\n\nSports media and technology is what I do. Broadcasting, streaming, data, fan engagement. Every week I write about this industry for A Guy With A Scarf. I like my lane.\n\n🧠 But AI is reshaping things well beyond our corner. And lately, three very different pieces have been circling in my head. They come from macro-economics, strategic foresight, and creator economy. They all land on the same question: what happens when the relationship between AI, human work, content, and value gets renegotiated all at once?\n1️⃣📉 The Citrini Report (\"The 2028 Global Intelligence Crisis\") — a scenario analysis that went viral, rattled markets, and drew responses from Citadel Securities and the White House. It describes AI-driven white-collar displacement triggering a feedback loop with no brake. The doom scenario feels excessive to me. The cascading logic behind it does not.\n2️⃣🌀 Amy Webb's Convergence Outlook (SXSW 2026) — she retired her 18-year-old trend report and replaced it with a framework built on convergences: what happens when AI, labor, biotech, capital, and behavior all collide at the same time. The rebranding is partly positioning. The underlying idea, that we can no longer track these forces in isolation, is genuinely useful.\n3️⃣🎵 Jack Conte's keynote (SXSW 2026) — the Patreon co-founder placed AI in the long arc of creative destruction and named the current moment the \"slop phase.\" Then landed a sharp point: AI companies claim fair use on creators' work while signing multimillion-dollar licensing deals with Disney and Condé Nast. If it's legal to just use it, why pay anyone?\n\n⚽ My brain keeps dragging all of this into sports media territory. What does cascading displacement mean for the thousands of knowledge workers in broadcasting, production, and analytics? What does the convergence lens mean for how we think about AI tools, automated content, and data rights as one system? What does selective fair use mean for sports organizations sitting on massive content libraries?\n\nI don't have a finished take. I have a growing list of questions and I'd get further with other people in the room.\n📣 An open invitation:\n→ ✍️ Write something with me on A Guy With A Scarf\n→ 🎙️ Come on as a guest for an episode of A Guy With A Scarf\n→ 🔴 Join a public live session where we think through this in the open\nThe only pre-requisite is that you have read and watched all three content pieces 🙂\nDrop me a message or comment. The thinking is better when it's shared.\nCitrini Research Untangling AI Amy Webb Jack Conte\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 943, "rx": 14, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Citrini Research", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["Amy Webb", "person"], ["Jack Conte", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 943, 14, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 943, 14, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 198, "id": 7441847996096618496, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7441847996096618496/", "ts": "2026-03-23T14:07:05+00:00", "date": "2026-03-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the NFL Media Rights Renegotiation", "snippet": "🏈 📺 10 things to consider About the NFL Media Rights Renegotiation", "chars": 1736, "text": "🏈 📺 10 things to consider About the NFL Media Rights Renegotiation\nI started working with the NFL in 2016. What began as a professional engagement turned into something closer to a personal fascination. Their media rights structure is one of the most sophisticated constructions in global sports.\nRight now the league is renegotiating deals worth $10 billion a year, with a target closer to $18-20 billion. All before the 2026 season starts in September.\nI know this is a US-centric topic but I always felt that, even if that does not apply to other markets as-is and the NFL is so much bigger than anything else, there are lot of rationales to learn from there.\n\nHere are the 10 things to consider👇\n1️⃣ The NFL is renegotiating deals that don't expire for another seven years\n2️⃣ Paramount goes first because the NFL has specific leverage over them\n3️⃣ The cost-per-viewer-hour argument is the analytical engine of the process\n4️⃣ The ESPN equity deal is the structural foundation for everything else\n5️⃣ Four games fell out of the ESPN deal, and the NFL is already selling them\n6️⃣ The Paramount-WBD merger creates the largest sports rights portfolio in media history\n7️⃣ Fox and Versant are already signaling portfolio shifts\n8️⃣ The FCC is watching, and has opened a formal inquiry\n9️⃣ Streaming spend on sports rights will hit $14.2 billion this year\n🔟 The NFL's real target is $25 billion in total annual revenue by 2027\nFull article with sources below 👇\n\n#sportsbusiness #sportstech #sportsmedia #nfl\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1391, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportsbusiness", "sportstech", "sportsmedia", "nfl"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1390, 10, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1391, 10, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 199, "id": 7440720448658714624, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7440720448658714624/", "ts": "2026-03-20T11:26:36+00:00", "date": "2026-03-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Happy Friday 🙌 Here's something to help you close the laptop with a lighter head this weekend.", "snippet": "Happy Friday 🙌 Here's something to help you close the laptop with a lighter head this weekend.", "chars": 1207, "text": "Happy Friday 🙌 Here's something to help you close the laptop with a lighter head this weekend.\nIf you've ever posted something on LinkedIn, checked the impressions two hours later, and felt that little knot in your stomach... this table is for you 👇\n\nThe Shield Index analyzed 50,000 LinkedIn posts in February 2026 and broke down reach by follower bracket. The numbers are honest and grounding.\nIf you have, say, 5K to 10K followers and your post got 800 impressions, you're right at the median. That's normal. You're doing fine ✅\nIf it hit 2,000, you're in the top 25%. If it crossed 5,000, you're in the top 10%. And if somehow you landed above 48,000... welcome to the 1% club 🏆\nThe point is: most posts get modest reach. That's the reality of the platform for everyone, at every level. A post with 500 impressions in the 1-5K bracket is a solid post. Full stop.\n\nSo before you spend the weekend second-guessing your last publish, check where you actually land on this table. You might be doing better than you think 😉\nHave a good one ☀️\n📊 Source: TheShieldIndex.com, February 2026 (50,000 posts)\n\nCheck my previous post on the LinkedIn Algorithm...\n\nMake LinkedIn Better for Creators A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 800, "rx": 14, "cm": 11, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Make LinkedIn Better for Creators", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 800, 14, 11, 1], ["2026-10-17", 800, 14, 11, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 200, "id": 7440325519188959235, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7440325519188959235/", "ts": "2026-03-19T09:17:18+00:00", "date": "2026-03-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What a Former LinkedIn Insider Says About the Platform’s 2026 Algorithm, and What 279 Comments Tell Us About the Reaction", "snippet": "🤖 ⚙️ What a Former LinkedIn Insider Says About the 2026 Algorithm (and What 279 Comments Reveal)", "chars": 2894, "text": "🤖 ⚙️ What a Former LinkedIn Insider Says About the 2026 Algorithm (and What 279 Comments Reveal)\n\nI almost scrolled past it. A LinkedIn post about the LinkedIn algorithm is usually content I skip on principle. But this one had 279 comments, and Dani Markovits wrote it, he spent four years at LinkedIn, he knows how the platform works from the inside. So I stopped.\n\nI'm stubbornly committed to writing for people, not for algorithms. I get criticized for it regularly. But I work with clients in sports media and technology who ask me about these things, so understanding what's happening under the hood matters.\n\nI aggregated everything he wrote in the post and in his comment replies into a single view. The key points:\n\n→ Your profile is now a ranking signal. Headline, About section, career history are context tags the algorithm reads before distributing your content.\n\n→ Comments carry roughly 15x more weight than likes. A thoughtful comment means someone paused and engaged. A like barely registers.\n\n→ Dwell time and saves now heavily influence distribution. Someone saving your post signals lasting value.\n\n→ Content is matched to your demonstrated expertise. Consistency in a topic builds compounding visibility.\n\n→ External links won't kill a post if the content earns engagement first. Essential link: include it. Optional: put it in comments.\n\n→ Personal profiles have stronger organic reach than company pages because they're built on trust signals.\n\nMy take: Markovits brings insider credibility and clear communication, and that combination is what made the post travel. Whether the underlying information is as groundbreaking as the engagement suggests is a separate question. The 360Brew paper has been around since 2025. Dwell time has been discussed for years.\n\nThe point I'm least comfortable with is the expertise-matching logic. I have multiple identities on LinkedIn. I write about sports media, music, photography, Turin's cultural life. We live in an era of expert-generalists. If the algorithm penalizes human escapes from core competence, it's solving for a version of professional identity that is increasingly outdated. I'd hope a system with \"360\" in its name would try to build a full picture of who someone is.\n\nI went through most of the 279 comments. The reactions break down roughly like this:\n\n~15% welcomed the changes as overdue\n~12% asked clarifying questions\n~10% were critical of LinkedIn itself\n~10% shared niche content strategies\n~9% jumped into profile optimization tactics\n~8% challenged the accuracy of the claims\n~8% flagged concerns about AI-generated content\n~6% brought humor and meta-awareness\n~4% raised diversity and multi-expertise concerns\n~18% left generic praise or tagged colleagues\n\nMake LinkedIn Better for Creators\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf Advisor, Startegist and Content Creator in Sports and Media Tech.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1367, "rx": 11, "cm": 5, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Dani Markovits", "person"], ["Make LinkedIn Better for Creators", "showcase"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1367, 11, 5, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1367, 11, 5, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 201, "id": 7439958961497411585, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7439958961497411585/", "ts": "2026-03-18T09:00:44+00:00", "date": "2026-03-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider about the FIFA-YouTube Preferred Platform Deal", "snippet": "🔟 ⚽ 10 things to consider about the FIFA YouTube deal", "chars": 2285, "text": "🔟 ⚽ 10 things to consider about the FIFA YouTube deal\nJust saw the news last night, and started to do a bit of research. I fell asleep on it, so after breakfast and kids in school, here they are: 10 things to consider.\n\nI read the FIFA and YouTube press releases line by line, cross-referenced with the TikTok deal from January, and checked every claim against the official sources.\n\nWorth stating clearly from the start: YouTube has no match rights. All live match content flows through existing Media Partners on their own YouTube channels. The deal has three components: Media Partner provisions (10-minute live streams, select full matches, highlights, Shorts), a global creator program, and FIFA's Digital Archive on FIFA's own YouTube channel.\n\n1️⃣ What the YouTube deal actually says. Three components, and how many full matches will stream remains undefined.\n2️⃣ \"Preferred Platform\" is a specific FIFA category. YouTube is the second to receive it, TikTok was first in January.\n3️⃣ What the TikTok deal actually says. \"Parts of matches\" with no minutes specified, no full match streaming mentioned.\n4️⃣ Both deals enhance broadcaster value. The 10-minute stream is a promotional tool, not a rights transfer.\n5️⃣ Creator programs are central to both deals. YouTube's language is aspirational, TikTok's is more operationally specific.\n6️⃣ YouTube's scale explains the deal. 2.7 billion monthly users, every format, every screen type.\n7️⃣ The Digital Archive is underreported. Full-length historical matches on FIFA's own channel, separate from the Media Partner provisions.\n8️⃣ TikTok's US regulatory history adds context. FIFA announced TikTok as Preferred Platform two weeks before the divestiture deal closed.\n9️⃣ IP protection runs through both agreements. YouTube has Content ID, TikTok committed to anti-piracy policies. The mechanisms differ.\n🔟 The facts beyond the noise. What we know, what remains undefined, and why the open questions matter.\n\nFull analysis with TikTok comparison, Content ID enforcement breakdown, and 19 sources below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 3107, "rx": 21, "cm": 6, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["TikTok", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3107, 21, 6, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3107, 21, 6, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 202, "id": 7439229371963547649, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7439229371963547649/", "ts": "2026-03-16T08:41:36+00:00", "date": "2026-03-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About 10 Things to Consider", "snippet": "🔟 10 things to consider About 10 things to consider", "chars": 2677, "text": "🔟 10 things to consider About 10 things to consider\nThe framework behind the format\nYou may have noticed that I keep writing these \"10 things to consider\" pieces. You may not have. Either way, I want to explain what they are and why I keep doing them.\nAny numbered format carries a risk. It can slide into listicle territory, or start sounding like clickbait. I am aware of that risk every time I publish one. The reason I keep using the format is that I needed a framework to organize how I react to things that happen in our industry. When a deal drops, or a report lands, or a league makes a move, my head fills up fast. The \"10 things\" structure forces me to slow down, select what actually matters, and think about each aspect separately before putting it all together.\nEvery time I sit down to write one, the discipline is the same. I have to curate. I have to decide what makes the cut and what stays out. I have to find ten angles that are each worth the reader's time, and I have to make sure they connect into something coherent. That process challenges me every single time. Some pieces come together in a few hours. Others take days of reading, cross-referencing, and rewriting before the ten feel right.\nI trusted the format enough to build an entire book around it. \"Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\" is 51 lists of 10 things to consider, organized across 10 categories. That is 510 items drawn from almost four decades on the vendor side of this industry. If the framework had felt thin or gimmicky after the first few articles, I would never have committed 200 pages to it.\nI am not sure the format works for everyone. I do know it works for me as a thinking tool. It keeps me focused, it keeps me honest about what I actually understand, and it stops me from writing the kind of vague commentary that sounds smart and says nothing.\nHere are the 10 so far 👇\n1️⃣ 10 Things to Consider About the Genius Sports / Legend Deal\n2️⃣ 10 Things to Consider: Premier League Going DTC in Singapore\n3️⃣ 10 Things to Consider at Bundesliga SportsInnovation Düsseldorf\n4️⃣ 10 Things to Consider: Milano Cortina 2026 by the Numbers\n5️⃣ 10 Things to Consider When Reading the Global SportsTech Report 2026\n6️⃣ 10 Things to Consider: The League Buys the Game Inside the Game\n7️⃣ 10 Things to Consider About Apple TV's F1 Streaming in the US\n8️⃣ 10 Things to Consider: PGA TOUR's Automated Content System\n9️⃣ 10 Things to Consider About the CVC Capital Partners FY25 Results\n🔟 10 Things to Consider: Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\nFull list with links in the article below 👇\n\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportsbusiness #sportstech #sportsmedia", "qtext": "", "imp": 691, "rx": 10, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbusiness", "sportstech", "sportsmedia"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 691, 10, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 691, 10, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 203, "id": 7438494122623123457, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7438494122623123457/", "ts": "2026-03-14T07:59:59+00:00", "date": "2026-03-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The first 15 pages of my new PAPERBACK book:", "snippet": "The first 15 pages of my new PAPERBACK book:", "chars": 786, "text": "The first 15 pages of my new PAPERBACK book:\n10 things to consider Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n\n📕 Why paperback only?\nNostalgia? Not really, a recipe against doom scrolling on your smartphone.\n51 lists of 10 things to consider.\n\n🤖 I have tried to distill in an easy to consume format what I have seen and experienced in my career working with the biggest global entities in sports and media as a tech vendor.\nEven if you know it all it could be a great reinforcing reading.\nAlso, a way to check things you may have forgotten and can be useful again.\nGive it a try.\n\n🛒 Available on Amazon https://lnkd.in/dpaYk-3R\n📰 Or included in my Substack annual subscription\n🎒 or in my online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 435, "rx": 6, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 434, 6, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 435, 6, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 204, "id": 7438159603244113920, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7438159603244113920/", "ts": "2026-03-13T09:50:43+00:00", "date": "2026-03-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: PGA TOUR's Automated Content System Generates 1,200 Pieces a Week", "snippet": "🏌‍♂️ 10 things to consider: PGA TOUR's Automated Content System", "chars": 1878, "text": "🏌‍♂️ 10 things to consider: PGA TOUR's Automated Content System\nPGA TOUR built an AI content system that generates 1,200+ pieces of content every week. Round recaps, tournament recaps, betting profiles, player previews, shot commentaries. For every player in the field. Published within minutes. At less than 25 cents per piece. A 95% cost reduction.\nThe technology works. The economics are hard to argue with. Golf has always been one of the hardest sports to cover at scale, and this system covers everyone.\nMy questions start after the tech.\n\n⛳ I wrote 10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ The hypothetical phase is over\n2️⃣ The economics are brutal for the old model\n3️⃣ The architecture tells you where the industry is heading\n4️⃣ Speed is the real product\n5️⃣ 1,200 pieces a week — does the audience want all of it?\n6️⃣ 1,200 pieces a week also need somewhere to go\n7️⃣ Readability is the unsolved problem\n8️⃣ The fact-checking layer is impressive\n9️⃣ What happens to the humans?\n🔟 The right question is upstream of the technology\nFull article below 👇\nSource: AWS for M&E Blog, \"Accelerating sports content creation using agentic AI: PGA TOUR,\" March 12, 2026.\n\nOne thing worth noting: for all the detail on architecture, agents, and cost savings, the post does not include a single full example of the content the system produces. No round recap to read. No betting profile to evaluate. If you are going to make the case for automated content at this scale, showing what that content actually looks like would help.\n\nAmazon Web Services (AWS) CapTech Scott Gutterman United States Golf Association (USGA) Jonathan Schecter Luigi Goria Sébastien Audoux\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1857, "rx": 19, "cm": 3, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["PGA TOUR", "company"], ["Amazon Web Services (AWS)", "company"], ["CapTech", "company"], ["Scott Gutterman", "person"], ["United States Golf Association (USGA)", "company"], ["Jonathan Schecter", "person"], ["Luigi Goria", "person"], ["Sébastien Audoux", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1854, 19, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1857, 19, 3, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 205, "id": 7437898829430972416, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7437898829430972416/", "ts": "2026-03-12T16:34:30+00:00", "date": "2026-03-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "After a long career on the vendor side of sports and media technology, I put what I learned into a course.", "snippet": "After a long career on the vendor side of sports and media technology, I put what I learned into a course.", "chars": 1273, "text": "After a long career on the vendor side of sports and media technology, I put what I learned into a course.\n\n\"Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\" is a field manual for professionals who already have experience and want to sharpen their judgment.\n\nWho it's for:\n\n🎯 Sales professionals who want to understand what happens after the deal closes\n⚙️ Product and engineering leaders who want to see how decisions land in production\n🤝 Delivery and account leads navigating client expectations and internal constraints\n🏗️ Founders building long-term credibility with buyers\n🆕 People entering sports and media tech from other industries\n\nThis is a course about reading situations, understanding context, and making better decisions under pressure. It covers the patterns that repeat across this industry and the trade-offs that define real vendor work.\n\nAlso worth noting: if you work on the client side, buying from vendors or managing vendor relationships, you may find real value here too. Understanding how the other side thinks tends to make collaboration work better.\nIt includes the 10 things to consider 200 pages book.\n\nThe course is live now.\nhttps://lnkd.in/dadpU6Uu\n\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportsbusiness #mediatechnology #streaming #B2B #sportsinnovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 403, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbusiness", "mediatechnology", "streaming", "b2b", "sportsinnovation"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 402, 10, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 403, 10, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 206, "id": 7437488847707324416, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7437488847707324416/", "ts": "2026-03-11T13:25:23+00:00", "date": "2026-03-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the CVC Capital Partners FY25 Results", "snippet": "🔟 🏏 10 things to consider About the CVC Capital Partners FY25 Results", "chars": 1489, "text": "🔟 🏏 10 things to consider About the CVC Capital Partners FY25 Results\nThat tells you something about where sports sits inside a €205 billion alternative asset manager.\nI went through the full-year results presentation, the press release, and all 271 pages of the annual report.\nBy the way this is so complex I am sure I have made mistakes here and there.\n\n10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ The Gujarat Titans are the cover image of the annual report and the results deck\n2️⃣ CEO Rob Lucas personally oversees sports, media and entertainment investing globally\n3️⃣ Global Sport Group (GSG) consolidates seven league investments under a $13.6 billion structure\n4️⃣ Record financial results: €1.5bn management fees, €1.1bn EBITDA, €21.9bn in realisations\n5️⃣ The share price is down 40%+ over the past year, with analysts still targeting €18-19\n6️⃣ The new Catalyst mid-market fund lists Sports, Media and Entertainment as a core sector\n7️⃣ Private Wealth products grew from €0.8bn to €3.6bn in 12 months\n8️⃣ The Marathon acquisition takes CVC's credit platform past €60bn\n9️⃣ IFRS carry recognition creates real timing gaps between cash returns and reported earnings\n🔟 Sports is the asset class CVC uses as its investor communications centrepiece\n\nFull article below 👇\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1237, "rx": 17, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["CVC", "company"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Gujarat Titans", "company"], ["Global Sport Group (GSG)", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1236, 17, 1, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1237, 17, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 207, "id": 7437443229807120384, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7437443229807120384/", "ts": "2026-03-11T10:24:06+00:00", "date": "2026-03-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsInnovation 2026: Dolby Optiview, Sportec Solutions, and the Bundesliga Innovation", "snippet": "📣 Report from SportsInnovation 2026 in Düsseldorf, fourth edition.", "chars": 1696, "text": "📣 Report from SportsInnovation 2026 in Düsseldorf, fourth edition.\nI moderated two sessions (3D data in football and the future of officiating) and wrote about those separately.\nThis time I walked the floor with a camera and stopped at a few stands that caught my attention.\n🎥 Dolby OptiView showing managed latency (same stream at real-time, 1s, and 5s delay) and personalized ads through Server-Guided Ad Insertion. The side-by-side format where the live content stays visible while the ad runs alongside it is worth paying attention to.\n🚗 Dolby Vision and Atmos inside a car. The vehicle is becoming a media surface for sports, and they're positioning early.\n⚽ Sportec Solutions AG presenting two new projects: real-time event data from optical tracking, and handball detection using goal line technology cameras fed into an AI evaluation engine. The cameras are already in the stadium. The data pipeline already exists. Smart use of existing infrastructure.\n📢 And the DFL officially becomes Bundesliga from next season. A 63-year-old brand finally becoming the organization's name.\nFew leagues in the world would open up their stadium to competitors and collaborators to test and demo technology at this level. Bundesliga deserves credit for building this format.\n\nVideo podcast and full article below 👇\n\nBundesliga DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga Dolby Laboratories\n#SportsInnovation #Bundesliga #SportsTech #LiveStreaming #DolbyOptiview #SportecSolutions #SportsMedia\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 2951, "rx": 55, "cm": 3, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Sportec Solutions AG", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["Dolby Laboratories", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportsinnovation", "bundesliga", "sportstech", "livestreaming", "dolbyoptiview", "sportecsolutions", "sportsmedia"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2951, 55, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 2951, 55, 3, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 208, "id": 7437079654584406016, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7437079654584406016/", "ts": "2026-03-10T10:19:23+00:00", "date": "2026-03-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Punk Ethic for Professional Life", "snippet": "It is my birthday today.", "chars": 767, "text": "It is my birthday today.\n🥸 So I allowed myself to be a little reflective and wrote about something that has quietly shaped most of my professional life: a punk ethic.\n\n🎤 I have worked in media, technology, sports, music, photography, publishing, and cultural content for over thirty-five years.\n\n⚡ A punk ethic means you start things before you feel ready. You learn by doing, in public, with real constraints. You accept uneven outcomes as the cost of staying in motion. You let practice do the teaching.\n\n🤖 It is a way of staying in contact with reality in industries where the ground keeps shifting.\n\n✍️ I wrote about what this looks like in practice, where it works, where it does not, and why I would not change the approach.\n\nYou a punk too?\n\nFull piece below 👇", "qtext": "", "imp": 6161, "rx": 76, "cm": 32, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 6161, 76, 32, 4], ["2026-10-17", 6161, 76, 32, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 209, "id": 7436679703748411393, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7436679703748411393/", "ts": "2026-03-09T07:50:08+00:00", "date": "2026-03-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider about Apple TV’s F1 Streaming in the US", "snippet": "🔟 🏎️ 10 things to consider about Apple TV's F1 Streaming in the US", "chars": 2379, "text": "🔟 🏎️ 10 things to consider about Apple TV's F1 Streaming in the US\nI started my sports technology career at the F1 Grand Prix in Imola about 10,000 years ago. So when Apple launched its exclusive US streaming deal this past weekend at the Australian GP, I could not avoid having a look.\nThis is a US-only product and I'm writing from Turin. I researched as much as I could, cross-referencing official sources, fan reactions, and industry analysis. I may have missed something. If I did, let me know.\n\n10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ 📺 Apple is platforming F1, and that's a deliberate choice\nCommentary comes from F1 TV and Sky. Apple built the infrastructure around it.\n2️⃣ 🖥️ The Multiview is the real product statement, and it has a segmentation problem\n30 live feeds, four screens at once. Only on Apple hardware.\n3️⃣ 💰 The $12.99 price point absorbs F1 TV Premium, and that changes the math\nHardcore fans save money. Casual ESPN viewers face a new subscription.\n4️⃣ ⚽ Apple learned from MLS. Visibly.\nThe Season Pass disaster taught Apple everything it needed to know about paywalls.\n5️⃣ 🤝 The Netflix-Apple content swap is a first in streaming sports\nTwo competing streamers sharing a live race. That has never happened before.\n6️⃣ 📡 The Tubi altcast is the most interesting distribution experiment in the package\nFree creator-led broadcasts on AVOD. Plus Netflix, Yahoo, Univision, DIRECTV, EverPass, IMAX.\n7️⃣ 🍎 Apple's ecosystem integration goes deeper than any previous sports streaming product\nApple TV, Apple Sports, Apple Music, Apple Maps, Apple News, Apple Podcasts. All activated on race day.\n8️⃣ 📊 The measurement vacuum is real\nNo Nielsen. Apple promises some numbers. Sponsors are watching closely.\n9️⃣ 🎬 Five races in IMAX theaters is a format experiment with real upside\nMiami, Monaco, Silverstone, Monza, Austin. 50+ theaters. Communal viewing meets streaming.\n🔟 🇺🇸 This deal is US-only, and that's the quiet strategic tension\n$750M over five years for one market. The results will shape global sports rights strategy.\nFull piece below 👇\n\nFormula 1 Apple Netflix\n#F1 #Formula1 #AppleTV #SportsTech #SportsMedia #Streaming #MediaRights\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1915, "rx": 21, "cm": 5, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Formula 1", "company"], ["Apple", "company"], ["Netflix", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["f1", "formula1", "appletv", "sportstech", "sportsmedia", "streaming", "mediarights"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1915, 21, 5, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1915, 21, 5, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 210, "id": 7435690862677843968, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7435690862677843968/", "ts": "2026-03-06T14:20:50+00:00", "date": "2026-03-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "👨‍🏫 A online course and a book where I distilled most of my almost 40 years in sports and media tech on the vendor side.", "snippet": "👨‍🏫 A online course and a book where I distilled most of my almost 40 years in sports and media tech on the vendor side.", "chars": 612, "text": "👨‍🏫 A online course and a book where I distilled most of my almost 40 years in sports and media tech on the vendor side.\n\nOver the years, I noticed a recurring gap.\nMost vendor education focuses on tools, tactics, or success stories.\nVery little helps people understand how this industry actually behaves once systems are live, pressure is real, and responsibility becomes shared.\nThis course exists to address that gap.\n\nSubscribe for free to the limited access version:\nhttps://lnkd.in/dadpU6Uu\n\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf Advisor, Startegist and Content Creator in Sports and Media Tech.", "qtext": "", "imp": 292, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 292, 4, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 292, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 211, "id": 7435631807372382208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7435631807372382208/", "ts": "2026-03-06T10:26:10+00:00", "date": "2026-03-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Just back from SportsInnovation.", "snippet": "Just back from SportsInnovation.", "chars": 824, "text": "Just back from SportsInnovation.\nOne thing you get back from these events, beyond content and contacts, is people and the human connections you experienced.\nSo let's start with that.\n\nLoved sharing the stage with Seema Jaswal and Nick Meacham, Hendrik Weber, Christian Holzer, Nicolas Evans, Peter Irwin.\nGreat having time with team Dolby OptiView, Matthias Ankli, Tobias Habann.\nInteresting conversations with Shane Warden, Marco Lorenzi, George Smith, Marte Harkestad, Bernd Schusky, Ian Wray.\nA real team effort by DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga, D.SPORTS, Transform Sports, and SportsPro.\nLuccas Roznowicz, Lukas Glöckner, Johanna Bartsch, Henning Eberhardt, Philipp Klotz.\n\n#sportstech #Dolby #DolbyEurope\n-----\nCheck out my new A guy with a scarf book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 3025, "rx": 74, "cm": 7, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Seema Jaswal", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["Hendrik Weber", "person"], ["Christian Holzer", "person"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["Peter Irwin", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["Tobias Habann", "person"], ["Shane Warden", "person"], ["Marco Lorenzi", "person"], ["George Smith", "person"], ["Marte Harkestad", "person"], ["Bernd Schusky", "person"], ["Ian Wray", "person"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["D.SPORTS", "company"], ["Transform Sports", "company"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Luccas Roznowicz", "person"], ["Lukas Glöckner", "person"], ["Johanna Bartsch", "person"], ["Henning Eberhardt", "person"], ["Philipp Klotz", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "dolby", "dolbyeurope"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 1320, "watch_time_s": 15480, "avg_watch_s": 11, "saves": 2, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3025, 74, 7, 3], ["2026-10-17", 3025, 74, 7, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 212, "id": 7435220924070756352, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7435220924070756352/", "ts": "2026-03-05T07:13:28+00:00", "date": "2026-03-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsInnovation 2026: 3D Data Across Analytics, Officiating, and Fan Experience", "snippet": "⚽ 🤖 SportsInnovation 2026: 3D Data Across Analytics, Officiating, and Fan Experience", "chars": 2988, "text": "⚽ 🤖 SportsInnovation 2026: 3D Data Across Analytics, Officiating, and Fan Experience\nFourth time at SportsInnovation in Düsseldorf. This year I moderated two sessions on the main stage, both on 3D data in football. A live match was running on the pitch throughout. I have been involved in football data since 1994 and this felt like a day where things clicked into place.\n\n🎙️  Opening session: Connected Future Ahead — Unlocking the Power of 3D Data in Football\nWith Hendrik Weber (DFL / Bundesliga), Christian Holzer (Sportec Solutions AG Solutions), Nicolas Evans (FIFA), John Shepherd  (EA SPORTS).\n\nThree things that stood out:\n\n1. Hendrik Weber compared 3D data today to tracking data fifteen years ago, when nobody knew what to do with it. The market matured, sports data became essential. 3D data is at the same tipping point now, and DFL chose to open the event with it for that reason.\n\n2. The live automated event detection engine was processing the match in real time on screen. Christian Holzer showed how skeletal tracking identifies every touch and classifies events through ML. For the 2026 World Cup, Nicolas Evans confirmed FIFA will automate everything that is objectively automatable. Around seven top-level events will still need human judgment.\n\n3. John Shepherd presented Project Assist: live TRACAB volumetric data combined with the Frostbite engine. The demo rendered the match on the pitch in real time with new camera angles, tactical overlays, and prediction mechanics. The hardware is already installed in all 36 Bundesliga stadiums.\n\n🎙️ Afternoon session: Beyond the Whistle — The Future of Officiating Technology\nWith Nicolas Evans (FIFA) and Peter Irwin (Hawk-Eye Innovations).\n\nThree things that stood out:\n\n1. The Football Technology Centre is a joint venture between FIFA and Hawk-Eye designed to break the innovation deadlock. For years, vendors would say \"we think it's possible, but will you guarantee us it will be used?\" The joint venture is the structural answer: shared risk, shared investment, longer horizon.\n\n2. Advanced SAOT sends the offside alert directly to the on-field referee in real time, bypassing the VAR for a high majority of cases. Peter Irwin described four to five years of edge case testing. One failure destroys trust in the entire system. That is why full automation is still ahead.\n\n3. Any IP developed within the Football Technology Centre is licensable by any company. That was a non-negotiable condition from FIFA. When SAOT launched, Hawk-Eye was the only provider. Now there are three. Nicolas put it simply: you cannot democratize a product that does not exist.\n\nFull write-up below.\nBundesliga DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga Luccas Roznowicz Lukas Glöckner Johanna Bartsch\n#football #3ddata #ai\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1721, "rx": 26, "cm": 3, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Hendrik Weber", "person"], ["Christian Holzer", "person"], ["Sportec Solutions AG", "company"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["EA SPORTS", "company"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["Peter Irwin", "person"], ["Hawk-Eye Innovations", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "company"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["Luccas Roznowicz", "person"], ["Lukas Glöckner", "person"], ["Johanna Bartsch", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["football", "3ddata", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1721, 26, 3, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1721, 26, 3, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 213, "id": 7434848007138820096, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7434848007138820096/", "ts": "2026-03-04T06:31:37+00:00", "date": "2026-03-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider at Bundesliga SportsInnovation Düsseldorf", "snippet": "⚽ 🇩🇪 10 things to consider about SportsInnovation in Düsseldorf with the DFL/Bundesliga", "chars": 2845, "text": "⚽ 🇩🇪 10 things to consider about SportsInnovation in Düsseldorf with the DFL/Bundesliga\nI have now been on stage at all four editions of SportsInnovation in Düsseldorf.\nThis is one of the most unique events in our industry.\nReal football, and other sports, are played while technologies are demonstrated live.\n\n10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ An international platform for sports innovation\n What started as a DFL/Bundesliga initiative now attracts a global ecosystem. Football remains central, while other sports join the conversation. This year the NBA is part of the programme, following NFL participation in previous editions.\n2️⃣ Innovation tested during real matches\n The Innovation Games define the event. Technologies operate while a live football match unfolds on the pitch, connecting data, media and analytics directly to the rhythm of the game.\n3️⃣ Live demos on stage\n Many technologies are demonstrated live on stage while the match continues behind the presenters. That format adds pressure for everyone involved. Live demos always carry uncertainty. Trust me.\n4️⃣ DFL/Bundesliga as innovation orchestrator\n The league connects media organisations, technology companies, research groups and sports stakeholders in the same environment, shaping conversations around sport, data and innovation.\n5️⃣ Data as shared infrastructure\n The same positional data can power coaching analysis, broadcast graphics, fan visualisations and gaming environments. Different experiences increasingly depend on the same datasets.\n6️⃣ The rise of large-scale 3D data\n Tracking technologies continue to evolve toward richer spatial datasets. My opening session this year explores how 3D data moves from research into real operational use.\n7️⃣ AI entering operational workflows\n AI now appears across production tools, analytics systems and fan interaction platforms. The focus moves toward integration and real use cases.\n8️⃣ Strategic and technical discussions together\n The programme moves between big-picture industry conversations and deep technical sessions. Around the arena, the exhibition area offers many hands-on demos.\n9️⃣ Startups entering the innovation pipeline\n The Startup Award and pitch sessions give emerging companies visibility in front of leagues, clubs and industry leaders.\n🔟 A curated invite-only ecosystem\n SportsInnovation remains invite-only. The DFL/Bundesliga keep the audience curated, creating an environment where meaningful conversations happen easily.\nDay 1 starts in 2 hours!\nSee you there.\nBundesliga DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga D.SPORTS Sportec Solutions AG\n#football #innovation #ai #3d-data\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 2321, "rx": 49, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["D.SPORTS", "company"], ["Sportec Solutions AG", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["football", "innovation", "ai", "3d"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2321, 49, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2321, 49, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 214, "id": 7434641780937113600, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7434641780937113600/", "ts": "2026-03-03T16:52:09+00:00", "date": "2026-03-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Hi from Düsseldorf! SportsInnovation Bundesliga", "snippet": "Hi from Düsseldorf! SportsInnovation Bundesliga", "chars": 586, "text": "Hi from Düsseldorf! SportsInnovation Bundesliga\nRehearsing for the opening session on 3D Data.\n\n📕 Also, explaining why in this time and age I decided to publish a book only in physical format as an antidote to doomscrolling - perfect for those little pauses, read one of the 51 lists instead of staying on your smartphone again.\n10 things to consider\nBecoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n\nBook: https://amzn.eu/d/09cuCSkB\nCourse: https://lnkd.in/dadpU6Uu\n-----\nI am Carlo De Marchis - A guy with a scarf Advisor, Startegist and Content Creator in Sports and Media Tech.", "qtext": "", "imp": 628, "rx": 20, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": {"video_views": 263, "watch_time_s": 2812, "avg_watch_s": 10, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 627, 20, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 628, 20, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 215, "id": 7434547511933095936, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7434547511933095936/", "ts": "2026-03-03T10:37:34+00:00", "date": "2026-03-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 79: Cyprien Castanedo - How Ligue 1 Built a D2C Platform From Zero Subscribers in Two Months", "snippet": "🎙️ Ep. 79: Cyprien Castanedo - Why the First Season Is a Retention Lab, Not Just a Subscriber Count", "chars": 2724, "text": "🎙️ Ep. 79: Cyprien Castanedo - Why the First Season Is a Retention Lab, Not Just a Subscriber Count\n\nFor this episode of Retention Zone I spoke with Cyprien Castanedo, Head of Media Platform & Innovation at LFP Media, about what happens when a football league goes from selling broadcast rights to selling subscriptions directly to fans. Ligue 1 Plus launched in two months, from zero subscribers, and is now six months into one of the most fascinating D2C experiments in European sports media.\n\nHere are the key retention takeaways 👇\n\n1️⃣ 🤝 Trust before transactions. French fans had been through the MediaPro collapse and DAZN disappointment. Aggressive pricing was not just a commercial choice, it was a trust-rebuilding strategy.\n\n2️⃣ 📺 Hybrid distribution beats pure OTT. Ligue 1 Plus lives on streaming, linear TV, and telco partners. In European markets like France, Italy, and Belgium, you need to meet fans where they already are.\n\n3️⃣ 📊 Retention follows the football calendar. Subscriber spikes come with the big games. The real work is what happens after: CRM, notifications, storytelling that keeps fans engaged between matchdays.\n\n4️⃣ ⚽ Sporting results drive churn. When Marseille's title hopes faded, LFP shifted the narrative to Champions League qualification. Retention in football means adapting your messaging to what is happening on the pitch, week by week.\n\n5️⃣ 🧪 A/B testing is the missing piece. LFP has the data to see where people drop off in the subscription flow, but not yet the flexibility to experiment fast enough. Full A/B testing capability is a top priority for season two.\n\n6️⃣ 💳 Involuntary churn adds up. Failed payments, expired cards, banking issues. Small percentage gains in payment recovery compound into significant subscriber retention over a full season.\n\n7️⃣ 🤖 AI as advisor first, autopilot later. Cyprien wants AI to surface insights and flag at-risk subscribers, but giving it full autonomy on retention offers risks fairness issues that one social media post can turn into a trust problem.\n\n8️⃣ 🔄 The org shift matters most. Going from B2B to B2C in two months meant learning e-commerce, consumer marketing, and CRM from scratch. Cyprien called this agility \"maybe the number one success element on the project.\"\n\nThe first season is not just about counting subscribers. It is a retention lab. And season two is already in the works.\n\nLFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel\nGilles Domartini Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res\n\n#SportsMedia #D2C #Streaming #Retention #OTT #SportsTech #Football\n-----\n🎧Retention Zone – Episode 79\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 2053, "rx": 38, "cm": 5, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Ligue 1", "showcase"], ["LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "d2c", "streaming", "retention", "ott", "sportstech", "football"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2051, 38, 5, 4], ["2026-10-17", 2053, 38, 5, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 216, "id": 7434240587366723584, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7434240587366723584/", "ts": "2026-03-02T14:17:57+00:00", "date": "2026-03-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ 🇩🇪 I am at the SportsInnovation Event in Düsseldorf, organized every two years by the DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga this week.", "snippet": "⚽ 🇩🇪 I am at the SportsInnovation Event in Düsseldorf, organized every two years by the DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga this week.", "chars": 595, "text": "⚽ 🇩🇪 I am at the SportsInnovation Event in Düsseldorf, organized every two years by the DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga this week.\nAlso moderating a panel with Nicolas Evans (FIFA) and Peter Irwin (Hawk-Eye Innovations) on Day1.\n🤖 We will discuss their JV and the impact of advanced tech in improving the game  of football.\nLet me know if you are there too.\n\nMarco Lorenzi\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 1112, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["Peter Irwin", "person"], ["Hawk-Eye Innovations", "company"], ["Marco Lorenzi", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1112, 16, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1112, 16, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 217, "id": 7434157980692090881, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7434157980692090881/", "ts": "2026-03-02T08:49:42+00:00", "date": "2026-03-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider About the Genius Sports / Legend Deal", "snippet": "10 things to consider About the Genius Sports / Legend Deal", "chars": 1773, "text": "10 things to consider About the Genius Sports / Legend Deal\nI spent the past few days going down the rabbit hole of what is probably the most interesting acquisition in sports technology this year.\nGenius Sports agreed to pay up to $1.2 billion for Legend, the company behind Covers.com, Casino.org, Casino Guru and over 25 other sports and gaming media brands. The stock crashed 25% on announcement day. CEO Mark Locke wrote a public shareholder letter two weeks later to explain why everyone was wrong.\nI don't know if Genius overpaid.\nSo I went looking for the right questions instead of the easy answers.\n\n10 things to consider:\n1️⃣ The entire market reaction was triggered by a single word\n2️⃣ Locke's reframing is ambitious, and it has an internal logic worth following\n3️⃣ The AI argument is the most intellectually provocative part of the whole story\n4️⃣ The Sportradar comparison tells you everything about two different visions of the future\n5️⃣ The deal creates a \"toll booth\" scenario that nobody is talking about enough\n6️⃣ The $300 million earnout is doing a lot of quiet work\n7️⃣ The \"this has happened before\" argument is true and also incomplete\n8️⃣ The WPP partnership and the Fan Graph are the connective tissue people are underweighting\n9️⃣ The Stats Perform wild card is sitting right there\n🔟 The real question is whether \"the operating system of modern sport\" is a company or a metaphor\n\nFull piece below 👇\nDeep dive on Substack (paid)\n#SportsData #SportsTechnology #SportsBetting #GeniusSports #MediaTech #SportsBusiness\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 6380, "rx": 53, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Genius Sports", "company"], ["Legend", "company"], ["Mark Locke", "person"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportsdata", "sportstechnology", "sportsbetting", "geniussports", "mediatech", "sportsbusiness"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 6374, 53, 1, 3], ["2026-10-17", 6380, 53, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 218, "id": 7433056073244254209, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7433056073244254209/", "ts": "2026-02-27T07:51:07+00:00", "date": "2026-02-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: Premier League Going DTC in Singapore", "snippet": "🔟 10 things to consider: Premier League Going DTC in Singapore", "chars": 1427, "text": "🔟 10 things to consider: Premier League Going DTC in Singapore\n\nI was playing vinyl at my Thursday listening session in a hi-fi bar in Torino when I read about it. By the time I got home I had already started writing.\nYou will find the full news story told better elsewhere. What I wanted to do is think about what this actually means for the sports media industry.\n\nSo I wrote my 10 things to consider.\n1️⃣ The timing is the story. They are doing this from strength, not necessity.\n2️⃣ Singapore is a rehearsal room, not a stage.\n3️⃣ They have been building the machine for years. Alexandra Willis, Premier League Studios, Adobe, the new app.\n4️⃣ The Ligue 1+ precedent is useful, but the comparison has limits.\n5️⃣ DTC is a capability, not a product launch.\n6️⃣ The real shift is from rights cycles to product cycles.\n7️⃣ Premier League Studios is the infrastructure play that makes it all possible.\n8️⃣ The \"Premflix\" framing misses the point.\n9️⃣ \"I have been saying this for years\", get experienced before you need it.\n🔟 Success in year one should be measured in learning, not in subscribers.\n\nFull Article below 👇\nThe Premier League\n#sportsmedia #streaming #premierleague #dtc #sportsbusiness\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n-----\nCheck out my new book and online course Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "qtext": "", "imp": 3035, "rx": 36, "cm": 8, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Alexandra Willis", "person"], ["The Premier League", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "streaming", "premierleague", "dtc", "sportsbusiness"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3035, 36, 8, 4], ["2026-10-17", 3035, 36, 8, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 219, "id": 7432718621547782144, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7432718621547782144/", "ts": "2026-02-26T09:30:12+00:00", "date": "2026-02-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider - For B2B Tech Vendors in Sports and Media", "snippet": "10 things to consider…", "chars": 1562, "text": "10 things to consider…\nBecoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n200 PAGES -10 CATEGORIES - 51 LISTS - 510 THINGS\n\n📕 Content\n⚙️ Over the past months I organised my field notes into a structured framework of “10 things to consider” lists. Each one comes from real conversations, projects, and moments where delivery, governance, or expectations shifted.\n⚙️ The goal is simple. Give vendors a way to recognise patterns earlier. Help teams see where risk hides, where positioning drifts, where internal readiness matters more than features.\n⚙️ The lists move across the full lifecycle, from client understanding and go-to-market design, through commercial structure and delivery power dynamics, into architecture, partnerships, renewals, and the emerging signals shaping the next phase of sports media technology.\n⚙️ This is not theory. It is a working map built from years inside rooms where decisions were messy, imperfect, and often human before they were strategic.\n\nFrom the foreword by Giampiero Rinaudo:\n\"In many of the “10 things to consider” I see this: The experience we have been accumulating in this 40 years journey. The lessons learned, the strategic thinking, the innovation culture, the spirit. Something learned on the job, not studied in theory. I think it’s a great tool to read and consult whenever we have to take important business decision\"\n\n🤖 How to get it: Buy on Amazon, Subscribe to Substack (paid), Online course (included), all links in the article below.\n\nProduced by A guy with a scarf\n#sportstech #vendors #practicalguide", "qtext": "", "imp": 1148, "rx": 20, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Giampiero Rinaudo", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "vendors", "practicalguide"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1148, 20, 2, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1148, 20, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 220, "id": 7432333207657848832, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7432333207657848832/", "ts": "2026-02-25T07:58:42+00:00", "date": "2026-02-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Just got a great mention from Luca Politi on an article about technology at the Olympics.", "snippet": "Just got a great mention from Luca Politi on an article about technology at the Olympics.", "chars": 486, "text": "Just got a great mention from Luca Politi on an article about technology at the Olympics.\n\n[ITALIANO]\nGrazie Luca Politi per avermi citato nel tuo articolo su tecnologia e Olimpiadi\n\n«Il mondo dello sport è tradizionalmente conservatore – ha detto a Changes Carlo De Marchis, advisor ed esperto di media tech – ma la rivoluzione digitale ha costretto a mutare atteggiamento e a farlo velocemente».\n\nPotete leggere la versione integrale qui: https://lnkd.in/dv_Wmb52\n\n#tech #olympics #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 311, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Luca Politi", "person"], ["Luca Politi", "person"]], "tags": ["tech", "olympics", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 311, 6, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 311, 6, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 221, "id": 7432105270346125313, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7432105270346125313/", "ts": "2026-02-24T16:52:58+00:00", "date": "2026-02-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "50,000 for a U19 Youth Match: A Story from Cologne I Wanted to Share", "snippet": "⚽ 🇩🇪 50,000 for a Youth Match: The Night Cologne Rewrote the Record Books", "chars": 502, "text": "⚽ 🇩🇪 50,000 for a Youth Match: The Night Cologne Rewrote the Record Books\nI was in a call with Lukas Glöckner, preparing for the upcoming SportsInnovation event in Düsseldorf, when he casually mentioned he had been at the Köln U19 match against Inter Milan.\n\"Fifty thousand,\" he said.\nI asked him to repeat that.\nFifty thousand. For a youth game.\nA round of 32 tie in the UEFA Youth League. A Wednesday evening game for under-19 players.\nI wrote about it 👇\n\nDFL Deutsche Fußball Liga\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1139, "rx": 18, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Lukas Glöckner", "person"], ["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1139, 18, 2, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1139, 18, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 222, "id": 7431997146545098752, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7431997146545098752/", "ts": "2026-02-24T09:43:19+00:00", "date": "2026-02-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsInnovation Düsseldorf, where football tests its own future", "snippet": "🤖 🇩🇪 Heading back to Düsseldorf for SportsInnovation 2026, a really unique event I have had the privilege to be part of since its early editions.", "chars": 1225, "text": "🤖 🇩🇪 Heading back to Düsseldorf for SportsInnovation 2026, a really unique event I have had the privilege to be part of since its early editions.\n\nThis year I will be moderating the session “Connected Future ahead: Unlocking the Power of 3D Data in Football” together with an outstanding group of voices shaping the next layer of the game, Hendrik Weber (DFL), Nicolas Evans (FIFA), Christian Holzer (Sportec Solutions).\n\nSportsInnovation remains a special environment for our industry. Real matches on the pitch, live demos from the stage, and honest conversations about where football is heading across media, data, gaming, and performance.\n\nLooking forward to sharing the arena with fellow moderators and presenters including Seema Jaswal, Nick Meacham, Lisa Ramuschkat, Jan Alessie, Laura Papendick, Cosma von Haacke, Philipp Ostsieker, and Lea Rostek.\nIf you are in Düsseldorf, come and say hello. Always happy to exchange ideas around the future of football and sports media.\n\nRead more below. 👇\n\nLuccas Roznowicz Lukas Glöckner DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga D.SPORTS Transform Sports SportsPro\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 3678, "rx": 51, "cm": 9, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsInnovation", "company"], ["Hendrik Weber", "person"], ["Nicolas Evans", "person"], ["Christian Holzer", "person"], ["Seema Jaswal", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["Lisa Ramuschkat", "person"], ["Jan Alessie", "person"], ["Laura Papendick", "person"], ["Cosma von Haacke", "person"], ["Philipp Ostsieker", "person"], ["Lea Rostek", "person"], ["Luccas Roznowicz", "person"], ["Lukas Glöckner", "person"], ["DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga", "company"], ["D.SPORTS", "company"], ["Transform Sports", "company"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3678, 51, 9, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3678, 51, 9, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 223, "id": 7431652164348985344, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7431652164348985344/", "ts": "2026-02-23T10:52:29+00:00", "date": "2026-02-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Did Drones Win Gold at the Milano Cortina Olympics? Short Answer: Yes.", "snippet": "⛷️ 📹 Did Drones Win Gold at the Milano Cortina Olympics?", "chars": 1114, "text": "⛷️ 📹 Did Drones Win Gold at the Milano Cortina Olympics?\n🏅 Short Answer: Yes.\nI really liked what I saw in some sports and I have the impression this will be a shift that will stay in sport production.\n\nHow OBS turned 243 grams of custom-built flying camera into the most talked-about performer of the Winter Games.\nWhat's inside:\n1️⃣ The engineering: custom cinewhoops, sub-250g, dual cameras, no GPS\n2️⃣ The people: former ski jumpers and ice dancers turned drone pilots\n3️⃣ The coverage: inside a luge track for the first time, at 140 km/h\n4️⃣ The numbers: 93% audience growth in the US, Italy watching more than four previous Games combined\n5️⃣ The noise: vuvuzela comparisons, a near-miss with a 16-year-old snowboarder\n6️⃣ The legacy: what every sports broadcaster and league should be studying right now\n\nPlus an appendix covering drone use at every Olympics since Sochi 2014, FPV drones in F1, cycling, NFL, and the Canada Soccer spying scandal.\n\nRead more in the article below\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2146, "rx": 31, "cm": 8, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2146, 31, 8, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2146, 31, 8, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 224, "id": 7431617147098996736, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7431617147098996736/", "ts": "2026-02-23T08:33:20+00:00", "date": "2026-02-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: Milano Cortina 2026 by the Numbers", "snippet": "10 things to consider", "chars": 1621, "text": "10 things to consider\n⛷️ 📊 The IOC published its official Milano Cortina 2026 data. I went through all of it. I have been tracking Olympic media data for longer than I care to admit, and every edition I sit with the spreadsheets, the audience reports, the rights structures, the social numbers, trying to make sense of what actually happened versus what the press releases say happened.\n\nNothing is like the Olympics. Nothing in the sports calendar produces the kind of audience diversity, emotional intensity, and global simultaneity that the Olympics produce. Nothing. These are my 10 takeaways.\n1️⃣ The rights structure change in Italy produced a different country of viewers\n2️⃣ NBC's 93 per cent increase over Beijing deserves more attention than it's getting\n3️⃣ Brazil won a gold medal and broke Winter Games streaming records simultaneously\n4️⃣ 10 billion social engagements tripling Beijing 2022 needs a closer look at what drove it\n5️⃣ Japan reaching 80 per cent of its potential audience on linear TV is the outlier nobody is discussing\n6️⃣ 85 per cent existing or temporary venues is a genuinely good number\n7️⃣ The volunteer application ratio tells you something about soft power\n8️⃣ Two NOCs winning their first-ever Winter Games medals in the same edition is historically rare\n9️⃣ The digital numbers need a unit of analysis before you can use them\n🔟 France 2030 is the next test of whether any of this scales without a home crowd advantage\n\nDeep dive inside the article below.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 2400, "rx": 19, "cm": 5, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2396, 19, 5, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2400, 19, 5, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 225, "id": 7430944647834693633, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7430944647834693633/", "ts": "2026-02-21T12:01:04+00:00", "date": "2026-02-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Saturdays writing and reading...", "snippet": "Saturdays writing and reading...", "chars": 162, "text": "Saturdays writing and reading...\nJust posted on my Substack a reflection on the state of journalism, from an ignorant, curious observer.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/dUAS2KUR", "qtext": "", "imp": 168, "rx": 3, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 164, 3, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 168, 3, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 226, "id": 7430534360002383872, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7430534360002383872/", "ts": "2026-02-20T08:50:44+00:00", "date": "2026-02-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider: The League Buys the Game Inside the Game", "snippet": "10 things to consider", "chars": 2291, "text": "10 things to consider\n⚽ 📲 The League Buys the Game Inside the Game\nHow Lega Serie A's €18 million acquisition of Fantacalcio.it gave Italian football its first direct relationship with three million fans.\n\nLast Monday in Milan, the Lega Serie A voted to acquire 51% of Quadronica Srl — the Naples-based company behind Fantacalcio.it, Italy's dominant fantasy football platform. Fifteen clubs said yes. Three said no. Two abstained. The full company was valued at just under €40 million. The deal is financed by a self-liquidating loan repaid through Fantacalcio's own profits. The clubs spend nothing upfront.\nMost coverage filed it as Italian football diversifying its revenues. I think it is considerably more than that.\n\nI wrote the full analysis for my A guy with a scarf Untangling series. Here are the ten things worth considering:\n1️⃣ The Lega just acquired the largest authenticated fan database in Italian football, and it did not have to build it.\n2️⃣ The deal structure tells you as much about Italian football governance as the deal itself.\n3️⃣ The three clubs that voted no share something more interesting than a view on the price.\n4️⃣ Fantacalcio's advertising model is quietly more sophisticated than most sports sponsorship inventory.\n5️⃣ The founders staying is the single most important detail in the entire deal.\n6️⃣ The real risk is not that the Lega will charge for the app. The real risk is that the app starts to feel like the Lega made it.\n7️⃣ The Lega now has the infrastructure — on paper — to consider what managing its own broadcasting looks like.\n8️⃣ Private equity came calling more than once. Ragosta and Cutolo said no every time.\n9️⃣ In 2025, Fantacalcio produced its own scripted web series and attracted a Netflix feature film. The Lega bought a company that was already becoming a media company.\n🔟 Ninety percent market share built over twenty-five years by two guys from Naples who started with a website for their friends. That is not a metric. That is a relationship.\n\nRead more in the article below or find the full deep dive on my A guy with a scarf Substack.\n#SerieA #Fantacalcio #SportsMedia #FanEngagement #SportsBiz\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1628, "rx": 11, "cm": 2, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Fantacalcio", "company"], ["Quadronica", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["seriea", "fantacalcio", "sportsmedia", "fanengagement", "sportsbiz"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1628, 11, 2, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1628, 11, 2, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 227, "id": 7430249852153913344, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7430249852153913344/", "ts": "2026-02-19T14:00:12+00:00", "date": "2026-02-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "10 Things to Consider When Reading the Global SportsTech Report 2026", "snippet": "🔟 📣 10 things to consider When Reading the Global SportsTech Report 2026", "chars": 1401, "text": "🔟 📣 10 things to consider When Reading the Global SportsTech Report 2026\nThe Global SportsTech Report 2026 dropped today. SportsPro and Sportradar surveyed 160+ senior executives across the industry. Big numbers, useful data, worth your time.\n\nAs always I read it through my own lens and turned it into one of my \"10 things to consider\" lists. Here is what I think matters most.\n1️⃣ 82% deploying AI does not mean 82% doing it well\n2️⃣ The 63% signal is the most commercially significant number in the report\n3️⃣ 98% planning to increase AI investment is a planning figure, not a commitment figure\n4️⃣ Content creation and distribution as the top AI priority confirms that the infrastructure layer is commoditising\n5️⃣ Betting and gaming as the leading D2C revenue opportunity deserves scrutiny beyond the headline\n6️⃣ The 78% figure on data analytics and field of play is where AI's quietest transformation is happening\n7️⃣ 5G and connectivity as the top venue priority points to infrastructure as the precondition for everything else\n8️⃣ The report's sponsorship context matters\n9️⃣ The democratisation argument in the report's conclusion is the longest-horizon claim and deserves the most testing\n🔟 An inaugural report establishes a baseline, not a trend\n\n#sportbiz #ai\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1628, "rx": 18, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["10 things to consider", "showcase"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Sportradar", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "ai"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1628, 18, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1628, 18, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 228, "id": 7430190566874742786, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7430190566874742786/", "ts": "2026-02-19T10:04:37+00:00", "date": "2026-02-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ Great to be able to bring my 10 year old son to watch his team AC Milan at SanSiro and feel the vibe - see famous people - wander around t", "snippet": "⚽ Great to be able to bring my 10 year old son to watch his team AC Milan at SanSiro and feel the vibe - see famous people - wander around the stadium and the l", "chars": 1028, "text": "⚽ Great to be able to bring my 10 year old son to watch his team AC Milan at SanSiro and feel the vibe - see famous people - wander around the stadium and the lounges.\n\nI am a horrible sport dad - I always resist to bringing my kids to sport events live, seen too many myself - and being behind the scenes for so long makes it weird when you are in the \"public\" part of the event.\nAlso, in my whole career I never asked for an autograph - something you learn early when you are in constant contact with famous athletes is to be professional and not bother them. Another thing my kids do not appreciate at all.\n\nLast night I gained some points with my son, and took some notes for my reasoning on the multidimensional sport experience matter - see my latest article.\nThe only problem is that he did not want to leave the stadium and this morning immediately asked me when we can go back... 🙄\n\nThanks to Lamberto Siega for making this happen 🥸\n\n[Family policy forbid me to show the happy smiling Miles photos of which I have many]", "qtext": "", "imp": 1154, "rx": 25, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["AC Milan", "company"], ["Lamberto Siega", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1154, 25, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1154, 25, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 229, "id": 7429809269748776960, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7429809269748776960/", "ts": "2026-02-18T08:49:29+00:00", "date": "2026-02-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "❓ Who needs another online course?", "snippet": "❓ Who needs another online course?", "chars": 1276, "text": "❓ Who needs another online course?\nI asked myself that many times before deciding to build this one.\n⚡ Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n📣 Launching today!!!\n\n🏃‍♂️‍➡️ For almost forty years I have worked on the vendor side of sports and media. I have been inside negotiations, delivery crises, long-term partnerships, failed assumptions, successful recoveries. I have seen what looks good in a pitch and what actually survives in production.\n\n🦾 Over the past year I have put serious effort into structuring those learnings.\nStories. Frameworks. Patterns. Decision tools. Hard questions vendors should ask before signing.\n📲 Twelve video lessons. Clear structure. Real situations. Built from experience, refined with care.\n📕 A practical book with 51 lists of \"10 things to consider\" (that makes it 510), with a foreword by Giampiero Rinaudo.\n\nIf you work inside a B2B tech vendor in sports and media, or are approaching the sector this is the material I wish I had earlier in my career.\nIt took time to distil decades into something practical.\n\n🆓 FREE - Limited Access Version (with registration)\n💶 PAID - Full access (book included)\nStart here: https://lnkd.in/dadpU6Uu\n\n#sportstech #vendor\n-----\nA guy with a scarf Untangling AI The One Man Brand FACTORY63", "qtext": "", "imp": 1359, "rx": 24, "cm": 6, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["Giampiero Rinaudo", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"], ["FACTORY63", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "vendor"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider", "Untangling AI"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1359, 24, 6, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1359, 24, 6, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 230, "id": 7429437336825774080, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7429437336825774080/", "ts": "2026-02-17T08:11:33+00:00", "date": "2026-02-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Football Through Their Eyes - A Multidimentional Experience", "snippet": "⚽ Football Through Their Eyes - A Multidimentional Experience", "chars": 2362, "text": "⚽ Football Through Their Eyes - A Multidimentional Experience\n🏃‍♂️‍➡️ What Three Ten-Year-Olds Taught Me About the Sport I Thought I Knew\n\nA personal post. Observing my son relationship with football and applying a bit of my distorted sport and media glasses, I noticed that his approach has a wide 360 degrees view on the sport.\n\nIt's not only like we said in the past \"play and watch\" or \"viewers/participants\", it's a more multidimensional experience.\n\nSo as friends were visiting this weekend I took 15 minutes of their playing time and sit them done for a recorded audio interview based on a set of questions.\nMy angle was no to telkl a story of kids in football (I wrote a fun novel about that) but to grab insights for our industry.\n\nThey told me the why, how and what of football in their lives - quite a significant footprint at this age.\nWhat came out is an experience that includes:\n⚽ Playing on the pitch (5 days a week)\n- Training, matches, teammates, routines, and the physical feeling of the game. The centre of the ecosystem.\n⚽ Watching football\n- Live matches, highlights, and moments shared at home that extend what happens on the field.\n⚽ Digital football and Gaming\n- EA FC sessions where tactics, players, and imagination continue the experience between real games.\n⚽ Physical games\n Subbuteo, tabletop football, and small tactile recreations that keep the game present away from screens.\n⚽ Invented football\n- Calciomercato, Fantacalcio, Fields drawn with a pen on paper, improvised rules, games created from imagination and friendship.\n⚽ Collecting and stickers\n Panini albums, trading duplicates, discovering teams and players through images and memory.\n⚽ Stadium experience\n- Atmosphere, chants, sensory moments, and shared emotional release.\n⚽ Social football\n- Conversations at school and training, jokes, debates, and shared language before social media fully enters their lives.\n⚽ Everyday micro-moments\n- Small rituals, spontaneous discussions, and tiny gestures that keep football alive throughout the week.\n\nConsider the age - these kids do not have access to social yet - teenagers will have a different multidimensional experience shape.\n\n[Italiano] Nell'articolo c'e' anche la versione in Italiano.\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 3002, "rx": 22, "cm": 13, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3002, 22, 13, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3002, 22, 13, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 231, "id": 7429074774271692800, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7429074774271692800/", "ts": "2026-02-16T08:10:51+00:00", "date": "2026-02-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Sky Italia SVG Forum 2026 Where Sport, Storytelling, and GenAI Meet", "snippet": "🎙️ I had the pleasure of being invited to speak at the Sky Italia × SVG Europe Italy Forum, a gathering that brought together broadcasters, clubs, technology le", "chars": 2584, "text": "🎙️ I had the pleasure of being invited to speak at the Sky Italia × SVG Europe Italy Forum, a gathering that brought together broadcasters, clubs, technology leaders, and creators to reflect on how sport is evolving across production, storytelling, and technology.\n\n🤖 🎙️ My own session focused on how sports can drive value with GenAI, looking at infrastructure, production, and interface as three layers where AI can enable creativity, scalability, and new storytelling possibilities while keeping human judgment at the centre.\n\n📺 Federico Ferri opened a strong editorial perspective on Sky Sport’s strategic priorities, from the role of premium rights like the Champions League to the importance of multisport continuity and long-form storytelling in shaping the broadcaster’s identity.\n\n🤖 Vincenzo Lagattolla and Gabriele Ubertini, with Pierluigi Magliano, explored how Sky Italia is approaching GenAI, automation, and new production workflows, balancing experimentation with the realities of operating at scale across platforms.\n\n📲 The digital content panel moderated by Andrea M. Michelozzi brought together Giacomo Margutti from Juventus, Stefania Greco Lucchina from Sky Italia, and Lamberto Siega to discuss always-on publishing, data-driven editorial choices, and the changing grammar of fan engagement across social, OTT, and emerging platforms.\n\n⛷️ A particularly insightful moment came from Guillermo Jimenez Navarro, Director of Broadcast Engineering at Olympic Broadcasting Services, who shared an inside view of how OBS is preparing production for Milano Cortina 2026. The conversation touched on drone-based storytelling, AI-assisted workflows, advanced metadata, and the growing role of IP and cloud infrastructure, showing how Olympic production is evolving into a flexible platform designed to support multiple formats, rights holders, and new audience behaviours.\n\n🥸 What stayed with me most from the day was the sense that sport remains a powerful anchor in a media environment that keeps accelerating. Technology is moving fast, but identity, narrative, and creative intent still define how audiences connect with what we build.\nBut also the interesting people I met at the event.\n\nFull piece here if you want to dive deeper into the conversations and perspectives shared across the forum.\n\nManuela Callea George Bevir Popi Bonnici Luisella Fusco Fabio Guadagnini Luciano Consigli Dante de Iulio\n#ai #sport #digital #tv #olympics\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1563, "rx": 40, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sky Italia", "company"], ["SVG Europe", "company"], ["Vincenzo Lagattolla", "person"], ["Gabriele Ubertini", "person"], ["Pierluigi Magliano", "person"], ["Andrea M. Michelozzi", "person"], ["Giacomo Margutti", "person"], ["Stefania Greco Lucchina", "person"], ["Lamberto Siega", "person"], ["Guillermo Jimenez Navarro", "person"], ["Olympic Broadcasting Services", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"], ["George Bevir", "person"], ["Popi Bonnici", "person"], ["Luisella Fusco", "person"], ["Fabio Guadagnini", "person"], ["Luciano Consigli", "person"], ["Dante de Iulio", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "sport", "digital", "tv", "olympics", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1563, 40, 3, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1563, 40, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 232, "id": 7427974164122243072, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7427974164122243072/", "ts": "2026-02-13T07:17:25+00:00", "date": "2026-02-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Prediction Markets Arrive at All-Star Weekend, and Sport May Be Entering a New Layer of Fan Economics", "snippet": "🏀 🤑 After two days spent at sports tech events, my attention today went straight to the NBA All-Star Technology Summit in Los Angeles. An invite-only panel brin", "chars": 1103, "text": "🏀 🤑 After two days spent at sports tech events, my attention today went straight to the NBA All-Star Technology Summit in Los Angeles. An invite-only panel bringing together Kalshi and Polymarket founders with leaders from FanDuel, DraftKings Inc., MGM Resorts, and Sportradar puts prediction markets firmly on the executive agenda.\n\nI would genuinely love to be in that room, listening to how operators, leagues, and tech leaders describe responsibility, integrity, and opportunity around probabilistic engagement.\n\nWhat makes this moment interesting is the context. The conversation sits alongside AI, investment, and platform strategy discussions, which tells you how leagues are framing the topic. Prediction markets are becoming part of the broader dialogue about fan participation, data, and governance.\nI’ve written a short piece with a factual appendix on the summit and other league tech forums if you want the deeper context.\n\nNational Basketball Association (NBA)\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1750, "rx": 12, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Kalshi", "company"], ["Polymarket", "company"], ["FanDuel", "company"], ["DraftKings Inc.", "company"], ["Sportradar", "company"], ["National Basketball Association (NBA)", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1750, 12, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1750, 12, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 233, "id": 7427604715472916480, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7427604715472916480/", "ts": "2026-02-12T06:49:22+00:00", "date": "2026-02-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Getting ready for the first on stage presence of the year at Sky Italia in Milano for the SVG Europe event.", "snippet": "Getting ready for the first on stage presence of the year at Sky Italia in Milano for the SVG Europe event.", "chars": 931, "text": "Getting ready for the first on stage presence of the year at Sky Italia in Milano for the SVG Europe event.\n\n🎙️ How sports can drive value with GenAI 🤖\nA session examining the practical, scalable impact of GenAI in sports media. Topics to include: automated highlights and multilingual commentary, gen AI-enhanced metadata and rights exploitation, operational efficiencies for live production and post, plus ethical considerations, quality control, and governance.\n\nI will be talking about going into offense not defense, why sport may be the winner, and ask a fundamental question to the audience \"How do we win in this phase, and what will be our role in that?\"\nHope to see you in Milano today.\n\nPierluigi Magliano Vincenzo Lagattolla Giacomo Margutti Manuela Callea Lamberto Siega George Bevir\n\n------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 991, "rx": 24, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Sky Italia", "company"], ["SVG Europe", "company"], ["Pierluigi Magliano", "person"], ["Vincenzo Lagattolla", "person"], ["Giacomo Margutti", "person"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"], ["Lamberto Siega", "person"], ["George Bevir", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 991, 24, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 991, 24, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 234, "id": 7427341385294475264, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7427341385294475264/", "ts": "2026-02-11T13:22:59+00:00", "date": "2026-02-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ Today at Juventus Forward - Innovation day with Juventus Football Club Carolina Chiappero and Giorgio Chiellini.", "snippet": "⚽ Today at Juventus Forward - Innovation day with Juventus Football Club Carolina Chiappero and Giorgio Chiellini.", "chars": 1228, "text": "⚽ Today at Juventus Forward - Innovation day with Juventus Football Club Carolina Chiappero and Giorgio Chiellini.\n\n🤖 Forward. In Play – Innovation Takes the Field, where Juventus introduced the new Forward Squad, a group of startups selected to collaborate with the club this season across performance, media, AI, and fan experience initiatives.\n\nThe Forward Squad brings together a wide mix of technologies and perspectives. The startups involved include Ochy, KineMo, Valor Vision, SPORTHYPE (Holytics), Penguinpass, Lingopal, YouDub Studio, Profound, i-Braintech, Soccerment, and DHTA Sports. Each of them will work with Juventus to test, validate and develop projects directly inside the club’s innovation framework.\n\n⚙️ The range of solutions covered by the startups reflects where football innovation is moving today, from biomechanics and neurotechnology to multilingual media workflows, AI-driven visibility, digital human assets, and new forms of fan engagement and hospitality.\n\nCurious to see how these collaborations evolve during the season and which ideas will scale beyond the pilot phase.\n\nFederico Smanio A guy with a scarf Cristina Demarie\n#SportsTech #FootballInnovation #AI #JuventusForward #SportsMediaTech", "qtext": "", "imp": 3761, "rx": 75, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Juventus Football Club", "company"], ["Carolina Chiappero", "person"], ["Giorgio Chiellini", "person"], ["Ochy", "company"], ["KineMo", "company"], ["Valor Vision", "company"], ["SPORTHYPE", "company"], ["Penguinpass", "company"], ["Lingopal", "company"], ["YouDub Studio", "company"], ["Profound", "company"], ["Braintech", "company"], ["Soccerment", "company"], ["DHTA Sports", "company"], ["Federico Smanio", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Cristina Demarie", "person"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "footballinnovation", "ai", "juventusforward", "sportsmediatech"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3758, 75, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3761, 75, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 235, "id": 7427336055311351808, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7427336055311351808/", "ts": "2026-02-11T13:01:48+00:00", "date": "2026-02-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚽ 🦾 ⚙️ For years in sports and media tech we have debated fan engagement, D2C models, rights value, platform shifts, AI, monetisation.", "snippet": "⚽ 🦾 ⚙️ For years in sports and media tech we have debated fan engagement, D2C models, rights value, platform shifts, AI, monetisation.", "chars": 1451, "text": "⚽ 🦾 ⚙️ For years in sports and media tech we have debated fan engagement, D2C models, rights value, platform shifts, AI, monetisation.\nWe rarely debate the vendor side. 🥸\nHow decisions are really made inside vendor companies.\n💡 Why good technology still struggles.\n💡 Where margin disappears.\n💡 How credibility is built, and lost, over time.\n💡 What clients are actually buying when they say they are buying innovation.\n\nAfter almost four decades on the vendor side of this industry, across product, delivery, partnerships and strategy, I decided to address that gap, with the limited skills and tools I have.\nI will be launching soon:\n🎓 A 12-lesson course: Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\n💡A book with 40+ lists of \"10 things to consider...\" in sports and media tech\nFocused on judgment, risk, delivery under constraint, commercial reality, renewals, internal misalignment, and long-term credibility.\nIt is an attempt to document patterns I have seen repeat for decades.\n\nIf you work on the vendor side, or you lead a team inside one, I hope this contributes to a more open, mature conversation about our role in the ecosystem.\nBecause the fan experience matters.\nBut so does the quality and sustainability of the companies building the technology behind it.\nIf you are interested, message me or comment below and I will share details.\n⚡ Coming soon!\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#sportstech #vendors #clients #innovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 202, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "vendors", "clients", "innovation"], "series": ["10 Things To Consider"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 201, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 202, 6, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 236, "id": 7426624284388909056, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7426624284388909056/", "ts": "2026-02-09T13:53:29+00:00", "date": "2026-02-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ 🏅 📺 Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "snippet": "🎙️ 🏅 📺 Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media", "chars": 1245, "text": "🎙️ 🏅 📺 Becoming a Better B2B Tech Vendor in Sports and Media\nComing soon! 🔜 Launch the week of Feb 15 2026\n\nI’ve spent decades inside sports and media technology, working across product, delivery, partnerships, and strategy.\nI’ve been on the vendor side of the table for a long time, and I’ve seen how good teams succeed, stall, or quietly disappear, often for reasons that have little to do with the quality of their technology.\n\nThis is a 12-lesson online video course designed for people working in sports and media tech, especially on the vendor side.\n\nFor new entrants, it offers a clear way to understand the landscape, the dynamics, and the mistakes that are easy to make early on.\n\nFor people already in the industry, and for companies, it works as a checkpoint, a way to reflect on how you operate today and whether your current model is holding up. The course focuses on judgment, delivery under constraint, and patterns that repeat across projects, clients, and operating models.\n\nYou’ll find practical frameworks, real situations, and lessons learned you can use immediately.\n\nThis is about making better decisions, staying credible, and lasting in an industry that rarely slows down.\n\n#onlinecourse #sports #media #tech #vendor #B2B", "qtext": "", "imp": 948, "rx": 25, "cm": 1, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [], "tags": ["onlinecourse", "sports", "media", "tech", "vendor", "b2b"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 538, "watch_time_s": 7800, "avg_watch_s": 14, "saves": 1, "sends": 1, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 948, 25, 1, 5], ["2026-10-17", 948, 25, 1, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 237, "id": 7425471363655589888, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7425471363655589888/", "ts": "2026-02-06T09:32:11+00:00", "date": "2026-02-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The Week AI Broke Wall Street and the Gloves Came Off", "snippet": "🤖 ⛷️ 🏈 While our eyes are all on the Winter Olympic and the Super Bowl, AI-land decided to make news for real.", "chars": 1720, "text": "🤖 ⛷️ 🏈 While our eyes are all on the Winter Olympic and the Super Bowl, AI-land decided to make news for real.\n\nUntangling AI explains you what happened:\nAnthropic dropped Claude Opus 4.6 and expanded Cowork with open-source enterprise plugins. Within hours, $285 billion in software market cap was gone. Thomson Reuters had its worst day on record. LegalZoom, RELX, Gartner, WPP — all hit double-digit drops. The financial press coined a new term: the SaaSpocalypse.\nMinutes later, OpenAI fired back with GPT-5.3 Codex — the first AI model that helped build itself — carrying an unprecedented cybersecurity warning label.\nAnd then both companies took it to the Super Bowl.\n\nI've written an article breaking down what happened and what it means for sports and media technology:\n🤖 Agent teams are here — Opus 4.6 can coordinate multiple AI agents across complex jobs simultaneously\n💥 The SaaS model is under direct threat — one product demo triggered a $285 billion sell-off\n⚔️ AI coding just became a product category war — Anthropic and OpenAI launched competing models within minutes of each other\n📺 The ads-in-AI question matters — Anthropic committed to ad-free, OpenAI is building an ad business into ChatGPT\n💰 IPO pressure is reshaping everything — both companies racing to demonstrate dominance ahead of expected listings\n\nThe competition between these two companies is now driving the fastest capability improvements we've seen in AI. For anyone building or buying technology in sports and media, the decisions you make about which ecosystem to back are about to compound fast.\n\n------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 643, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Untangling AI", "showcase"], ["Anthropic", "company"], ["OpenAI", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ad"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 643, 11, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 643, 11, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 238, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-02-06", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Retention Zone", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #5", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #5", "chars": 1048, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #5\nDemand generation slows down and deepens\nDemand generation was discussed as an area where volume has grown while attention has become harder to earn. In my conversation with Gilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, and Joachim Bergman, COO, the focus shifted toward how growth is actually created today.\n📩 Automated outreach fills inboxes and feeds\n 🤝 Preparation and relevance shape real engagement\n 🗣️ Conversations and referrals carry more weight\nAs channels become saturated, teams spend more time preparing fewer, better interactions.\n🔍 Research and insight guide outreach\n 👂 Listening to customers informs next steps\n 🔁 Retention and acquisition become tightly connected\nGrowth increasingly comes from existing customers, their experience, and their willingness to advocate.\nDemand generation works best when it is built on trust, context, and continuity.\n\n-----\n🎧 Retention Zone – Episode 78\n Presented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf\n\n#subscription #retention #demandgen #DTC #B2B", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 18, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscription", "retention", "demandgen", "dtc", "b2b"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 18, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", null, 18, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 239, "id": 7425290455057244160, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7425290455057244160/", "ts": "2026-02-05T21:33:19+00:00", "date": "2026-02-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Coming soon...", "snippet": "Coming soon...", "chars": 14, "text": "Coming soon...", "qtext": "", "imp": 506, "rx": 15, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 505, 15, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 506, 15, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 240, "id": 7425123176352960512, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7425123176352960512/", "ts": "2026-02-05T10:28:37+00:00", "date": "2026-02-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Atlético de Madrid and Mr. Red, bringing marketing closer to the game", "snippet": "🔴 Club Atlético de Madrid brings marketing closer to football reality", "chars": 1927, "text": "🔴 Club Atlético de Madrid brings marketing closer to football reality\nThe launch of Mr. Red, Atlético’s in-house creative and marketing agency, is a move worth paying attention to.\nWhat stands out in Valerio Gori’s message is the emphasis on context:\n 👥 people who know the club\n ⚽ people who understand football dynamics\n 🤝 people who can connect brands to the game in a credible way\n\nFootball marketing lives inside tight calendars, emotional swings, and constant scrutiny. Placing creative judgment inside the club changes how ideas are shaped and how quickly they reach the pitch, the partner, and the fan.\nThe slightly ironic launch video was a nice signal too. Self-aware, confident, and very much rooted in the club’s identity.\n❓ Will other clubs follow this path and bring creative capability closer to the game itself?\n🔴 Atlético de Madrid brings marketing closer to football reality\n\nThe launch of Mr. Red, Atlético’s in-house creative and marketing agency, is a move worth paying attention to.\nWhat stands out in Valerio Gori’s message is the emphasis on context:\n 👥 people who know the club\n ⚽ people who understand football dynamics\n 🤝 people who can connect brands to the game in a credible way\n\nFootball marketing lives inside tight calendars, emotional swings, and constant scrutiny. Placing creative judgment inside the club changes how ideas are shaped and how quickly they reach the pitch, the partner, and the fan.\nThe smart ironic launch video was a nice signal too. Self-aware, confident, and very much rooted in the club’s identity.\n\n❓ Will other clubs follow this path and bring creative capability closer to the game itself?\nThe reaction from AS Monaco suggests this is a conversation already happening across clubs.\n👏 Well done to Valerio and the Atlético de Madrid team.\n\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.", "qtext": "", "imp": 1335, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Club Atlético de Madrid", "company"], ["Valerio Gori", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1332, 11, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1335, 11, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 241, "id": 7424730347747217408, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7424730347747217408/", "ts": "2026-02-04T08:27:39+00:00", "date": "2026-02-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What January 2026 Reveals About Sport Marketing", "snippet": "📅 January sport marketing, read from the inside.", "chars": 1053, "text": "📅 January sport marketing, read from the inside.\nI spent time with the January 2026 activations curated by Veronica Zanchi and tried to read them at decision level rather than campaign level.\n\nA few signals stood out 👇\n🕒 Context over calendar\nExperiences designed around a specific event, place, and moment.\n👟 Product carries meaning\nDesign, materials, and performance cues doing the work.\n🏥 B2B with cultural confidence\nFIGS and Lindsey Vonn using elite sport to honour professional identity.\n🧠 Innovation on the body\nOMORPHO showing how performance ideas live in materials and biomechanics.\n🔁 Loyalty as commitment\nDecathlon turning purchase into a behavioural choice.\nQuiet decisions. Clear intent.\nWorth studying.\n\nBrands mentioned: Lacoste, Afloat, Nike, SKIMS, Issey Miyake, ASICS, FIGS, OMORPHO, Decathlon\n\nFull post by Veronica Zanchi 👇 in the article.\n#SportsMarketing #BrandActivations #ExperienceDesign #AGWAS\n-----\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1186, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Veronica Zanchi", "person"], ["FIGS", "company"], ["OMORPHO", "company"], ["Decathlon", "company"], ["Lacoste", "company"], ["Afloat", "company"], ["Nike", "company"], ["SKIMS", "company"], ["Issey Miyake", "company"], ["ASICS", "company"], ["FIGS", "company"], ["OMORPHO", "company"], ["Decathlon", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmarketing", "brandactivations", "experiencedesign", "agwas", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1185, 10, 1, 3], ["2026-10-17", 1186, 10, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 242, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-02-04", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Retention Zone", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #4", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #4", "chars": 972, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #4\nTrust becomes a structural issue\nTrust came up repeatedly in my conversation with Gilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, and Joachim Bergman, COO, as something that now sits at the core of subscription businesses.\n🤖 AI-generated content is increasingly realistic\n 🎥 Demos and marketing assets look polished by default\n 🔍 Verification and authenticity matter more than appearance\nAs signals become easier to fabricate, trust shifts toward transparency and verifiable experience.\n🔓 Clear processes build confidence\n 👥 Real customer references carry weight\n 🔐 Security and payments are part of the trust equation\nTrust now affects how users subscribe, how long they stay, and how willing they are to deepen engagement.\nRetention improves when trust is built into the system, not added at the surface.\n\n🎧Retention Zone – Episode 78\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf\n#subscription #retention #trust #DTC #AI", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 9, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscription", "retention", "trust", "dtc", "ai"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 9, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", null, 9, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 243, "id": 7424008903941820416, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7424008903941820416/", "ts": "2026-02-02T08:40:54+00:00", "date": "2026-02-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Trying to understand the YouTube vs. BARB measurement hiccup", "snippet": "📺 📲 YouTube vs. BARB: Another Clearing in the Measurement Jungle 🌳", "chars": 1510, "text": "📺 📲 YouTube vs. BARB: Another Clearing in the Measurement Jungle 🌳\nNew article on A Guy with a Scarf 👇\nIn late January, YouTube sent cease-and-desist letters asking BARB (UK's TV measurement body) to stop reporting YouTube viewing on TV screens.\nThe data is paused. The debate is not.\n\nWhat happened 📊\nBARB had begun measuring YouTube channels on TV sets using the same panel framework as broadcasters. Goal: one shared view of TV-screen viewing.\nGoogle cited platform terms and creator attribution. Broadcasters and advertisers raised transparency concerns.\n\nAs Jen Topping wrote on Business of TV (hat tip Jo Redfern 🔜 MIPLONDON), we're \"swimming in a miasma\" when it comes to measurement.\nThe episode reveals:\n🔒 Measurement exists where access is granted—and disappears where it's withdrawn\n⚔️ Two cultures colliding: broadcast's independent governance vs. platforms' internal control\n📈 Advertisers want unified reach, but comparability remains conditional\n🏛️ Largest platforms control both data and the rules around its use\nThe clearing, not the end 🗺️\n\nViewing behaviour changes faster than the rules used to describe it.\nThe work ahead: understanding who draws the maps, who controls the paths, and what happens when familiar routes suddenly close.\nFull analysis + BARB deep dive 👇[link]\n\n#MediaMeasurement #TVAdvertising #YouTube #BARB #StreamingTV\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1487, "rx": 19, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["Jen Topping", "person"], ["Jo Redfern 🔜 MIPLONDON", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["mediameasurement", "tvadvertising", "youtube", "barb", "streamingtv", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1479, 19, 1, 2], ["2026-10-17", 1487, 19, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 244, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-02-02", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Retention Zone", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #3", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #3", "chars": 1085, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #3\n🧠 🤖 Human value moves up the stack\nA recurring theme in my conversation with Gilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, and Joachim Bergman, COO, was how automation is reshaping where people create value in subscription businesses.\n\n⚙️ Analysis and reporting are increasingly automated\n 📊 Synthesis and summarisation happen in real time\n 🧠 Judgment and system thinking become the differentiators\n\nAs routine work is absorbed by AI, expectations rise for how teams design, connect, and simplify systems.\n🔍 Understanding architecture matters more\n 🧩 Reducing unnecessary complexity becomes a skill\n 🎯 Decisions carry more weight across product and retention\nThe shift is not only about efficiency. It changes how time is spent and where attention goes.\n\nRetention benefits when people are freed to focus on interpretation, priorities, and long-term choices rather than producing reports or chasing signals.\n\n🎧 Retention Zone – Episode 78\n#subscription #retention #AI #DTC #product\n------\nPresented by Cleeng\nHosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscription", "retention", "ai", "dtc", "product"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 11, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", null, 11, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 245, "id": 7423298285055934464, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7423298285055934464/", "ts": "2026-01-31T09:37:09+00:00", "date": "2026-01-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🥶 January was a full sprint.", "snippet": "🥶 January was a full sprint.", "chars": 2834, "text": "🥶 January was a full sprint.\nI started 2026 in “deep dive mode”, moving across venues, rights, regulation, ad systems, creator economy finance, and the capital picture behind sports tech.\n\nHere are the posts that travelled furthest, and the questions they pulled me into.\n🏟️ Checkout-free stadiums and the hidden tax of queues\n The Tennessee Titans and Amazon Just Walk Out deal is bigger than concessions. It’s a venue design signal about throughput, time, and the revenue that disappears when friction becomes normal.\n⚽ 🇮🇹 Lega Serie A audiences are up, and the reasons are messy\n I rebuilt the picture from the source PDFs and a match-by-match model, then looked at what Total Audience does and does not capture, what free-to-air windows change, and why fixture quality still drives the top of the table.\n📺 🇮🇹 How Sky Italia and DAZN actually sell Serie A advertising\n Three commercial documents, two rights positions, two industrial playbooks. I tried to understand how pack design, matchday classification, delivery logic, and sales structure translate into two coherent ad systems.\n🇮🇹 ⚽ 📡 Piracy Shield, Cloudflare, and a debate that drifted into politics\n AGCOM, Serie A, and Cloudflare turned one enforcement dispute into a wider argument about infrastructure, due process, and collateral damage. I’m interested in the real trade-offs, and uncomfortable when the framing becomes pure symbolism.\n Small bonus, this piece also got picked up, I was quoted by Jacob Feldman at Sportico.\n🤑 📲 Prediction markets entering sport, slowly and visibly\n DAZN plus Polymarket felt like a small step, but an important one. A new “probability layer” is creeping into the broadcast experience. I mapped the players, the regulatory tension, and the risks worth naming early.\n🙌🏼📲 Khaby Lame going public, and what people misread\n The point was never “exit”. It was structure. An all-stock deal, public-market exposure, and a creator stepping into governance and scrutiny that most of the creator economy still avoids.\n🧠🤖 The AI retention story I can’t unsee\n The more I use AI tools, the more I think “memory” becomes the real lock-in mechanism, because effort accumulates. That single shift changes how product teams should think about retention.\n🤖🧠 Five takeaways from the Drake Star Sports Tech Report 2025\n A quick synthesis of what the data suggests about institutional capital, consolidation, larger bets, prediction markets as infrastructure, and why predictability is getting priced in.\n\nIf you read one of these and disagreed, or think I missed a key angle, tell me. The goal is to get closer to how the systems really work.\nRead all by subscribing to the A guy with a scarf newsletter on LinkedIn\nhttps://lnkd.in/ddmvMF-Q\nor on Substack\n\n#SportsBusiness #SportsTech #Streaming #MediaRights #SerieA #Advertising #Stadiums #AI #Creators", "qtext": "", "imp": 352, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Tennessee Titans", "company"], ["Amazon", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Cloudflare", "company"], ["Jacob Feldman", "person"], ["Sportico", "company"], ["Polymarket", "company"], ["Drake Star", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["LinkedIn", "company"], ["Substack", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbusiness", "sportstech", "streaming", "mediarights", "seriea", "advertising", "stadiums", "ai", "creators"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "text", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 351, 4, 0, 3], ["2026-10-17", 352, 4, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 246, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2026-01-31", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Retention Zone", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #2", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #2", "chars": 1128, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78 | Trend #2\n2️⃣ Subscription models are becoming modular\nOne of the strongest themes from my conversation with Gilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, and Joachim Bergman, COO, was how subscription models are evolving in practice.\n\n📦 Monthly subscriptions now sit inside broader access systems\n 🎟️ Day passes and seasonal access are back in the mix\n 📺 Pay-per-view and platform-specific bundles are increasingly common\nThis modularity creates flexibility for users.\n It also adds layers of complexity for teams.\n💡 Every offer shapes user behavior over time\n 💰 Pricing decisions influence retention, not just conversion\n 🔗 Coherence across access types matters\nWhat stood out is how pricing still tends to focus on acquisition. Using pricing deliberately to support long-term engagement remains a major opportunity.\nIn modular subscription systems, retention improves when the experience feels intentional and connected across products, time windows, and platforms.\n\n🎧 Retention Zone – Episode 78\n Presented by Cleeng Hosted by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf\n\n#subscription #retention #AI #DTC #data", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 16, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscription", "retention", "ai", "dtc", "data"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 16, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", null, 16, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 247, "id": 7422927841627111425, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7422927841627111425/", "ts": "2026-01-30T09:05:08+00:00", "date": "2026-01-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Five things I learned from the Drake Star Sports Tech Report 2025", "snippet": "🤖 🧠 5 things I learned from the Drake Star Sports Tech Report 2025", "chars": 1603, "text": "🤖 🧠 5 things I learned from the Drake Star Sports Tech Report 2025\nA very dense report, packed with data. Beyond the headline numbers, it offers a clear picture of how capital is behaving around sports tech today.\n\nHere are five takeaways that stood out.\n1️⃣ Sports tech has become institutional\n Deal volume and deal size point to a sector now dominated by strategics, private equity, and late-stage investors focused on scale, governance, and operating discipline.\n2️⃣ Consolidation is the main growth lever\n M&A activity shows buyers assembling platforms across media, youth sports, analytics, and venues, rather than acquiring isolated products or features.\n3️⃣ Capital is concentrating in fewer, larger bets\n Private financings reached record levels while average deal size increased, with more capital flowing to companies able to operate globally and absorb regulatory complexity.\n4️⃣ Prediction markets moved to the core\n Large investments in prediction platforms signal their role as probability and data infrastructure, with sports providing frequent, structured inputs.\n5️⃣ Predictability now drives valuation\n Across private and public markets, businesses with repeatable demand, clear cash flows, and operational resilience attract the strongest interest.\nHappy to discuss what this means for founders, vendors, and operators navigating the next phase of sports tech.\n\n#SportsTech #SportsBusiness #InvestmentTrends #MediaAndTech #AGWAS\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1415, "rx": 23, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Drake Star", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "sportsbusiness", "investmenttrends", "mediaandtech", "agwas", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1414, 23, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1415, 23, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 248, "id": 7422531196355641345, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7422531196355641345/", "ts": "2026-01-29T06:49:01+00:00", "date": "2026-01-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Khaby Lame isn’t Exiting. He Is Re-Positioning and Going Public", "snippet": "🙌🏼 📲 Khaby Lame isn’t Exiting. He Is Re-Positioning and Going Public", "chars": 1992, "text": "🙌🏼 📲 Khaby Lame isn’t Exiting. He Is Re-Positioning and Going Public\n\n😵 I don’t usually react to misinterpreted news on LinkedIn. But this time the superficiality was hard to ignore.\n😱 Most coverage missed the central point.\nKhaby Lame comes from Chivasso, just outside Torino, where I’ve lived my whole life. That proximity alone made me look twice at the recent headlines about his “exit”.\n❌ This was not an exit.\n✅  It was an all-stock deal.\n\n🏦 Khaby Lame merged his operating company into the Nasdaq-listed Rich Sparkle in an all-stock deal, becoming a controlling shareholder, with no cash exit and his future value now tied to public market performance.\n\nHere’s what actually matters 👇\n💵  Was this a cash deal?\n No. It was share-for-share. No big cheque, just equity in a public company.\n💵 Does the $900m+ number mean money earned?\n No. It’s a valuation reference, not cash in the bank.\n💵 Did Khaby sell everything?\n No. He owned 49% of the operating company. Outcomes are proportional.\n💵 Did this reduce his risk?\n No. It increased exposure. His future is now tied to public market performance.\n💵 Why go public this way?\n Because merging into an already listed company is a shortcut to market visibility, without a traditional IPO.\nOne more thing most people skipped: this makes Khaby a controlling shareholder in a Nasdaq-listed entity, with governance, disclosure, and investor pressure that creators rarely face.\n\nThis is not a fairytale ending.\n It’s a repositioning, from platform-native creator growth to public-market scrutiny.\n\nI wrote a long-form piece breaking this down calmly and structurally, including why Rich Sparkle matters and why this pattern keeps appearing in the creator economy.\nThis story is about what the creator economy is becoming when it runs out of simple paths forward.\nRead the full article below👇\n\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1607, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1606, 16, 2, 4], ["2026-10-17", 1607, 16, 2, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 249, "id": 7422205562316013568, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7422205562316013568/", "ts": "2026-01-28T09:15:03+00:00", "date": "2026-01-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 78: Gilles Domartini & Joachim Bergman – Six Retention Realities for Subscription Businesses in 2026", "snippet": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78", "chars": 1670, "text": "🎙️ Retention Zone – Episode 78\n Six retention realities for subscription businesses in 2026\n\nTo open 2026, I sat down with Gilles Domartini, CEO of Cleeng, and Joachim Bergman, COO, to talk about where subscription businesses are actually going.\nNo hype. No generic AI takes.\n Just product reality and field experience.\n\nThe six retention realities that matter 👇\n1️⃣ Understanding replaces dashboards\n AI shifts the work from reading charts to surfacing insight and action, if data is structured properly.\n2️⃣ Subscriptions become modular\n Monthly plans now sit alongside passes, seasons, pay-per-view, and platform bundles. More choice, more complexity, direct impact on retention.\n3️⃣ Human value moves up the stack\n Automation handles analysis. Humans focus on judgment, system design, and simplification.\n4️⃣ Trust becomes structural\n When AI looks real, polish stops working. Transparency, authenticity, and proof matter more.\n5️⃣ Demand gen slows down and deepens\n Automated outreach is saturated. Preparation, relevance, referrals, and real conversations cut through.\n6️⃣ Payments fade into the background\n Smoother, more secure, more local. When payments work, users forget them. When they fail, retention drops.\n\nBottom line\n Retention is no longer a funnel metric.\n It’s the result of disciplined choices across product, pricing, trust, payments, organization, and human judgment.\n\n🎧 Retention Zone – Ep. 78\nAlexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res\n#subscription #streaming #DTC #D2C\n-----\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis – A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 338, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscription", "streaming", "dtc", "d2c"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 337, 6, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 338, 6, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 250, "id": 7421844927078313984, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7421844927078313984/", "ts": "2026-01-27T09:22:01+00:00", "date": "2026-01-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Cronache di Spogliatoio: An Italian Story", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 💶  Cronache di Spogliatoio: An Italian Story", "chars": 1624, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 💶  Cronache di Spogliatoio: An Italian Story\nI’ve been writing more about Italy lately, because some local media moves say something broader about where sports content is growing.\nThis is one of them.\nCronache di spogliatoio S.p.A., one of Italy’s most recognisable digital-native football media brands, has joined Be Water Group, entering the same editorial ecosystem as Chora & Will Media.\n\n💶 Deal facts\n ➡️ Acquisition announced in January 2026\n ➡️ Be Water acquired 100% of Cronache di Spogliatoio\n ➡️ Reported valuation around €7.5m\n ➡️ Transaction structured with cash and Be Water shares\n ➡️ Founders Stefano Bagnasco and Giulio Incagli retain a small equity stake and remain involved\n ➡️ Previous institutional round led by Planven, with GEDI Gruppo Editoriale as minority shareholder\n\n📰  In the article, I look at\n ⚽ Why football remains Italy’s dominant content category\n ⚽ How Cronache was built around football culture, not football news\n ⚽ The role of format, storytelling, and platform-native language\n ⚽ Why this growth happened outside the traditional media rights system\n ⚽ What this move signals about second-tier, creator-led media consolidation\n ⚽ Why Italy is starting to follow patterns already seen in the US and UK\nIt’s a small story, but a useful one, especially if you care about how sports media evolves beyond rights, broadcasters, and platforms.\nFull article below 👇🏼\n\n#sportsmedia #football #media #creatoreconomy #italy #digitalmedia\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1249, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Cronache di spogliatoio S.p.A.", "company"], ["Be Water Group", "company"], ["Chora & Will Media", "company"], ["Stefano Bagnasco", "person"], ["Giulio Incagli", "person"], ["Planven", "company"], ["GEDI Gruppo Editoriale", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "football", "media", "creatoreconomy", "italy", "digitalmedia", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1248, 11, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1249, 11, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 251, "id": 7421566516560748544, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7421566516560748544/", "ts": "2026-01-26T14:55:43+00:00", "date": "2026-01-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Come SKY e DAZN Vendono la Pubblicità per  Serie A - Deep Dive", "snippet": "[ITALIAN 🇮🇹 VERSION]", "chars": 2219, "text": "[ITALIAN 🇮🇹 VERSION]\n📺 Come Sky Italia e DAZN vendono la ⚽ Lega Serie A – Un’analisi approfondita\nSono partito da una cosa molto semplice 👇\n Stavo cercando i dati di audience di Sky sulla Serie A, soprattutto per fare un controllo di realtà su alcune affermazioni che continuavo a vedere ripetute nelle conversazioni di settore. Durante quella ricerca mi sono imbattuto in un documento commerciale completo di Sky Media Italia, liberamente accessibile, dettagliato e chiaramente pensato per le agenzie, non per il pubblico generalista 📄\nQuella scoperta ha acceso la curiosità.\n Se Sky aveva un playbook pubblicitario così visibile e strutturato, com’era messo il lato DAZN 🤔\nSeguendo questa domanda sono arrivato a due documenti commerciali completi sulla Serie A pubblicati tramite Digitalia ’08, concessionaria pubblicitaria di DAZN in Italia. Leggere tutti e tre i documenti uno dopo l’altro si è trasformato in un esercizio di apprendimento inatteso su come la pubblicità legata alla Serie A viene oggi realmente strutturata, prezzata e venduta.\nNon sono uno specialista di vendita pubblicitaria. Questo pezzo nasce dal tentativo di capire come funziona il sistema dall’interno 🧠\nNell’articolo affronto:\n📺 il contesto dei diritti della Serie A e perché determina tutto\n 📘 cosa sono questi documenti commerciali, chi li produce e a chi sono destinati\n 🏟️ come Sky racconta e struttura la pubblicità sulla Serie A, tra pacchetti, piattaforme e messaggi\n 📊 come DAZN racconta la pubblicità sulla Serie A, tra classificazione delle giornate, formati e logica di delivery\n 🏢 il ruolo di Sky Media e Digitalia ’08 come organizzazioni, non solo come marchi\n 💶 perché i prezzi non sono direttamente confrontabili e quali segnali corretti emergono comunque\n 🧩 un confronto strutturato dei due modelli, capitolo per capitolo\n 📎 appendici di approfondimento su Sky, DAZN e sulle società che stanno dietro alle offerte\nQuesto non è un confronto su chi sia migliore o peggiore.\n È un tentativo di capire come due posizioni sui diritti molto diverse si traducano in due sistemi pubblicitari industriali coerenti.\nDAZN Sky Italia Sky Media Italia Digitalia '08 Lega Serie A\nA guy with a scarf\n#football #advertising #tv #streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 1068, "rx": 13, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sky Italia", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["Sky Media Italia", "showcase"], ["Digitalia '08", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "advertising", "tv", "streaming"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1064, 13, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1068, 13, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 252, "id": 7421483692591603713, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7421483692591603713/", "ts": "2026-01-26T09:26:36+00:00", "date": "2026-01-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "How SKY And DAZN Sell Serie A - A Deep Dive", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 📺 How Sky Italia and DAZN Sell Lega Serie A – A Deep Dive", "chars": 2041, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 📺 How Sky Italia and DAZN Sell Lega Serie A – A Deep Dive\nI started from a very simple place 👇\nI was looking for Sky’s Serie A audience numbers, mainly to sanity-check a few claims I kept seeing repeated in industry conversations. While doing that, I stumbled on a full Sky Media Italia commercial document, publicly accessible, detailed, and clearly written for agencies rather than for public consumption 📄\nThat discovery triggered curiosity.\n If Sky had such a visible advertising playbook, what did the DAZN side look like 🤔\nFollowing that question led me to two full Serie A commercial documents published via Digitalia '08, DAZN’s advertising sales partner in Italy. Reading all three documents back to back turned into an unexpected learning exercise on how Serie A advertising is actually structured, priced, and sold.\nI’m not an advertising sales specialist. This piece is the result of trying to understand how the system works from the inside 🧠\n\nIn the article I cover:\n📺 The Serie A rights context and why it shapes everything\n 📘 What these commercial documents are, who produces them, and who they are for\n 🏟️ How Sky frames Serie A advertising, including packs, platforms, and messaging\n 📊 How DAZN frames Serie A advertising, including matchday classification, formats, and delivery logic\n 🏢 The role of Sky Media and Digitalia ’08 as organisations, not just brands\n 💶 Why pricing cannot be compared directly, and what factually correct signals still emerge\n 🧩 A structured comparison of the two models, chapter by chapter\n 📎 Deep-dive appendices on Sky, DAZN, and the companies behind the offers\n\nThis is not about who is better or worse.\n It’s about understanding how two very different rights positions translate into two coherent, industrial advertising systems.\n\nDAZN Sky Italia Sky Media Italia Digitalia '08 Lega Serie A\n#football #ads #tv #streaming\n\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. hashtag\n#ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 3746, "rx": 25, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sky Italia", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Sky Media Italia", "showcase"], ["Digitalia '08", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["Sky Media Italia", "showcase"], ["Digitalia '08", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "ads", "tv", "streaming", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3744, 25, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 3746, 25, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 253, "id": 7420515029655015424, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7420515029655015424/", "ts": "2026-01-23T17:17:29+00:00", "date": "2026-01-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Great to be featured in a Sportico article about the Cloudflare vs. Lega Serie A feud by Jacob Feldman to finish a quite complex week with a", "snippet": "Great to be featured in a Sportico article about the Cloudflare vs. Lega Serie A feud by Jacob Feldman to finish a quite complex week with a high note.", "chars": 691, "text": "Great to be featured in a Sportico article about the Cloudflare vs. Lega Serie A feud by Jacob Feldman to finish a quite complex week with a high note.\n\n😎 “Yeah, the arrogance of threatening the Olympics, that was not really in good taste,” said Carlo De Marchis, an Italian sports tech adviser who broke down the dispute in a series of Substack posts. “Not very smart.” 😎\n\nMy articles series is here:\nhttps://lnkd.in/daV59U64\n\nJacob's article is here:\nhttps://lnkd.in/dPH9Ex-3\n\nGood to see someone overseas noticing my content, not so much in my own country he he he 😅, I should maybe start writing in Italian.\n\nA guy with a scarf International Olympic Committee – IOC\n#cloud #cybersecurity", "qtext": "", "imp": 566, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Sportico", "company"], ["Cloudflare", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Jacob Feldman", "person"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["International Olympic Committee – IOC", "company"]], "tags": ["cloud", "cybersecurity"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 566, 9, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 566, 9, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 254, "id": 7419635941507649536, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7419635941507649536/", "ts": "2026-01-21T07:04:18+00:00", "date": "2026-01-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Prediction markets and sport, from niche tools to mainstream signals", "snippet": "🤑  📲 Prediction markets are entering sport, slowly and visibly", "chars": 1732, "text": "🤑  📲 Prediction markets are entering sport, slowly and visibly\n\nI’ve wanted to write about prediction markets and sport for a while. The draft sat in my drawer, waiting for the right trigger. The recent news around DAZN and Polymarket made that moment feel right.\nThis is not a revolution. It is a small, public step from the traditional sports ecosystem. Still, it is the first time a major sports platform has openly integrated prediction market signals into the broadcast layer.\nIn the article, I try to put structure around what is happening, without overselling it.\nI cover:\n📈 what prediction markets actually are, and how they differ from betting and polling\n📈 Polymarket and Kalshi, their positioning, leadership, and regulatory paths\n📈 how prediction markets intersect with live sport and fan engagement\n📈 why DAZN’s integration with Polymarket matters, even at this early stage\nathlete endorsements, league restrictions, and why those signals should be read carefully\n📈 the legal and regulatory attention surrounding this space\n📈 the risks for sports and fans\n📈 why sports organisations should pay attention now, even if they decide not to act yet\n\nThe piece is my exploration, shared. It’s about understanding a new layer that is starting to appear in sports media, one that sits somewhere between data, sentiment, and live interpretation.\nIf you work in sports media, rights, platforms, or product, this is a topic worth following closely.\n\nUPDATE: Jan 27 - MLS joins the party!\n\n👉 Article here below and in A guy with a scarf Substack.\n#sport #betting #predictions\n\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 1690, "rx": 24, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["DAZN", "company"], ["Polymarket", "company"], ["Polymarket", "company"], ["Kalshi", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sport", "betting", "predictions", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1688, 24, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1690, 24, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 255, "id": 7419330886846009345, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7419330886846009345/", "ts": "2026-01-20T10:52:07+00:00", "date": "2026-01-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Serie A TV Audience (First Half) Deep Dive", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 Serie A audiences are up. Understanding why took more work than it looks.", "chars": 1765, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 Serie A audiences are up. Understanding why took more work than it looks.\nOver the past few days I’ve seen a lot of commentary around Lega Serie A TV audiences in Italy. Numbers shared, charts reposted, conclusions drawn quickly.\nI wanted to slow it down and deep dive - a bit chaotic to be honest...\nSo I went back to the source material and rebuilt the picture from the ground up. That meant reading the full set of Auditel and Audicom PDFs, cross-checking them with reporting from Calcio e Finanza ( Marco Sacchi, Matteo Spaziante ), and isolating what actually changed in the current rights cycle. For DAZN specifically, I worked through the full dataset match by match and consolidated it into a working model.\nThis piece is the result of that process.\nIt looks at:\n⚽ how Total Audience measurement really works and what it does and does not\n⚽ the impact of DAZN’s free-to-air matches within the current rights framework\n⚽ the role of Zona DAZN\n⚽ what changed in the Sky Italia–DAZN allocation and why Sky’s curve flattened after 2024/25\n⚽ how club draw and fixture quality shape the top of the table more than platform effects alone\n\nThis is a long read, built for people who care about measurement, rights design, and how distribution mechanics translate into reported numbers.\nIf you want the full breakdown, the article is here below and on my A guy with a scarf Substack.\n\n📑 And if you’re interested in the raw DAZN dataset, I’m happy to share the full excel file - just comment \"Forza Toro\" below and I can send in a DM.\n\nLega Serie A Auditel Audicom DAZN Sky Italia\n#calcio #football #audience\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 3074, "rx": 24, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Auditel", "company"], ["Audicom", "company"], ["Calcio e Finanza", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Auditel", "company"], ["Audicom", "company"], ["DAZN", "company"], ["Sky Italia", "company"]], "tags": ["calcio", "football", "audience", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3066, 24, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3074, 24, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 256, "id": 7418969375556161536, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7418969375556161536/", "ts": "2026-01-19T10:55:36+00:00", "date": "2026-01-19", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "After all that chat, a real vibe coding example...", "snippet": "After all that chat, a real vibe coding example...", "chars": 50, "text": "After all that chat, a real vibe coding example...", "qtext": "Claude + Cleeng = a global subscription platform in 60 minutes 🤩\n\nWe challenged our Product Designer, Anna Sobiak, to build a fully functional educational subscription platform from scratch in under an hour.\n\nThe result is \"Bloompath\" – and it’s not just a prototype. It’s a business ready to accept payments in 230 countries.\n\nIn our latest video, Anna breaks down exactly how she did it using vibe coding principles:\n\n🔹 The UI: built instantly using Claude 3.5 Sonnet\n🔹 The business logic: powered by Cleeng’s Hosted Widgets\n\nInstead of coding billing systems, tax compliance, or customer support f", "imp": 143, "rx": 1, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": null, "watch_time_s": null, "avg_watch_s": null, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 142, 1, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 143, 1, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Cleeng"}, {"i": 257, "id": 7418959891454713856, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7418959891454713856/", "ts": "2026-01-19T10:17:55+00:00", "date": "2026-01-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What the Titans’ Checkout-Free Stadium Says About Lost Concession Revenue", "snippet": "🏟️ What the Tennessee Titans’ Checkout-Free Stadium Says About Lost Concession Revenue", "chars": 1431, "text": "🏟️ What the Tennessee Titans’ Checkout-Free Stadium Says About Lost Concession Revenue\n🍺 I have been paying attention to stadium food and concessions for a long time. It started as a fan reaction, missing moments while standing in line. Over time, it became a professional curiosity about how much revenue quietly slips away on matchday.\n🤑 Queues shape behaviour. Fans simplify purchases, rush decisions, or skip transactions altogether. This happens in one of the few areas clubs and venues fully control.\n🏈 The recent Tennessee Titans agreement with Amazon Just Walk Out stood out to me. A stadium designed to be entirely checkout-free sends a clear signal. This is part of the core infrastructure, planned early, scaled across the venue.\n\nI wrote an article to explore why this matters:\n🏟️ how concession friction affects spending patterns\n🏟️ why throughput matters more than most pricing debates\n🏟️ what the Titans–Amazon deal represents at industry level\n🏟️ five stadiums already setting the operational benchmark\n🏟️ a closer look at the technology behind Amazon Just Walk Out\n\nThe topic goes beyond food and drink. It touches time, flow, and how modern stadiums should be designed.\n\nInspired by an article on Sports Business Journal by Bret McCormick\n\n#stadium #sportbiz\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 5541, "rx": 52, "cm": 9, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Tennessee Titans", "company"], ["Amazon", "company"], ["Sports Business Journal", "company"], ["Bret McCormick", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["stadium", "sportbiz", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 5540, 52, 9, 2], ["2026-10-17", 5541, 52, 9, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 258, "id": 7418579939152797697, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7418579939152797697/", "ts": "2026-01-18T09:08:08+00:00", "date": "2026-01-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 📲 As everybody's talking about the BBC/YouTube deal (with lots of big statements) and before diving deeper and seeing if I could anything ", "snippet": "📺 📲 As everybody's talking about the BBC/YouTube deal (with lots of big statements) and before diving deeper and seeing if I could anything to the discourse, I ", "chars": 1940, "text": "📺 📲 As everybody's talking about the BBC/YouTube deal (with lots of big statements) and before diving deeper and seeing if I could anything to the discourse, I manually (my friend Paola Marinone at BuzzMyVideos will do it more professionally) took a look at the current BBC YouTube Channels.\n\n📺 BBC News\nJoined YouTube: 8 April 2006\nSubscribers: 19.1M\nTotal videos: ~28.2K\nTotal channel views: ~6.57B\nMost watched video (all time)\nTitle: Moment judge recognised school friend in dock\nViews: ~70M\nPublished: 2014\nLength: 0:56\nMost popular Short (all time)\nTitle: Matthew Perry went head-to-head with journalist Peter Hitchens on drug addiction in 2013\nViews: ~31 million\nPublished: 2023 (clip from a 2013 interview)\nNumber of videos in the last 7 days: 61 (Average length 11:32 mins)\n\n📺 BBC\nJoined YouTube: 11 November 2005\nSubscribers: 15.3M\nTotal videos: ~26K\nTotal channel views: ~11.9B\nMost watched video (all time)\nTitle: The incredible ibex defies gravity and climbs a dam\nProgramme: Forces of Nature with Brian Cox\nViews: ~231M\nPublished: 2016\nLength: 3:53\nNumber of videos in the last 7 days: 5 (Average length 1:41 mins)\n\n📺 BBC Earth\nJoined YouTube: 2009\nSubscribers: ~14.3M\nTotal videos: ~3.2K\nTotal channel views: ~4.5B\nMost watched video (all time)\nTitle: Lion Trapped by Clan of Hyenas | Dynasties\nViews: ~106M\nPublished: 2017\nLength: 3:34\n\n📺 BBC World Service\nJoined YouTube: 2010 (public About page)\nSubscribers: 1.8M\nTotal videos: ~2.2K\nTotal channel views: ~212M\nMost watched video (all time)\nTitle: Inside the world of Radhe Maa: India’s “Godwoman”\nViews: ~21M\nPublished: 2024\nLength: 15:56\n\nBy the way BBC News also has 11M followers on TikTok and  29.8M on Instagram\nNext I may check Italy, France, Germany and Spain public broadcasters...\nA guy with a scarf\n\n#video\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 592, "rx": 14, "cm": 5, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["BBC", "company"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["video", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 591, 14, 5, 1], ["2026-10-17", 592, 14, 5, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 259, "id": 7418196138840965120, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7418196138840965120/", "ts": "2026-01-17T07:43:02+00:00", "date": "2026-01-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "PERMACRISIS: Is Sport The Ultimate Drama Queen?", "snippet": "😵 😱 🥰 Permacrisis: Is sport the ultimate drama queen?", "chars": 1770, "text": "😵 😱 🥰 Permacrisis: Is sport the ultimate drama queen?\n\n🥇 For a long time, the sports industry operated in a protected environment. Revenues felt structurally secure, media rights sat at the centre, and long cycles created a sense of permanence. Stability became an assumption.\nThat assumption has been eroding slowly. Not through collapse, but through friction. Renewals take longer. Margins tighten unevenly. Outcomes become less predictable. Some positions that once felt guaranteed no longer are.\n\n⚽ In that environment, urgency starts to shape everything. Decisions arrive framed as unavoidable. Change accelerates. Emergency language becomes normal. This condition has been described clearly in a recent Future Proof News Nick Hilton's article on permacrisis, which gave a name to something many people recognise intuitively.\n\n🏈 I found that framing useful, and it pushed me to write my own piece. Not to repeat the argument, but to look at how permacrisis shows up in everyday decision-making, language, and behaviour across sport.\n\n😎 I am not outside this dynamic. I have worked for years helping a conservative industry engage with innovation as it arrived. That meant encouraging openness, experimentation, and curiosity. Working in that space also meant living close to urgency, and absorbing some of its language along the way.\n\n🎾 The question worth asking now concerns emphasis. How often calm and judgment were reinforced while organisations were being encouraged to open themselves to change.\nMy article is an attempt to slow the conversation down, name the pattern, and create space to think with more solid rationales. Or should we?\n\nThanks to Jen Topping, Richard Gillis and Unofficial Partner\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportbiz #sport #leadership #innovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 1108, "rx": 15, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Nick Hilton", "person"], ["Jen Topping", "person"], ["Richard Gillis", "person"], ["Unofficial Partner", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "sport", "leadership", "innovation"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1107, 15, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1108, 15, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 260, "id": 7417499301666439169, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7417499301666439169/", "ts": "2026-01-15T09:34:03+00:00", "date": "2026-01-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Amagi, FAST, and an Indian IPO - A Deep Dive", "snippet": "📺 📲 I came across the news of Amagi preparing for an IPO and decided to spend some time digging into it properly.", "chars": 1817, "text": "📺 📲 I came across the news of Amagi preparing for an IPO and decided to spend some time digging into it properly.\nI looked the announcement, the timing, the market choice, and what this move might say about the current state of media, video infrastructure, and advertising-led distribution.\nI went through filings, financial press, and market analysis and pulled everything together in a long-form piece. It’s an attempt to make sense of the signal behind the event.\n\nI structured the article around four very concrete questions:\n❓ Why is Amagi going public now\n❓ Why is the listing taking place on the Indian stock market\n ❓ What, if anything, does this signal to the wider media and sport ecosystem\n ❓ How have other advertising and video infrastructure IPOs in this market actually performed\nAlso, between you and me, I expected they had more revenues 🤨\n\nThe article is deliberately long and modular. You can read it end to end, or jump straight to the parts you care about.\nWhat’s inside:\n➡️ A factual IPO timeline with real subscription data and consolidated revenues\n➡️ An explanation of why FAST mattered for Amagi’s growth, without treating it as dogma\n ➡️ A capital-markets view on why India makes sense as a listing venue today\n ➡️ What this means for media and sport organisations looking at infrastructure partners\n ➡️ A detailed appendix on how similar US IPOs in ad-tech and CTV infrastructure played out\n ➡️ Full sources, filings, and financial documents linked at the end\nDisclosures\nThis article is independent analysis, written in my personal capacity, if I made mistakes let me know.\n\nA guy with a scarf Amagi #IPO #CTV #FAST #video #investing\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 2193, "rx": 24, "cm": 7, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Amagi", "company"]], "tags": ["ipo", "ctv", "fast", "video", "investing", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2192, 24, 7, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2193, 24, 7, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 261, "id": 7417117216674013184, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7417117216674013184/", "ts": "2026-01-14T08:15:47+00:00", "date": "2026-01-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "FREE ebook: D2C Retention Benchmarks 2026", "snippet": "😎 FREE ebook", "chars": 495, "text": "😎 FREE ebook\nD2C Retention Benchmarks 2026\nDownload here: https://lnkd.in/dFFBxd3S\n\n📖 Table of content\nExecutive summary\nThe evolution of the subscription model\nRetention, the secret to sustainable growth\nUnderstanding the full subscriber lifecycle\nPhase one, acquisition and conversion\nPhase two, revenue optimization\nPhase three, retention and loyalty\nPhase four, customer experience\nSummary of key takeaways\nThe power of an integrated approach\nNext steps\n\nCleeng A guy with a scarf #retention", "qtext": "", "imp": 527, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Cleeng", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["retention"], "series": [], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 526, 12, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 527, 12, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 262, "id": 7416815942590103552, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7416815942590103552/", "ts": "2026-01-13T12:18:38+00:00", "date": "2026-01-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "💡 Top subscription businesses generate up to 80% more revenue per free trial than the rest.", "snippet": "💡 Top subscription businesses generate up to 80% more revenue per free trial than the rest.", "chars": 941, "text": "💡 Top subscription businesses generate up to 80% more revenue per free trial than the rest.\nThat gap shows up very early in the subscriber journey and it compounds over time.\nCleeng has just released the Retention Playbook 2026, a FREE 34 pages ebook built on aggregated data from thousands of real subscription flows across media, sports, and D2C.\n\n📖 The report walks through the full subscriber lifecycle with concrete benchmarks:\n➡️ how free trials are structured to create revenue, not just sign-ups\n➡️ how discounts perform when they are part of a designed journey\n➡️ where churn is introduced through payments, onboarding, and support\n➡️ how teams connect acquisition, monetization, and retention operationally\n\n📊 It is a document you can read alongside your own numbers, phase by phase, from first signup through long-term loyalty.\nFree ebook link:\nhttps://shorturl.at/LAVdo\n\nRetention Zone\n#Subscriptions #D2C #MediaTech #SportsMedia", "qtext": "", "imp": 436, "rx": 12, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Cleeng", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"]], "tags": ["subscriptions", "d2c", "mediatech", "sportsmedia"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 435, 12, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 436, 12, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 263, "id": 7416580093084385280, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7416580093084385280/", "ts": "2026-01-12T20:41:27+00:00", "date": "2026-01-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🌃 I am not usually posting so late but this has been a boring Monday (maybe not) and in the last few hours I read about:", "snippet": "🌃 I am not usually posting so late but this has been a boring Monday (maybe not) and in the last few hours I read about:", "chars": 357, "text": "🌃 I am not usually posting so late but this has been a boring Monday (maybe not) and in the last few hours I read about:\n1️⃣ Amagi goes IPO tomorrow\n2️⃣ Stats Perform buys Phenix assets (what a long story)\n3️⃣ Apple AI will be on Gemini going forward\n😎 Good Night and Good luck!\n\nA guy with a scarf Untangling AI\n#ai #lowlatency #streaming #FAST #cloudmedia", "qtext": "", "imp": 689, "rx": 16, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai", "lowlatency", "streaming", "fast", "cloudmedia"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 257, "watch_time_s": 5460, "avg_watch_s": 21, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 688, 16, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 689, 16, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 264, "id": 7416394227510796288, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7416394227510796288/", "ts": "2026-01-12T08:22:53+00:00", "date": "2026-01-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Serie A, Piracy Shield, The Olympics and Cloudflare", "snippet": "🇮🇹 ⚽ 📡 Serie A, Piracy Shield, The Olympics and Cloudflare", "chars": 1613, "text": "🇮🇹 ⚽ 📡 Serie A, Piracy Shield, The Olympics and Cloudflare\nIntriguing matter as you can read it as 3 different stories or more.\n\nWhat happened:\n🇮🇹 AGCOM fined Cloudflare over non-compliance with Piracy Shield, the system designed to rapidly block illegal live streams of football matches.\n⚽ Serie A responded by rejecting claims of censorship and framing the issue as copyright enforcement.\n📡 Cloudflare’s CEO took a very different approach, moving the discussion quickly onto political ground. The response leaned heavily on US political language and figures, and escalated the dispute with the same current US global postures by linking it to broader themes such as free speech, trade, and even cybersecurity services connected to Milano Cortina 2026.\n\nSpeaking personally, and from years spent working around streaming and live media systems, this shift in tone makes me uncomfortable. There is a legitimate and important debate to have about IP enforcement at infrastructure level, speed versus due process, and the risk of collateral damage. But when that debate is framed primarily through political alignment and symbolism, it becomes harder to discuss the technical and media realities calmly.\n\nYou can find below a longer piece that reconstructs what actually happened, why it matters for football and streaming, and what this episode says about the current relationship between global tech platforms and national regulation.\n\n😎 Update: Part 2 Deep dive on the Italian side\nhttps://lnkd.in/dZeSV-5k\n\n🤖 Update: Part 3 - The Tech behind it\nhttps://lnkd.in/dPPVGvJR\n\n#piracy #streaming #football #uspolitics", "qtext": "", "imp": 3443, "rx": 24, "cm": 7, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": ["piracy", "streaming", "football", "uspolitics"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3442, 24, 7, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3443, 24, 7, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 265, "id": 7415336884765343744, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7415336884765343744/", "ts": "2026-01-09T10:21:23+00:00", "date": "2026-01-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "My friends at Cleeng have worked hard on an insightful report on the state of retention in D2C subscription-based products/services.", "snippet": "My friends at Cleeng have worked hard on an insightful report on the state of retention in D2C subscription-based products/services.", "chars": 709, "text": "My friends at Cleeng have worked hard on an insightful report on the state of retention in D2C subscription-based products/services.\n\nOne of the included findings is that the top 25% of D2C brands are keeping their subscribers for an average of 502 days, while the rest of the market lose them after just 185 days.\nThat is nearly a 3x difference in retention.\n\nSignup now to gain access for next week drop: https://lnkd.in/dFFBxd3S\n\nAlexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Kübra Güven\n\n#DTC #D2C #subscription #retentionx\n----------------\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 627, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Cleeng", "company"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["dtc", "d2c", "subscription", "retentionx"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 626, 16, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 627, 16, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 266, "id": 7415134727935844352, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7415134727935844352/", "ts": "2026-01-08T20:58:05+00:00", "date": "2026-01-08", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Reposting for my network of startuppers 😎 😲 🦾", "snippet": "Reposting for my network of startuppers 😎 😲 🦾", "chars": 60, "text": "Reposting for my network of startuppers 😎 😲 🦾\nI'll be there!", "qtext": "⏰ Last call for innovators!\n \nThe applicaton deadline for the SportsInnovation Startup Award 2026 is almost here! This is your final chance to be part of the stage where the future of sport is experienced live.\n \nPitch your technology, gain global visibility and connect with decision-makers shaping fan experiences, broadcasting, sports performance and stadium innovation.💡\n \nWhat’s in it for you?\n🏆 €5,000 cash prize\n🌍 €29,000 SportsPro media value\n🎤 Live presentation at SportsInnovation 2026\n \n🗓️ Application deadline: January 15, 2026\n👉 Apply now: https://lnkd.in/dGfjGHH8\n\nDon’t miss this chanc", "imp": 309, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 309, 6, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 309, 6, 0, 0]], "quoted": "SportsInnovation"}, {"i": 267, "id": 7414958710134988802, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7414958710134988802/", "ts": "2026-01-08T09:18:39+00:00", "date": "2026-01-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The AI That Remembers You Is the AI You Don’t Want To Leave - Retention Zone Exclusive", "snippet": "🤖 Why memory, not intelligence, is becoming the real retention engine of AI 🤖", "chars": 2400, "text": "🤖 Why memory, not intelligence, is becoming the real retention engine of AI 🤖\nThe AI That Remembers You Is the AI You Don’t Want To Leave\nRetention Zone Exclusive\n\nI think I've discovered why I can't leave my main AI tool. And it's not what you'd expect.\nOver the past year, I've been using dozens of AI platforms for my work as a media tech advisor. Some are faster, some are smarter, some excel at specific tasks.\nBut without planning it, I realized I'd started treating one platform completely differently. It became \"home\" while the others became utilities.\n\n🤖 The reason wasn't price or performance. It was memory.\n\nNot marketing-speak memory, but real accumulated context. This system learned how I work, which voice I use for different projects, the rules I set once and don't want to repeat daily. It remembered that I switch between 4-5 distinct personas throughout the day.\nThat made a difference. The AI stopped feeling like a vending machine and started feeling like a workspace where my effort accumulates rather than evaporates.\n\nHere's what struck me: Traditional retention tactics (onboarding flows, notifications, pricing bundles) feel primitive compared to this. Memory creates retention without trying. You don't decide to stay – you simply stop considering leaving.\nI think we're seeing the emergence of a hub-and-spoke model: one \"home\" AI that knows your context, surrounded by utility tools for specific tasks.\n\nBut here's my confession: This is based entirely on my personal experience. I obsess over retention for subscription services, but I'm essentially a sample size of one here. The data isn't in yet.\nQuestions for the community:\n🤖 Are you experiencing this \"home AI\" pattern too?\n🤖 Have you noticed yourself getting attached to one platform over others?\n🤖 If so, what's driving that attachment?\n\nI wrote about this extensively in the article below because I think other subscription services could learn from AI's accidental discovery that retention often comes from remembering better, not adding more features.\nBut I could be completely wrong.\nWhat's your experience been?\n\nAlexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res Tom Burrell\n\n#AI #Retention #SubscriptionEconomy #CustomerExperience\n------------\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1037, "rx": 15, "cm": 5, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Tom Burrell", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "retention", "subscriptioneconomy", "customerexperience"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1037, 15, 5, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1037, 15, 5, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 268, "id": 7414577712197017601, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7414577712197017601/", "ts": "2026-01-07T08:04:42+00:00", "date": "2026-01-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🇮🇹 30 days to the start of the Milano Cortina Olympic Games.", "snippet": "🇮🇹 30 days to the start of the Milano Cortina Olympic Games.", "chars": 816, "text": "🇮🇹 30 days to the start of the Milano Cortina Olympic Games.\n⛷️ 20 years after the Torino 2006 Winter Olympics.\nI just realized that I have been involved in some ways in ALL Olympic Games in between these two Italian games, but I had ZERO involvement both in Torino (my own city) and the upcoming ones in Milano.\nNemo profeta in patria 😎.\nMy most exciting times, deep learnings, career defining moments and fondest memories, came from 🇨🇳 Beijing 2008, 🇨🇦 Vancouver and 🇬🇧 London.\nI will observing innovations and new fans, athletes, creators, and brands, behaviours globally during next month.\n➡️ There's nothing like the Olympics.\n\nA guy with a scarf #olympics\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 4954, "rx": 46, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["olympics", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 4953, 46, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 4954, 46, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 269, "id": 7414227419987066880, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7414227419987066880/", "ts": "2026-01-06T08:52:46+00:00", "date": "2026-01-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Serie A media rights post-2029, early chatter to keep an eye on", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 📺 Serie A rights post-2029 — early Italian chatter", "chars": 746, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 📺 Serie A rights post-2029 — early Italian chatter\nSome early Italian indiscrezioni are starting to circulate around Serie A media rights beyond 2029. Nothing formal, just market noise worth tracking.\nThe chain appears to start with Paolo Maroni on X, then picked up by Italian outlets:\n🕵🏼 Paolo Maroni (X), commenting on possible future moves involving Netflix\n🕵🏼 Calciomercato.it, referencing a possibile asse Sky-Netflix for the post-2029 cycle\n🕵🏼 Gonfialarete, expanding on the same hypothesis around Netflix entering the rights conversation\n\nFor context, DAZN and Sky hold domestic Serie A rights through the 2028-2029.\nEarly chatter only. Worth noting, then watching.\nRead more below 👇🏼\n\nA guy with a scarf #mediarights #italia #calcio", "qtext": "", "imp": 3090, "rx": 23, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["DAZN", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["mediarights", "italia", "calcio"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 3089, 23, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 3090, 23, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 270, "id": 7413871719360405504, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7413871719360405504/", "ts": "2026-01-05T09:19:20+00:00", "date": "2026-01-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I will not be at CES 2026 in Las Vegas but will observe, spy and scout what all of you being there will surface and will be impressed by.", "snippet": "I will not be at CES 2026 in Las Vegas but will observe, spy and scout what all of you being there will surface and will be impressed by.", "chars": 444, "text": "I will not be at CES 2026 in Las Vegas but will observe, spy and scout what all of you being there will surface and will be impressed by.\n😎 I am curious to understand the vibe\n😎 I am curious to see what beyond the obvious 1st and 9th letters of the alphabet will take center stage\n😎 I am curious about what of that may impact our sports and media industry\nFeel free to share in DM if you see anything really cool.\nA guy with a scarf CES #sports", "qtext": "", "imp": 1361, "rx": 13, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["CES", "company"]], "tags": ["sports"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1360, 13, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1361, 13, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 271, "id": 7411026703302586368, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7411026703302586368/", "ts": "2025-12-28T12:54:16+00:00", "date": "2025-12-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 I got distracted by agentic AI hype and, to be honest, I’ve become quite skeptical about its immediate value.", "snippet": "🤖 I got distracted by agentic AI hype and, to be honest, I’ve become quite skeptical about its immediate value.", "chars": 1950, "text": "🤖 I got distracted by agentic AI hype and, to be honest, I’ve become quite skeptical about its immediate value.\nThat’s probably partly my fault, and partly the lack of truly convincing, everyday use cases I see around me. Lots of demos, lots of promises, not enough clear “this changed how we work” 🤨 stories, but also some quite negative media coverage.\nSo I was ready to dismiss most of it.\nThen I read the latest Claude report on AI agents and thought, ok, maybe I’m being too harsh. Or at least too impatient.\n\nA few things stood out for me:\n🤖 Over half of the companies surveyed are already using agents for multi-step workflows, and a meaningful share for processes that cut across teams. 🤨\n🤖 Coding is where agents are already boringly effective. Close to everyone uses them, mostly in production, not experiments. Time saved is spread across planning, writing, testing, documentation. That matters. 🤫\n🤖 The next wave is less glamorous but more real: research, reporting, data analysis, internal process automation. The work nobody brags about on stage, but everyone needs done. 🦾\n🤖 Most teams don’t go “all in” or “DIY everything”. They mix off-the-shelf agents with custom pieces where it really counts. That feels very enterprise-real. 😎\n🤖 The most interesting signal: many organisations say they’re already seeing measurable ROI. Not projected value, not pilots. Actual impact, mostly through speed and efficiency gains.\n❓ Will they perform long term?\n\nWhat this report did not magically give me is a killer, universal agentic use case. But it did reframe things.\nAgentic AI seems to work best when:\n🤖 the task is well defined,\n🤖 context is available,\n🤖  integration is taken seriously,\n🤖  and humans are willing to redesign workflows, not just bolt tools on top.\n\nI’m still skeptical about sweeping claims.\nHappy to be proven wrong. Or right. Either way, this one is worth reading.\n\nA guy with a scarf Untangling AI\n#ai #agents #howwework", "qtext": "", "imp": 1106, "rx": 16, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai", "agents", "howwework"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1105, 16, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1106, 16, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 272, "id": 7410624142334492672, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7410624142334492672/", "ts": "2025-12-27T10:14:38+00:00", "date": "2025-12-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Companies are now looking for storytellers. For real? Good to know.", "snippet": "🗣️ Companies are now looking for storytellers. For real? Good to know.", "chars": 1929, "text": "🗣️ Companies are now looking for storytellers. For real? Good to know.\nThe The Wall Street Journal recently published an article (Katie Deighton) with a telling signal.\nCompanies are increasingly hiring for roles explicitly called “storyteller.”\nThe piece reads as a labour-market indicator. As organisations grow more complex and communication happens continuously and in public, narrative ownership is becoming a structural function rather than a side activity.\nThat article made me pause.\nI spent 35 years inside the same company, across technology, product, partnerships, and innovation roles. None of my job titles included storytelling. Yet a large part of the work involved explanation. Translating strategy into something teams could act on. Translating product decisions into language partners could trust. Maintaining coherence as initiatives moved from idea to launch.\nI was also in a particularly fortunate position. I was often involved/driving, from early, strategy shaping through idea generation and into product launches. That continuity made it easier to speak with accuracy and restraint.\nOnly later did I realise how much that interpretive layer mattered.\n\nIn the full article, I explore:\n ➡️ what the WSJ article actually says and how it defines the storyteller role\n ➡️ why organisations are formalising narrative ownership now\n ➡️ how proximity to strategy and product enables authentic storytelling\n ➡️ why cost pressure makes coherence more important, not less\n ➡️ a practical Why / How / What guide for hiring a storyteller\n ➡️ a role description template\nRead it all here below or on Substack 👇\n\nCurious to hear how others are approaching this inside their organisations\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#storytelling #organization #corporate\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 2346, "rx": 17, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["The Wall Street Journal", "company"], ["Katie Deighton", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["storytelling", "organization", "corporate", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2345, 17, 4, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2346, 17, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 273, "id": 7410292258824081408, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7410292258824081408/", "ts": "2025-12-26T12:15:51+00:00", "date": "2025-12-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Web3 and AI: Two waves, one recent memory, very different trajectories", "snippet": "🤖 Web3 and AI: Two waves, one recent memory, very different trajectories", "chars": 1049, "text": "🤖 Web3 and AI: Two waves, one recent memory, very different trajectories\n\nIt may be the right moment to publish a few thoughts I’ve been sitting on for weeks.\nOur industry is producing so much, so fast, that reacting in real time often feels less useful than stepping back. I’ve never been a news breaker. I prefer letting things settle, then looking for what’s interesting beyond the first wave of commentary.\nThis piece comes from there.\n\nIt looks at four things that have dominated recent tech and media conversations, and tries to connect them at a deeper, structural level:\n➡️ why Web3 asked too much of users, institutions, and culture\n➡️ why AI crossed the adoption threshold by hiding complexity and delivering value first\n➡️ why the Metaverse mattered so much to sports and media, and why it stalled\n➡️ why crypto didn’t disappear with Web3, and may be quietly becoming infrastructure\n\nIt’s a long-form read, not a hot take.\nMore about behavioural change, cost of adoption, and trust than about trends.\n\nA guy with a scarf Untangling AI\n#ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 850, "rx": 19, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": ["Untangling AI"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 849, 19, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 850, 19, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 274, "id": 7409874231469006849, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7409874231469006849/", "ts": "2025-12-25T08:34:45+00:00", "date": "2025-12-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 A thought for 2026.", "snippet": "🤖 A thought for 2026.", "chars": 734, "text": "🤖 A thought for 2026.\nI would love for people to stop creating AI artifacts whose only purpose is to show what AI can do, or how smart the person using it is.\nI can’t stand the idea of another year spent scrolling past “see what AI/I can do” posts.\nWe already have enough proof of capability. What we need is intent.\nWhat I would love instead is to see AI used quietly, almost invisibly, to support meaningful, original, authentic work. The kind of work someone genuinely needs to make, or has been wanting to make for a long time.\nTry. You can do it.\nIt is already feasible.\nWhat matter is the outcome not the technology.\nIt is a cultural shift.\nAnd yes, I know it won’t happen.\nBut still...\n\n#ai A guy with a scarf The One Man Brand", "qtext": "", "imp": 1192, "rx": 24, "cm": 3, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1191, 24, 3, 0], ["2026-10-17", 1192, 24, 3, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 275, "id": 7409510638332346368, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7409510638332346368/", "ts": "2025-12-24T08:29:58+00:00", "date": "2025-12-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”", "snippet": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”", "chars": 1393, "text": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\n🧩 Case 3: B2B Tech Vendors\n\nIf I were CEO of a B2B tech vendor in the sports, media and entertainment ecosystem in 2026, I would focus on three things.\n1️⃣ First, sell outcomes, not architecture.\nClients do not buy stacks. They buy reduced risk, saved time, and measurable impact.\n2️⃣ Second, become easier to buy than to benchmark.\nProcurement friction, integration fear, and internal alignment kill more deals than competitors ever will.\n3️⃣ Third, choose a core customer and say no more often.\nServing everyone usually means building for no one. Focus is not a constraint, it is a signal.\n🦾 The radical move that ties these together?\nMove to cancellable, outcome-based commitments.\n👩🏼‍🎓 And the thing I would study hardest?\nHow buying decisions are actually made inside sports and media organisations, beyond job titles.\nI explore this thought exercise, alongside the sports entity and media CEO chairs, in my latest LinkedIn newsletter. It reflects perspective rather than prescription. I know it’s generic and oversimplified, on purpose, to start a debate. Let me know where you agree or disagree.\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\n\nYouTube version available: https://lnkd.in/ghx3CBny\nA guy with a scarf | The One Man Brand\n#sportstech #B2B #SaaS #CEO #strategy #advisory", "qtext": "", "imp": 641, "rx": 11, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "b2b", "saas", "ceo", "strategy", "advisory"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 639, 11, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 641, 11, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 276, "id": 7409140115807076352, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7409140115807076352/", "ts": "2025-12-23T07:57:38+00:00", "date": "2025-12-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”", "snippet": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”", "chars": 1396, "text": "From my “A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026”\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\n📺 Case 2: Media\n\nIf I were CEO of a media company in 2026, I would focus on three things.\n1️⃣ First, put retention ahead of reach.\nReach is easy to celebrate. Loyalty is harder to build. Growth without staying power is just noise.\n2️⃣ Second, treat rights as a portfolio, not trophies.\nSome rights define the brand, some drive frequency, some are there to test formats. Not every deal needs to be exclusive or global.\n3️⃣ Third, accept that distribution is not neutral.\nWhere content lives shapes how it is perceived. Designing for platforms is product strategy, not logistics.\n🦾 The radical move that ties these together?\nMake one major sports property deliberately non-exclusive and over-distributed.\n👩🏼‍🎓 And the thing I would study hardest?\nHow audience tolerance changes by platform, not by demographic.\n\nI explore this thought exercise, alongside the sports entity and tech vendor CEO chairs, in my latest LinkedIn newsletter. It reflects perspective rather than prescription. I know it’s generic and oversimplified, on purpose, to start a debate. Let me know where you agree or disagree.\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\nYouTube version available: https://lnkd.in/ghx3CBny\n\nA guy with a scarf | The One Man Brand\n#sportbiz #sportsmedia #streaming #CEO #strategy #advisory", "qtext": "", "imp": 440, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "sportsmedia", "streaming", "ceo", "strategy", "advisory"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 439, 4, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 440, 4, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 277, "id": 7408790687288066048, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7408790687288066048/", "ts": "2025-12-22T08:49:08+00:00", "date": "2025-12-22", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "From my \"A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026\"", "snippet": "From my \"A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026\"", "chars": 1399, "text": "From my \"A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026\"\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\n\n⚽ Case 1: Sports\nIf I were CEO of a sports entity in 2026, I would focus on three things.\n1️⃣ First, control what we actually own.\n Not “the fan”, but competitions, brands, moments, and the data we are structurally allowed to keep.\n2️⃣ Second, reduce internal friction before chasing innovation.\n Cleaner production, clearer ownership, fewer parallel tools. Speed comes from simplicity, not from slogans.\n3️⃣ Third, decide what must remain centralised and what should be deliberately fragmented.\n Uniformity is often confused with stability. Learning comes from variation done on purpose.\n🦾 The radical move that ties these together?\n Rewrite rights deals so fan data travels with the rights by default.\n👩🏼‍🎓 And the thing I would study hardest?\n How governance models inside sports organisations slow or accelerate real decision-making.\n\nI explore this thought exercise, alongside the media and tech vendor CEO chairs, in my latest LinkedIn newsletter,  and it reflects perspective rather than prescription, I know it's  generic and oversimplified, to start a debate. Let me know where you agree or disagree.\n\nRead it here: https://lnkd.in/gTY_8rY8\nYouTube version available: https://lnkd.in/ghx3CBny\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#sportbiz #sportstech #CEO #strategy #advisory", "qtext": "", "imp": 591, "rx": 4, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "sportstech", "ceo", "strategy", "advisory"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 590, 4, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 591, 4, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 278, "id": 7408469439630909440, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7408469439630909440/", "ts": "2025-12-21T11:32:37+00:00", "date": "2025-12-21", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Good read from Michael Broughton on books, people, and podcast, to read follow watch and listen to.", "snippet": "Good read from Michael Broughton on books, people, and podcast, to read follow watch and listen to.", "chars": 99, "text": "Good read from Michael Broughton on books, people, and podcast, to read follow watch and listen to.", "qtext": "Strong opinions, loosely held.\n\nThat’s advice i have tried to embrace.  The thinking being that having an opinion matters (whether or not others agree), but you should always be open to changing your mind.\n\nSo today i chose to share some of the books, personalities and podcasts that I consume to help keep me informed, challenged and asking questions.  Many are younger than me and see things that I need to be aware of.  Others perhaps older with wisdom that i have yet to earn…all with an opinion that i think matters regardless of if i agree.\n\nAnyone who meets me i think probably understands the", "imp": 311, "rx": 7, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link", "link"], "mentions": [["Michael Broughton", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 311, 7, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 311, 7, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Michael Broughton"}, {"i": 279, "id": null, "url": null, "ts": null, "date": "2025-12-21", "approx": true, "kind": "repost_other", "author": "Retention Zone", "with": null, "title": "Retention Zone Wrapped 2025 - Throwback", "snippet": "Retention Zone Wrapped 2025 - Throwback", "chars": 784, "text": "Retention Zone Wrapped 2025 - Throwback\n🎙️ Ep. 61: Kübra Güven - Navigating the Complexity of Multi-Channel Billing in OTT Streaming\n\nIn this enlightening episode of Retention Zone, I sat down with Kübra Güven , a product marketing manager at Cleeng, to dive deep into the intricate world of billing and payment systems for subscription-based streaming services. With over a decade of experience in the media industry—spanning from TÜRK TELEKOM to Rakuten and now Cleeng — Güven shares invaluable insights on how the fragmentation of OTT platforms has created complex challenges for content providers, particularly in managing subscription revenues across multiple platforms.\n\n📰 Article: https://lnkd.in/g59jBbf5\n📺 YouTube: https://lnkd.in/gU2j7Gcp\n🎧 Spotify: https://lnkd.in/gxv9ADUN", "qtext": "", "imp": null, "rx": 5, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 3, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", null, 5, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", null, 5, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 280, "id": 7408074761244774400, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7408074761244774400/", "ts": "2025-12-20T09:24:18+00:00", "date": "2025-12-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026", "snippet": "🩷 📨 A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026 ⚽ 🎾 🏈", "chars": 1007, "text": "🩷 📨 A Love Letter to CEOs in Sports, Media and Tech, Entering 2026 ⚽ 🎾 🏈\nI’ve been playing with a deliberately generic question.\n❓ If I were CEO in 2026, what would be my three focus points?\n⚽  a sports entity\n📺 a media company\n🤖 a B2B tech vendor\n❓ Also what would be a radical move and what I would study in 2026.\nSame ecosystem. Very different risks.\nSome of the answers are conservative. A few are intentionally uncomfortable. One role, in particular, looks far harder than the other two.\nI wrote it as a kind of love letter to CEOs. Honest, not flattering.\nI know this is generic and oversimplified at best but it tested my position on many aspects of our industry - good exercise.\nI am sure many will disagree. Happy to hear that.\n\nFull piece in today’s A guy with a scarf LinkedIn newsletter and Substack.\n\n#CEO #sports #media #tech #B2B #vendor\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 2332, "rx": 18, "cm": 6, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ceo", "sports", "media", "tech", "b2b", "vendor", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2331, 18, 6, 2], ["2026-10-17", 2332, 18, 6, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 281, "id": 7407719795514818560, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7407719795514818560/", "ts": "2025-12-19T09:53:48+00:00", "date": "2025-12-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 Retention Zone, Wrapped 2025", "snippet": "🤖 Retention Zone, Wrapped 2025", "chars": 1380, "text": "🤖 Retention Zone, Wrapped 2025\nI went back through every Retention Zone conversation from this year and pulled out what really stayed with me more.\nNot trends.\nNot buzzwords.\nActual sentences said by people who operate this stuff every day.\n\nA few of them say more about retention than a hundred slides:\n🎙️ John-Paul McKerlie\n“You can have the best product in the world, but if paying is painful, customers will leave.”\n🎙️ Tom Burrell\n“Retention doesn’t happen everywhere. It happens in very specific moments that really matter. Moments of truth.”\n🎙️ Kübra Güven\n“A lot of churn issues come from teams (content, marketing, product, tech) not being aligned internally.”\n🎙️ Motasem El Bawab\n“Retention is a mindset. It’s how the organisation thinks and behaves, not just a number on a dashboard.”\n🎙️ Gilles Domartini\n“If AI just gives you more dashboards, it doesn’t help. It has to help people take decisions.”\n\nThat’s the year, summed up better than I ever could.\nHuge thanks to everyone who joined Retention Zone in 2025, on the show and at Retention Zone LIVE in Amsterdam. More conversations ahead.\nSee you in 2026.\n\nAlexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Chris Welsh Donald Res\n\n#subscriptions #streaming #DTC #D2C\n------------\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 576, "rx": 19, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["John-Paul McKerlie", "person"], ["Tom Burrell", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Motasem El Bawab", "person"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["subscriptions", "streaming", "dtc", "d2c"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 576, 19, 2, 0], ["2026-10-17", 576, 19, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 282, "id": 7407380954744442880, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7407380954744442880/", "ts": "2025-12-18T11:27:22+00:00", "date": "2025-12-18", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🩷 Ah!", "snippet": "🩷 Ah!", "chars": 232, "text": "🩷 Ah!\nDid not expect this!\nLove that it comes from Yessi Bello-Perez a great community manager at LinkedIn.\nI truly appreciate her very relatable \"Yessi-vibe\" that emanates to all of us:\nkind, human, engaged, open.\n#noflattery #true", "qtext": "I joined LinkedIn in 2010 (madness!!) after my aunt Pilar Perez encouraged me to do so. She was also my first ever connection 🫶\n\nThis year, I’ve grown my network significantly:\nover 1000 new connections and more than 3000 followers.\n\nI’ve published more than once a week (69 posts in total) and my #YearinReview tells me I was a Catalyst (I’ll take it 😊).\n\nWho did I connect with the most? None other than Carlo De Marchis 🔥\n\nHere’s to 2026 🎉\n\nP.S: If you’re eligible for #YearInReview, you will see a banner at the top of your feed in the LinkedIn mobile app — alternatively, you can search “Year in", "imp": 560, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Yessi Bello-Perez", "person"], ["LinkedIn", "company"]], "tags": ["noflattery", "true"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 559, 9, 1, 0], ["2026-10-17", 560, 9, 1, 0]], "quoted": "Yessi Bello-Perez"}, {"i": 283, "id": 7407346953019322368, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7407346953019322368/", "ts": "2025-12-18T09:12:15+00:00", "date": "2025-12-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The Oscars Move to YouTube... in 2029", "snippet": "🎬 The Oscars are moving to YouTube... in 2029.", "chars": 941, "text": "🎬 The Oscars are moving to YouTube... in 2029.\nI took some time to elucubrate (!?) and wrote a maybe too long article on what this very early announcement means for the movie/media/streaming industry and for viewers.\nCurious and impatient now.\n\nWhat’s inside:\n➡️ Why this will finally make the Oscars easier to watch globally\n➡️ Do the Oscars still matter in our culture\n➡️ Why this is an unlikely marriage for Hollywood\n➡️ And whether Netflix is actually the loser here\n➡️ Why 2029 is a long way away in platform time\n➡️ Why access was never the real problem\n➡️ Why YouTube now works as a living-room platform, not just a mobile one\n➡️ What sits beyond distribution in the YouTube–Academy partnership\n\nRead the full article below or on my Substack 👇\n\n#oscars #youtube #movie #streaming\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 2680, "rx": 14, "cm": 6, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["oscars", "youtube", "movie", "streaming", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2678, 14, 6, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2680, 14, 6, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 284, "id": 7407007584815087616, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7407007584815087616/", "ts": "2025-12-17T10:43:43+00:00", "date": "2025-12-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Just got these two Streaming Media Magazine top companies list, which can be useful when you want to scout the market for streaming in gener", "snippet": "Just got these two Streaming Media Magazine top companies list, which can be useful when you want to scout the market for streaming in general.", "chars": 367, "text": "Just got these two Streaming Media Magazine top companies list, which can be useful when you want to scout the market for streaming in general.\nSome you know may be missing but it's a good start.\n🇪🇺 EU/UK companies (51) - Aug 2025\nhttps://lnkd.in/gkbeCPRM\n🇺🇸 US/Canada (100) - Dec 2025\nhttps://lnkd.in/gqmF-JWW\n\nLove that both Cleeng and Dolby OptiView are included 😎", "qtext": "", "imp": 298, "rx": 7, "cm": 0, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Streaming Media Magazine", "company"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 2, "links": 2, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 296, 7, 0, 1], ["2026-10-17", 298, 7, 0, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 285, "id": 7406253962372268032, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7406253962372268032/", "ts": "2025-12-15T08:49:06+00:00", "date": "2025-12-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Tether, Juventus and the Italian Idea of Ownership", "snippet": "🤖 💶 🇮🇹 ⚽ Tether, Juventus and the Italian Idea of Ownership", "chars": 1758, "text": "🤖 💶 🇮🇹 ⚽ Tether, Juventus and the Italian Idea of Ownership\n\nI live in Turin. My football heart is granata — Toro — but when something shakes Juventus, it inevitably shakes the whole city.\nSo I wrote a long-form piece on the Tether bid, the Agnelli response, and what this moment really tells us about ownership, identity and the economics of Italian football.\n\nAnd yes, there’s a moment of Turin irony: the Agnelli family built FIAT, while Tether issues fiat-backed stablecoins… somewhere out there a confused strategy deck probably merged the two, with a ChatGPT hallucination whispering “perfect alignment” for an investment in football. #joke\n\nHere’s what you’ll find in the article:\n➡️ The Italian view from inside Turin, even from the Toro side of town\n➡️ What Tether actually is — and why its Juventus bid was serious\n➡️ Why Exor’s “no” wasn’t financial, but cultural and structural\n➡️ How Juventus’s valuation compares to other top clubs\n➡️ The emerging context: Exor selling its newspaper group, Cairo’s dual role, and why people connect dots\n➡️ A full media review with literal quotes from Corriere, Repubblica, La Stampa, Tuttosport, Gazzetta, Il Sole\n➡️ The FIAT/fiat connection that made the locals smile\n➡️ Three appendices: Who Tether is, Who Exor is, A 102-year timeline of the Agnelli–Juventus relationship (with nine Scudetti and that 1996 Champions League win)\n\nIf you’re interested in ownership structures, sports economics, or the way identity still shapes big decisions in European football, this one is worth the full read.\n\n#investing #football #stablecoin #torino #italy\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. hashtag\n#ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 5200, "rx": 23, "cm": 13, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["joke", "investing", "football", "stablecoin", "torino", "italy", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 5199, 23, 13, 1], ["2026-10-17", 5200, 23, 13, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 286, "id": 7405195900387573760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7405195900387573760/", "ts": "2025-12-12T10:44:44+00:00", "date": "2025-12-12", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "It’s always a good day when you get mentioned in James Emmett newsletter for Leaders in Sport 😀🥸😎", "snippet": "It’s always a good day when you get mentioned in James Emmett newsletter for Leaders in Sport 😀🥸😎", "chars": 97, "text": "It’s always a good day when you get mentioned in James Emmett newsletter for Leaders in Sport 😀🥸😎", "qtext": "- What (or who) is Fifa Creep? (cc Gianni Infantino)\n- Oh lord, won't you buy me a Mercedes-Benz? (cc Marina Storti)\n- What have the creators done now? (cc Carlo De Marchis)\n- Does Piers Morgan know what he's doing? (cc Joe Ravitch)\n- Why is Netflix paying $82bn for something worth $30bn? (cc Ted Sarandos)\n- Any new leagues worth watching? (cc Tammy Henault)\n\nThe questions on the tip of the collective sports industry tongue this week. As ever, answered in full and for free in the Leaders Worth Knowing newsletter I write with David Cushnan.\n\nhttps://lnkd.in/e4UY_3mi", "imp": 336, "rx": 0, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["James Emmett", "person"], ["Leaders in Sport", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 336, 0, 0, 0], ["2026-10-17", 336, 0, 0, 0]], "quoted": "James Emmett"}, {"i": 287, "id": 7404510993034448897, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7404510993034448897/", "ts": "2025-12-10T13:23:09+00:00", "date": "2025-12-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "MediaKind’s Move for Harmonic Video: Consolidation with Consequences", "snippet": "MediaKind + Harmonic Video, a consequential deal", "chars": 1246, "text": "MediaKind + Harmonic Video, a consequential deal\nI’ve tried to look at it from every angle: technology, strategy, consolidation, and the implications for the wider video infrastructure market.\nMy deep dive covers:\n➡️ What’s actually included in the deal and what isn’t\n➡️ How the two product portfolios line up, and where the real overlap sits\n➡️ Why the valuation signals something about the state of the sector\n➡️ An insider look from Thierry Fautier\n➡️ The NBA’s September investment in MediaKind and why it matters now\n➡️ What this means for broadcasters, streamers, sports leagues, and vendors\n➡️ The ripple effects for competitors in encoding, playout, and cloud streaming\n➡️ What happens to companies running Harmonic Video while using other OTT app providers\nI’ve also added a factual appendix comparing MediaKind’s and Harmonic’s video-product lines — only those included in the transaction.\n\nFull article here ⬇️\n\nI have tried to be as accurate as I could but I am sure I could have messed up some parts - let me know in case please.\n\n#streaming #video #deal #sportstech #investing\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 9501, "rx": 49, "cm": 10, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["MediaKind", "company"], ["Harmonic", "company"], ["Thierry Fautier", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["streaming", "video", "deal", "sportstech", "investing", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 9501, 49, 10, 1], ["2026-10-17", 9501, 49, 10, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 288, "id": 7404090617418485761, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7404090617418485761/", "ts": "2025-12-09T09:32:44+00:00", "date": "2025-12-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 77:  Merchant of Record, Explained for Real - with Enzo Bermond", "snippet": "📺 💶 📲 Retention Zone - Ep. 77: Merchant of Record, Explained for Real", "chars": 1407, "text": "📺 💶 📲 Retention Zone - Ep. 77: Merchant of Record, Explained for Real\nThere are two things I still struggle to explain properly to other people: cricket and Merchant of Record. For episode 77 of Retention Zone, I invited Enzo Bermond from Cleeng to at least solve one of the two.\n\nA few angles we cover in the episode and the article that goes with it:\n➡️ What a Merchant of Record actually is in practice, beyond the textbook definition\n➡️ Why PSP ≠ MoR, and what gets lost if you treat them as the same thing\n➡️ How MoR turns tax, compliance, and chargebacks into part of your retention machine\n➡️ When a growing platform should seriously consider MoR (hint: earlier than most people think)\n➡️ How Cleeng approaches MoR as part of a wider stack for recovery, fraud, and customer care\n\nIf you work on streaming, OTT, or any subscription product that wants to go beyond one country, this isn’t a “back-office” topic anymore. It’s part of how you scale safely, keep revenue predictable, and free your team to focus on content and product instead of tax codes.\nFull episode and video below.\n\nGilles Domartini Benedicte Guichard Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Kübra Güven Chris Welsh Donald Res\n\n#d2c #dtc #streaming #subscription #payments\n------------\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1131, "rx": 20, "cm": 0, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Benedicte Guichard", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["d2c", "dtc", "streaming", "subscription", "payments"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1131, 20, 0, 5], ["2026-10-17", 1131, 20, 0, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 289, "id": 7402984828259713024, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402984828259713024/", "ts": "2025-12-06T08:18:43+00:00", "date": "2025-12-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🇮🇹 ⚽ New on Substack: “DAZN and Serie A: The Sport Varou-fake Take”", "snippet": "🇮🇹 ⚽ New on Substack: “DAZN and Serie A: The Sport Varou-fake Take”", "chars": 1152, "text": "🇮🇹 ⚽ New on Substack: “DAZN and Serie A: The Sport Varou-fake Take”\nA conversation with my fictional alter ego, The Sport Varou-fake, to look at the DAZN–Serie A equity rumours with a fact-based/contrarian angle.\n\nInside the article:\n➡️ What Italian media are really reporting on DAZN, Lega Serie A and the 10% TV-money-for-equity swap\n➡️ Why the “NFL model” is a useful benchmark, but structurally different from what’s on the table in Italy\n➡️ Who actually carries the risk if clubs convert guaranteed cash into DAZN shares\n➡️ How sovereign capital, IPO stories and fragile club balance sheets intersect in this deal\n\nA quick note on The Sport Varou-fake:\nIt is a fictional alter ego I created, inspired (with admiration) by Yanis Varoufakis. He looks at sport as a system: geo-politics, macro-economics, capital flows, governance. When the conversation moves beyond tactics and formats into ownership, leverage and power, that’s his territory.\n\nFrom now on, The Sport Varou-fake will live exclusively on Substack – long-form, footnotes, uncomfortable questions included.\n\n👉 Read the full article on Substack (and subscribe).\nhttps://lnkd.in/d48ebim3", "qtext": "", "imp": 1313, "rx": 5, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1313, 5, 1, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1313, 5, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 290, "id": 7402756432259002368, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402756432259002368/", "ts": "2025-12-05T17:11:10+00:00", "date": "2025-12-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Several people asked why I haven’t posted about the “agreed to buy” news around Netflix and WBD.", "snippet": "Several people asked why I haven’t posted about the “agreed to buy” news around Netflix and WBD.", "chars": 366, "text": "Several people asked why I haven’t posted about the “agreed to buy” news around Netflix and WBD.\nSimple: this time I didn’t have anything useful to add to an already massive flood of posts and articles.\nSo I tried a radical strategy in the attention economy: saying nothing.\nApart from this post, of course, which is already breaking my own rule.\n\nA guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 10486, "rx": 78, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 10486, 78, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 10486, 78, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 291, "id": 7402709092764397568, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402709092764397568/", "ts": "2025-12-05T14:03:03+00:00", "date": "2025-12-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Un bellissimo regalo di Natale per ogni bambino/a che gioca a calcio!", "snippet": "Un bellissimo regalo di Natale per ogni bambino/a che gioca a calcio!", "chars": 431, "text": "Un bellissimo regalo di Natale per ogni bambino/a che gioca a calcio!\nCalcio Ribelle\n\nhttps://amzn.eu/d/gAbfX0Z\n\nEnglish version available too.\nJuventus Football Club A.S. Torino Calcio AC Milan FC Internazionale Milano AS Roma S.S. Lazio Como 1907 Bologna FC 1909 Atalanta B.C. U.S. Cremonese U.S. Sassuolo Hellas Verona FC ACF Fiorentina\nLega Serie A FIGC - Federazione Italiana Giuoco Calcio\n#calcio #sportpulito #regalo #natale", "qtext": "", "imp": 196, "rx": 5, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["Calcio Ribelle", "showcase"], ["Juventus Football Club", "company"], ["A.S. Torino Calcio", "company"], ["AC Milan", "company"], ["FC Internazionale Milano", "company"], ["AS Roma", "company"], ["S.S. Lazio", "company"], ["Como 1907", "company"], ["Bologna FC 1909", "company"], ["Atalanta B.C.", "company"], ["U.S. Cremonese", "company"], ["U.S. Sassuolo", "company"], ["Hellas Verona FC", "company"], ["ACF Fiorentina", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["FIGC - Federazione Italiana Giuoco Calcio", "company"]], "tags": ["calcio", "sportpulito", "regalo", "natale"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 196, 5, 0, 2], ["2026-10-17", 196, 5, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 292, "id": 7402654157188501504, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402654157188501504/", "ts": "2025-12-05T10:24:45+00:00", "date": "2025-12-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "LiveModeTV, Portugal and 34 Free World Cup Games on YouTube: Why This Matters Far Beyond Lisbon", "snippet": "⚽ 🏆 🇵🇹 LiveModeTV, Portugal and 34 Free World Cup Games on YouTube: Why This Matters Far Beyond Lisbon", "chars": 1435, "text": "⚽ 🏆 🇵🇹 LiveModeTV, Portugal and 34 Free World Cup Games on YouTube: Why This Matters Far Beyond Lisbon\n\nI started the day early writing about two big media stories, and then this one landed on my desk and changed the plan.\nLiveModeTV is launching in Portugal and will show 34 matches of the 2026 FIFA World Cup for free on YouTube — including every Portugal game. That is not a side story. It’s a signal.\n\nI’ve covered CazéTV before, but this move opens a new chapter. A Brazilian creator-driven sports network stepping into Europe with a World Cup package is something the industry has not really seen before.\nThe article breaks down what matters:\n➡️ what changes for fans in Portugal\n➡️ how this shifts the competitive picture with Sport TV\n➡️ why the creator-led model LiveMode built in Brazil now arrives in Europe\n➡️ what Sergio Lopes explained on stage in Madrid that suddenly makes perfect sense\n➡️ the external reactions\n➡️ the hanging doubt question: who is actually funding this?\nAnd finally, why this may be the first tile in a larger European mosaic.\nFull piece below 👇\n\nLiveMode LiveModeTV CazéTV Sergio Lopes Jo Redfern YouTube Dawid Prokopowicz Nick Meacham SportsPro\nFIFA FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States\n#sportbiz #mediarights #creators\n---------------------------\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad", "qtext": "", "imp": 2671, "rx": 25, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LiveModeTV", "company"], ["CazéTV", "company"], ["LiveMode", "company"], ["LiveModeTV", "company"], ["CazéTV", "company"], ["Sergio Lopes", "person"], ["Jo Redfern", "person"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["Dawid Prokopowicz", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["FIFA", "company"], ["FIFA World Cup 2026™ - Canada, Mexico and the United States", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "mediarights", "creators", "ad"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 2670, 25, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 2671, 25, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 293, "id": 7402272512547487744, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402272512547487744/", "ts": "2025-12-04T09:08:14+00:00", "date": "2025-12-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "What's Going on Between Serie A and DAZN?", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 What's going on between DAZN and Serie A?", "chars": 1512, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 What's going on between DAZN and Serie A?\nIn Italy everyone is talking about DAZN and Lega Serie A again, but this time it’s not about latency, piracy or production quality.\nIt’s about equity.\n\nOver the last few days, Milano Finanza, Calcio e Finanza and others have reported two parallel hypotheses being explored:\n1️⃣ A small equity stake in DAZN Group for Lega Serie A, “sul modello NFL”\n2️⃣ A 10% swap of TV money into DAZN shares for some clubs, starting with those owned by funds\nI try to do one thing clearly: separate what the Italian media actually report from what is still financial fiction.\n\nI go through:\n➡️ The numbers and language used by Milano Finanza, Calcio e Finanza, TiVuBiz, Business People, etc...\n➡️ How the 2024–29 DAZN/Sky deal really works today (fixed fees + revenue share)\n➡️ What changes if clubs start converting part of those guaranteed cheques into equity\n➡️ Why everyone keeps citing the “NFL model”, and why the NFL–ESPN deal is not the same thing as what’s on the table in Italy\n\nAs always, the interesting part is not the headline rumour but the second-order effects: governance, conflicts of interest, valuation risk, and what this says about the future relationship between leagues and platforms.\n\nIf you work in sport, media or private equity, this one is worth a careful read.\n 👉 Full article below.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad\n\n#football #sportbiz #investing #italy", "qtext": "", "imp": 7391, "rx": 23, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["DAZN", "company"], ["Lega Serie A", "company"], ["Milano Finanza", "company"], ["Calcio e Finanza", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["ad", "football", "sportbiz", "investing", "italy"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 7389, 23, 4, 1], ["2026-10-17", 7391, 23, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 294, "id": 7402244339214508032, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7402244339214508032/", "ts": "2025-12-04T07:16:17+00:00", "date": "2025-12-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ A new chapter for A guy with a scarf 📺 📲", "snippet": "🎙️ A new chapter for A guy with a scarf 📺 📲", "chars": 1051, "text": "🎙️ A new chapter for A guy with a scarf 📺 📲\nOver the next months, my newsletter and video work will be collaborating with a partner whose focus fits perfectly with everything I’ve been exploring around streaming, product, and fan experience.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\nIf you follow my work, you know how much time I spend trying to understand the mechanics behind live and on-demand experiences — the real engines, not the buzzwords. Dolby OptiView is working exactly in that space, helping teams, leagues, and platforms raise the quality and performance of what fans actually see.\nThis partnership allows me to go deeper, publish more often, and keep experimenting across formats.\n\n😎 New episodes, new analysis, new field notes coming soon.\nThanks for reading, sharing, and challenging my work — it’s what keeps this whole one-man project alive.\n\nThe One Man Brand Matthias Ankli David Miller Paul Boustead\n\n#sportstech #video #SGAI #adtech #streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 1273, "rx": 27, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"], ["David Miller", "person"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "video", "sgai", "adtech", "streaming"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-16", 1272, 27, 2, 1], ["2026-10-17", 1273, 27, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 295, "id": 7401985985107890176, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7401985985107890176/", "ts": "2025-12-03T14:09:41+00:00", "date": "2025-12-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A Personal Reflection on Innovation and Marketing", "snippet": "😎 A personal reflection on Marketing and Innovation.", "chars": 1290, "text": "😎 A personal reflection on Marketing and Innovation.\n🤷 I’ve just published one of the most personal pieces I’ve written in a long time.\n\nIt started with a podcast episode featuring Rory Sutherland, and within minutes I found myself hearing someone else describe the exact tension I’ve lived for more than three decades: why meaningful innovation inside organisations so often looks irrational, fragile, or even subversive.\nNot because ideas are bad, but because the system is built to defend itself.\nRory articulates this dynamic with a clarity that hits uncomfortably close to home: the asymmetry of risk, the cult of rationality, the tyranny of metrics, the gravitational pull of CFO logic, and the way distinctiveness gets filtered out before it can breathe.\n\nIf you’ve ever seen a brilliant idea die slowly, or watched something succeed only because someone protected it quietly, this one will resonate.\nThe full article digs into the parts of the conversation that matter most for anyone working in marketing, product, innovation, or the messy space where creativity meets corporate structure.\n\n👉 Full story below.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad\n\nThe One Man Brand\n\n#marketing #innovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 1141, "rx": 14, "cm": 2, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ad", "marketing", "innovation"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1141, 14, 2, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 296, "id": 7401621235505303552, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7401621235505303552/", "ts": "2025-12-02T14:00:18+00:00", "date": "2025-12-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025", "snippet": "🤖 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025", "chars": 2274, "text": "🤖 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025\nEvery December, AWS offers a glimpse into where cloud, AI, and data are heading. I look at it through a sports-tech lens. Not because I’m in Las Vegas, and not because I’m connected to AWS, but because these sessions act as an annual snapshot of how the cloud is reshaping sport. It’s a useful way to understand what leagues, platforms, and vendors are actually building at the end of 2025.\n\n🏈 🏀 ⚽ 🏌‍♀️ The NFL shows how a decade of Next Gen Stats is evolving into real-time agents, predictive insights, and richer tactical models. The NBA goes even further, revealing a full-stack cloud migration and a new intelligence layer that brings advanced stats and data-driven storytelling closer to fans.\nThe Bundesliga highlights the shift toward real-time access, smarter workflows, and global multi-language content powered by GenAI. The PGA TOUR demonstrates what agentic automation can really do, with fast, deep, highly structured content built within minutes of play concluding.\n\n🏆 Motorsport adds another angle. Formula 1 and NASCAR treat telemetry as a live data product, turning raw signal into strategy, predictions, and broadcast intelligence. Fan-experience teams across major leagues show how open lakehouse architectures, Bedrock, and ML are enabling personalised, immersive sports journeys.\n\n⚙️ On the infrastructure side, Sky, Redis, and Ticketmaster demonstrate what it takes to handle extreme peaks in streaming and ticketing. Their architectures show where media and live-event technology are heading. Betting platforms like DraftKings and FanDuel reveal how AI assistants and Local Zones are reshaping product design, UX, and regulatory compliance. Performance vendors like Catapult illustrate the move from tracking athletes to making real-time decisions with IoT and ML. And behind it all, security leaders from global sports and entertainment brands outline how platform resilience must evolve to keep pace with these shifts.\n\nThis is the sports-tech picture coming out of AWS re:Invent 2025, click through the 10+slides below.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView – Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\nAmazon Web Services (AWS)\n#sportstech #ai #cloud #data", "qtext": "", "imp": 3232, "rx": 26, "cm": 2, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Amazon Web Services (AWS)", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "ai", "cloud", "data"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3232, 26, 2, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 297, "id": 7401541600566231040, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7401541600566231040/", "ts": "2025-12-02T08:43:51+00:00", "date": "2025-12-02", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Reading 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025", "snippet": "🤖 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025", "chars": 1183, "text": "🤖 10 Sports Tech Signals from AWS re:Invent 2025\nEvery December, AWS gives a preview of how cloud, AI, and data are about to impact entire industries, my focus is on Sport, so I dissected what is being presented to get a sense of the status of Sport Tech at end of 2025.\n\n 1️⃣ 🏈 NFL, Ten Years of Next Gen Stats and a Sprint Into AI Agents\n 2️⃣ 🏀 The NBA’s Full-Stack AI Vision\n 3️⃣ ⚽ Bundesliga’s Push For Real-Time Data Access\n 4️⃣ ⛳ The PGA TOUR Shows What Agentic AI Actually Does\n 5️⃣ 🏎️ F1, NASCAR, and Real-Time Models\n 6️⃣ 🎮 Fan Engagement and the New Architecture of Experience\n 7️⃣ 📺 Managing Peaks in Streaming and Beyond, Sky, Redis, and Ticketmaster\n 8️⃣ 💸 Betting and Gaming, DraftKings and FanDuel Rebuild the Edge\n 9️⃣ ⚙️ Performance and Wearables, Catapult’s IoT Leap\n 🔟 🔐 Security, The Platforms That Never Sleep\n\nRead more in the article below\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\nAmazon Web Services (AWS) National Football League (NFL) National Basketball Association (NBA) NASCAR PGA TOUR FanDuel Ticketmaster Julie Neenan Souza Bundesliga Formula 1\n\n#sportstech #ai #cloud", "qtext": "", "imp": 2128, "rx": 26, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Amazon Web Services (AWS)", "company"], ["National Football League (NFL)", "company"], ["National Basketball Association (NBA)", "company"], ["NASCAR", "company"], ["PGA TOUR", "company"], ["FanDuel", "company"], ["Ticketmaster", "company"], ["Julie Neenan Souza", "person"], ["Bundesliga", "showcase"], ["Formula 1", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "ai", "cloud"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2128, 26, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 298, "id": 7401204735401181184, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7401204735401181184/", "ts": "2025-12-01T10:25:16+00:00", "date": "2025-12-01", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Fast Tracks & Dark Deals, Up Close: Deals, Politics, and the Product We Ship. Michael Payne's Book", "snippet": "📖 Fast Tracks & Dark Deals - Michael Payne's Book", "chars": 1147, "text": "📖 Fast Tracks & Dark Deals - Michael Payne's Book\nNot a traditional review, more of a reading map for the massive endeavour.\n\nA lively, insider memoir about how modern sport was built and sold — from the Olympic turnaround to F1 and the era of “mega deals.” Payne writes like someone who’s been in the room for 40 years because… he has. There’s swagger, scars, and enough play-by-play to make any sports-media exec nod, wince, and take notes.\n\nIn the article I share:\n➡️ why some chapters hit closer to home than others\n➡️ the sections anyone in sport, media, tech or investment should read first\n➡️ a simple role-by-role guide for navigating the book\n➡️ and a final appendix “for those who like spicy readings”\n🤖 ... and a short The Sport Varou-fake take at the very end\n\nNo spoilers — just pointers, context, and the bits that intersect most with the work we all do now.\n\nIf you’re curious about the power plays behind how the industry was shaped — and where it might go next — you may enjoy this one.\n\n😎 A guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\n#sportbiz #books", "qtext": "", "imp": 1923, "rx": 11, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Michael Payne", "person"], ["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "books"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1923, 11, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 299, "id": 7400126361568354305, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7400126361568354305/", "ts": "2025-11-28T11:00:12+00:00", "date": "2025-11-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "When Football Stories Reach Film Festivals: Juventus, Torino, and a Decade That Still Shapes Us", "snippet": "⚽ 🇮🇹 Juventus - Il decennio d'oro ⚽", "chars": 1558, "text": "⚽ 🇮🇹 Juventus - Il decennio d'oro ⚽\nThis time it’s about a film. But really it’s about sport, culture, and the strange alchemy that happens when a club’s history becomes storytelling — not marketing.\n\n🎬 I watched Juventus – Il decennio d’oro at the Torino Film Festival and something clicked.\nNot because it’s about “the other” club in my city — I’m granata, after all.\nBut because it shows how far football content has travelled, and what it can become when ambition, access, and craft align.\n⚽ We talk a lot about clubs becoming media companies, about “originals,” about access and authenticity. But seeing a football documentary presented inside an author-driven festival, surrounded by filmmakers and cultural critics, brings a different perspective. It tells you something about where the industry is going — and what kind of storytelling will matter next.\nIn the article, I look at that shift through the eyes of a Torinese observer of sport, journalism, and content production. Not a review of the film, not nostalgia, but a reflection on why this kind of project lands differently today and what it signals for anyone working on the intersection of sport, media, and culture.\nIf you’re in content strategy, production, rights, or sports storytelling, there’s something in this decade-long tale that speaks directly to the present moment.\nFull piece below.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans. #ad\n\nJuventus Football Club\n\n#football #soccer #creators #movie #43TFF", "qtext": "", "imp": 2216, "rx": 16, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Juventus Football Club", "company"]], "tags": ["ad", "football", "soccer", "creators", "movie", "43tff"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2216, 16, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 300, "id": 7399747830992486400, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7399747830992486400/", "ts": "2025-11-27T09:56:03+00:00", "date": "2025-11-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "NBA Europe - The Sport Varou-fake Take", "snippet": "🏀 The NBA Europe project is being discussed everywhere, but most reactions focus on format and timelines.", "chars": 1048, "text": "🏀 The NBA Europe project is being discussed everywhere, but most reactions focus on format and timelines.\nThe real story sits deeper — in the geo-political and macro-economic architecture forming beneath the announcement.\n\n🕵 In today’s piece, I look at NBA Europe through The Sport Varou-fake lens:\n a structural reading of who carries the burden, who consolidates the upside, and how the balance of power in European basketball may shift over the next decade.\nIf you’re interested in the underlying mechanics rather than the surface narrative, the full take is now live on the newsletter.\n\n🤷 Disclaimer\nThe Sport Varou-fake is a fictional persona created by A guy with a scarf to explore the geo-political and macro-economic layers of the sport–media ecosystem. The character is inspired, with admiration, by the clarity and structural thinking of Yanis Varoufakis. It offers a sharper voice distinct from my usual tone and is entirely fictional. 🇬🇷\n\nNational Basketball Association (NBA) FIBA Euroleague Basketball\n#basketball #sportbiz #creators", "qtext": "", "imp": 5466, "rx": 26, "cm": 6, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["National Basketball Association (NBA)", "company"], ["FIBA", "company"], ["Euroleague Basketball", "company"]], "tags": ["basketball", "sportbiz", "creators"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 5466, 26, 6, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 301, "id": 7399509468453089280, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7399509468453089280/", "ts": "2025-11-26T18:08:53+00:00", "date": "2025-11-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🎙️ Introducing a New Voice in My Work: \"The Sport Varou-fake\"", "snippet": "🎙️ Introducing a New Voice in My Work: \"The Sport Varou-fake\"", "chars": 1564, "text": "🎙️ Introducing a New Voice in My Work: \"The Sport Varou-fake\"\nBringing a contrarian macro-economic/geo-political lens into the conversations shaping our industry.\n\n🌎 I’ve been spending a lot of time lately watching/listening to Yanis Varoufakis.\nIf you don't know him you should. His point of views on geo-political and macro-economic matters always make me think.\nNot for the political theatre, but for the way he dissects systems — incentives, power structures, the geo-economic layer behind decisions that look purely tactical on the surface.\nAnd it made me think:\nour sport-media world has its own version of that layer.\nBut we rarely talk about it with the same clarity and bluntness.\nSo I’m adding a new character to my work: The Sport Varou-fake.\nNot an imitation of Varoufakis.\nNot a joke.\nJust a fictional persona that gives me the freedom to explore the structural side of sport and media with a sharper, colder tone — when the topic demands it.\nHe’ll show up in dedicated sections inside my long-forms — the Sport Varou-fake Angle — and sometimes as standalone pieces when a full macro take is needed.\n\n🤷 Why do this?\nBecause some dynamics in our industry are better understood when a different voice steps into the room. A voice that follows the incentives first, ignores the noise, and doesn’t mind being a bit uncomfortable.\nThe first “Sport Varou-fake Take” will be out tomorrow, looking at NBA Europe with that lens.\nCurious to see where this new voice will take the conversation.\nExpect a \"take\" for most major topics.\n\n#sportbiz #creators #greece", "qtext": "", "imp": 4483, "rx": 13, "cm": 7, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"], ["The Sport Varou-fake", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "creators", "greece"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 4483, 13, 7, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 302, "id": 7399385401913688064, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7399385401913688064/", "ts": "2025-11-26T09:55:53+00:00", "date": "2025-11-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "NBA Europe: Fixing the Game or Taking It Over?", "snippet": "🏀 NBA Europe: Fixing the Game or Taking It Over?", "chars": 1522, "text": "🏀 NBA Europe: Fixing the Game or Taking It Over?\nHow the NBA–FIBA super league could solve Europe’s basketball economics while rewriting the role of EuroLeague and domestic clubs.\n\n🕵 I was really fascinated when I saw the NBA Europe announcement and thought I had to deep dive because I was already confused before - my fault, not following it as I should - about basketball in Europe before the NBA announced they will invade our continent.\nJokes apart, in terms of fans and audience, I don’t need data to state that the NBA has a huge cultural influence on anything basketball here, and with all respect any other non-NBA content has more of a niche flavour than a mainstream one.\nSo I started looking at official announcement and informed media (and people) posts and analyze what this move could mean for the existing basketball ecosystem.\nHere is the results. All from public sources, with my usual ignorant curiosity applied, so let me know of any mistakes and misinterpretations, because I am sure there are some.\n\n📖 I am urging you to have a look at the whole long form article below, it was quite some work. No spoilers in the post. Anti-algorithm and growth - but who cares.\nEnjoy.\n\n😎 By the way, I have friends working at all 3 entities, hope they sill like me (if they ever did) after this.\n\nNational Basketball Association (NBA) FIBA Euroleague Basketball\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView - Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\n#basketball #sportbiz #sport", "qtext": "", "imp": 4936, "rx": 39, "cm": 8, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["National Basketball Association (NBA)", "company"], ["FIBA", "company"], ["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["basketball", "sportbiz", "sport"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 4936, 39, 8, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 303, "id": 7398990273998913536, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7398990273998913536/", "ts": "2025-11-25T07:45:47+00:00", "date": "2025-11-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 76 — Tom Burrell: The Moments of Truth That Decide Retention", "snippet": "🎙️ Just recorded Episode 76 (Wow!) of Retention Zone — and it’s one of the clearest conversations I’ve had about what retention really is in 2025.", "chars": 1508, "text": "🎙️ Just recorded Episode 76 (Wow!) of Retention Zone — and it’s one of the clearest conversations I’ve had about what retention really is in 2025.\nMy guest was Tom Burrell, who spent 26 years across agencies, telcos, Manchester United and DAZN before launching Retention Coach.\nWhat I enjoyed most is how honestly he breaks the myth of “retention as a marketing task.”\nA few things that stayed with me:\n➡️ “To be a specialist in retention requires you to be a generalist in multiple disciplines.”\n ➡️ “No one wants a dashboard… they want the answer.”\n ➡️ “AI will amplify rubbish. If your data is weak, the decisions will be wrong.”\n ➡️ “The biggest driver of cancellation on Netflix is doom-scrolling.”\n ➡️ “If you can’t provide the payment method a customer wants… you might not get that customer.”\nWe talked about moments of truth, data foundations, agentic AI, creators launching their own apps, and why smaller brands have a much tougher battle than the giants.\nIf you work in streaming, subscriptions, sports, media, or the creator economy, this is a conversation worth your time.\n📩 Full article here below.\nLet me know what you think.\n\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf\n\nGilles Domartini Benedicte Guichard Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Kübra Güven Chris Welsh Donald Res\n\n#RetentionZone #Streaming #Subscriptions #CRM #AI #SportsBiz #DTC #ProductManagement #CustomerExperience", "qtext": "", "imp": 838, "rx": 18, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Tom Burrell", "person"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Benedicte Guichard", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Donald Res", "person"]], "tags": ["retentionzone", "streaming", "subscriptions", "crm", "ai", "sportsbiz", "dtc", "productmanagement", "customerexperience"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 838, 18, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 304, "id": 7398678336115314688, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7398678336115314688/", "ts": "2025-11-24T11:06:16+00:00", "date": "2025-11-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Voices from SportsPro Media Summit Madrid", "snippet": "Last week in Madrid, during the SportsPro Media Summit, I tried something simple: I asked a few people I respect to give me one thought about 2026. No long inte", "chars": 764, "text": "Last week in Madrid, during the SportsPro Media Summit, I tried something simple: I asked a few people I respect to give me one thought about 2026. No long interviews, no scripts — just quick takes on what they see coming next.\nI’ve now pulled all the videos together.\nHere are the voices who shared their view:\n🎙️ Andreas Heyden – Dyn\n🎙️ Julien Segui – Euroleague Basketball\n🎙️ Paul Boustead – Dolby OptiView\n🎙️ Hjalmar Koedijker – Cleeng\n🎙️ Ophir Zardok Shemer – LiveU\n🎙️ Paola Marinone – BuzzMyVideos\n🎙️ Jo Redfern – Creator & Strategist\nEach of them picked a different angle — platforms, leagues, quality, subscriptions, live production, creators — and together they sketch a sharper picture of what 2026 might look like.\n\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportstech #event", "qtext": "", "imp": 5124, "rx": 56, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Andreas Heyden", "person"], ["Dyn", "company"], ["Julien Segui", "person"], ["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["Paul Boustead", "person"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Ophir Zardok Shemer", "person"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["Jo Redfern", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "event"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 5124, 56, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 305, "id": 7397616682871152640, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7397616682871152640/", "ts": "2025-11-21T12:47:38+00:00", "date": "2025-11-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "UEFA Club Competitions 2027–2031: A New Map of Europe’s Media Landscape", "snippet": "⚽ 📺 📲 UEFA Club Competitions 2027–2031: A New Map of Europe’s Media Landscape", "chars": 1667, "text": "⚽ 📺 📲 UEFA Club Competitions 2027–2031: A New Map of Europe’s Media Landscape\nI have been involved in the UEFA Champions League since the very start, so I can't avoid keeping a constant eye on its development.\n\nJust finished pulling together a long read on the new UEFA club-competition rights for 2027–2031.\nToday’s announcements across the UK, Germany, Spain, Italy and France say much more than “who won the tender.”\nThey reveal a Europe splitting in two.\nOn one side, France, Spain and Italy double down on simplicity with a single, dominant broadcaster.\nOn the other, the UK and Germany move decisively into a multi-platform world led by streaming players — Paramount+, Amazon, and potentially Netflix.\n\nThe more I looked at it, the clearer the pattern became: this isn’t just a reshuffle of packages. It’s the first rights cycle shaped by the UC3–Relevent commercial model, with structures built for cross-market bids and platform-specific products. And it shows.\n\nI also pulled in reactions from two strong industry voices – Dawid Prokopowicz and Paolo Pescatore – who both spot the same fault line: the gravity of European football is drifting toward US-owned platforms, and subscription stacking is going to be the new normal in certain markets.\n\n👉 If you want the full picture – the structure, the geography, the winners, the tensions, and the unseen architecture behind all of it – the article is here below.\n Happy to hear where you think this is heading next.\n\nA guy with a scarf is collaborating with Dolby OptiView — Enabling live sports experiences that engage and captivate fans.\n\n#UEFA #ChampionsLeague #MediaRights #Streaming #SportsBusiness #AGWAS", "qtext": "", "imp": 4418, "rx": 41, "cm": 14, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["UEFA", "company"], ["UEFA Champions League", "company"], ["Dawid Prokopowicz", "person"], ["Paolo Pescatore", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"]], "tags": ["uefa", "championsleague", "mediarights", "streaming", "sportsbusiness", "agwas"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "footer"}], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 4418, 41, 14, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 306, "id": 7397250945904029696, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7397250945904029696/", "ts": "2025-11-20T12:34:19+00:00", "date": "2025-11-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Just back home after Madrid - saw this photo on the SportsPro photo gallery.", "snippet": "Just back home after Madrid - saw this photo on the SportsPro photo gallery.", "chars": 598, "text": "Just back home after Madrid - saw this photo on the SportsPro photo gallery.\nPretty accurate depiction of my last two days: sitting in front row with many devices at hand and a scarf, asking questions when relevant, taking photos and few notes, researching when I don't know what or who is mentioned, save potential contacts.\nI follow as much content/sessions as I can.\nMeet people in between.\nI am almost always online.\nI guess everybody has its own way to be at events and conferences in our industry.\nCurious about how others operate.\nWhat's your way?\n\n#sportbiz #sportstech #events #conferences", "qtext": "", "imp": 3397, "rx": 34, "cm": 7, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "sportstech", "events", "conferences"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3397, 34, 7, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 307, "id": 7396939400141684736, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396939400141684736/", "ts": "2025-11-19T15:56:21+00:00", "date": "2025-11-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Day 2 at the SportsPro Media Summit — with Paola Marinone & Jo Redfern joining me for another roundup.", "snippet": "Day 2 at the SportsPro Media Summit — with Paola Marinone & Jo Redfern joining me for another roundup.", "chars": 1772, "text": "Day 2 at the SportsPro Media Summit — with Paola Marinone & Jo Redfern joining me for another roundup.\nI opened the morning listening about the Olympics with Yiannis Exarchos from OBS — always an eye-opener — and then pushed out my long piece article reviewing Nick Meacham keynote (make sure you read that here on linkedIn or my Substack).\n\nBut the real highlight was joining Paola and Jo for a quick debrief on what we’d just lived on stage at the YouTube forum.\n🎙️ Paola had a full house for her session on UR and the Cristiano Ronaldo channel — 77 million subscribers built at speed, tripling FIFA’s own presence. What came through clearly: the YouTube playbook is trial, error, iteration, and global intent. And, as they pointed out, Javi Gómez explained it simply — Ronaldo conquered Instagram, so YouTube became the natural next home for both his story and his future business.\n\n🎙️ Jo then jumped to CazéTV and her interview with Sergio Lopes from LiveMode. A reminder that “YouTube-first” doesn’t mean cheap filler. Their work is now tied to major partners, strong RPMs, and serious brands like Coca-Cola and Hellmann’s. Real scale, real business.\nPaola nailed it: CazéTV isn’t an experiment — it’s a model to study. Built on data, intent, and an ability to monetise with discipline.\n And Sergio made one thing very clear: YouTube isn’t a cannibal to FIFA+ or Prime Video — it’s a funnel. It grows the pie.\nIn between all this, half the summit walked past our camera proving, for once, that this was not AI-generated.\n\nFrom Paola (BuzzMyVideos), Jo, and me (A guy with a scarf): Madrid has been loud, lively, and genuinely insightful.\nMy full write-up is already on LinkedIn and Substack.\nCiao from Madrid.\n\n#SportsPro #SportsMedia #YouTube #CreatorEconomy #CazeTV", "qtext": "", "imp": 934, "rx": 15, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"], ["Jo Redfern", "person"], ["Yiannis Exarchos", "person"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["YouTube", "company"], ["Javi Gómez", "person"], ["CazéTV", "company"], ["Sergio Lopes", "person"], ["LiveMode", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportspro", "sportsmedia", "youtube", "creatoreconomy", "cazetv"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 960, "watch_time_s": 18180, "avg_watch_s": 18, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 934, 15, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 308, "id": 7396822921991569408, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396822921991569408/", "ts": "2025-11-19T08:13:30+00:00", "date": "2025-11-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "SportsPro Media Summit - State of the Industry: The Hard Questions We Can’t Avoid", "snippet": "⚽ 🏀 🏈 🎾 📺 📲 State of the Industry", "chars": 1464, "text": "⚽ 🏀 🏈 🎾 📺 📲 State of the Industry\nSportsPro Media Summit - Nick Meacham’s Keynote: The Hard Questions We Can’t Avoid\nNick mapped out where sports media stands in late 2025 — and as I sat there, I felt the urge to go deeper, to challenge each point, and to test the numbers behind the narrative.\nSo I did exactly that.\nI spent last night pulling apart the full transcript, debating every argument, cross-checking the data, and rebuilding the picture in a way that makes sense to me and hopefully for anyone working in sport, media or tech.\n\nThe result is a long, structured analysis — not a recap, but a proper conversation with Nick’s keynote.\nInside you’ll find:\n➡️ Why a handful of properties now take half of global media-rights revenue\n➡️ How to read the latest streamer moves from Apple, Amazon, Netflix and DAZN\n➡️ Why YouTube is both the future of TV — and a trap\n➡️ What Gen Z behaviour actually looks like once you strip out clichés\n➡️ The real shift in ads, hybrid monetisation and data ownership\n➡️ Why piracy is exploding — and how distribution choices contribute\n➡️ Ten questions Nick raised - and my views on that\n\nThe industry feels in motion again — but only if we’re honest about what’s really changing.\nIf you’ve been in the room here this week, I’d love to hear where you disagree, and Nick if you see anything i misrepresented let me know 😎\n\nA guy with a scarf\n#SportsBiz #MediaRights #Streaming #SportsTech #ContentStrategy #AI #SportsIndustry", "qtext": "", "imp": 3225, "rx": 30, "cm": 7, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Nick Meacham", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbiz", "mediarights", "streaming", "sportstech", "contentstrategy", "ai", "sportsindustry"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3225, 30, 7, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 309, "id": 7396599617573879808, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396599617573879808/", "ts": "2025-11-18T17:26:11+00:00", "date": "2025-11-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🏆 Live from Madrid! It's SportsPro Media Summit.", "snippet": "🏆 Live from Madrid! It's SportsPro Media Summit.", "chars": 1872, "text": "🏆 Live from Madrid! It's SportsPro Media Summit.\nA guy with a scarf with Jo Redfern 🔜 SportsPro Media and Paola Marinone\n\n🤖 What captured our attention and what we think will be the focus for 2026.\n\n🤖 Jo’s take — social-first is now mainstream\nJo locked onto one theme: social platforms are no longer the side dish, they’re the main distribution layer for a big part of the industry.\n She referenced TikTok’s numbers — millions of sports posts per day, and the dominance of sport among its top publishers — as proof of that gravitational pull.\n Her view is clear: creators, alternative channels, live social formats, and hybrid deals (like CazeTV) now sit at the centre of any serious distribution strategy.\n\n🤖 Paola’s view — every rights-holder is searching for the right medium\nPaola appreciated how the Summit treated “media” in its broadest sense.\n She highlighted how federations, leagues and broadcasters — big and small — are all experimenting with formats and platforms, trying to find where their audiences actually are.\n What she sees for 2026: trial-and-error, micro-risks, and new distribution patterns.\n And she added a note on AI: we will soon understand what is real, what is noise, and what becomes fully integrated into workflows.\n\n🤖 Carlo’s perspective — constant deal flow and overdue innovation\nCarlo focused on momentum.\n He noted how, week after week, new deals appear — deals that five years ago nobody would have imagined.\n The combination of opportunity and pressure pushes everyone to innovate and find fresh revenue paths.\n He also pointed out something ironic: many “new” innovations discussed on stage (instant recap, automated formats, real-time content) have been technically possible for 10–15 years — but are only now getting traction.\n\n...and getting ready for the Awards night.\n\n#sportsbiz #sportstech #creators #socialmedia #youtube #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 2724, "rx": 64, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Jo Redfern 🔜 SportsPro Media", "person"], ["Paola Marinone", "person"]], "tags": ["sportsbiz", "sportstech", "creators", "socialmedia", "youtube", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 1272, "watch_time_s": 26580, "avg_watch_s": 20, "saves": 2, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2724, 64, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 310, "id": 7396546096807690240, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396546096807690240/", "ts": "2025-11-18T13:53:30+00:00", "date": "2025-11-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Cloudflare down.", "snippet": "Cloudflare down.", "chars": 929, "text": "Cloudflare down.\nToo early to comment on this but...\nI wrote this some weeks ago:\n----------------------------------------------\nI’ve finally earned the right to launch a new tech conspiracy theory 😅🕵️‍♂️\n\n• ⚠️ AWS went down hard last week (hours of pain)\n• ⚠️ Azure stumbled yesterday (global, very visible)\n• 👀 Now I’m… patiently waiting for Google Cloud — not out of malice, out of logic\n\nHere’s the twist: these outages won’t shrink the cloud market — they’ll grow it 📈\n\nIf you run anything mission-critical on one cloud, you’re one change/config/region away from darkness 🌑. Boards will ask for resilience 🛡️, CTOs will answer with multi-cloud 🔁, and budgets will follow 💸.\n\n🔮 Prediction: if GCP hiccups next, the trilogy is complete and spending tilts further toward redundancy, portability, and failover by design. If not… just a late-day thought from A guy with a scarf.\n\nSingle-cloud is a vibe. Resilience is a strategy.", "qtext": "", "imp": 721, "rx": 7, "cm": 5, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 721, 7, 5, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 311, "id": 7396477699453685760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396477699453685760/", "ts": "2025-11-18T09:21:43+00:00", "date": "2025-11-18", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "LIVE Sport Beyond Live Sport", "snippet": "📺 📲 LIVE sport beyond live sport", "chars": 2070, "text": "📺 📲 LIVE sport beyond live sport\n[English and Italian version]\nOver the past few years, we’ve talked endlessly about streaming, VOD, algorithms, platforms, subscriptions.\n But the more the industry evolves, the more one thing becomes clear: live is still the centre of gravity.\nNot the old linear feed — but the expanded live ecosystem that sits around it.\n During a recent webinar with LiveU and Manuela Callea, I tried to unpack why live still matters so much, how new generations actually experience it, and how technology is finally enabling formats that didn’t exist a few years ago.\nFrom alt-casts to creators pitch-side, from cloud production to distributed workflows, live sport is no longer a single feed — it’s a network of perspectives, moments and emotions.\nAnd LiveU’s work shows what happens when technology becomes the bridge between imagination and production.\n👉 I wrote it all down here — the full story, in depth:\n\nIf you care about sports media, fan engagement, or the evolution of live production, I think you’ll enjoy this one.\n—\n[ITALIANO]\n Negli ultimi anni abbiamo parlato tantissimo di streaming, VOD, algoritmi e piattaforme.\n Eppure, più il settore cambia, più una cosa diventa evidente: il live resta il punto attorno a cui tutto ruota.\nNon il live tradizionale, ma l’ecosistema di contenuti che nasce intorno all’evento.\n Nel webinar con LiveU e Manuela Callea ho provato a spiegare perché il live oggi conta più che mai, come le nuove generazioni lo vivono davvero e come la tecnologia stia finalmente aprendo possibilità che prima non esistevano.\nDagli alt-cast ai creator a bordocampo, dalle produzioni cloud ai workflow distribuiti, il live sport non è più un singolo segnale — è una rete di prospettive ed emozioni.\nE la visione tecnologica di LiveU dimostra cosa succede quando l’innovazione permette di raccontare ciò che prima restava invisibile.\n👉 Ho raccolto tutto qui, in modo completo:\n\nSe lavorate nello sport, nei media o nell’innovazione digitale, credo vi piacerà.\nOphir Zardok Shemer Renata María Basso\n#sportstech #live #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 785, "rx": 14, "cm": 4, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"], ["Ophir Zardok Shemer", "person"], ["Renata María Basso", "person"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "live", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 785, 14, 4, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 312, "id": 7396098760725925888, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7396098760725925888/", "ts": "2025-11-17T08:15:57+00:00", "date": "2025-11-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "After a week of ATP Finals in the city, culminating with an amazing win for Jannik Sinner... I am getting ready to fly to Madrid for the Spo", "snippet": "After a week of ATP Finals in the city, culminating with an amazing win for Jannik Sinner... I am getting ready to fly to Madrid for the SportsPro Media Summit,", "chars": 953, "text": "After a week of ATP Finals in the city, culminating with an amazing win for Jannik Sinner... I am getting ready to fly to Madrid for the SportsPro Media Summit, where I will:\n🎙️  Moderate the panel: \"From Stadium to Studio: How Technology Is Redefining Live Sports\" with Jose Luis G. (Telefónica) and Sergio Ruiz Ranero (Vizrt)\n🏆 Attend the Award Ceremony and even present the Best Emerging Tech Award\n🤖 Support my clients at the event: Cleeng, Dolby OptiView, LiveU, WSC Sports, BuzzMyVideos\n🥰 Look for new clients who need to grow, amplify and innovate\n\nMeet me at the event, the award or at my usual early breakfast - I guess you know how to find me (A guy with a scarf).\n\nSad I can not also be with Massimo Tucci and the fantastic team at SFS - Social Football Summit which this year are in my own Torino, in the same days. Good luck for the event!\n\nATP Tour ATP Media\nRetention Zone The One Man Brand FACTORY63\n#sportbiz #sportstech #ai #innovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 1108, "rx": 35, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Jose Luis G.", "person"], ["Telefónica", "company"], ["Sergio Ruiz Ranero", "person"], ["Vizrt", "company"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["WSC Sports", "company"], ["BuzzMyVideos", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Massimo Tucci", "person"], ["SFS - Social Football Summit", "showcase"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["ATP Media", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"], ["FACTORY63", "company"]], "tags": ["sportbiz", "sportstech", "ai", "innovation"], "series": ["Retention Zone"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 399, "watch_time_s": 6480, "avg_watch_s": 16, "saves": 0, "sends": 1, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1108, 35, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 313, "id": 7395513817843253248, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7395513817843253248/", "ts": "2025-11-15T17:31:36+00:00", "date": "2025-11-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Andrea Gaudenzi in Turin: Inside the Vision Shaping the Future of Men’s Tennis", "snippet": "🎾 🎾 🎾 I spent the last days around the Nitto ATP Finals in Turin, listening closely to Andrea Gaudenzi’s press conference. 🎾 🎾 🎾", "chars": 1238, "text": "🎾 🎾 🎾 I spent the last days around the Nitto ATP Finals in Turin, listening closely to Andrea Gaudenzi’s press conference. 🎾 🎾 🎾\nIt was one of the clearest windows into how men’s tennis is changing — and how complex the next five years will be.\n\nI’ve written a long-form breakdown of everything that matters: strategy, governance, media economics, and the very Italian debate over where the Finals will actually be played until 2030.\n\nHere’s what you’ll find in the article:\n🎾 How Gaudenzi evaluates the first five years of the ATP Finals in Turin\n🎾 The real story behind the “where will the Finals go?” debate through 2030\n🎾 Why the ATP is reducing the number of 250s and reshaping the calendar\n🎾 The logic behind longer Masters 1000 events and the new economics\n🎾 The push for unified governance under OneVision\n🎾 Player pressures, scheduling tensions, and the call for a longer off-season\n\nMy own take on Gaudenzi as a leader and what his plan means for the sport\nIf you follow the intersection of sport, media, and product strategy, this one is worth your time.\n\nFull article below ⬇️\n\n🎙️ Subscribe to my newsletter A guy with a scarf on LinkedIn or Substack\n\nATP Tour ATP Media\n#sportsbiz #tennis #ATP #NittoATPFinals #AGuyWithAScarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 9460, "rx": 81, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Andrea Gaudenzi", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["ATP Media", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbiz", "tennis", "atp", "nittoatpfinals", "aguywithascarf"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 9460, 81, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 314, "id": 7395038721911271424, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7395038721911271424/", "ts": "2025-11-14T10:03:44+00:00", "date": "2025-11-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "MLS × Apple TV 2026: The First Global Streaming Reset in Modern Football", "snippet": "⚽ 🌎 MLS × Apple TV 2026: The First Global Streaming Reset in Modern Football", "chars": 1303, "text": "⚽ 🌎 MLS × Apple TV 2026: The First Global Streaming Reset in Modern Football\n\nOver the last few hours I’ve been working through the MLS × Apple TV news for 2026.\n➡️ Season Pass disappears, every match moves into the standard Apple TV subscription, and all season-ticket holders get Apple TV included.\nAt first even I asked myself the obvious question: “So… is MLS now basically free for everyone?”\n➡️ The answer is more interesting than it looks.\nIn the long-form piece I’ve just finished, I try to unpack the difference between removing a paywall and removing the cost. Apple isn’t giving away the league — it’s simplifying the route to it, shifting the gate from a niche add-on to the main subscription where people already spend their time.\n\nThis changes the product, the value perception, the league’s global reach, and the identity of Apple TV itself.\nIt raises bigger questions about how live sport fits into streaming platforms in the next decade.\nIf you follow sports media, product evolution, or the future of distribution, this is a moment worth watching.\nA guy with a scarf\n\nFull article here ⬇️\n\n🎙️ Subscribe to my newsletter A guy with a scarf on LinkedIn or Substack\n\n#SportsBusiness #MediaTech #Streaming #AppleTV #MLS #SportsMedia #FutureOfSports #DigitalDistribution #FanEngagement #OTT", "qtext": "", "imp": 2972, "rx": 29, "cm": 3, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsbusiness", "mediatech", "streaming", "appletv", "mls", "sportsmedia", "futureofsports", "digitaldistribution", "fanengagement", "ott"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2972, 29, 3, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 315, "id": 7394286165320863744, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7394286165320863744/", "ts": "2025-11-12T08:13:21+00:00", "date": "2025-11-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Live Webinar: Occhi puntati sul Live - Focus on Live Sport with LiveU", "snippet": "Registrati per 🇮🇹 🎙 il mio primo webinar in italiano!", "chars": 373, "text": "Registrati per 🇮🇹 🎙 il mio primo webinar in italiano!\nIl 13 novembre alle 15:00 sarò Special Guest di un webinar con LiveU: “Occhi puntati sul Live: conquista i fan con il potere del video in diretta”. Host del webinar Manuela Callea (LiveU Italia).\nPiccolo segreto... Una offerta promozionale LiveU per tutti gli iscritti al webinar\n👉 Iscriviti qui: https://bit.ly/47mWRSs", "qtext": "", "imp": 609, "rx": 15, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 609, 15, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 316, "id": 7393926913502728192, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7393926913502728192/", "ts": "2025-11-11T08:25:48+00:00", "date": "2025-11-11", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 75: Mota Elb – From Data to Devotion: The Retention Mindset in Sports", "snippet": "🎧 Retention Zone Ep. 75: Motasem El Bawab (CIO at N3XT SPORTS) – From Data to Devotion: The Retention Mindset in Sports", "chars": 1021, "text": "🎧 Retention Zone Ep. 75: Motasem El Bawab (CIO at N3XT SPORTS) – From Data to Devotion: The Retention Mindset in Sports\n\nWe go deep into what it really takes to build and keep a digital audience in sport — from OTT cycles to data, localization, and the real meaning of retention.\n👉 We talk about:\n➡️ The early days of federations going D2C\n➡️ Why so many OTT projects failed (and what changed)\n➡️ The “fan as customer” mindset\n➡️ Retention as orchestration: tech, data, content, and marketing playing in sync\n➡️ AI’s new role — moving from dashboards to decisions\n\n🎙️ “It’s easier to attract new users than to retain the ones you already have. The real work begins once they’re inside your ecosystem.” — Mota Elb\n\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf\n\nGilles Domartini Benedicte Guichard Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Simo Enzo Bermond Kübra Güven Chris Welsh\n\n#Retention #SportsTech #OTT #FanEngagement #Streaming #AI", "qtext": "", "imp": 947, "rx": 19, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["N3XT SPORTS", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Benedicte Guichard", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka Simo", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"]], "tags": ["retention", "sportstech", "ott", "fanengagement", "streaming", "ai"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 947, 19, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 317, "id": 7393664889191301120, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7393664889191301120/", "ts": "2025-11-10T15:04:37+00:00", "date": "2025-11-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Apollo Takes Control: What the Atlético de Madrid Deal Reveals About the New Age of Private Equity in Football", "snippet": "⚽ 🇪🇸 Re-Bundling the Game: Apollo Takes Majority in Atlético de Madrid", "chars": 1075, "text": "⚽ 🇪🇸 Re-Bundling the Game: Apollo Takes Majority in Atlético de Madrid\n A major shift in European football ownership — and a clear sign of where capital, media, and innovation now meet.\n\nIn my latest A guy with a scarf analysis, I break down the Apollo Sports Capital × Atlético deal — what it means for clubs, investors, and the digital ecosystem around them.\n\nInside the article:\n➡️ Context and timeline of the deal (€2.5 B valuation, ~55% stake)\n➡️ What Apollo Sports Capital is and how it operates\n➡️ The new platform logic: clubs as media + infrastructure businesses\n➡️ Key risks and cultural tensions around PE ownership\n➡️ European press and expert voices on the move\n➡️ Why this matters for sport-tech, fan engagement, and future club models\n\nIt’s not just an ownership change — it’s the latest chapter in football’s transformation into a global entertainment and technology platform.\n\n👉 Read the full analysis below\nApollo Global Management, Inc. Club Atlético de Madrid\n\n🎙️ Subscribe to my newsletter A guy with a scarf on LinkedIn or Substack\n\n#football #investing", "qtext": "", "imp": 2892, "rx": 10, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Apollo Global Management, Inc.", "company"], ["Club Atlético de Madrid", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["football", "investing"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2892, 10, 0, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 318, "id": 7393661879677546496, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7393661879677546496/", "ts": "2025-11-10T14:52:39+00:00", "date": "2025-11-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Live Webinar alle 15:00 CET Giovedì 13 Novembre 2025", "snippet": "Live Webinar alle 15:00 CET Giovedì 13 Novembre 2025", "chars": 136, "text": "Live Webinar alle 15:00 CET Giovedì 13 Novembre 2025\nA guy with a scarf per LiveU (Manuela Callea)\nIn Italiano - Registrazione gratutita", "qtext": "", "imp": 310, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"]], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "text", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 310, 9, 1, 0]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 319, "id": 7393601282181148674, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7393601282181148674/", "ts": "2025-11-10T10:51:52+00:00", "date": "2025-11-10", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Re-Bundling Europe: How Sky, ITV, RTL and Warner Bros. Discovery Are Redrawing the Continent’s Media Map", "snippet": "📺 📲 Re-Bundling Europe: How Sky, ITV, RTL and Warner Bros. Discovery Are Redrawing the Continent’s Media Map", "chars": 1706, "text": "📺 📲 Re-Bundling Europe: How Sky, ITV, RTL and Warner Bros. Discovery Are Redrawing the Continent’s Media Map\n\nIt took me some days, but I wanted to dive deep (a bit) on what's going on with SKY and other media groups in Europe.\nFrom London to Cologne, a new map of European broadcasting is taking shape.\n🇬🇧 Sky is in talks to buy ITV’s TV and streaming business — the UK’s free-to-air heart merging into its pay-TV disruptor.\n🇩🇪 RTL Group is buying Sky Deutschland GmbH from Comcast — a mirror deal where pay-TV moves under a free-to-air roof.\n🇺🇸 And Warner Bros. Discovery is splitting itself in two, separating studios/streaming from its global networks and maybe going on sale\n\nThree different plays, one shared logic: scale, control, and the race to own the fan relationship.\nIn the UK, this could create a “super-broadcaster” where every screen — free, paid, or streamed — sits under one gatekeeper.\n In Germany, it’s the end of fragmentation: RTL and Sky merge reach and rights in a single DACH powerhouse.\n For WBD, the split (and possible asset sales) could reshape who bids, who buys, and who distributes sport in Europe.\n\nAfter a decade of unbundling — cord-cutting, DTC platforms, standalone apps — we’re now seeing the opposite:\n the re-bundling of Europe’s media.\n\nIt’s not just a business story. It’s cultural. It’s about how sport, news, and entertainment are re-assembled in fewer, larger ecosystems — and what that means for access, creativity, and innovation.\n\n👉 Read the full analysis below\n\n🎙️ Subscribe to my newsletter A guy with a scarf on LinkedIn or Substack\n\n#SportsMedia #MediaStrategy #StreamingWars #Rebundling #SportsBusiness #MediaInnovation #OTT #Broadcasting #EuropeanMedia", "qtext": "", "imp": 1601, "rx": 13, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sky", "company"], ["RTL Group", "company"], ["Sky Deutschland GmbH", "company"], ["Comcast", "company"], ["Warner Bros. Discovery", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "mediastrategy", "streamingwars", "rebundling", "sportsbusiness", "mediainnovation", "ott", "broadcasting", "europeanmedia"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1601, 13, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 320, "id": 7393186214054346752, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7393186214054346752/", "ts": "2025-11-09T07:22:32+00:00", "date": "2025-11-09", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 📲 Getting ready for SportsPro Media Summit in Madrid where I will moderate the panel: \"From Stadium to Studio: How technology is redefinin", "snippet": "📺 📲 Getting ready for SportsPro Media Summit in Madrid where I will moderate the panel: \"From Stadium to Studio: How technology is redefining live sport\" with J", "chars": 628, "text": "📺 📲 Getting ready for SportsPro Media Summit in Madrid where I will moderate the panel: \"From Stadium to Studio: How technology is redefining live sport\" with Jose Luis G. Garcia Cabrera (Telefónica) and Sergio Ruiz Ranero (Vizrt).\nWill be exploring how technology and collaboration are redefining live content creation and empowering broadcasters to connect with audiences in new more meaningful ways.\n\nI will also be at the SportsPro Media Awards on Tuesday night and will award the Best Emerging Tech Company prize to.... 🤷\n\n🎙️ Subscribe to my newsletter A guy with a scarf on LinkedIn or Substack\n\n#sportstech #broadcast #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 732, "rx": 16, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SportsPro", "company"], ["Jose Luis G.", "person"], ["Telefónica", "company"], ["Sergio Ruiz Ranero", "person"], ["Vizrt", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "broadcast", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "text", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 732, 16, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 321, "id": 7392863166894055424, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7392863166894055424/", "ts": "2025-11-08T09:58:51+00:00", "date": "2025-11-08", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "⚙️ Product people talking product to product people.", "snippet": "⚙️ Product people talking product to product people.", "chars": 458, "text": "⚙️ Product people talking product to product people.\nProduct Byte Live in Shoreditch (London) organized by Tom McDonnell and Holly Tyler was again a very no-nonsense and interesting event.\n\nI was on stage with Iain Smith (ITVx), Russell Barder (World Horse Racing), Simon Christie (Amazon Web Services (AWS)) to discuss: \"Value v Gimmick: Which features do audiences really love and how do you balance innovation with authenticity?\"\n\n#ai #sportstech #product", "qtext": "", "imp": 1272, "rx": 38, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Product Byte Live", "showcase"], ["Tom McDonnell", "person"], ["Holly Tyler", "person"], ["Iain Smith", "person"], ["ITV", "company"], ["Russell Barder", "person"], ["World Horse Racing", "company"], ["Simon Christie", "person"], ["Amazon Web Services (AWS)", "company"]], "tags": ["ai", "sportstech", "product"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1272, 38, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 322, "id": 7392452934107652096, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7392452934107652096/", "ts": "2025-11-07T06:48:44+00:00", "date": "2025-11-07", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Spotify and the ATP Tour: A New Kind of Sports Deal", "snippet": "🎧 🎾 Spotify × ATP Tour: sport as story, not sponsorship", "chars": 1201, "text": "🎧 🎾 Spotify × ATP Tour: sport as story, not sponsorship\nOn Monday I was on stage in Madrid with FC Barcelona, talking about the new generation of sponsorships — the ones that mix content, culture, and storytelling instead of logos and boards.\n Next Monday I’ll be with my ATP Media friends here in Turin, as the Finals bring the tennis world to my city.\nAnd right in between, this drops: ATP Tour × Spotify, a global content collaboration that runs through 2026.\n No naming rights, no shirts — just stories.\n Episodic shows, behind-the-scenes access, and a December documentary built for Spotify’s growing video ecosystem.\nIt’s the same logic as the Barça deal, seen through another lens:\n 👉 Barcelona = brand mass\n 👉 ATP = content finesse\n\nThe big question: is this fan value or platform sizzle?\n That’s what I unpacked in my latest piece — connecting these two Spotify moments and what they say about the future of sport, media, and culture.\n📍From Madrid to Turin. From stadium naming to storytelling.\n 🎾 From surface area to attention span.\n👇 Read the full article below by A guy with a scarf\n\n#SportsMedia #ATPFinals #Spotify #Barça #SportsTech #ContentStrategy #FanEngagement #Storytelling #Turin", "qtext": "", "imp": 3732, "rx": 21, "cm": 3, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Spotify", "company"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "atpfinals", "spotify", "barça", "sportstech", "contentstrategy", "fanengagement", "storytelling", "turin"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3732, 21, 3, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 323, "id": 7392122503676108800, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7392122503676108800/", "ts": "2025-11-06T08:55:43+00:00", "date": "2025-11-06", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 WSC Sports Huddle in Madrid, an exclusive end-of-year gathering for 150+ sports media leaders, innovators, and professionals from across t", "snippet": "🤖 WSC Sports Huddle in Madrid, an exclusive end-of-year gathering for 150+ sports media leaders, innovators, and professionals from across the globe.", "chars": 1138, "text": "🤖 WSC Sports Huddle in Madrid, an exclusive end-of-year gathering for 150+ sports media leaders, innovators, and professionals from across the globe.\nIt was fascinating to be able to moderate different perspectives from leagues, clubs and broadcasters with insightful panels on GenAI, strategy, product innovation, and audience growth.\n\nI shared the stage with:\n⚽ Alfredo Bermejo — Digital Strategy Director, LALIGA\n🏀 Julien Segui — Head of Content, Euroleague Basketball\n📺 📲 Martin AURENCHE — Chief Media Officer, LFP Media\n📺 📲 Cyprien Castanedo — Head of Media Platform & Innovation, LFP Media\n🏀 Bob Carney — SVP, Digital & Social Content, National Basketball Association (NBA)\n📺 📲Alessandro Reitano — SVP Sports Production, Sky Deutschland GmbH\n📺 📲Matthieu Montigaud — Lead Sport Product Manager, CANAL+ Group\n⚽Jordi Mompart — Director of Barça Vision, FC Barcelona\n⚽Roger Forns — Technology & Innovation Director, RCD Mallorca\n🤖 Daniel Shichman Aviv Arnon Ben Mirvis Ido Ratzon Itai Epstein Tomer Coreanu - WSC Sports\n\nThanks to Ranit Etzion Weiss and the team for putting together a well produced and attended event.\n\n#ai #sportstech", "qtext": "", "imp": 2614, "rx": 76, "cm": 2, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["WSC Sports", "company"], ["Alfredo Bermejo", "person"], ["LALIGA", "company"], ["Julien Segui", "person"], ["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["Martin AURENCHE", "person"], ["Cyprien Castanedo", "person"], ["Bob Carney", "person"], ["National Basketball Association (NBA)", "company"], ["Alessandro Reitano", "person"], ["Sky Deutschland GmbH", "company"], ["Matthieu Montigaud", "person"], ["CANAL+ Group", "company"], ["Jordi Mompart", "person"], ["FC Barcelona", "company"], ["Roger Forns", "person"], ["RCD Mallorca", "company"], ["Daniel Shichman", "person"], ["Aviv Arnon", "person"], ["Ben Mirvis", "person"], ["Ido Ratzon", "person"], ["Itai Epstein", "person"], ["Tomer Coreanu", "person"], ["WSC Sports", "company"], ["Ranit Etzion Weiss", "person"]], "tags": ["ai", "sportstech"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 4, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2614, 76, 2, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 324, "id": 7391757749509853185, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7391757749509853185/", "ts": "2025-11-05T08:46:19+00:00", "date": "2025-11-05", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🖤⚪ JUVENTUS LIVE SHOPPING", "snippet": "⚽ 📩 I just got an email from Juventus Football Club this morning while I am in London ready to get on stage at Product Byte Live, even if I am a Toro loyal fan,", "chars": 1305, "text": "⚽ 📩 I just got an email from Juventus Football Club this morning while I am in London ready to get on stage at Product Byte Live, even if I am a Toro loyal fan, but have worked with the other Torino club in different capacity through the years.\n\n⚫⚪ JUVENTUS LIVE SHOPPING ⚪⚫\n When football becomes a storefront — and the fan becomes part of the show.\n🕖 Tonight, from 7:00 to 8:30 PM CET, Juventus goes live with its first-ever Live Shopping event, together with adidas and Fanatics.\n\n It’s not just a merch push — it’s a test of a new model: where content, commerce, and community merge in real time.\n👕 Exclusive first look at the adidas Lifestyler Collection\n 💥 Offers available only during the live\n 🎥 Special guests and surprises\n 🛒 And yes — you can buy directly from the stream\n\nI’ll be flying while it happens ✈️ — so I’ll miss the live moment, but I’ll definitely watch the replay. Because this is more than a novelty; it’s a glimpse of how clubs will monetize fan attention in the years ahead.\n\nThink Twitch meets QVC, powered by fandom.\nAfter all, if Real Madrid and now Juventus are exploring this, it’s not just a trend — it’s the start of the moment economy in sport. or at least a try.\n\nA guy with a scarf\n#LiveShopping #SportsInnovation #FanEngagement #Ecommerce #SportsBusiness #DirectToFan", "qtext": "", "imp": 2610, "rx": 29, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Juventus Football Club", "company"], ["Product Byte Live", "showcase"], ["Fanatics", "company"], ["adidas", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["liveshopping", "sportsinnovation", "fanengagement", "ecommerce", "sportsbusiness", "directtofan"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2610, 29, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 325, "id": 7391361102560866304, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7391361102560866304/", "ts": "2025-11-04T06:30:11+00:00", "date": "2025-11-04", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🇮🇹 🎙 Il mio primo webinar in italiano!", "snippet": "🇮🇹 🎙 Il mio primo webinar in italiano!", "chars": 1266, "text": "🇮🇹 🎙 Il mio primo webinar in italiano!\n Il 13 novembre alle 15:00 sarò Special Guest di un webinar con LiveU: “Occhi puntati sul Live: conquista i fan con il potere del video in diretta”. Host del webinar Manuela Callea (LiveU Italia).\nParleremo di come il live — prima, durante e dopo l’evento — possa aumentare reach, interazioni e fedeltà dei fan, con esempi concreti e best practice.\n📺 📲  Se lavori in media, produzione, club/leghe/federazioni, questo ti riguarda: idee pratiche, dietro le quinte e uno sguardo alle soluzioni LiveU per portare più contenuti, meglio e ovunque.\n👉 Iscriviti qui: https://bit.ly/47mWRSs\n\n🇬🇧 🎙 My first webinar in Italian!\n On November 13 at 3:00 PM (CET) I’ll be a guest webinar with LiveU: “Spotlight on Live: win fans with the power of live video.” Joining me is Manuela Callea (LiveU Italy) as host. We’ll unpack how live — before, during, and after the event — drives reach, interaction, and loyalty, with real examples and best practices. If you work in media, production, or clubs/leagues/federations, expect practical ideas, behind-the-scenes insights, and a look at LiveU solutions to deliver more, better, anywhere.\n👉 Register here: https://bit.ly/47mWRSs\n\n#LiveU #sportsmedia #liveproduction #fanengagement #AGuyWithAScarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 1171, "rx": 26, "cm": 2, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Manuela Callea", "person"]], "tags": ["liveu", "sportsmedia", "liveproduction", "fanengagement", "aguywithascarf"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 2, "pa": {"video_views": 407, "watch_time_s": 5640, "avg_watch_s": 13, "saves": 0, "sends": 1, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1171, 26, 2, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 326, "id": 7391048758513442816, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7391048758513442816/", "ts": "2025-11-03T09:49:03+00:00", "date": "2025-11-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Bending Spoons’ Luca Ferrari, in his own words: the AOL bet, the playbook, and the line between PE and product", "snippet": "🤖 I get asked about Bending Spoons a lot—maybe because I’m Italian, maybe because their deals are loud. I don’t have inside info; just a long view of this indus", "chars": 913, "text": "🤖 I get asked about Bending Spoons a lot—maybe because I’m Italian, maybe because their deals are loud. I don’t have inside info; just a long view of this industry and what’s public.\nThis time, though, the CEO Luca Ferrari finally spelled out a strategy that feels coherent and different: operator-first, product-heavy, buy-to-hold. That fits their roots as top-tier app builders. If they really put hands on product, modernize distribution, and squeeze more life (and cash flow) out of assets that were under-loved, the thesis makes sense.\nThe question is durability: can they keep doing this at scale, over years, without drifting into pure financial engineering?\nWe’ll see—but for once the story aligns with how they actually know how to create value: by fixing the product.\nThat’s why I like this story for now - product matters!\nHere it is… below\n\nA guy with a scarf CNBC\n#investing #digital #italy #business", "qtext": "", "imp": 11214, "rx": 36, "cm": 5, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Bending Spoons", "company"], ["Luca Ferrari", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["CNBC", "company"]], "tags": ["investing", "digital", "italy", "business"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 11214, 36, 5, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 327, "id": 7390981845812760577, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7390981845812760577/", "ts": "2025-11-03T05:23:09+00:00", "date": "2025-11-03", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "A very early start to fly to Madrid and be on stage with LALIGA, Euroleague Basketball, LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel, FC Barcelona,", "snippet": "A very early start to fly to Madrid and be on stage with LALIGA, Euroleague Basketball, LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel, FC Barcelona, RCD Mallorca, Sky, ", "chars": 510, "text": "A very early start to fly to Madrid and be on stage with LALIGA, Euroleague Basketball, LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel, FC Barcelona, RCD Mallorca, Sky, CANAL+ Group, WSC Sports.\nThen London for Product Byte Live.\nAlso, first live webinar in Italian on Nov 13 to be announced tomorrow.\nAnd SportsPro Madrid on Nov 18-19.\n\nMeanwhile the Los Angeles Dodgers have won the Major League Baseball (MLB) and India has won the Women’s Cricket World Cup\nHave a great week everyone.\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportstech", "qtext": "", "imp": 4053, "rx": 91, "cm": 6, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["LALIGA", "company"], ["Euroleague Basketball", "company"], ["LFP - Ligue de Football Professionnel", "company"], ["FC Barcelona", "company"], ["RCD Mallorca", "company"], ["Sky", "company"], ["CANAL+ Group", "company"], ["WSC Sports", "company"], ["Product Byte Live", "showcase"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["Los Angeles Dodgers", "company"], ["Major League Baseball (MLB)", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 4053, 91, 6, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 328, "id": 7390010594080391168, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7390010594080391168/", "ts": "2025-10-31T13:03:45+00:00", "date": "2025-10-31", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "From Intent to Execution: FIFA+ and DAZN’s Two-Step Move Toward the “Ultimate Global Home of Football”", "snippet": "⚽️ From Intent to Execution: FIFA+ finds its next life on DAZN", "chars": 1172, "text": "⚽️ From Intent to Execution: FIFA+ finds its next life on DAZN\nI have two questions:\n1️⃣ What is the difference between June and October’s announcement?\n2️⃣ What will happen to the current FIFA+ platform of Chili origin?\n\nWhen FIFA+ launched, it felt like an experiment — a rare moment when a federation decided to go direct-to-fan.\nNow, two years later, the partnership with DAZN marks the next phase.\n June was about intent — a strategic handshake.\n October is about execution — a global rollout in 2026, a freemium model, and a product that finally looks ready to scale.\nWhat’s interesting isn’t just the deal.\n It’s the shift in mindset: from content owner to platform partner, from talking about “reach” to building actual access.\nIn my latest A guy with a scarf reading note, I unpack the two announcements, the timeline, and what this means for rights-holders, tech vendors, and startups that build around the future of football streaming.\n👇 Read the full piece below:\n From Intent to Execution: FIFA+ and DAZN’s Two-Step Move Toward the “Ultimate Global Home of Football”\n\n#sportsmedia #streaming #football #AGWAS #FIFA #DAZN #sportsbusiness #digitaltransformation", "qtext": "", "imp": 2509, "rx": 24, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "streaming", "football", "agwas", "fifa", "dazn", "sportsbusiness", "digitaltransformation"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2509, 24, 1, 1]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 329, "id": 7389761466343714817, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7389761466343714817/", "ts": "2025-10-30T20:33:48+00:00", "date": "2025-10-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 📲 YouTube hits $10.26B in ad revenue for Q3. Alphabet crosses $100B for the first time.", "snippet": "📺 📲 YouTube hits $10.26B in ad revenue for Q3. Alphabet crosses $100B for the first time.", "chars": 1596, "text": "📺 📲 YouTube hits $10.26B in ad revenue for Q3. Alphabet crosses $100B for the first time.\n(Inspired by Bengu Atamer post)\nA few quick takeaways:\nYouTube ads: $10.261B, ~+15% YoY. Consistent momentum at meaningful scale.\n📺 Alphabet revenue: $102.3B, +16% YoY. First-ever $100B quarter; broad-based growth across Search, YouTube, Subscriptions/Devices, and Cloud.\n📺 Ads engine still strong: Google ads at $74.18B, up from $65.85B last year.\n📺 Cloud keeps climbing: $15.16B this quarter as AI workloads scale.\nNote: YouTube subs (Premium/Music/TV) aren’t broken out separately—revenue sits inside “Google subscriptions, platforms, and devices.”\n\nWhy it matters (in plain English): YouTube keeps compounding at ad scale while Alphabet’s mix (ads + AI-driven Cloud) gives it multiple levers. For creators, rights holders, and sports properties, the signal is clear: short-form, CTV, and performance tooling are now core to brand dollars, not side projects.\n\nA simple story: shorts + CTV momentum + better DR tooling are keeping YouTube’s ad flywheel healthy, with the seasonal Q4 bulge back above $10B and a new high-water mark this quarter. For sports and creators, this is more signal that brand + performance budgets are flowing into video where attention actually lives.\nYouTube ad revenue trend: eight quarters, steady climb back to $10B+\nQ3 2025: $10.261B (up ~15% YoY).\nQ2 2025: $9.796B.\nQ1 2025: $8.927B.\nQ4 2024: $10.473B (seasonal spike).\nQ3 2024: $8.921B.\nQ2 2024: $8.663B.\nQ1 2024: $8.090B.\nQ4 2023: $9.200B.\n\n#YouTube #Alphabet #Earnings #AdTech #Creators #CTV #AI #SportsMedia #Streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 423, "rx": 7, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["YouTube", "company"], ["Bengu Atamer", "person"]], "tags": ["youtube", "alphabet", "earnings", "adtech", "creators", "ctv", "ai", "sportsmedia", "streaming"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 423, 7, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 330, "id": 7389732582319489024, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7389732582319489024/", "ts": "2025-10-30T18:39:02+00:00", "date": "2025-10-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "I’ve finally earned the right to launch a new tech conspiracy theory 😅🕵️‍♂️", "snippet": "I’ve finally earned the right to launch a new tech conspiracy theory 😅🕵️‍♂️", "chars": 876, "text": "I’ve finally earned the right to launch a new tech conspiracy theory 😅🕵️‍♂️\n\n• ⚠️ AWS went down hard last week (hours of pain)\n• ⚠️ Azure stumbled yesterday (global, very visible)\n• 👀 Now I’m… patiently waiting for Google Cloud — not out of malice, out of logic\n\nHere’s the twist: these outages won’t shrink the cloud market — they’ll grow it 📈\n\nIf you run anything mission-critical on one cloud, you’re one change/config/region away from darkness 🌑. Boards will ask for resilience 🛡️, CTOs will answer with multi-cloud 🔁, and budgets will follow 💸.\n\n🔮 Prediction: if GCP hiccups next, the trilogy is complete and spending tilts further toward redundancy, portability, and failover by design. If not… just a late-day thought from A guy with a scarf.\n\nSingle-cloud is a vibe. Resilience is a strategy.\n\n#cloud #multicloud #resilience #SRE #DevOps #AWS #Azure #GCP #architecture", "qtext": "", "imp": 208, "rx": 9, "cm": 1, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["cloud", "multicloud", "resilience", "sre", "devops", "aws", "azure", "gcp", "architecture"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 208, 9, 1, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 331, "id": 7389627998410461184, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7389627998410461184/", "ts": "2025-10-30T11:43:27+00:00", "date": "2025-10-30", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Bending Spoons buys AOL: what’s really happening", "snippet": "🛜 Bending Spoons x AOL: why this deal matters (and why it feels surreal) 🌀", "chars": 1257, "text": "🛜 Bending Spoons x AOL: why this deal matters (and why it feels surreal) 🌀\nI entered sports-media-tech in 1987—pre-web, pre-broadband. Back then, AOL was the training wheels of the internet. Seeing an Italian operator buy AOL in 2025 hits both head and heart.\n\nIn my new long-form I unpack the facts, the financing, and the pattern behind this move:\n🤷 What exactly Bending Spoons is buying in 2025 (the real AOL, not nostalgia).\n🤷 Reported price ranges and the new $2.8B debt package behind the strategy.\n🤷 How this fits a serial-acquisitions playbook (WeTransfer, Meetup, StreamYard, Brightcove, komoot, Vimeo, Evernote).\n🤷 Why AOL’s daily habits (mail/portal) are a perfect canvas for product, pricing, and monetisation upgrades.\n🤷 What changes users and advertisers should expect post-close.\n🤷 What this means for Yahoo/Apollo—and what to watch next.\n🤷 My personal take: the endgame possibilities vs the evidence today.\n\nIf you care about operator models, legacy brands with durable cashflow, and how consumer internet gets rewired in 2025, this one is for you.\nA guy with a scarf\n👉 Full article below\n\n#BendingSpoons #AOL #MediaTech #ConsumerInternet #MergersAndAcquisitions #OperatorPlaybook #RollUpStrategy #DebtFinancing #PrivateEquity #Yahoo #Apollo", "qtext": "", "imp": 2657, "rx": 17, "cm": 3, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["bendingspoons", "aol", "mediatech", "consumerinternet", "mergersandacquisitions", "operatorplaybook", "rollupstrategy", "debtfinancing", "privateequity", "yahoo", "apollo"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2657, 17, 3, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 332, "id": 7389221842064121856, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7389221842064121856/", "ts": "2025-10-29T08:49:32+00:00", "date": "2025-10-29", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Jensen Huang in Washington: 10 Things NVIDIA Announced — and Why They Matter", "snippet": "🚀 From Chips to Qubits: NVIDIA’s Washington Blueprint for the Next Era of AI", "chars": 1644, "text": "🚀 From Chips to Qubits: NVIDIA’s Washington Blueprint for the Next Era of AI\nAt #GTC2025, Jensen Huang didn’t just show faster GPUs — he redrew the map of where AI lives.\nFrom cloud to edge. From classical compute to quantum.\n\nAnd he did it with two lines that might define the decade.\n🎙️ “This new cloud will be an edge industrial robotics cloud.\n Cloud computing will be able to go right out to the edge — where data centers are not — because we have base stations all over the world.”\n🎙️ “Quantum computing won’t replace classical systems.\n They will work together — fused into one accelerated quantum supercomputing platform.”\n\nHere’s what stood out 👇\n10 Things NVIDIA Just Announced (in plain English):\n 1️⃣ 6G becomes an AI platform (Nokia + $1B GPU bet)\n 2️⃣ Quantum meets GPU (NVQLink bridge)\n 3️⃣ Two DOE AI supercomputers for U.S. science\n 4️⃣ Omniverse DSX for gigawatt-scale AI factories\n 5️⃣ Uber + NVIDIA: robotaxis as a global platform\n 6️⃣ Palantir + NVIDIA: operational AI for real businesses\n 7️⃣ Secure AI factories for government workloads\n 8️⃣ America’s first AI-native wireless stack\n 9️⃣ A new federation of AI plants across clouds\n 🔟 Open backbones + NIM microservices (the “boring magic” layer)\n\nIt’s more than a product roadmap — it’s a playbook for how AI moves from the data center to the real world, and how quantum might just meet it there.\n🎯 Read my full A guy with a scarf executive summary below — including how AI-native 6G, Omniverse DSX, and robotaxi logistics could reshape live media, sports, and event operations.\n\n#AI #QuantumComputing #EdgeComputing #GTC2025 #Innovation #SportsTech #MediaTransformation", "qtext": "", "imp": 1107, "rx": 16, "cm": 4, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["NVIDIA", "company"], ["Jensen Huang", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["gtc2025", "ai", "quantumcomputing", "edgecomputing", "gtc2025", "innovation", "sportstech", "mediatransformation"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1107, 16, 4, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 333, "id": 7388846264823726082, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7388846264823726082/", "ts": "2025-10-28T07:57:07+00:00", "date": "2025-10-28", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 74: Annie Krukowska – The Magic (and Reality) of Retention in the New TV Era", "snippet": "🎙️ New Episode — Retention Zone #74", "chars": 1348, "text": "🎙️ New Episode — Retention Zone #74\nThis week I sat down with Agnieszka Krukowska (Annie), founder of annimoIQ, and one of the sharpest minds at the crossroads of TV, product innovation, and commercial strategy.\nFrom Microsoft and BBC to IMG Arena, Annie has spent her career where media reinvents itself — and she’s still there, now helping others unlock the next wave of growth.\nWe talked about:\n → why subscription is here to stay, but risks creating new inequalities;\n → how AI will transform content discovery (semantic, not just metadata);\n → the rise of creators as media companies;\n → and why, in her words, we must “preserve the magic of TV” even as everything becomes an app, a feed, or a scroll.\nIt’s one of those conversations that connects the dots — between past innovation and future disruption, between human experience and machine intelligence.\n\n🎧 Watch or listen: Retention Zone Ep. 74 — Annie Krukowska: The Magic (and Reality) of Retention in the New TV Era ( YouTube, Spotify and also on Substack now)\n\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf\n\nGilles Domartini Benedicte Guichard Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Enzo Bermond Kübra Güven Chris Welsh\n\n#StreamingMedia #TV #AI #CreatorsEconomy #SubscriptionBusiness #MediaInnovation", "qtext": "", "imp": 763, "rx": 25, "cm": 1, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Agnieszka Krukowska", "person"], ["annimoIQ", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Benedicte Guichard", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"]], "tags": ["streamingmedia", "tv", "ai", "creatorseconomy", "subscriptionbusiness", "mediainnovation"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 763, 25, 1, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 334, "id": 7388465969762910208, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7388465969762910208/", "ts": "2025-10-27T06:45:58+00:00", "date": "2025-10-27", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 Quite a month. These are the things I talked about in my various channels.", "snippet": "🤖 Quite a month. These are the things I talked about in my various channels.", "chars": 2825, "text": "🤖 Quite a month. These are the things I talked about in my various channels.\nI am at the intersection of sport media tech and innovation.\nI am an informed generalist.\n❓ I have started asking people if this is too wide of a field. Most said no. I still have doubts. 🤷\n\n1️⃣ 🧩 I am a Microshifters. Is that Good or Bad?\n 10 thoughts from my experience — on rhythm, focus, and working solo.\n2️⃣ 🏀 NBA 2025–26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off.\n How the League turned streaming, data, and innovation into its most ambitious season yet.\n3️⃣ 🔢 11,111 at 11:11 — My Love for Numbers.\n A reflection on data, creativity, and how AI might connect the two like never before.\n4️⃣ ⚽ Breaking Records and Breaking Rules — Why MLS’s 30th Season Matters.\n 30 years in, still a startup — experimenting its way into the future of football.\n5️⃣ 🏎️ Dolby OptiView — StreamOn Summit.\n Following the live panel on interactive fan experiences and motorsport streaming.\n6️⃣ ☁️ When the Cloud Sneezes — What the AWS Outage Really Means.\n A translation of a tech event into business language for sports and media execs.\n7️⃣ 🧠 Sportel Monaco 2025 — Less Spectacle, More Signal.\n Where the industry finally talked about what really matters: problems worth solving.\n8️⃣ ⚡ AWS Outage Live — Breaking News at Sportel.\n The moment the web went down during a streaming panel — and what it revealed.\n9️⃣ 🎙️ A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Yann Colléter.\n MIPCOM 2025 in three themes — creators, AI, and vertical storytelling.\n🔟 🧠 The AI Field Guide to Sportel Monaco (Vendors Only).\n Who really builds with AI — who just says they do.\n1️⃣1️⃣ 💬 OTTRED Story.\n How a WhatsApp group became a true OTT community.\n1️⃣2️⃣ 🏎️ Apple, Formula 1, and the New Acceleration of Sports Media.\n A creative partnership turns into a full-stack ecosystem move.\n1️⃣3️⃣ 📅 Whereabouts.\n My fall event lineup — from Sportel to Art Week Torino to ATP Finals.\n1️⃣4️⃣ 🎙️ A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jim Irving.\n Finding what to watch — and how Recce brings trust back into discovery.\n1️⃣5️⃣ 💸 Are We Entering the Season of Strategic Deals in Sports Tech?\n From building products to owning workflows — capability over scale.\n1️⃣6️⃣ 🎙️ Retention Zone Ep. 73 — Guillaume Devezeaux.\n From telco roots to wellness platforms: the evolution of streaming technology. Cleeng\n1️⃣7️⃣ 🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — Feeling the Pulse of the Industry.\n Listening, connecting, observing — before reporting what’s real and what’s noise.\n1️⃣8️⃣ 🎙️ A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Chris Redmond.\n The new game of hiring — productise yourself, farm jobs, don’t hunt them.\n1️⃣9️⃣ 📺 The Creator Economy: Powering MIPCOM 2025.\n Where YouTube meets TV — the creator and commissioning economies collide.\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand Untangling AI\n#sport #tech #media #innovation #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 386, "rx": 6, "cm": 0, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["Microshifters", "showcase"], ["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["OTTRED", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["SPORTEL", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"], ["Untangling AI", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sport", "tech", "media", "innovation", "ai"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Untangling AI", "Episode", "Dataviz"], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}, {"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView", "Cleeng"], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 386, 6, 0, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 335, "id": 7388130023775289344, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7388130023775289344/", "ts": "2025-10-26T08:31:02+00:00", "date": "2025-10-26", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🧩 I am a Microshifter. Is that Good or Bad? 10 Thoughts from my Experience", "snippet": "📖 SUNDAY READ: 💡 I realised something recently:", "chars": 881, "text": "📖 SUNDAY READ: 💡 I realised something recently:\n>>> I’m a MICROSHIFTER <<<\nIs that Good or Bad?\n10 Thoughts from my Experience\n\nWorking alone, I’ve stopped forcing a 9-to-5 rhythm.\nMy days move in blocks — bursts of focus, breaks to reset, new starts when energy returns.\nIt’s not chaos. It’s choreography.\nAnd it turns out there’s science behind it — short, intentional shifts can boost well-being and creativity, if you protect their edges.\n\nIn my new piece, I explore:\n • the freedom trap of too much flexibility\n • the solo advantage of designing your own rhythm\n • the discipline it takes to make freedom work\n\nRead the full article below or on Substack\n\nIf you’ve built your own rhythm — outside the traditional workday — this one might resonate.\nHow do you shape your day?\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#futureofwork #microshifting #solowork #creatoreconomy #mindset", "qtext": "", "imp": 1166, "rx": 16, "cm": 6, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["futureofwork", "microshifting", "solowork", "creatoreconomy", "mindset"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1166, 16, 6, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 336, "id": 7387768626994380800, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7387768626994380800/", "ts": "2025-10-25T08:34:58+00:00", "date": "2025-10-25", "approx": false, "kind": "quote_own", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "NBA 2025–26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off", "snippet": "🏀 NBA 2025-26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off", "chars": 39, "text": "🏀 NBA 2025-26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off", "qtext": "🏀 NBA 2025-26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off\n\nHow the League turned streaming, data and innovation into the most ambitious season yet.\nThis is not just basketball.\n It’s product design, media architecture and fan-experience engineering all happening at league scale.\n💡 11-year rights reshuffle — Disney, NBC & Amazon\n ⚙️ AWS under the hood — real-time data, new stats, AI storytelling\n 🎥 LED courts, multi-view feeds, auto-editing workflows\n 🌍 Global reach re-imagined — the same product everywhere, localised by language not by rights.\n\nAnd beyond the floor, the ripple hits startups and creators too:\n →", "imp": 189, "rx": 4, "cm": 0, "rp": 0, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": [], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 189, 4, 0, 0]], "quoted": "Carlo De Marchis"}, {"i": 337, "id": 7387768493414318081, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7387768493414318081/", "ts": "2025-10-25T08:34:26+00:00", "date": "2025-10-25", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "NBA 2025–26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off", "snippet": "🏀 NBA 2025-26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off", "chars": 1162, "text": "🏀 NBA 2025-26: The Tech-Powered Tip-Off\n\nHow the League turned streaming, data and innovation into the most ambitious season yet.\nThis is not just basketball.\n It’s product design, media architecture and fan-experience engineering all happening at league scale.\n💡 11-year rights reshuffle — Disney, NBC & Amazon\n ⚙️ AWS under the hood — real-time data, new stats, AI storytelling\n 🎥 LED courts, multi-view feeds, auto-editing workflows\n 🌍 Global reach re-imagined — the same product everywhere, localised by language not by rights.\n\nAnd beyond the floor, the ripple hits startups and creators too:\n → Real-time analytics for teams & coaches\n → Fan-centric products: micro-moments, personalisation, regional extensions\n → Production intelligence: automated editing, multi-camera orchestration, social-clip workflows\n → Global localisation & distribution tools\n\nThe NBA has become a platform — and every partner, from Prime Video to Peacock, is now a product designer.\n👇 Read my full piece in A Guy With a Scarf\nHow the league turned streaming, data and innovation into the most ambitious season yet.\nA guy with a scarf\n#sportsmedia #sportstech #NBA #streaming #AI", "qtext": "", "imp": 1142, "rx": 13, "cm": 4, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportsmedia", "sportstech", "nba", "streaming", "ai"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1142, 13, 4, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 338, "id": 7387415405004509184, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7387415405004509184/", "ts": "2025-10-24T09:11:24+00:00", "date": "2025-10-24", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "11,111 at 11:11 — My Love for Numbers", "snippet": "😎 11,111 at 11:11 ⏱️  — My Love for Numbers", "chars": 1455, "text": "😎 11,111 at 11:11 ⏱️  — My Love for Numbers\nYes, I’m the kind of (silly) person who posts a screenshot of 11,111 followers at 11:11.\nAnd yes, I know that sounds ridiculous.\n\n🔢 But that tiny moment made me think again about my lifelong love for numbers — and how they’ve shaped everything I’ve done in sport, tech, and media.\nI’ve always been a data freak with a creative brain that never stops (ask anyone who’s worked with me — they’ll confirm the “never stops” part).\n\n🧠 That mix of numbers and creativity has been my balance for years: the thing that helped me turn intuition into products, ideas into innovation.\n❤️  What I love: when numbers reveal patterns we didn’t expect.\n💔 What I hate: when numbers are twisted to justify decisions already made.\n🤖 Now AI is changing the game again — if we use it well, it can help us discover new patterns, test new ideas, and push creative thinking further than before.\n⚽ 🏌‍♀️ ⛹‍♀️ 🏈 🎾 In sport, tech, and media, everything we do is built on numbers — performance data, fan analytics, streaming quality, engagement metrics…\n🎱  And maybe this is finally the golden age for numbers, if we stay curious enough to listen to what they’re trying to tell us.\n\n👉 Read my latest piece: “11,111 at 11:11 — My Love for Numbers”\nA personal reflection on data, creativity, and how AI might help us connect the two like never before.\n\nA guy with a scarf Rich Mills\n#SportsTech #AI #Data #Creativity #Innovation #Storytelling", "qtext": "", "imp": 1906, "rx": 19, "cm": 14, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Rich Mills", "person"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "ai", "data", "creativity", "innovation", "storytelling"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1906, 19, 14, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 339, "id": 7387155420567470081, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7387155420567470081/", "ts": "2025-10-23T15:58:18+00:00", "date": "2025-10-23", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Breaking Records and Breaking Rules: Why MLS's 30th Season Matters", "snippet": "I🇺🇸 Breaking Records and Breaking Rules — Why MLS’s 30th Season Matters ⚽️", "chars": 1242, "text": "I🇺🇸 Breaking Records and Breaking Rules — Why MLS’s 30th Season Matters ⚽️\n30 years in, Major League Soccer still feels like a startup.\nThat’s probably its greatest advantage.\nWhile Europe’s big leagues wrestle with legacy structures and cultural inertia, MLS keeps doing what it does best — experimenting.\nSometimes awkwardly. Sometimes brilliantly. Always forward.\n\nThis season closed with record attendance, rising streaming numbers, and a growing sense that MLS might be building a different kind of football industry — one that lives comfortably between tech, sport, and entertainment.\n\nWe finally have some numbers to talk about!\n\nIn my latest long read for A guy with a scarf, I dig into:\n ⚽️ The hard metrics of passion — 11 million fans in stadiums\n 📱 The Apple TV Season Pass experiment (and what the numbers really mean)\n 💡 Why MLS behaves more like a startup than a legacy league\n 🔍 The measurement transparency gap — and why it matters for sponsors\n 🏆 The road to the 2026 World Cup\n\nBecause MLS isn’t just growing soccer in the U.S.\n It’s growing a new way to think about sports.\n👇 Read the full story on A guy with a scarf\n\nMajor League Soccer\n\n#SportsTech #MLS #Streaming #AppleTV #AGuyWithAScarf #Innovation #FootballBusiness", "qtext": "", "imp": 716, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": true, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Major League Soccer", "company"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "mls", "streaming", "appletv", "aguywithascarf", "innovation", "footballbusiness"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 716, 11, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 340, "id": 7387114839397109760, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7387114839397109760/", "ts": "2025-10-23T13:17:03+00:00", "date": "2025-10-23", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Getting ready to follow this Dolby OptiView live panel for the StreamOn Summit.", "snippet": "Getting ready to follow this Dolby OptiView live panel for the StreamOn Summit.", "chars": 198, "text": "Getting ready to follow this Dolby OptiView live panel for the StreamOn Summit.\nMotorsport are fast and so needs to be their streaming I guess 😎.\n\nA guy with a scarf Matthias Ankli\n#streaming #sport", "qtext": "➡️ Swipe to meet the speakers for our #StreamOnSummit session, \"Accelerating viewer engagement—Interactive fan experiences.\"\n\n🏎️ 𝗔𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝘀𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻\nDiscover how racing leagues like NASCAR and F1 leverage interactive technologies to enhance viewer engagement and transform the way audiences interact with their favorite motorsports.\n\n🎤 𝗙𝗲𝗮𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲𝗱 𝘀𝗽𝗲𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗿𝘀\nPatrick Carroll, Managing Director of Digital Technology @ NASCAR\nRobert Garcia-Brooks, Senior Product Manager, Digital @ F1\nRose Power, Sr. Product Marketing Manager @ Dolby OptiView\n\n𝗦𝗮𝘃𝗲 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝘀𝗽𝗼𝘁\n🗓️ Thursday, October 23rd\n⏰ 7am PT | 10am ET | 4", "imp": 357, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 1, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["document"], "mentions": [["Dolby OptiView", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Matthias Ankli", "person"]], "tags": ["streaming", "sport"], "series": [], "disc": ["Dolby OptiView"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Dolby OptiView", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Dolby OptiView"], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 357, 11, 1, 1]], "quoted": "Dolby OptiView"}, {"i": 341, "id": 7386332174431629312, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7386332174431629312/", "ts": "2025-10-21T09:27:01+00:00", "date": "2025-10-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🧨 What the AWS Outage Really Means Written for Sports and Media Execs", "snippet": "🧨 What the AWS Outage Really Means Written for Sports and Media Execs", "chars": 1789, "text": "🧨 What the AWS Outage Really Means Written for Sports and Media Execs\nEven if I’m an ex-Chief Technology Officer (before morphing into A guy with a scarf), I’m very far from deeply understanding all the complexity of what happened. But I can talk the language — or at least listen to it — and I tried to follow those who can explain it better than I ever could. One of them is Dave Plummer, a retired Microsoft systems engineer who posted a brilliant YouTube explainer titled “Why the Web Was Down Today.” That video became my starting point to piece together what really went wrong.\n\nMy idea with this long article is simple: to translate what haqppened and its impacts described into something our side of the industry can understand — especially the sports and media executives who don’t live in command lines or cloud consoles but still depend on them every single day. It’s a generic label, but it’s who I’m writing for: the people who need to make sense of tech when it stops making sense.\n\n📘 Table of Contents\n1️⃣ What Actually Happened\n2️⃣ Why It Spread So Fast — and Lasted So Long\n3️⃣ The Hollywood Failover Myth — Explained Better\n4️⃣ The CFO–CTO Reality Check\n5️⃣ The Sports & Media Lens — When “Always On” Fails\n6️⃣ What Are the Solutions?\n7️⃣ The Cost Question: Is Once a Year Acceptable?\n8️⃣ The Architecture Shift Ahead\n9️⃣ A Final Reflection\n⏱️  Appendix — What SLAs Really Mean in Hours\n🧮 Appendix — AWS Standard SLAs (as of late 2025)\n🏗️ Appendix — Understanding AWS Regions\n🌐 Appendix — Other Major Cloud and Platform Outages\n☁️ Appendix — How Other Major Clouds Compare to AWS\n📖 Glossary — Understanding the Tech Behind the AWS Outage\n\nQuite some work behind it, also with many extras so the full article is on my Substack: https://lnkd.in/dUzwyEXJ\n\n#cloud #ai #outage", "qtext": "", "imp": 507, "rx": 10, "cm": 1, "rp": 2, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["link"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["cloud", "ai", "outage"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "link", "images": 0, "links": 1, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 507, 10, 1, 2]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 342, "id": 7386302935783063552, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7386302935783063552/", "ts": "2025-10-21T07:30:50+00:00", "date": "2025-10-21", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "My Monday at Sportel Monaco 2025", "snippet": "🚨 SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — Less spectacle, more signal.", "chars": 1850, "text": "🚨 SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — Less spectacle, more signal.\nThis year Sportel felt different — in a good way.\nA better layout, calmer rhythm, sharper people.\nLess “who’s selling what,” more “what are we actually solving.”\nYou could feel it in the halls: fewer booths shouting, more conversations happening.\nIt’s becoming what it should be — a place where the sports business talks to itself honestly.\n\nBetween 8 a.m. coffees on the beach (under the rain) and constant encounters with friends and innovators — from Alexis Ohanian Sr. to Cleeng, WSC Sports, LiveU, Ateme, ScorePlay, and Kings League — I spent most of my time doing what I love most: 🎙️ asking one sharp question at a time.\n\nAt Sportel, my A guy with a scarf asks a question to... series at Sportel included:\n ➡️ Murray Barnett — how do you actually make money in sports in 2026?\n ➡️ Danny Townsend (SURJ Sports Investment) — why sport as an asset class needs patient capital\n ➡️ Frank Leenders (FIBA) — 2026 will be the year of women’s basketball\n ➡️ Gilles Domartini (Cleeng) — engagement → retention → monetisation\n ➡️ Ophir Zardok Shemer (LiveU) — the rise of authentic live beyond the game\n\nPlus, a standout panel: Investment in Sport — Where is the Next $1B Coming From?\nConsensus? Returns will take longer — but they’ll be bigger for those who stay.\n Smart money is moving from speculation to stewardship.\n\nAnd yes — I did interrupt a live Unofficial Partner podcast about Streaming vs. TV when the AWS outage hit mid-session.\n That’s when everyone went: “Imagine that during a Champions League Final…” 😅\n\nNext year, I’d love to help push that further with a deep, insightful, sharp format:\n “Sportel Conversations by A guy with a scarf”\n\nFull story below — including all interviews and reflections 👇\n\n#SportelMonaco #SportsBusiness #Streaming #AI #Investment #FanEngagement #SportsTech", "qtext": "", "imp": 3857, "rx": 69, "cm": 5, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["Alexis Ohanian Sr.", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["WSC Sports", "company"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Ateme", "company"], ["ScorePlay", "company"], ["Kings League", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Murray Barnett", "person"], ["Danny Townsend", "person"], ["SURJ Sports Investment", "company"], ["Frank Leenders", "person"], ["FIBA", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Ophir Zardok Shemer", "person"], ["LiveU", "company"], ["Unofficial Partner", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportelmonaco", "sportsbusiness", "streaming", "ai", "investment", "fanengagement", "sportstech"], "series": [], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3857, 69, 5, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 343, "id": 7386013962624118784, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7386013962624118784/", "ts": "2025-10-20T12:22:34+00:00", "date": "2025-10-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🤖 True story. 😎", "snippet": "🤖 True story. 😎", "chars": 2103, "text": "🤖 True story. 😎\nI was at SPORTEL Monaco, in front of a live panel-podcast with Richard Gillis, when my phone started lighting up: major internet outage.\nSubstack wouldn’t load. Ring was down. Disney+ frozen (pun intended).\n\nThey were on stage debating Streaming vs. TV — so I couldn’t resist breaking in, for once not with a question, but with a news alert.\nEveryone went, “OMG — imagine that during a UEFA Champions League final… or the Super Bowl.”\nThat’s a genuinely scary scenario — and suddenly the debate about resilience, redundancy, and broadcast reliability became very, very real.\n\nUPDATE:  outage is not over. More problems are continuing during the day. Also Microsoft seems affected ?!?\nAlso Google and YouTube. Boh…\n\n🚨 #AWSOutage – What we know:\n\n➡️ Amazon Web Services (AWS) faced a major disruption centred on its US-East-1 (Northern Virginia) region, causing elevated error rates and latency across multiple services.\n➡️ Platforms hit included Snapchat, Fortnite, Duolingo, Robinhood, and others.\n➡️ AWS traced the root cause to DNS resolution issues affecting the DynamoDB service API endpoint in that region. Recovery is ongoing, though queued requests and latency remain.\n➡️ While AWS promotes globally independent regions, US-East-1 remains a hidden single point of failure for some global services — a legacy issue that keeps resurfacing.\n➡️ And let’s be honest — some third-party outages likely stemmed from lazy architecture: over-reliance on one region should now be viewed as a serious business-continuity risk.\n\nSome reports mentioned Lloyds Bank and HMRC in the UK, though these remain unconfirmed. If any large organisations were impacted, it’s time for serious conversations about Cloud Architecture and Digital Sovereignty.\n\nThe irony? While everyone in the room was talking about the future of streaming, the present was reminding us that the future still depends on a few cables, servers — and one region in Virginia.\n(via Paul Markham)\n\n💭 Were your systems affected today? Or did your day run just fine?\n\n#CloudComputing #Resilience #MediaTech #SaaS #Sportel #Streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 4286, "rx": 40, "cm": 12, "rp": 6, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["Richard Gillis", "person"], ["Paul Markham", "person"]], "tags": ["awsoutage", "cloudcomputing", "resilience", "mediatech", "saas", "sportel", "streaming"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 4286, 40, 12, 6]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 344, "id": 7385936388560510976, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7385936388560510976/", "ts": "2025-10-20T07:14:19+00:00", "date": "2025-10-20", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 6: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Yann Colléter", "snippet": "Ep. 6: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Yann Colléter", "chars": 1200, "text": "Ep. 6: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Yann Colléter\nLast week I couldn’t make it to MIPCOM, but my friend Yann was there — so I asked him to give me his quick field report.\n\n🎙️ Q: How was MIPCOM 2025?\n💬 Yann’s take:\n Reporting from Marseille, Yann shared three big themes that defined this year’s edition — and how they signal a shift in the media landscape.\n➡️ Creators take center stage. YouTube was everywhere — not as a buyer, but as a platform sitting confidently beside broadcasters and streamers, looking for creative partnerships instead of licensing deals.\n ➡️ AI steps into the mainstream. After seeing AI filmmakers at the Dubai AI Film Festival, Yann noticed how they’ve now entered MIPCOM panels and workshops — proof that the industry is starting to embrace AI, not fear it.\n ➡️ Vertical storytelling becomes a format. The rise of “micro-drama” as a genre, led by Hollywater and Fox, hints that vertical series could soon include anime and unscripted content too.\n🎬 From creators to AI to new storytelling formats, MIPCOM 2025 sounded more like the future of media in motion than a traditional TV market.\nMerci, Yann — next one in Dubai? 🇦🇪\n\n#MIPCOM #SportsTech #ai #streaming", "qtext": "", "imp": 994, "rx": 14, "cm": 4, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Yann Colléter", "person"]], "tags": ["mipcom", "sportstech", "ai", "streaming"], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 92, "watch_time_s": 1408, "avg_watch_s": 15, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 994, 14, 4, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 345, "id": 7385625309989122048, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7385625309989122048/", "ts": "2025-10-19T10:38:12+00:00", "date": "2025-10-19", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "The AI Field Guide to Sportel Monaco (Vendors)", "snippet": "🤖 The AI Field Guide to Sportel Monaco (Vendors only)", "chars": 1560, "text": "🤖 The AI Field Guide to Sportel Monaco (Vendors only)\nA field guide to Sportel’s AI reality: vendors who build it, ship it, say it—or don’t.\n\nEven if tech isn’t the core of SPORTEL Monaco, our industry has changed so much—and SPORTEL itself has evolved—that looking at the “tech layer” feels like the right angle this year. I’m in Monaco tomorrow and I wanted to see what AI really looks like on the floor, beyond panel chatter.\n\nSo I did a quick pass on vendors I know. I grouped them like this:\n➡️ AI-based companies — the product only makes sense because of AI.\n➡️ Companies with some AI features — meaningful AI modules, not the whole company.\n➡️ Companies that mention AI — it’s in the story, but I didn’t see a concrete product claim.\n➡️ No AI mention — nothing explicit on public pages in my sweep.\n\nIt’s 38 companies in total. And the key angle this week: maturity. Are features live in-market and operating at scale, or just launched/announced? That difference matters.\n\nI put the full list and notes in my write-up. If I misread anyone’s positioning—or if you’ve shipped something new—tell me and I’ll update. I’m not here for stickers; I’m here for demos that cut steps, speed turnaround, and move the needle for rights holders and broadcasters.\n\nLooking for clients who want help turning solid tech into adoption—supercharged content, marketing, or straight-talk advising—I’m around at the Grimaldi Forum.\n\nSee you in Monaco.\nHow to find me? Silly question... look for A guy with a scarf\n\n#SPORTEL #SportsTech #AI #Broadcast #OTT #MediaTech #Monaco", "qtext": "", "imp": 3130, "rx": 36, "cm": 8, "rp": 7, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["sportel", "sportstech", "ai", "broadcast", "ott", "mediatech", "monaco"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 3130, 36, 8, 7]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 346, "id": 7385200781785784320, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7385200781785784320/", "ts": "2025-10-18T06:31:16+00:00", "date": "2025-10-18", "approx": false, "kind": "quote", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "OTTRED story.", "snippet": "OTTRED story.", "chars": 1020, "text": "OTTRED story.\n\nI vividly remember when I first joined the OTTRED WhatsApp group.\nIt immediately felt like more than just another chat — it was a community in the making, not an audience.\nThat’s what struck me about what Chris and the team were trying to build: a sense of belonging around shared curiosity in OTT, not just another industry forum.\n\nAfter a while, though, as the conversations got deeper and more complex, I caught myself thinking: but who’s actually here? who are these voices I’m talking to?\n\nAround the same time, I was experimenting with no-code tool Glide (glideapps.com) — and the idea clicked: maybe we could give this community a face. A place. So I quickly created the app and Chris was fast to adopt the idea.\nSomething that connects people not just by topic, but by identity.\n\nBut that was only the start.\n\nThat’s why OTTRED has always fascinated me — it started in the most human way possible: a group chat that became a true community.\n\n#OTTRED #community A guy with a scarf The One Man Brand", "qtext": "I’ve been so focused on building OTTRED that I rarely stop to look at what we’ve actually built. So this week, I asked ChatGPT to do it for me and design a Netflix series about OTTRED\n\nI asked it to create a six-part Netflix-style series that tells the story of OTTRED, from the WhatsApp group that started it all, to the global community, the podcasts, the events, the personal highs, lows and lessons, and everything in between.\n\nIt was both surreal and strangely moving to read what it came up with.\n\nAnd there is still so much I’d have included\n\n👉 the moment Carlo De Marchis stepped up and helpe", "imp": 419, "rx": 11, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": true, "attachments": ["link", "document"], "mentions": [["OTTRED", "company"], ["Chris", "person"], ["Glide (glideapps.com)", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["ottred", "community"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "document", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 419, 11, 1, 4]], "quoted": "Chris Redmond ©️®️"}, {"i": 347, "id": 7384952615744286720, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7384952615744286720/", "ts": "2025-10-17T14:05:09+00:00", "date": "2025-10-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Apple, Formula 1, and the New Acceleration of Sports Media: When Two Giants Align", "snippet": "🏎️ Formula 1 and Apple have signed a five-year deal that makes Apple the exclusive U.S. broadcast partner from 2026.", "chars": 1322, "text": "🏎️ Formula 1 and Apple have signed a five-year deal that makes Apple the exclusive U.S. broadcast partner from 2026.\n\nI started my career in 1987 (!!!) with Formula One, at Imola... so everything transformational in F1 gets my immediate attention\n\nI’ve been around long enough to hear, “that will never happen.”\nBut this one did — and it changes a lot more than who shows the races.\nBecause this isn’t just a broadcast deal.\nIt’s the continuation of a story that started with F1 The Movie — a creative partnership that’s now turning into a full ecosystem move across Apple TV, Apple Music, Apple News, and even Fitness+.\n\nI wrote a new long-form piece about what this really means — for F1, for Apple, and for the future of live sports in digital ecosystems.\nHere’s what I explore:\n → How F1 The Movie became a test drive for Apple’s sports ambitions\n → Why the deal is less about “showing races” and more about owning the fan experience\n → The data, demographics, and design logic behind Apple’s full-stack strategy\n → Media reactions and why traditional broadcasters should pay attention\n → The bigger question: when tech platforms become media companies, who really owns the story?\nRead it here → Apple, Formula 1, and the New Acceleration of Sports Media\n\n#SportsTech #Media #Streaming #Formula1 #Apple #AGuyWithAScarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 6423, "rx": 54, "cm": 10, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [], "tags": ["sportstech", "media", "streaming", "formula1", "apple", "aguywithascarf"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 6423, 54, 10, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 348, "id": 7384914795713445888, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7384914795713445888/", "ts": "2025-10-17T11:34:52+00:00", "date": "2025-10-17", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🛫 WHEREABOUTS 🛬", "snippet": "🛫 WHEREABOUTS 🛬", "chars": 698, "text": "🛫 WHEREABOUTS 🛬\n📆 Sharing my event agenda se we can meet if you attend any of the following:\nAlso announcing I will be a speaker/moderator at some of them.\n\n1. SPORTEL Monaco - Oct 20 (Podcast)\n2. Art week Torino - Oct 29 - Nov 2\n3. C2C Electronic Music festival Torino - Oct 31 - Nov2\n4. Corporate AI-based event Madrid - Nov 3 - (Host and moderator)\n5. Product Byte Live London - Nov 5 - (Speaker)\n6. Live Webinar (in Italian!!!) - Nov 13 - (Speaker) to be announced soon\n6. ATP Tour Finals Torino - Nov 9 - Nov 16\n7. SportsPro Media Summit Madrid - Nov 18-19 - (judge and moderator)\n8. TFF Torino Film festival - Nov 21 - 29\n\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n\n#sport #media #ai #tech #events", "qtext": "", "imp": 743, "rx": 19, "cm": 4, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["Product Byte Live", "showcase"], ["ATP Tour", "company"], ["SportsPro", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sport", "media", "ai", "tech", "events"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 743, 19, 4, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 349, "id": 7384490338708795392, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7384490338708795392/", "ts": "2025-10-16T07:28:13+00:00", "date": "2025-10-16", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 5: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jim Irving", "snippet": "Ep. 5: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jim Irving", "chars": 1465, "text": "Ep. 5: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... Jim Irving\nThis week, I asked my ex-colleague Jim Irving about one of the biggest frustrations in streaming — finding what to watch. His new app, Recce, takes a surprisingly human approach to solving it.\n\nThis week, I asked my ex-colleague Jim Irving about one of the biggest frustrations in streaming — finding what to watch. His new app, Recce, takes a surprisingly human approach to solving it.\n\n🎙️ Q: What is Recce?\n💬 Jim’s take:\nFinding what to watch has become one of the biggest pain points in media — and Recce wants to fix that by bringing trust back into recommendations.\n➡️ Recce is a movie and TV review app built around trusted recommendations — not algorithms, but real people you actually know.\n➡️ It digitizes the most natural discovery habit we all have: word of mouth. That moment in a café or pub when someone says, “You’ve got to see this.”\n➡️ Users can share, rate, and discuss shows within their communities while building their own curated watchlists.\n➡️ Through Recce Rewards, engagement is rewarded — since people create value on the platform, they also share in it through access to exclusive content and prizes.\nAs Jim puts it: “We’re trying to digitize the most trusted way of finding great content — word of mouth.”\n📱 Recce goes on pre-order this week — a small but meaningful step towards a more personal way to discover what’s worth watching.\n\n#streaming #content #ai A guy with a scarf", "qtext": "", "imp": 2226, "rx": 31, "cm": 4, "rp": 6, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Jim Irving", "person"], ["Jim Irving", "person"], ["Recce", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["streaming", "content", "ai"], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 118, "watch_time_s": 1551, "avg_watch_s": 13, "saves": 0, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2226, 31, 4, 6]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 350, "id": 7384135622233427968, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7384135622233427968/", "ts": "2025-10-15T07:58:42+00:00", "date": "2025-10-15", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Are We Entering the Season of Strategic Deals in Sports Tech?", "snippet": "🤷 Are we entering the season of strategic deals in sports tech? 😎", "chars": 1593, "text": "🤷 Are we entering the season of strategic deals in sports tech? 😎\nOver the past few days, three seemingly unrelated moves caught my eye:\n📲 Sportway → SPORTALL.\n🏃‍♂️ Catapult → IMPECT.\n 📺 Media Press Group → DataTv S.r.l..\nAt first, they look like routine acquisitions — one in production, one in data, one in metadata.\nBut taken together, they might signal something deeper: that sports-tech companies aren’t just chasing scale anymore, they’re expanding strategically along the value chain.\nWe’re seeing a quiet pivot:\n- From building products to building stacks.\n- From selling tools to owning workflows.\n- From fixing a single problem to controlling an entire journey — from capture to context to discovery.\n\nIn my new article, I look at what these deals actually mean, why they matter, and whether they mark the start of a new consolidation cycle driven by capability, not size.\n\nHere’s what you’ll find inside 👇\n➡️ The 3 recent deals that define this new pattern — Sportway/Sportall, Catapult/IMPECT, Media Press/DataTV\n➡️ How clients are pushing for fewer integration points and more turnkey stacks\n➡️  Why capital is returning to strategic M&A in sports tech despite a tough market\n➡️ The risks and limits of this full-stack logic — from culture clashes to margin dilution\n➡️ Two appendices with deep dives into the acquiring and acquired companies (people, founders, context)\nIf you work in sports, media, or tech — this one’s worth a read.\n It’s not about who’s buying who. It’s about why they’re buying what they’re buying.\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#sportstech #investing", "qtext": "", "imp": 2548, "rx": 19, "cm": 1, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Sportway", "company"], ["SPORTALL", "company"], ["Catapult", "company"], ["IMPECT", "company"], ["Media Press Group", "company"], ["DataTv S.r.l.", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportstech", "investing"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 2548, 19, 1, 4]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 351, "id": 7383799004788195328, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7383799004788195328/", "ts": "2025-10-14T09:41:07+00:00", "date": "2025-10-14", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 73: Guillaume Devezeaux - From Telco Roots to Wellness Platforms. The Evolution of Video Streaming Technology", "snippet": "🎙️ Ep. 73: Guillaume Devezeaux - From Telco Roots to Wellness Platforms: The Evolution of Video Streaming Technology", "chars": 2097, "text": "🎙️ Ep. 73: Guillaume Devezeaux - From Telco Roots to Wellness Platforms: The Evolution of Video Streaming Technology\n\nFrom telco pioneers to wellness innovators—Guillaume Devezeaux, CEO and Founder of Alpha Networks, shares 17 years of streaming evolution and what's next for video platforms.\n\nRecorded live in Amsterdam before IBC 2025, at our event Retention Zone LIVE, this conversation unpacks how modular architecture and smart partnerships are reshaping who can launch and monetize streaming platforms.\n\n🔑 KEY INSIGHTS:\n➡️ How Alpha Networks evolved from IPTV solutions to serving broadcasters, sports, and now wellness platforms in streaming \n➡️ Why microservice architecture (Tucano + Gecko) enables rapid market expansion across unexpected verticals\n➡️ The game-changing Alpha Networks + Cleeng integration that eliminates payment complexity for non-technical companies\n➡️ Real-world case study: How Le Tigre Yoga transformed from Stripe struggles to seamless operations with Cleeng Pro\n➡️ Why proprietary platforms beat YouTube for wellness and fitness content creators seeking diverse monetization\n➡️ Preview of Delta UX: Deploy video platforms by configuration alone—no coding required\n\n💡 STANDOUT QUOTE:\n\n\"It's possible today to have a platform that you can deploy just by configuration, in any country, with zero blocking, with any type of payment. And just by configuring your front end, you can manage any brand.\"\n\nThe streaming landscape is fragmenting into specialized verticals, and the barriers to entry are collapsing. Whether you're in sports, media, wellness, or exploring new markets—this episode reveals the infrastructure making it all possible.\n\nRetention Zone is presented by Cleeng\nHosted and produced by Carlo De Marchis: A guy with a scarf\n\nGilles Domartini Benedicte Guichard Alexis Gaï Kirstin White Hjalmar Koedijker Joachim Bergman Kamila Palka Enzo Bermond Kübra Güven Chris Welsh Giorgia Dolfini\n\n🎧 Listen, watch or read now and discover how the next generation of video platforms prioritizes configuration over complexity.\n\n#streaming #subscription #retention", "qtext": "", "imp": 1880, "rx": 51, "cm": 1, "rp": 6, "boosted": false, "article": true, "edited": false, "attachments": ["article"], "mentions": [["Guillaume Devezeaux", "person"], ["Guillaume Devezeaux", "person"], ["Alpha Networks", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Le Tigre Yoga", "company"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Retention Zone", "showcase"], ["Cleeng", "company"], ["Carlo De Marchis", "person"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["Gilles Domartini", "person"], ["Benedicte Guichard", "person"], ["Alexis Gaï", "person"], ["Kirstin White", "person"], ["Hjalmar Koedijker", "person"], ["Joachim Bergman", "person"], ["Kamila Palka", "person"], ["Enzo Bermond", "person"], ["Kübra Güven", "person"], ["Chris Welsh", "person"], ["Giorgia Dolfini", "person"]], "tags": ["streaming", "subscription", "retention"], "series": ["Retention Zone", "Episode"], "disc": ["Cleeng"], "sponsors": [{"name": "Cleeng", "where": "body"}], "subject": ["Cleeng"], "media": "article", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1880, 51, 1, 6]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 352, "id": 7383433858400030720, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7383433858400030720/", "ts": "2025-10-13T09:30:09+00:00", "date": "2025-10-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — feeling the pulse of the industry", "snippet": "🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — feeling the pulse of the industry", "chars": 1093, "text": "🏟️ SPORTEL Monaco 2025 — feeling the pulse of the industry\n\nOn Monday, October 20, I’ll be in Monaco for SPORTEL 2025 — a close-to-home focused visit to feel the vibe of the industry.\nEvery year, SPORTEL is a thermometer for how the sports media business really feels — behind the glossy decks and the new slogans.\n It’s where rights, technology, storytelling, and investment meet in the same corridors.\n\nAnd this year, the big undercurrents are easy to sense already:\n ➡️ AI quietly becoming part of production and fan engagement\n ➡️ The balance between broadcast, streaming, and creator-led distribution\n ➡️ Investors looking for sustainable growth, not just scale\n ➡️ And the eternal question — what fans truly value in a live experience\nI’ll be there to listen, connect, and take notes.\n\nThen, as always, I’ll share my takeaways and a short field report a few days later — my read of what’s real, what’s noise, and what’s next.\nIf you’re around on Monday, let’s connect — coffee, corridor, or just a quick hello.\nA guy with a scarf The One Man Brand\n#SPORTEL2025 #SportsBusiness #MediaTech", "qtext": "", "imp": 1398, "rx": 22, "cm": 0, "rp": 3, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["SPORTEL", "company"], ["A guy with a scarf", "company"], ["The One Man Brand", "showcase"]], "tags": ["sportel2025", "sportsbusiness", "mediatech"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1398, 22, 0, 3]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 353, "id": 7383370058552475648, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7383370058552475648/", "ts": "2025-10-13T05:16:38+00:00", "date": "2025-10-13", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "Ep. 4: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... to Chris Redmond ©️®️", "snippet": "Ep. 4: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... to Chris Redmond ©️®️", "chars": 1209, "text": "Ep. 4: A guy with a scarf asks a question to... to Chris Redmond ©️®️\n🎙️ Q: What are the traits and skills to get hired in our industry in 2026?\n\n💬 Chris’s take:\n The game of hiring has changed — and not always for the better.\n AI and automation aren’t necessarily improving recruitment; they’re exposing how broken and outdated many processes already were.\n➡️ Recognize that job hunting today can be demoralizing — applications often disappear into a black hole.\n ➡️ Stop “job hunting” and start “job farming”: use your LinkedIn network as fertile ground. Those connections are your community, not random numbers.\n ➡️ Productise yourself: craft your narrative — where you’ve been, where you are, where you want to go — and anchor it around what you truly love.\n ➡️ And finally, don’t underestimate likability. Skills matter, but positivity and energy are what make people want to work with you.\nChris puts it perfectly: “If you go in with years of experience but speak in a polluted way about where the industry is going, that’s going to put people off.”\n\n🎧 Watch or listen the full 3-minute video on A Guy with a Scarf asks a question to… Chris Redmond below.\n\nOTTRED RedHolt\n\n#hiring #media #sportstech #ai", "qtext": "", "imp": 653, "rx": 9, "cm": 2, "rp": 5, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": ["video"], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf asks a question to...", "showcase"], ["Chris Redmond ©️®️", "person"], ["OTTRED", "company"], ["RedHolt", "company"]], "tags": ["hiring", "media", "sportstech", "ai"], "series": ["Episode"], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "video", "images": 0, "links": 0, "pa": {"video_views": 24, "watch_time_s": 99, "avg_watch_s": 4, "saves": 1, "sends": 0, "fetched": "2026-10-24"}, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 653, 9, 2, 5]], "quoted": null}, {"i": 354, "id": 7383163535813586944, "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7383163535813586944/", "ts": "2025-10-12T15:35:59+00:00", "date": "2025-10-12", "approx": false, "kind": "post", "author": "Carlo De Marchis", "with": null, "title": "📺 📲 The Creator Economy: Powering MIPCOM 2025", "snippet": "📺 📲 The Creator Economy: Powering MIPCOM 2025", "chars": 2126, "text": "📺 📲 The Creator Economy: Powering MIPCOM 2025\nI will not be there... I am in full FOMO and so I asked myself:\n❓ Why should I be there?\n🎯 Not to buy or sell shows, but to observe a world in transformation.\n1️⃣ Because the line between TV, streaming, and creators just blurred\n This year MIPCOM isn’t only about broadcasters selling shows — it’s openly courting creators and digital storytellers.\n YouTube has a full program, there’s a “Creator–TV matchmaking” track, and brands like Snap and TikTok are pitching narrative collaborations.\n It’s the moment where the creator economy meets the commissioning economy — your natural intersection.\n2️⃣ Because AI and real-time tools are quietly reshaping production\n Panels on AI-assisted workflows, synthetic actors, metadata-driven commissioning, and audience intelligence are everywhere.\n For someone who talks about real-time fan engagement and AI as a digital touchpoint, this is a great place to hear how traditional TV is absorbing these ideas — often two years behind sports.\n3️⃣ Because global buyers are back — but confused\n The streamers are tightening belts, broadcasters are regrouping, FAST channels are multiplying.\n You’ll find the same tension you discuss around retention vs. reach — but in a different domain.\n It’s valuable context for anyone advising on product, UX, or monetization in media.\n4️⃣ Because brand storytelling is finally mainstream there\n There’s a BrandStorytelling Summit on Monday — Cannes Lions meets MIPCOM.\n That’s where sports-adjacent brands, sponsors, and content studios are pitching long-form branded entertainment.\n You could easily connect dots between fan engagement models in sports and branded content in entertainment.\n5️⃣ Because the conversations are widening\n MIPCOM used to be “sell, buy, repeat.”\n Now it’s co-creation, rights innovation, IP as a service, global storytelling — the same vocabulary you’ve been using.\n Being there isn’t about buying or selling shows; it’s about reading the temperature of the entire content ecosystem from another angle.\n\nThoughts for A guy with a scarf?\n\n#media #ai #content #creators #mipcom", "qtext": "", "imp": 1338, "rx": 10, "cm": 6, "rp": 4, "boosted": false, "article": false, "edited": false, "attachments": [], "mentions": [["A guy with a scarf", "company"]], "tags": ["media", "ai", "content", "creators", "mipcom"], "series": [], "disc": [], "sponsors": [], "subject": [], "media": "images", "images": 1, "links": 0, "pa": null, "hist": [["2026-10-17", 1338, 10, 6, 4]], "quoted": null}]};
